"To find the missing value for assets, we need to use the accounting equation:\n\n\\[ \\text{Assets} = \\text{Liabilities} + \\text{Equity} \\]\n\nIn this context, Equity can be calculated as:\n\nEquity = Common Stock + (Revenue - Expenses) - Dividends\n\nFirst, calculate the equity:\n\n1. Revenue - Expenses = $785,000 - $234,750 = $550,250\n2. Equity = $60,000 (Common Stock) + $550,250 - $15,000 (Dividends)\n3. Equity = $60,000 + $550,250 - $15,000 = $595,250\n\nNow apply the accounting equation:\n\nAssets = Liabilities + Equity\nAssets = $110,500 (Liabilities) + $595,250 (Equity)\nAssets = $705,750\n\nTherefore, the missing value for assets is $705,750, corresponding to option A.\n\nANSWER: A"
"To calculate the expected risk premium of investing in equities versus risk-free T-bills, follow these steps:\n\n1. **Calculate the Expected Return from Investing in Equities:**\n\n   - Probability (Equity Up) = 0.6\n   - Return (Equity Up) = $50,000\n   - Probability (Equity Down) = 0.4\n   - Return (Equity Down) = -$30,000\n\n   Expected Return (Equities) = (0.6 * $50,000) + (0.4 * -$30,000)\n                             = $30,000 + (-$12,000)\n                             = $18,000\n\n2. **Return from Risk-free T-bills:**\n\n   Return (T-bills) = $5,000\n\n3. **Calculate the Risk Premium:**\n\n   Risk Premium = Expected Return (Equities) - Return (T-bills)\n                = $18,000 - $5,000\n                = $13,000\n\nBased on this calculation, the expected risk premium in dollars is $13,000.\n\nANSWER: C."
