"To find the value of the investor's portfolio at the end of the year for the stand-alone (SA) hedge fund:\n\n1. **Calculate the Ending Value for Each Hedge Fund:**\n\n   - **Hedge Fund 1:**\n     - Start of year value: $100 million\n     - Rate of return: 20%\n     - Ending value = $100 million \u00d7 (1 + 0.20) = $120 million\n\n   - **Hedge Fund 2:**\n     - Start of year value: $100 million\n     - Rate of return: 10%\n     - Ending value = $100 million \u00d7 (1 + 0.10) = $110 million\n\n   - **Hedge Fund 3:**\n     - Start of year value: $100 million\n     - Rate of return: 30%\n     - Ending value = $100 million \u00d7 (1 + 0.30) = $130 million\n\n2. **Calculate the Total Ending Value for the SA Fund:**\n\n   - Total ending value = $120 million + $110 million + $130 million = $360 million\n\n3. **Calculate the Incentive Fee for the SA Fund:**\n\n   - Incentive fee = 20% of total returns\n   - Total returns = Total ending value - Total initial value\n   - Total initial value = $100 million + $100 million + $100 million = $300 million\n   - Total returns = $360 million - $300 million = $60 million\n   - Incentive fee = $60 million \u00d7 0.20 = $12 million\n\n4. **Calculate the Net Ending Value for the Investor:**\n\n   - Net ending value = Total ending value - Incentive fee\n   - Net ending value = $360 million - $12 million = $348 million\n\nTherefore, the value of the investor's portfolio at the end of the year is $348 million.\n\nANSWER: C."
"To compute the cost of goods sold, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. **Calculate Total Manufacturing Costs:**\n\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total Raw Materials Used} = \\text{Beginning Raw Materials Inventory} + \\text{Purchases} - \\text{Ending Raw Materials Inventory}\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total Raw Materials Used} = 17,433 + 77,321 - 16,428 = 78,326\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total Manufacturing Costs} = \\text{Total Raw Materials Used} + \\text{Direct Labor} + \\text{Overhead}\n   \\]\n\n   Overhead is 75% of Direct Labor:\n\n   \\[\n   \\text{Overhead} = 0.75 \\times 125,900 = 94,425\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total Manufacturing Costs} = 78,326 + 125,900 + 94,425 = 298,651\n   \\]\n\n2. **Calculate Cost of Goods Manufactured (COGM):**\n\n   \\[\n   \\text{COGM} = \\text{Total Manufacturing Costs} + \\text{Beginning Work in Process Inventory} - \\text{Ending Work in Process Inventory}\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   \\text{COGM} = 298,651 + 241,439 - 234,423 = 305,667\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculate Cost of Goods Sold (COGS):**\n\n   \\[\n   \\text{COGS} = \\text{COGM} + \\text{Beginning Finished Goods Inventory} - \\text{Ending Finished Goods Inventory}\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   \\text{COGS} = 305,667 + 312,842 - 342,384 = 276,125\n   \\]\n\nANSWER: C."
"To find the missing amounts for Company D, we need to calculate the sales in dollars and total fixed expenses based on the given information.\n\nFor Company D:\n- Units sold = 900\n- Total variable expenses = $144,000\n- Per unit contribution margin (C/M) = $140\n- Net operating income (loss) = ($24,000)\n\n**Step 1: Calculate sales in dollars**\n\\[\n\\text{Sales in dollars} = \\text{Units sold} \\times (\\text{Per unit C/M} + \\text{Variable expenses per unit})\n\\]\nFirst, find the variable expenses per unit:\n\\[\n\\text{Variable expenses per unit} = \\frac{\\text{Total variable expenses}}{\\text{Units sold}} = \\frac{144,000}{900} = 160\n\\]\nTherefore,\n\\[\n\\text{Sales in dollars} = 900 \\times (140 + 160) = 900 \\times 300 = 270,000\n\\]\n\n**Step 2: Calculate total fixed expenses**\n\\[\n\\text{Net operating income (loss)} = \\text{Sales} - \\text{Total variable expenses} - \\text{Total fixed expenses}\n\\]\nRearrange to find total fixed expenses:\n\\[\n\\text{Total fixed expenses} = \\text{Sales} - \\text{Total variable expenses} - \\text{Net operating income (loss)}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\text{Total fixed expenses} = 270,000 - 144,000 - (-24,000) = 270,000 - 144,000 + 24,000 = 150,000\n\\]\n\nNow that both missing pieces are calculated, we look at the answer choices, which provide:\nA. $270,000, $150,000.\n\nANSWER: A."
