"To determine which stock is the best choice for an investor holding only a single risky asset, we can calculate the Sharpe ratio for each stock. The Sharpe ratio is a measure of the risk-adjusted return, calculated as \\((\\text{Expected Excess Return}) / (\\text{Standard Deviation of Excess Return})\\).\n\n**Stock A:**\n\n- Expected Excess Return = Alpha + Beta * (Market Excess Return)\n  - Alpha (A) = 1%\n  - Beta (A) = 1.2\n\n\\[ \\text{Excess Return (A)} = 1\\% + 1.2 \\times (14\\% - 6\\%) = 1\\% + 9.6\\% = 10.6\\% \\]\n\n- Standard Deviation of Excess Return = 21.6%\n\n\\[ \\text{Sharpe Ratio (A)} = \\frac{10.6\\%}{21.6\\%} \\approx 0.4917 \\]\n\n**Stock B:**\n\n- Expected Excess Return = Alpha + Beta * (Market Excess Return)\n  - Alpha (B) = 2%\n  - Beta (B) = 0.8\n\n\\[ \\text{Excess Return (B)} = 2\\% + 0.8 \\times (14\\% - 6\\%) = 2\\% + 6.4\\% = 8.4\\% \\]\n\n- Standard Deviation of Excess Return = 24.9%\n\n\\[ \\text{Sharpe Ratio (B)} = \\frac{8.4\\%}{24.9\\%} \\approx 0.3373 \\]\n\n**Conclusion:**\n\nComparing the Sharpe ratios:\n- Stock A: 0.4917\n- Stock B: 0.3373\n\nStock A has a higher Sharpe ratio, indicating it offers a better risk-adjusted return compared to Stock B.\n\nANSWER: A"
