"To find the missing value for Company 2, we use the formula for calculating the ending balance of owner's equity:\n\n\\[ \\text{Ending Balance} = \\text{Beginning Balance} + \\text{Net Income} - \\text{Net Loss} + \\text{Investments} - \\text{Distributions} \\]\n\nGiven for Company 2:\n\n- Beginning Balance = $63,180\n- Net Income = 0\n- Net Loss = $12,000\n- Investments = 0\n- Ending Balance = $44,880\n\nPlug the known values into the formula and solve for Distributions:\n\n\\[ 44,880 = 63,180 + 0 - 12,000 + 0 - \\text{Distributions} \\]\n\n\\[ 44,880 = 51,180 - \\text{Distributions} \\]\n\n\\[ \\text{Distributions} = 51,180 - 44,880 \\]\n\n\\[ \\text{Distributions} = 6,300 \\]\n\nANSWER: D"
"To find out how much you will pay for the February 110 call options, use the following steps:\n\n1. **Option Price**: The price for one February 110 call option is $7.60.\n2. **Contracts**: You are buying 10 contracts.\n3. **Shares per Contract**: Each contract typically covers 100 shares.\n\nCalculate the total cost:\n\n\\[ \\text{Total Cost} = \\text{Option Price} \\times \\text{Contracts} \\times \\text{Shares per Contract} \\]\n\n\\[ \\text{Total Cost} = 7.60 \\times 10 \\times 100 = 7,600 \\]\n\nANSWER: A"
