"To determine which project to choose, we need to compare their Net Present Values (NPVs). Since Project A is in real terms and Project B is in nominal terms, we need to adjust Project A using the real discount rate and Project B using the nominal discount rate.\n\n### Calculating the real discount rate:\n1. Nominal discount rate (\\(i\\)): 13%\n2. Inflation rate (\\(r\\)): 4%\n3. Real discount rate (\\(r_{\\text{real}}\\)): \n\n\\[\nr_{\\text{real}} = \\frac{1 + i}{1 + r} - 1 = \\frac{1 + 0.13}{1 + 0.04} - 1 = 0.0865 \\text{ or } 8.65\\%\n\\]\n\n### NPV of Project A\nUsing the real discount rate (8.65%):\n\n\\[\n\\text{NPV}_{A} = \\frac{-30,000}{(1 + 0.0865)^0} + \\frac{18,000}{(1 + 0.0865)^1} + \\frac{16,000}{(1 + 0.0865)^2} + \\frac{12,000}{(1 + 0.0865)^3}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\text{NPV}_{A} = -30,000 + \\frac{18,000}{1.0865} + \\frac{16,000}{1.0865^2} + \\frac{12,000}{1.0865^3}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\text{NPV}_{A} \\approx -30,000 + 16,562.78 + 13,556.96 + 9,320.56 \\approx 9,440.30\n\\]\n\n### NPV of Project B\nUsing the nominal discount rate (13%):\n\n\\[\n\\text{NPV}_{B} = \\frac{-45,000}{(1 + 0.13)^0} + \\frac{21,000}{(1 + 0.13)^1} + \\frac{23,000}{(1 + 0.13)^2} + \\frac{25,000}{(1 + 0.13)^3}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\text{NPV}_{B} = -45,000 + \\frac{21,000}{1.13} + \\frac{23,000}{1.13^2} + \\frac{25,000}{1.13^3}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\text{NPV}_{B} \\approx -45,000 + 18,584.07 + 18,025.83 + 17,465.68 \\approx 9,075.58\n\\]\n\n### Decision:\nCompare NPVs:\n- \\(\\text{NPV}_{A} = 9,440.30\\)\n- \\(\\text{NPV}_{B} = 9,075.58\\)\n\nProject A has a higher NPV. \n\nANSWER: A."
"To find the expected market risk premium using the Capital Asset Pricing Model (CAPM), we need to use the formula:\n\n\\[\nE(R_i) = R_f + \\beta_i \\times (E(R_m) - R_f)\n\\]\n\nwhere:\n- \\(E(R_i)\\) is the expected return of the stock,\n- \\(R_f\\) is the risk-free rate,\n- \\(\\beta_i\\) is the beta of the stock,\n- \\(E(R_m)\\) is the expected return of the market.\n\nGiven that Stock A's beta is 0.25 greater than Stock B's beta, and since we want to find the expected market risk premium (i.e., \\(E(R_m) - R_f\\)), we can derive this from the expected returns of the stocks.\n\n### Step-by-step Calculation:\n\n1. **Calculate the Expected Return of Stock A \\(E(R_A)\\):**\n\n   \\[\n   E(R_A) = 0.15 \\times (-0.10) + 0.60 \\times 0.09 + 0.25 \\times 0.32 \n          = -0.015 + 0.054 + 0.080 \n          = 0.119 \n   \\]\n\n2. **Calculate the Expected Return of Stock B \\(E(R_B)\\):**\n\n   \\[\n   E(R_B) = 0.15 \\times (-0.08) + 0.60 \\times 0.08 + 0.25 \\times 0.26 \n          = -0.012 + 0.048 + 0.065 \n          = 0.101 \n   \\]\n\n3. **Set up the equations for both stocks using CAPM:**\n\n   \\[\n   0.119 = R_f + (\\beta_B + 0.25) \\times \\text{(Market Risk Premium)}\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   0.101 = R_f + \\beta_B \\times \\text{(Market Risk Premium)}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Subtract the second equation from the first to find the Market Risk Premium:**\n\n   \\[\n   0.119 - 0.101 = 0.25 \\times \\text{(Market Risk Premium)}\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   0.018 = 0.25 \\times \\text{(Market Risk Premium)}\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   \\text{Market Risk Premium} = \\frac{0.018}{0.25} = 0.072 \n   \\]\n\nThus, the expected market risk premium is \\(7.2\\%\\).\n\nANSWER: C"
