"To calculate the expected return for each stock, we'll use the formula for expected return:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Expected Return} = (\\text{Probability of Recession} \\times \\text{Return in Recession}) + (\\text{Probability of Normal} \\times \\text{Return in Normal}) + (\\text{Probability of Boom} \\times \\text{Return in Boom})\n\\]\n\nLet's calculate the expected return for Stock A:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Expected Return for Stock A} = (0.30 \\times 0.06) + (0.55 \\times 0.07) + (0.15 \\times 0.11)\n\\]\n\n\\[\n= 0.018 + 0.0385 + 0.0165 = 0.073\n\\]\n\nStock A's expected return is 7.30%.\n\nNow, let's calculate the expected return for Stock B:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Expected Return for Stock B} = (0.30 \\times -0.20) + (0.55 \\times 0.13) + (0.15 \\times 0.33)\n\\]\n\n\\[\n= -0.06 + 0.0715 + 0.0495 = 0.061\n\\]\n\nStock B's expected return is 6.10%.\n\nBased on the calculations, the correct choice is:\n\nANSWER: A. (1):7.30% (2):6.10%"
