"To find the post-merger price per share of Firm A, follow these steps:\n\n1. **Calculate the Total Earnings Post-Merger:**\n\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total earnings of A+B} = 2100 + 750 = 2850\n   \\]\n\n2. **Determine the Exchange Ratio:**\n\n   Price offered for each share of Firm B = $13\n   \n   Current price of Firm A\u2019s share = $60\n   \n   Exchange ratio = $13 / $60\n\n3. **Calculate the Number of New Shares Issued:**\n\n   \\[\n   \\text{Number of shares of Firm B} = 300\n   \\]\n   \n   \\[\n   \\text{Number of new shares issued} = 300 \\times \\left(\\frac{13}{60}\\right) = 65\n   \\]\n\n4. **Calculate the Total Shares Outstanding Post-Merger:**\n\n   \\[\n   \\text{Total shares outstanding of A post-merger} = 900 + 65 = 965\n   \\]\n\n5. **Calculate the Post-Merger Earnings Per Share (EPS):**\n\n   \\[\n   \\text{EPS post-merger} = \\frac{2850}{965} \\approx 2.9541\n   \\]\n\n6. **Determine Firm A\u2019s Price Per Share Post-Merger:**\n\n   Since the P/E ratio is assumed to remain constant:\n\n   \\[\n   \\text{Firm A's initial P/E ratio} = \\frac{60}{\\frac{2100}{900}} = \\frac{60}{2.3333} \\approx 25.714\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   \\text{New price per share} = \\text{EPS post-merger} \\times \\text{P/E ratio}\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   \\text{New price per share} \\approx 2.9541 \\times 25.714 \\approx 75.94\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the answer is:\n\nANSWER: A."
"To determine the allocations to T-bills and equity, we use the following formula for the optimal proportion \\( y \\) to invest in the risky asset (S&P 500) given a risk-aversion coefficient \\( A \\):\n\n\\[ y = \\frac{E(R_p) - R_f}{A \\cdot \\sigma_p^2} \\]\n\nWhere:\n- \\( E(R_p) \\) is the expected return of the risky portfolio.\n- \\( R_f \\) is the risk-free rate (T-bills return).\n- \\( \\sigma_p^2 \\) is the variance of the risky portfolio's returns (square of the standard deviation).\n\nFrom the data in the period 1926-2009:\n- Risk Premium: 7.93%\n- Standard Deviation: 20.81%\n\nConvert percentages to decimals:\n- \\( E(R_p) - R_f = 0.0793 \\)\n- \\( \\sigma_p = 0.2081 \\)\n- \\( \\sigma_p^2 = (0.2081)^2 = 0.0433 \\)\n\nGiven \\( A = 4 \\), plug the values into the formula:\n\n\\[ y = \\frac{0.0793}{4 \\cdot 0.0433} \\approx \\frac{0.0793}{0.1732} \\approx 0.4578 \\]\n\nSo, approximately 45.78% of the portfolio should be allocated to the S&P 500 (equity). The remainder, \\( 1 - y = 0.5422 \\), or 54.22%, should be allocated to T-bills.\n\nANSWER: B."
