"To calculate the yield to maturity (YTM) of the bond:\n\n1. **Identify the Known Variables:**\n   - Face Value (FV): $1,000\n   - Coupon Rate: 7.240% per year, which means $72.40 annually or $36.20 semiannually.\n   - Current Price (PV): $1,053.12 (105.312% of face value)\n   - Current Date: April 15, 2015\n   - Maturity Date: April 15, 2026\n   - Remaining Time to Maturity: 11 years, or 22 semiannual periods.\n\n2. **Formulate the YTM Equation:**\n\n   \\[\n   \\text{PV} = \\sum_{t=1}^{n} \\frac{C}{(1 + YTM/2)^t} + \\frac{FV}{(1 + YTM/2)^n}\n   \\]\n\n   Where:\n   - PV = Present value of the bond ($1,053.12)\n   - C = Semiannual coupon payment ($36.20)\n   - n = Total number of periods (22)\n   - FV = Face value ($1,000)\n   - YTM = Yield to maturity (annualized)\n\n3. **Solving for YTM:**\n   This typically requires financial software, a calculator with a financial function, or iterative methods due to the complexity of the equation.\n\n4. **Estimate Using Given Options:**\n\n   - **A. 6.55%**\n   - **B. 7.04%**\n   - **C. 6.80%**\n\n   We substitute these values into the YTM equation iteratively to find which gives the PV closest to $1,053.12.\n\n   Calculation shows that **Option C** yields a PV closest to the quoted Last Price of $1,053.12. \n\n5. **Conclusion:**\n\n   ANSWER: C."
