"To find the prices of the call and put options, we can use the Black-Scholes option pricing model. Here are the steps involved:\n\n1. **Identify the variables:**\n   - Stock price (S) = $57\n   - Exercise price (K) = $60\n   - Risk-free rate (r) = 6% per year, compounded continuously\n   - Time to maturity (T) = 4 months = 4/12 years\n   - Volatility (\u03c3) = 54% per year\n\n2. **Black-Scholes formula:**\n   The formula for the call option (C) is:\n   \\[\n   C = S \\cdot N(d_1) - K \\cdot e^{-rT} \\cdot N(d_2)\n   \\]\n\n   For the put option (P), it is:\n   \\[\n   P = K \\cdot e^{-rT} \\cdot N(-d_2) - S \\cdot N(-d_1)\n   \\]\n\n   where:\n   \\[\n   d_1 = \\frac{\\ln(S/K) + (r + \\sigma^2/2) \\cdot T}{\\sigma \\sqrt{T}}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   d_2 = d_1 - \\sigma \\sqrt{T}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculate d\u2081 and d\u2082:**\n   \\[\n   d_1 = \\frac{\\ln(57/60) + (0.06 + 0.54^2/2) \\cdot (4/12)}{0.54 \\sqrt{4/12}}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   d_2 = d_1 - 0.54 \\sqrt{4/12}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Compute N(d\u2081) and N(d\u2082):**\n   Calculate the cumulative normal distribution values N(d\u2081) and N(d\u2082) for the computed d\u2081 and d\u2082.\n\n5. **Compute the option prices:**\n   - Call option price (C)\n   - Put option price (P)\n\n6. **Compare with given choices to find the correct pair:**\n\nAfter calculating these values, the call and put options prices match with choice C: $6.25 for the call option and $7.90 for the put option.\n\nANSWER: C"
