"To solve for the missing amounts for Company B, we need to use the contribution margin concepts and other given data.\n\n1. **Determine Units Sold:**\n   - The formula for Net Operating Income (NOI) is:\n     \\[\n     \\text{NOI} = (\\text{Units Sold} \\times \\text{Per Unit C/M}) - \\text{Total Fixed Expenses}\n     \\]\n   - Using the data for Company B:\n     \\[\n     \\$6,000 = (\\text{Units Sold} \\times \\$80) - \\$50,000\n     \\]\n   - Solve for Units Sold:\n     \\[\n     \\text{Units Sold} \\times \\$80 = \\$6,000 + \\$50,000 = \\$56,000\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     \\text{Units Sold} = \\frac{\\$56,000}{\\$80} = 700\n     \\]\n\n2. **Calculate Total Variable Expenses:**\n   - Sales Revenue is given as $70,000.\n   - Using the formula:\n     \\[\n     \\text{Sales Revenue} = (\\text{Units Sold} \\times \\text{Selling Price per Unit})\n     \\]\n   - If Units Sold = 700 and Sales Revenue = $70,000:\n     \\[\n     \\text{Selling Price per Unit} = \\frac{\\$70,000}{700} = \\$100\n     \\]\n   - Contribution Margin per Unit = $80, so Variable Cost per Unit = $100 - $80 = $20.\n   - Total Variable Expenses = Units Sold \u00d7 Variable Cost per Unit:\n     \\[\n     \\text{Total Variable Expenses} = 700 \\times \\$20 = \\$14,000\n     \\]\n\nThus, the missing amounts for Company B are:\nUnits Sold = 700, Total Variable Expenses = $14,000\n\nANSWER: A"
"To calculate the 6-month horizon return for each bond, we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. **Interest Rate Change Impact:**\n   - Calculate the price change caused by a 50 basis point (0.50%) decline in interest rates using the duration formula:\n     \\[\n     \\text{Price Change} = -(\\text{Effective Duration}) \\times \\Delta \\text{Yield} \\times \\text{Initial Price}\n     \\]\n\n2. **Calculate Horizon Price:**\n   - Add the price change to the initial market price to estimate the bond price after the rate change.\n\n3. **Calculate Horizon Return:**\n   - Use the actual bond prices at the end of 6 months to calculate the return:\n     \\[\n     \\text{Horizon Return} = \\frac{\\text{Actual Price at Horizon} - \\text{Initial Price} + \\text{Coupon Payment}}{\\text{Initial Price}}\n     \\]\n\n**Calculations for CIC Bond:**\n\n- Initial Price: 101.75\n- Duration: 7.35\n- Price Change:\n  \\[\n  \\text{Price Change} = -7.35 \\times (-0.005) \\times 101.75 = 3.7388125\n  \\]\n- Estimated Horizon Price: \n  \\[\n  101.75 + 3.7388125 = 105.4888125\n  \\]\n- Actual Horizon Price: 105.55\n- Coupon Payment (5.25% / 2): 2.625\n- Horizon Return:\n  \\[\n  \\left(\\frac{105.55 - 101.75 + 2.625}{101.75}\\right) \\times 100 = 6.314\\%\n  \\]\n\n**Calculations for PTR Bond:**\n\n- Initial Price: 101.75\n- Duration: 5.40\n- Price Change:\n  \\[\n  \\text{Price Change} = -5.40 \\times (-0.005) \\times 101.75 = 2.74725\n  \\]\n- Estimated Horizon Price:\n  \\[\n  101.75 + 2.74725 = 104.49725\n  \\]\n- Actual Horizon Price: 104.15\n- Coupon Payment (6.35% / 2): 3.175\n- Horizon Return:\n  \\[\n  \\left(\\frac{104.15 - 101.75 + 3.175}{101.75}\\right) \\times 100 = 5.479\\%\n  \\]\n\nThe correct answer is:\n\nANSWER: B. (1)6.314% ; (2)5.479%."
