"To determine the correct choice, let's review the definition of a necrotroph:\n\n- Necrotrophs are a type of plant pathogen that kill host tissue before colonizing it. They secrete enzymes and toxins to break down plant cells, causing tissue death, and then feed on the dead organic matter.\n\nGiven this definition, let's evaluate the options:\n\nA. It means that the pathogen kills the host tissue before colonising it.  \nThis is an accurate description of necrotrophs.\n\nB. It means that the pathogen will only grow on material that has died previously.  \nThis is not specific to necrotrophs, as necrotrophs actively kill tissue themselves.\n\nC. It describes the fungal group that the pathogen is in.  \nWhile necrotrophs can be fungi, the term itself describes their mode of action, not a taxonomic group.\n\nD. I don't know and don't want to guess.  \nWe have a strong answer based on our knowledge, so this can be reasonably eliminated.\n\nE. It means that the pathogen will rot the host plant.  \nWhile related to rotting, the key distinction is that necrotrophs kill the tissue actively.\n\nTherefore, the most accurate choice is:\n\nANSWER: A."
