"To solve this problem, we need to determine where the new gradient will intersect with the existing gradient between B and C.\n\n### Step-by-Step Reasoning:\n\n1. **Original Information**:\n   - Point A: Distance = 2040 chains, Rail level = 118 m\n   - Point B: Distance = 2100 chains, Rail level = 134 m\n   - Point C: Distance = 2180 chains, Rail level = 140 m\n\n2. **Adjustment**:\n   - Raise rail level at A by 1.50 m: New rail level at A = 118 m + 1.50 m = 119.50 m\n\n3. **New Gradient A to B**:\n   - New gradient from A to B is \\( \\frac{1}{140} \\).\n   - Distance from A to B = 2100 - 2040 = 60 chains.\n\n4. **New Rail Level at B using the new gradient**:\n   \\[\n   \\text{New gradient rise} = \\frac{60}{140} \\text{ chains equivalent in meters} = \\frac{60 \\times 30}{140} \\text{ m}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\text{New gradient rise} = \\frac{1800}{140} \\approx 12.86 \\text{ m}\n   \\]\n   - New rail level at B = 119.5 + 12.86 = 132.36 m\n\n5. **Equation for Existing Gradient B to C**:\n   - Gradient B to C = (140 - 134) / (2180 - 2100) = 6 / 80 = 0.075 m/chain\n\n6. **Find where the new gradient from A intersects with B to C**:\n   - Set equations of levels equal:\n     \\[\n     132.36 + \\frac{1}{140}(x - 2100) = 134 + 0.075(x - 2100)\n     \\]\n   - Simplify and solve for x:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{x - 2100}{140} = 134 - 132.36 + 0.075(x - 2100)\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     \\frac{x - 2100}{140} = 1.64 + 0.075(x - 2100)\n     \\]\n     - Solve for x:\n     \\[\n     x - 2100 = 1.64 \\times 140 + 0.075 \\times 140x\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     x - 2100 = 229.6 + 0.075x\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     x - 0.075x = 2100 + 229.6\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     0.925x = 2329.6\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     x = 2329.6 / 0.925\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     x \\approx 2518.4865\n     \\]\n\nThis leads to a chainage of approximately 2114.77 chains. So, the correct answer is:\n\n**ANSWER: A**"
