"To solve for the radius \\( R \\) of the reverse curve, we set up the problem as follows:\n\nWe know:\n- The distance between the straights is 30 m.\n- The distance between the tangent points A and B is 120 m.\n- \\( R_1 = R_2 = R \\).\n\nA reverse curve consists of two circular arcs, in this case, they are symmetrical. The configuration forms a rectangle with the distance between the arcs across the straights, with the following relevant geometry:\n\n1. The tangential lengths \\( V_1 \\) and \\( V_2 \\) from the tangent points (A and B) to the point of intersection of parallel straights.\n\nUsing the properties of a reverse curve:\n\nThe relation between the tangent length, radius, and offset from the centerline (in this case, half of the 30 m between straights) is given by:\n\\[ V = R \\cdot \\sin(\\Delta/2) \\]\n\nFor these symmetrical arcs:\n\\[ \\Delta = \\frac{120}{R} \\]\n\\[ \\Delta/2 = \\frac{60}{R} \\]\n\nThe perpendicular distance from the center of the curve to the chord (half of 30 m) is equal to:\n\\[ R(1 - \\cos(\\Delta/2)) = 15 \\]\n\nUsing the small angle approximation for \\( \\cos \\), approximately:\n\\[ R \\cdot \\left(1 - \\left(1 - \\frac{(\\Delta/2)^2}{2}\\right)\\right) = 15 \\]\n\\[ R \\cdot \\frac{\\Delta^2}{8R^2} = 15 \\] \n\\[ \\frac{120^2}{8R} = 15 \\]\n\\[ 14400 = 120R \\]\n\\[ R = 120 \\]\n\nThus, the calculated radius \\( R \\) is 120 m.\n\nANSWER: B"
