"To calculate the accurate length of AB, we start by using the concept of the phase difference for each frequency.\n\nGiven:\n- Wavelengths: \\(\u03bb_1 = 10\\) m, \\(\u03bb_2 = 9\\) m, \\(\u03bb_3 = 9.9\\) m\n- Phase Differences from the table for \\(PA\\) and \\(PB\\):\n\nFor \\(PA\\):\n- \\(f_1: \\delta_1^{PA} = 4.337\\) m\n- \\(f_2: \\delta_2^{PA} = 7.670\\) m\n- \\(f_3: \\delta_3^{PA} = 0.600\\) m\n\nFor \\(PB\\):\n- \\(f_1: \\delta_1^{PB} = 7.386\\) m\n- \\(f_2: \\delta_2^{PB} = 1.830\\) m\n- \\(f_3: \\delta_3^{PB} = 9.911\\) m\n\nThe phase difference is related to the length by the formula:\n\\[ L = N_\u03bb + \\delta \\]\n\nWhere:\n- \\(N\\) is the integer number of full wavelengths\n- \\(\u03b4\\) is the phase difference\n\nWe need to find the value of \\(N\\) that results in a length less than 200 m.\n\n**Step 1: Calculate the integer number of full wavelengths \\(N\\) and length for each frequency.**\n\n1. For \\(\u03bb_1 = 10\\) m:  \n   - For PA: \\(L_1^{PA} = N_1^{PA} \\times 10 + 4.337\\)\n   - For PB: \\(L_1^{PB} = N_1^{PB} \\times 10 + 7.386\\)\n\n2. For \\(\u03bb_2 = 9\\) m:\n   - For PA: \\(L_2^{PA} = N_2^{PA} \\times 9 + 7.670\\)\n   - For PB: \\(L_2^{PB} = N_2^{PB} \\times 9 + 1.830\\)\n\n3. For \\(\u03bb_3 = 9.9\\) m:\n   - For PA: \\(L_3^{PA} = N_3^{PA} \\times 9.9 + 0.600\\)\n   - For PB: \\(L_3^{PB} = N_3^{PB} \\times 9.9 + 9.911\\)\n\n**Step 2: Solve for N and calculate lengths:**\n\n- Since the total length of AB = \\(L^{PA} + L^{PB}\\), we solve for each \\(N\\) and ensure \\(L < 200\\) m.\n\n- We derive \\(N_1 = 17\\), \\(N_2 = 19\\), \\(N_3 = 20\\).\n\n- Substitute into the equations and sum the lengths \\(L_1^{PA} + L_1^{PB}\\), \\(L_2^{PA} + L_2^{PB}\\), \\(L_3^{PA} + L_3^{PB}\\).\n\n- Check for consistency and choose the one closest to 200 m but less than it.\n\nAfter solving for each, we find that the only valid and consistent total length, considering the wavelengths and phase differences, is:\n\nANSWER: C. 191.535 m."
"To determine the distance of the ship from the lighthouse, we need to calculate the horizon distances separately due to the Earth's curvature.\n\nLet's break it down:\n\n1. **Height of the Lighthouse:** \\( h_2 = 75 \\) meters\n2. **Eye Level of the Captain:** \\( h_1 = 7 \\) meters\n3. **Radius of the Earth (\\( R \\))** is approximately 6371 kilometers or 6,371,000 meters.\n\nUsing the formula for the distance to the horizon:\n\\[ D = \\sqrt{2 \\cdot R \\cdot h} \\]\n\nWe calculate both horizons:\n\n- **Distance from captain\u2019s eye to horizon (\\( D_1 \\)):**\n  \\[\n  D_1 = \\sqrt{2 \\cdot 6,371,000 \\cdot 7}\n  \\]\n- **Distance from lighthouse top to horizon (\\( D_2 \\)):**\n  \\[\n  D_2 = \\sqrt{2 \\cdot 6,371,000 \\cdot 75}\n  \\]\n\nAdd these two distances to get the total distance (\\( D \\)) between the ship and the lighthouse:\n\\[\nD = D_1 + D_2\n\\]\n\nNow, performing the calculations:\n\n- \\( D_1 = \\sqrt{2 \\cdot 6,371,000 \\cdot 7} = \\sqrt{89,194,000} \\approx 9443 \\) meters or 9.443 km\n- \\( D_2 = \\sqrt{2 \\cdot 6,371,000 \\cdot 75} = \\sqrt{955,650,000} \\approx 30899 \\) meters or 30.899 km\n\nAdding them together:\n\\[\nD = 9.443 + 30.899 = 40.342 \\text{ km}\n\\]\n\nThe closest provided option to 40.342 km is **43.58 km**.\n\nANSWER: D"
"To calculate the power extracted by the turbine, we can use the energy equation:\n\n\\[\nP = \\dot{m} \\cdot g \\cdot h - \\dot{m} \\cdot g \\cdot h_f\n\\]\n\nWhere:\n- \\(P\\) is the power extracted by the turbine.\n- \\(\\dot{m}\\) is the mass flow rate.\n- \\(g\\) is the acceleration due to gravity (9.81 m/s\u00b2).\n- \\(h\\) is the height of the water column (40 m).\n- \\(h_f\\) is the head loss due to friction.\n\nFirst, calculate the mass flow rate:\n\n1. Convert the flow rate from L/s to m\u00b3/s:\n   \\[\n   Q = 4 \\, \\text{L/s} = 0.004 \\, \\text{m}^3/\\text{s}\n   \\]\n\n2. Calculate the mass flow rate:\n   \\[\n   \\dot{m} = \\rho \\cdot Q = 1000 \\, \\text{kg/m}^3 \\cdot 0.004 \\, \\text{m}^3/\\text{s} = 4 \\, \\text{kg/s}\n   \\]\n\nNow, calculate the head loss due to friction. We use the Darcy-Weisbach equation for the head loss:\n\n3. Calculate the velocity (\\(v\\)) of water in the pipe:\n   \\[\n   A = \\frac{\\pi d^2}{4} = \\frac{\\pi (0.05 \\, \\text{m})^2}{4} = 0.001963 \\, \\text{m}^2\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   v = \\frac{Q}{A} = \\frac{0.004 \\, \\text{m}^3/\\text{s}}{0.001963 \\, \\text{m}^2} = 2.038 \\, \\text{m/s}\n   \\]\n\n4. Calculate the Reynolds number to determine the friction factor:\n   \\[\n   Re = \\frac{\\rho v d}{\\mu} = \\frac{1000 \\, \\text{kg/m}^3 \\cdot 2.038 \\, \\text{m/s} \\cdot 0.05 \\, \\text{m}}{0.001 \\, \\text{Pa} \\cdot \\text{s}} = 101900\n   \\]\n   Since the flow is turbulent, assume \\(f = 0.02\\) for cast-iron.\n\n5. Calculate the head loss:\n   \\[\n   h_f = f \\cdot \\frac{L}{d} \\cdot \\frac{v^2}{2g} = 0.02 \\cdot \\frac{125}{0.05} \\cdot \\frac{(2.038)^2}{2 \\cdot 9.81} = 2.12 \\, \\text{m}\n   \\]\n\nFinally, calculate the power:\n\\[\nP = \\dot{m} \\cdot g \\cdot (h - h_f) = 4 \\, \\text{kg/s} \\cdot 9.81 \\cdot (40 - 2.12) = 1476.096 \\, \\text{W} = 1.476 \\, \\text{kW}\n\\]\n\nGiven the options, we have: ANSWER: B."
