"To estimate the moment at point B, we can use the concept of static equilibrium and the moment distribution method. Here\u2019s a step-by-step breakdown of the process:\n\n1. **Free Body Diagram and Reactions:**\n   - The loading is a concentrated load of 16 kips at 10 ft from B towards C.\n   - The vertical reaction at A and the horizontal reaction at C can be found first by taking moments around point C and A, respectively.\n\n2. **Moment at B:**\n   - Assume the point of inflection as being where the beam is simply supported due to bending, effectively assuming the beam can be treated as a cantilever.\n   - By taking the moment around point B due to the applied load at C, calculate the moment using the following:\n\n   \\[\n   M_B = P \\times d \n   \\]\n\n   where \\( P = 16 \\text{ kips} \\) and the perpendicular distance \\( d = 10 \\text{ ft} \\).\n\n3. **Calculation:**\n   - \\( M_B = 16 \\text{ kips} \\times 10 \\text{ ft} = 160 \\text{ kip-ft} \\).\n\n   - Divide this by two, since the moment is evenly distributed from B to C in a symmetrical frame.\n   \\[\n   Moment \\, at \\, B = \\frac{160}{4} = 40 \\, \\text{kip-ft} \n   \\]\n\n4. **Conclusion:**\n   From static analysis simplifications based on guessed point load distribution and distance from B, since EI is constant.\n\nANSWER: B. 40 kip-ft."
