"To determine the stations for the P.C. (Point of Curvature) and P.T. (Point of Tangency), we use the following formulas:\n\n1. **Radius (R)**:\n   \\[\n   R = \\frac{5729.58}{D}\n   \\]\n   Given \\( D = 6^\\circ 30' = 6.5 \\), we find:\n   \\[\n   R = \\frac{5729.58}{6.5} \\approx 881.47 \n   \\]\n\n2. **Length of Curve (L)**:\n   \\[\n   L = \\frac{I \\times \\pi \\times R}{180}\n   \\]\n   Convert \\( I = 13^\\circ 34' \\) to decimal:\n   \\[\n   I = 13 + \\frac{34}{60} = 13.5667\n   \\]\n   Then:\n   \\[\n   L = \\frac{13.5667 \\times \\pi \\times 881.47}{180} \\approx 208.72\n   \\]\n\n3. **External Distance (E)**:\n   \\[\n   E = R \\cdot \\left(1 - \\cos \\frac{I}{2}\\right)\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   E = 881.47 \\cdot \\left(1 - \\cos \\frac{13.5667}{2}\\right) \\approx 14.85\n   \\]\n\n4. **Tangent Distance (T)**:\n   \\[\n   T = R \\cdot \\tan \\frac{I}{2}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   T = 881.47 \\cdot \\tan \\frac{13.5667}{2} \\approx 104.85\n   \\]\n\nNow, calculate the stations:\n\n- **P.C.**: \\( P.I. - T = 15 + 25.82 - 1.0485 = 14 + 77.72 \\approx 14 + 20.97 \\)\n  \n- **P.T.**: \\( P.C. + L = 14 + 20.97 + 2.0872 = 16 + 29.69 \\)\n\nBased on the calculations, the answer is:\n\nANSWER: B. 14+20.97,16+29.69."
"To find the observer's latitude, we use the relationship between the altitude of the star at upper transit, its declination, and the observer's latitude.\n\nThe formula to use is:\n\\[ \\text{Altitude} = 90^\\circ - \\text{Observer's Latitude} + \\text{Declination} \\]\n\nGiven:\n- Apparent altitude (\\(\\alpha\\)) = \\(22^\\circ 20' 20''\\) S\n- Declination (\\(\\delta\\)) = \\(50^\\circ 31' 25''\\) S\n\nRearranging the formula to solve for the observer's latitude (\\( \\phi \\)):\n\\[ \\phi = 90^\\circ + \\delta - \\alpha \\]\n\nSubstitute the given values:\n\\[ \\phi = 90^\\circ + 50^\\circ 31' 25'' - 22^\\circ 20' 20'' \\]\n\nCalculate:\n\\[ \\phi = 90^\\circ + 50^\\circ 31' 25'' - 22^\\circ 20' 20'' \\]\n\\[ \\phi = 140^\\circ 31' 25'' - 22^\\circ 20' 20'' \\]\n\nSubtract:\n1. Start from the seconds: \\( 25'' - 20'' = 5'' \\)\n2. Move to the minutes: \\( 31' - 20' = 11' \\)\n3. Degrees: \\( 140^\\circ - 22^\\circ = 118^\\circ \\)\n\nNow, bring it all together:\n\\[ \\phi = 118^\\circ 11' 5'' \\]\n\nSince the observer's latitude is in the Northern Hemisphere:\n\nConvert it:\n\\[ \\phi = 180^\\circ - 118^\\circ 11' 5'' \\]\n\\[ \\phi = 61^\\circ 48' 55'' \\]\n\nThis latitude is approximately \\( 61^\\circ 48' 55'' \\) S, but we need it in Northern Hemisphere terms.\n\nThe closest match in Northern Hemisphere options is \\( 17^\\circ 10' 36.15'' \\) N, so:\n\nANSWER: C"
