"To solve for the elevation of the lower station and the stationing chainage, follow these steps:\n\n1. **Initial Information:**\n   - Upper station elevation: 318.71 ft\n   - Slope distance between stations: 148.61 ft\n   - Vertical angle: -21\u00b026'\n   - Instrument height at upper station: 4.66 ft\n   - Target height at lower station: 4.88 ft\n   - Initial chainage at upper station: 110 + 71.25\n\n2. **Calculate the vertical component of the distance:**\n   - Vertical angle in degrees: \\(-21\u00b026'\\).\n   - Convert angle to decimal degrees: \\(-21 - 26/60 = -21.4333\\) degrees.\n   - Calculate vertical distance: \\(148.61 \\times \\sin(-21.4333^\\circ)\\).\n\n3. **Correct for instrument and target heights:**\n   - Add instrument height (4.66 ft) to initial elevation and subtract vertical distance.\n   - Subtract target height (4.88 ft) from this result to get the elevation of the lower station.\n\n4. **Calculate the horizontal component for chainage:**\n   - Horizontal distance: \\(148.61 \\times \\cos(-21.4333^\\circ)\\).\n   - Add horizontal distance to initial chainage to get the chainage of the lower station.\n\n**Calculations:**\n\n1. **Vertical component:**\n   \\[\n   \\text{Vertical distance} = 148.61 \\times \\sin(-21.4333) \\approx -54.525 \\text{ ft}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Elevation of the lower station:**\n   \\[\n   \\operatorname{elev}_{\\text{lower}} = 318.71 + 4.66 - 54.525 - 4.88 = 264.185 \\text{ ft}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Horizontal component:**\n   \\[\n   \\text{Horizontal distance} = 148.61 \\times \\cos(-21.4333) \\approx 138.333 \\text{ ft}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Chainage of the lower station:**\n   \\[\n   \\text{Initial chainage} = 110 + 71.25 = 110 + 71.25/100 = 111.7125\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\text{Final chainage} = 111.7125 + 138.333/100 = 113.09583\n   \\]\n   - Written as: \\(113 + 9.583\\)\n\nANSWER: A"
"To calculate the area of the closed traverse, we can use the formula for the area of a polygon given its vertices' coordinates, known as the Shoelace Theorem (or Gauss's area formula):\n\n\\[ \\text{Area} = \\frac{1}{2} \\left| \\sum (x_i y_{i+1} - y_i x_{i+1}) \\right| \\]\n\nThe coordinates are:\n\n- A (0, 0)\n- B (170, 320)\n- C (470, 90)\n- D (340, -110)\n- E (-40, -220)\n- Closing back to A (0, 0)\n\nPlug in the coordinates:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\n\\text{Area} = \\frac{1}{2} \\big| & (0 \\cdot 320 + 170 \\cdot 90 + 470 \\cdot (-110) + 340 \\cdot (-220) + (-40) \\cdot 0) \\\\\n& - (0 \\cdot 170 + 320 \\cdot 470 + 90 \\cdot 340 + (-110) \\cdot (-40) + (-220) \\cdot 0) \\big|\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nCalculate each term:\n\n- Sum of \\( x_i y_{i+1} \\): \\( 0 + 15300 + (-51700) + (-74800) + 0 = -111200 \\)\n- Sum of \\( y_i x_{i+1} \\): \\( 0 + 150400 + 30600 + 4400 + 0 = 185400 \\)\n\nPlug these into the formula:\n\n\\[ \\text{Area} = \\frac{1}{2} \\big| -111200 - 185400 \\big| = \\frac{1}{2} \\big| -296600 \\big| = 148300 \\, m^2 \\]\n\nConvert to hectares (\\(1 \\, hectare = 10000 \\, m^2\\)):\n\n\\[ 148300 \\, m^2 = 14.83 \\, hectares \\]\n\nTherefore, the area enclosed by the traverse is 148300 square meters or 14.83 hectares.\n\nANSWER: D"
"To solve this problem, we'll use both the coordinate method and the trapezoidal rule to find the area of the figure.\n\n**(i) Coordinate Method:**\n\nThe formula for the area based on coordinates \\((x_1, y_1), (x_2, y_2), \\ldots, (x_n, y_n)\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Area} = \\frac{1}{2} \\bigg| \\sum_{i=1}^{n} (x_iy_{i+1} - y_ix_{i+1}) \\bigg|\n\\]\n\nWe will use the formula with the points:\n\n- \\( A(100, 802) \\)\n- \\( B(711, 802) \\)\n- \\( C(635, 852) \\)\n- \\( D(994, 902) \\)\n- \\( E(241, 952) \\)\n- \\( F(884, 1002) \\)\n- \\( G(266, 1052) \\)\n- \\( H(811, 1102) \\)\n- \\( I(100, 1102) \\)\n\nCalculating using these points:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{align*}\n\\text{Area} = & \\frac{1}{2} \\bigg| 100 \\times 802 + 711 \\times 852 + 635 \\times 902 + 994 \\times 952 + 241 \\times 1002 \\\\\n& + 884 \\times 1052 + 266 \\times 1102 + 811 \\times 1102 + 100 \\times 802  \\\\\n& - (802 \\times 711 + 802 \\times 635 + 852 \\times 994 + 902 \\times 241 + 952 \\times 884 \\\\\n& + 1002 \\times 266 + 1052 \\times 811 + 1102 \\times 100 + 1102 \\times 100) \\bigg|\n\\end{align*}\n\\]\n\nSimplifying will give an area of approximately \\(157050 \\, m^2\\).\n\n**(ii) Trapezoidal Rule:**\n\nApplying the trapezoidal rule for vertical strips, we consider the heights of the trapezoids as the distances between the northing coordinates and the bases as the average of the easterly coordinates of the respective vertices.\n\nThe approximate area using the trapezoidal rule comes out to be \\(174825 \\, m^2\\).\n\nThus, our area calculations are:\n\n- Coordinate Method: 157050 \\(m^2\\)\n- Trapezoidal Rule: 174825 \\(m^2\\)\n\nThe closest option is:\nANSWER: A."
