"To find the plasticity index (PI) of the soil, we need to determine the liquid limit (LL) first.\n\n1. **Calculate the Liquid Limit (LL):**\n   - Plot the number of blows (N) versus moisture content and fit a straight line on a semi-log plot.\n   - The liquid limit corresponds to 25 blows.\n   - Use the two closest data points to 25 blows: \\( N = 19 \\) and \\( N = 27 \\).\n   - Apply interpolation:\n     \\[\n     \\text{Moisture content at 25 blows} = 40.8 + \\frac{25 - 19}{27 - 19} \\times (39.1 - 40.8)\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     = 40.8 + \\frac{6}{8} \\times (-1.7)\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     = 40.8 - 1.275\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     \\approx 39.525\\%\n     \\]\n   - Hence, the liquid limit (LL) = 39.5%.\n\n2. **Calculate the Plasticity Index (PI):**\n   - The formula for PI is: \\( PI = LL - PL \\)\n   - Given, Plastic Limit (PL) = 19.2%\n   - \\( PI = 39.5\\% - 19.2\\% \\)\n   - \\( PI = 20.3\\% \\)\n\nSince none of the options directly match 20.3%, rounding might be applied or a simplification error could slightly adjust the given options. The closest option is:\n\nANSWER: C. PI=20.2%."
