"To calculate the volume between chainages 1000 m and 1120 m using both the prismoidal and end area formulae, we follow these steps:\n\n### End Area Method\n\nThe end area method calculates the volume \\( V \\) between sections as:\n\n\\[ V = \\frac{d}{2} (A_1 + A_2) \\]\n\nwhere:\n- \\( d \\) is the distance between two consecutive sections.\n- \\( A_1 \\) and \\( A_2 \\) are the areas of two consecutive sections.\n\nCalculate each segment between the given chainages:\n\n1. **1000 m to 1020 m**:  \n   \\[ V_1 = \\frac{20}{2} (18.4 + 29.6) = 480 \\, m^3 \\]\n\n2. **1020 m to 1040 m**:  \n   \\[ V_2 = \\frac{20}{2} (29.6 + 38.4) = 680 \\, m^3 \\]\n\n3. **1040 m to 1060 m**:  \n   \\[ V_3 = \\frac{20}{2} (38.4 + 37.6) = 760 \\, m^3 \\]\n\n4. **1060 m to 1080 m**:  \n   \\[ V_4 = \\frac{20}{2} (37.6 + 25.5) = 630 \\, m^3 \\]\n\n5. **1080 m to 1100 m**:  \n   \\[ V_5 = \\frac{20}{2} (25.5 + 15.0) = 405 \\, m^3 \\]\n\n6. **1100 m to 1120 m**:  \n   \\[ V_6 = \\frac{20}{2} (15.0 + 9.7) = 248 \\, m^3 \\]\n\nTotal volume using end area formula:\n\n\\[ V_{\\text{end area}} = 480 + 680 + 760 + 630 + 405 + 248 = 3203 \\, m^3 \\]\n\n### Prismoidal Method\n\nThe prismoidal formula is:\n\n\\[ V = \\frac{d}{6} (A_1 + 4A_m + A_2) \\]\n\nwhere \\( A_m \\) is the area at the middle section between \\( A_1 \\) and \\( A_2 \\).\n\nCalculate each segment:\n\n1. **1000 m to 1040 m**:  \n   \\[ V_1 = \\frac{40}{6} (18.4 + 4(29.6) + 38.4) = 1161.3 \\, m^3 \\]\n\n2. **1040 m to 1080 m**:  \n   \\[ V_2 = \\frac{40}{6} (38.4 + 4(37.6) + 25.5) = 1390.7 \\, m^3 \\]\n\n3. **1080 m to 1120 m**:  \n   \\[ V_3 = \\frac{40}{6} (25.5 + 4(15.0) + 9.7) = 709 \\, m^3 \\]\n\nTotal volume using prismoidal formula:\n\n\\[ V_{\\text{prismoidal}} = 1161.3 + 1390.7 + 709 = 3261 \\, m^3 \\]\n\nComparing calculations to the provided options:\n\n- End area: \\( 3203 \\, m^3 \\)\n- Prismoidal: \\( 3261 \\, m^3 \\)\n\nANSWER: A"
