"To calculate the bearing and distance from point A to point B, we need to determine the total change in direction and the vector sum of all distances.\n\n### Steps:\n\n1. **Convert Bearings to Azimuth:** \n   - A-1: 86\u00b037'\n   - 1-2: 165\u00b018'\n   - 2-3: 223\u00b015'\n   - 3-B: 159\u00b053'\n\n2. **Calculate the Change in Coordinates for Each Line:**\n   - Convert each length and bearing to their respective \u0394x and \u0394y components using trigonometry:\n     - \\(\\Delta x = L \\cdot \\cos(\\text{bearing})\\)\n     - \\(\\Delta y = L \\cdot \\sin(\\text{bearing})\\)\n\n   **Line A-1:**\n   - \\(\\Delta x_{A-1} = 128.88 \\cdot \\cos(86\u00b037')\\)\n   - \\(\\Delta y_{A-1} = 128.88 \\cdot \\sin(86\u00b037')\\)\n\n   **Line 1-2:**\n   - \\(\\Delta x_{1-2} = 208.56 \\cdot \\cos(165\u00b018')\\)\n   - \\(\\Delta y_{1-2} = 208.56 \\cdot \\sin(165\u00b018')\\)\n\n   **Line 2-3:**\n   - \\(\\Delta x_{2-3} = 96.54 \\cdot \\cos(223\u00b015')\\)\n   - \\(\\Delta y_{2-3} = 96.54 \\cdot \\sin(223\u00b015')\\)\n\n   **Line 3-B:**\n   - \\(\\Delta x_{3-B} = 145.05 \\cdot \\cos(159\u00b053')\\)\n   - \\(\\Delta y_{3-B} = 145.05 \\cdot \\sin(159\u00b053')\\)\n\n3. **Sum Up All the Changes in Coordinates:**\n   - \\( \\Delta x_{total} = \\Delta x_{A-1} + \\Delta x_{1-2} + \\Delta x_{2-3} + \\Delta x_{3-B} \\)\n   - \\( \\Delta y_{total} = \\Delta y_{A-1} + \\Delta y_{1-2} + \\Delta y_{2-3} + \\Delta y_{3-B} \\)\n\n4. **Calculate the Resultant Distance and Bearing:**\n   - **Distance:**\n     - Distance \\( D = \\sqrt{(\\Delta x_{total})^2 + (\\Delta y_{total})^2} \\)\n\n   - **Bearing:**\n     - Bearing angle \\(\\theta = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{\\Delta y_{total}}{\\Delta x_{total}}\\right)\\)\n     - Convert the angle if needed to match the compass bearing.\n\nAfter performing these calculations, the solution should fall in line with one of the provided options. \n\n### Calculation:\nLet's quickly calculate:\nUsing trigonometric functions:\n\n- \\(\\Delta x_{A-1} = 12