"To determine which postulate does not support a law of linear perspective projection, let's analyze each option:\n\nA. The length of the side parallel to Eye Level determines the perceived nearness of the solid figure.\n- This relates to perspective, as the perceived size can indeed relate to the object's distance from the observer, but it\u2019s not a fundamental geometric principle in projection.\n\nB. Every line not perpendicular to Eye Level will converge on point V.\n- This is a core principle of perspective projection. Lines that are not perpendicular will appear to converge towards the vanishing point.\n\nC. No angle can ever equal 180$^\\circ$.\n- This is a general geometric truth but does not pertain specifically to perspective projection principles.\n\nD. Any solid (three-dimensional) figure can be projected.\n- This is a fundamental aspect of projection, as any 3D figure should theoretically be represented in a 2D plane through projection.\n\nThe option that does not directly support the principles of linear perspective as related to projection is C, since it addresses a general geometric property not specific to the projection or perspective methodology.\n\nANSWER: C."
