"To determine which option both sculptures exemplify, let's analyze the options:\n\nA. Grisaille - This refers to a painting technique executed entirely in shades of gray or of another neutral grayish color. It is mostly related to two-dimensional art, not sculpture.\n\nB. Trompe l'oeil - This is an artistic technique that uses realistic imagery to create the optical illusion that depicted objects exist in three dimensions. It is more applicable to painting or visual tricks, rather than sculpture itself.\n\nC. Contrapposto - This is an Italian term used in the visual arts to describe a human figure standing with most of its weight on one foot, so that its shoulders and arms twist off-axis from the hips and legs. It is highly relevant to sculpture, particularly of the human figure.\n\nD. Polychromy - This refers to the practice of decorating architectural elements, sculpture, etc., in a variety of colors. It involves using multiple colors, which doesn\u2019t seem as relevant with these images as they appear in monochrome.\n\nBoth images appear to depict figures with a naturalistic stance that indicates a shift of weight onto one leg, typical of contrapposto.\n\nANSWER: C"
