"To determine where the cell bodies for the auditory part of the nerve shown in the image are located, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. **Identify the Nerve**: The image shows the brainstem with cranial nerves. The auditory component is related to the cochlear part of the vestibulocochlear nerve (CN VIII).\n\n2. **Understand Auditory Pathway**: The cochlear nerve is responsible for transmitting auditory information from the cochlea to the brain.\n\n3. **Locate Cell Bodies**: The cell bodies for the auditory (cochlear) part of the vestibulocochlear nerve are located in the spiral ganglion, which is situated in the cochlea of the inner ear.\n\n4. **Evaluate Options**:\n   - **A. Otic ganglion**: This is associated with parasympathetic innervation to the parotid gland, not related to hearing.\n   - **B. Nodose ganglion**: This is associated with the vagus nerve (CN X) and not involved in the auditory pathway.\n   - **C. Vestibular nuclei**: These are centers in the brainstem where vestibular (balance-related) information is processed, but they do not house the cell bodies of the cochlear nerve.\n   - **D. Spiral ganglion**: This is where the cell bodies of the auditory nerve are located, making it the correct choice.\n\nANSWER: D. Spiral ganglion."
