"To determine how olivocerebellar axons terminate in the cerebellum, we need to understand the types of fibers involved:\n\n1. **Mossy fibers**: These are the axons from pontine nuclei, spinal cord, and vestibular nuclei that enter the cerebellum and synapse on granule cells.\n\n2. **Climbing fibers**: These originate from the inferior olivary nucleus in the medulla and terminate on Purkinje cells in the cerebellum.\n\n3. **Basket cell axons**: These are intrinsic to the cerebellar cortex and synapse on Purkinje cells, but they are not the termination of olivocerebellar axons.\n\nGiven this information, olivocerebellar axons terminate on Purkinje cells as climbing fibers.\n\nANSWER: B. Climbing fibers."
