"To determine which vessel serves the primary somatosensory cortex, we need to understand the blood supply to this particular brain region. The primary somatosensory cortex is located in the postcentral gyrus of the parietal lobe.\n\n1. **Middle Cerebral Artery (MCA):** This artery is responsible for supplying the lateral portions of the frontal, parietal, and temporal lobes. The primary somatosensory cortex, located on the lateral side of the brain, receives significant blood supply from the MCA.\n\n2. **Anterior Cerebral Artery (ACA):** This artery supplies the medial portions of the frontal and parietal lobes. However, it only supplies the medial aspect of the primary somatosensory area, which corresponds primarily to the representation of the lower limb and genitalia in the sensory homunculus.\n\nSince the primary somatosensory cortex is largely on the lateral surface, the dominant blood supply is from the middle cerebral artery.\n\nANSWER: B. Middle cerebral artery."
