"To determine which cell bodies can be recognized in white matter, let's look at each option:\n\nA. Cell bodies of glial cells.\n\n- White matter primarily contains axons, but glial cells such as oligodendrocytes and astrocytes are present. Glial cells support neuron function, provide myelin sheaths for axons, and maintain homeostasis. Therefore, it's possible to find cell bodies of glial cells in white matter.\n\nB. Cell bodies of ependymal cells.\n\n- Ependymal cells line the ventricles of the brain and the central canal of the spinal cord, not typically located within the white matter.\n\nC. Cell bodies of pial cells.\n\n- Pial cells are part of the pia mater, a layer of meninges covering the brain and spinal cord. They are not usually found within the white matter itself.\n\nD. Cell bodies of neurons.\n\n- The cell bodies of neurons are primarily located in the gray matter, not in the white matter.\n\nBased on this reasoning, the most appropriate choice is:\n\nANSWER: A"
