"To determine the phase of mitosis depicted in the image:\n\n1. **Prophase**: Chromosomes condense and become visible. The nuclear envelope begins to break down, and the mitotic spindle starts to form.\n2. **Metaphase**: Chromosomes line up along the metaphase plate in the center of the cell.\n3. **Anaphase**: Chromatids (now individual chromosomes) are pulled apart toward opposite poles of the cell.\n4. **Telophase**: Chromosomes arrive at the poles, begin to de-condense, and are enclosed by a new nuclear envelope. The cell is almost completely divided.\n\nIn the image, the daughter chromosomes are being pulled toward opposite poles, which indicates:\n\n- Chromosomes are separated and segregated to opposite ends of the cell.\n\nThis description matches the characteristics of:\n\n- **Anaphase**: The chromatids have been separated and are moving to opposite poles.\n\nANSWER: C"
