"To safely insert a needle into the subarachnoid space for sampling cerebrospinal fluid (CSF), a lumbar puncture is typically performed. The most common and safest point of insertion is between the L3 and L4 vertebrae. This area is chosen because the spinal cord usually ends at the level of L1-L2 in adults, reducing the risk of damaging the spinal cord.\n\nNow, let\u2019s assess each option:\n\nA. Between C-7 and T-1: This is in the cervical region and too high, risking injury to the spinal cord.\nB. Between L-3 and L-4: This is the preferred and safest location for a lumbar puncture, as it is below the termination of the spinal cord.\nC. Between L-1 and L-2: This is risky because the spinal cord ends around L1-L2.\nD. Between T-12 and L-1: This is also potentially risky as it's close to the end of the spinal cord.\n\nANSWER: B."
