"To determine the likelihood and status of the daughter having Tay-Sachs disease, we must consider the inheritance pattern of Tay-Sachs, which is autosomal recessive. This means that an individual must inherit two copies of the defective gene (one from each parent) to express the disease.\n\nGiven:\n1. The son has Tay-Sachs disease, indicating he inherited two defective alleles.\n2. Both parents must be carriers (heterozygous) because the son has the disease. This means each parent has one normal allele and one defective allele.\n\nFor the daughter, we have these possibilities:\n- 25% chance she has Tay-Sachs disease (inherits two defective alleles, one from each parent).\n- 50% chance she is a carrier (inherits one normal allele and one defective allele).\n- 25% chance she is homozygous normal (inherits two normal alleles, one from each parent).\n\nTherefore, the most accurate statement regarding the couple's daughter is that she is a carrier for Tay-Sachs disease, given the dominant probability.\n\nANSWER: C"
