"To find the ratio of the surface-to-volume ratios for the sphere and the cube, we need to calculate each shape's surface area and volume.\n\n**Sphere:**\n- Diameter \\(d = 1 \\, \\text{mm} \\), so radius \\( r = \\frac{d}{2} = 0.5 \\, \\text{mm} \\).\n- Surface area of a sphere \\( A_s = 4\\pi r^2 \\).\n  \\[\n  A_s = 4\\pi (0.5)^2 = \\pi\n  \\]\n- Volume of a sphere \\( V_s = \\frac{4}{3}\\pi r^3 \\).\n  \\[\n  V_s = \\frac{4}{3}\\pi (0.5)^3 = \\frac{\\pi}{6}\n  \\]\n- Surface-to-volume ratio for the sphere:\n  \\[\n  \\frac{A_s}{V_s} = \\frac{\\pi}{\\frac{\\pi}{6}} = 6\n  \\]\n\n**Cube:**\n- Side length \\( a = 1 \\, \\text{mm} \\).\n- Surface area of a cube \\( A_c = 6a^2 \\).\n  \\[\n  A_c = 6 \\times (1)^2 = 6\n  \\]\n- Volume of a cube \\( V_c = a^3 \\).\n  \\[\n  V_c = (1)^3 = 1\n  \\]\n- Surface-to-volume ratio for the cube:\n  \\[\n  \\frac{A_c}{V_c} = \\frac{6}{1} = 6\n  \\]\n\n**Ratio of the ratios:**\n- For the sphere: \\( \\frac{A_s}{V_s} = 6 \\)\n- For the cube: \\( \\frac{A_c}{V_c} = 6 \\)\n\n\\[\n\\text{Ratio of the ratios} = \\frac{6}{6} = 1\n\\]\n\nANSWER: C"
