"To determine the mode of inheritance, let's examine the pedigree:\n\n1. **Autosomal Recessive (AR):**\n   - Typically, skips generations.\n   - Affects both genders equally.\n   - In this pedigree, both affected individuals (third generation) have unaffected parents. This suggests AR inheritance.\n\n2. **Autosomal Dominant (AD):**\n   - Does not skip generations; affected individuals have an affected parent.\n   - In this pedigree, the affected individuals do not have affected parents, which conflicts with AD inheritance.\n\n3. **X-linked Recessive (XR):**\n   - More common in males.\n   - Affected males typically have carrier mothers.\n   - In this pedigree, males and females are affected, and both affected individuals share unaffected parents, suggesting non-XR inheritance.\n\n4. **X-linked Dominant (XD):**\n   - Affected males pass the trait to all daughters, not sons.\n   - Affected females pass the trait to 50% of children.\n   - This pattern does not fit because males and females are affected without consistent parent-child transmission.\n\nConsidering these points, the most fitting mode of inheritance is Autosomal Recessive.\n\nANSWER: A. autosomal recessive (AR)."
