"In paper chromatography, the primary factor contributing to the separation of pigments is differences in polarity. The paper (stationary phase) and the solvent (mobile phase) interact differently with each pigment based on polarity. Non-polar pigments travel further up the paper as they are more soluble in the non-polar solvent, whereas polar pigments adhere more to the polar paper, therefore traveling less.\n\nNow, let's evaluate the options:\n\nA. Differences in the polarity of the pigments - **Correct**: Polarity differences are key in how far pigments travel on the chromatography paper.\n\nB. pH gradient - This is not typically a factor in paper chromatography separation.\n\nC. Differences in the molecular weight (mass) of the pigments - While molecular weight can affect migration, polarity is the dominant factor in paper chromatography.\n\nD. Amount of sugars produced by the plants used to generate the pigment sample - This does not influence the chromatography process.\n\nE. Amount of oxygen produced by the plants used to generate the pigment sample - This is also irrelevant to the chromatographic separation.\n\nANSWER: A."
