"To determine which mutants will not survive, we need to evaluate whether the pac operon can be successfully expressed. The key components for operon expression are the promoter (P), the operator (O), and the regulatory protein (pacR).\n\n1. **pacR**: It must be activated by alcohol for the operon to be expressed.\n2. **Promoter (P)**: Necessary for RNA polymerase binding to initiate transcription.\n3. **Operator (O)**: The binding site for the activated pacR, allowing transcription.\n\nLet's analyze each mutant:\n\n- **A. pacR-P+O+/pacR+P-O-:**\n  - The first copy has a mutated pacR (inactive) and wild-type P and O.\n  - The second copy has wild-type pacR, mutated P (no transcription initiation), and mutated O (no pacR binding).\n  - Neither copy can express the operon. **This mutant will not survive.**\n\n- **B. pacR+P+O+/pacR-P-O+:**\n  - First copy has all wild-type components. \n  - Operon can be expressed.\n  \n- **C. pacR+P-O+/pacR-P+O+:**\n  - Neither copy can express the operon because at least one crucial component (P in the first copy or pacR in the second copy) is mutated.\n  - However, since pacR+ is operational in the first copy, it might compensate partially.\n  \n- **D. pacR-P+O-/pacR+P-O+:**\n  - Neither copy can express the operon due to mutated O in the first and P in the second, but pacR+ might still compensate with functional P+ in the first segment.\n\nAfter evaluating the possibilities, **A** is the scenario where the operon cannot be expressed at all, leading to inability to metabolize alcohol as a carbon source.\n\nANSWER: A"
