"To determine how many genes are involved, we can analyze the number of phenotypes and use the formula for polygenic traits, where the number of phenotypic categories is equal to \\(2n+1\\), where \\(n\\) is the number of gene pairs.\n\nHere, we have 9 phenotypes:\n\n1. Red\n2. Smoky red\n3. Cross-red\n4. Blended-cross\n5. Standard silver\n6. Substandard silver\n7. Alaskan silver\n8. Sub-Alaskan silver\n9. Double-black\n\nUsing the formula:\n\n\\[ 2n + 1 = 9 \\]\n\nSolving for \\(n\\):\n\n\\[ 2n = 8 \\]\n\\[ n = 4 \\]\n\nThus, there are 4 pairs of genes involved in this cross.\n\nANSWER: A. 4 pairs of genes."
