"To determine if the pedigree is consistent with an autosomal recessive mode of inheritance assuming individual 18 is a carrier, follow these steps:\n\n1. **Identify Affected Individuals**: Shaded individuals (3, 7, and 18) are affected by the disease.\n\n2. **Inheritance Pattern**:\n   - Autosomal recessive traits appear only when an individual has two copies of the mutated gene (homozygous recessive).\n   - Carriers (heterozygous) have one copy of the mutated gene and one normal gene but do not express the disease.\n\n3. **Analyze Generations**:\n   - Parent generation (1 and 2) shows no disease expression, suggesting they could be carriers without exhibiting the trait.\n   - Individual 3 (affected) and parent 4 (unaffected) have children 11 (affected) and 12 (affected), supporting the recessive hypothesis if parent 4 is a carrier.\n   - Individual 7 (affected) marries individual 8 (unaffected), and their child 18 is affected. This implies individual 8 is a carrier.\n\n4. **Consider Individual 18**:\n   - If individual 18 is affected, both parents (15 and 16) must carry at least one recessive allele for 18 to be homozygous recessive.\n   - If individual 18 were a carrier, they would not display the disease, contradicting the affected status shown in the pedigree.\n\nBased on the information and the pedigree, individual 18 showing the disease (i.e., being affected) does not fit if they are only a carrier for autosomal recessive inheritance. Therefore, the statement is false.\n\nANSWER: B. False."
