"To calculate the recombination frequency between the shrunken (sh/sh) and waxy (wx/wx) loci, we need to identify the parental and recombinant phenotypes in the progeny.\n\n### Parental Phenotypes:\nParental types will have the combinations present in the initial heterozygous plant:\n1. Colorless, Waxy, Shrunken (c wx sh)\n2. Colored, Nonwaxy, Plump (C Wx Sh)\n\nFrom the progeny:\n- Colorless, Waxy, Shrunken (6)\n- Colored, Nonwaxy, Plump (4)\n\nTotal parental: \\(6 + 4 = 10\\)\n\n### Recombinant Phenotypes:\nThe other phenotypes are recombinants which represent crossovers:\n1. Colorless, Nonwaxy, Shrunken (25)\n2. Colorless, Nonwaxy, Plump (290)\n3. Colorless, Waxy, Plump (700)\n4. Colored, Waxy, Shrunken (284)\n5. Colored, Waxy, Plump (30)\n6. Colored, Nonwaxy, Shrunken (661)\n\nTotal recombinants: \\(25 + 290 + 700 + 284 + 30 + 661 = 1990\\)\n\n### Recombination Frequency:\nThe recombination frequency is calculated as the number of recombinant progeny divided by the total number of progeny:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Recombination Frequency} = \\left( \\frac{1990}{2000} \\right) \\times 100\\% = 99.5\\%\n\\]\n\nHowever, since we only need recombination between \"sh\" and \"wx\", let's reconsider only those phenotypes involving switching between shrunken and waxy:\n\n1. Colorless, Waxy, Shrunken (6) \n2. Colored, Nonwaxy, Plump (4)\n3. Colorless, Nonwaxy, Plump (290)\n4. Colored, Waxy, Shrunken (284)\n\nRecombination occurs in those that are neither completely parental nor completely recombinant:\n- Colorless, Nonwaxy, Shrunken + Waxy combinations + Colored, Waxy, Shrunken + Nonwaxy combinations would contribute. The exact parental vs. recombinant classification is crucial, so calculation will focus on linkage without ambiguity scenarios:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Recombination Frequency} = \\left( \\frac{6 + 290 + 284 + 4}{2000} \\right) \\times 100\\% = 17.7\\%\n\\]\n\nTherefore:\n\nANSWER: C"
