"The graph shown is a Lineweaver-Burk plot, which is a double reciprocal graph of the Michaelis-Menten equation. This plot is used to understand enzyme kinetics and identify the effects of inhibitors on enzyme activity. In this plot:\n\n- The x-axis represents \\(1/[S]\\) (the reciprocal of the substrate concentration).\n- The y-axis represents \\(1/v\\) (the reciprocal of the reaction velocity).\n- The y-intercept corresponds to \\(1/V_{max}\\), where \\(V_{max}\\) is the maximum rate of the reaction.\n- The x-intercept represents \\(-1/K_m\\), where \\(K_m\\) is the Michaelis constant.\n\nWhen there is no inhibitor present, the enzyme operates at its natural parameters with the lowest intercept values:\n\n- The line with the lowest y-intercept represents the highest \\(V_{max}\\).\n- The line with the farthest left x-intercept represents the lowest \\(K_m\\).\n\nConsidering the plot, the line D has the lowest y-intercept, indicating the highest possible enzyme activity without any inhibitors present.\n\nANSWER: D."
