"When a peripheral membrane protein is synthesized in the lumen of the ER, it eventually reaches the plasma membrane via vesicles. Here's the reasoning:\n\n1. **Synthesis in ER Lumen:** The protein starts in the lumen, corresponding to what will be the extracellular side of the plasma membrane.\n\n2. **Transport via Vesicles:** The vesicles bud off from the ER and Golgi, maintaining the orientation of the protein. The side facing the lumen of the vesicle becomes the extracellular side once the vesicle fuses with the plasma membrane.\n\n3. **Final Positioning:** Upon fusion with the plasma membrane, the lumen side of the vesicle becomes the outside/extracellular side.\n\nTherefore, a peripheral membrane protein synthesized in the ER lumen will end up on the outside of the plasma membrane.\n\nANSWER: B. outside."
