"To solve this problem, we need to understand the ABC model of flower development. The ABC model explains how the identity of floral organs is determined by three classes of genes:\n\n- A class genes specify sepals.\n- A + B class genes specify petals.\n- B + C class genes specify stamens.\n- C class genes specify carpels.\n\nGiven the transformation: a chimeric gene consisting of an A class promoter fused to a B class coding sequence.\n\n**Impact of Transformation:**\n- The A class promoter makes the B class gene expressed where A class genes normally act.\n- In the whorls where A class genes are expressed (1 and 2), B class genes will be active due to the fusion.\n\n**Predicted Outcomes in Each Whorl:**\n1. **Whorl 1:** Normally produces sepals (A class).\n   - With B class expressed: A + B = Petals.\n\n2. **Whorl 2:** Normally produces petals (A + B class).\n   - With B class expressed: A + B = Petals (remains same).\n\n3. **Whorl 3:** Normally produces stamens (B + C class).\n   - No change in promoter influence, so it remains Stamens.\n\n4. **Whorl 4:** Normally produces carpels (C class).\n   - A promoter doesn't affect this whorl, remains Carpels.\n\nTherefore, the arrangement from inner to outer would be:\n- Whorl 4: Carpel (Ca)\n- Whorl 3: Stamen (St)\n- Whorl 2: Petal (Pe)\n- Whorl 1: Petal (Pe)\n\nThis corresponds to option C.\n\nANSWER: C"
