"To determine the priority of substituents using the Cahn-Ingold-Prelog (CIP) system, we need to consider the atomic numbers of the atoms directly attached to the carbon. Higher atomic numbers take precedence.\n\n1. **Substituent 1**: \\(-CH(CH_3)_2\\)\n   - Directly attached atom is carbon, bonded to two other carbons (from the methyl groups).\n\n2. **Substituent 2**: \\(-CH_2Br\\)\n   - Directly attached atom is carbon, bonded to a bromine, which has a higher atomic number than carbon.\n\n3. **Substituent 3**: \\(-CH_2CH_2Br\\)\n   - Directly attached first carbon is bonded to another carbon, and then to a bromine.\n\n**Determination:**\n- **Substituent 2** (\\(-CH_2Br\\)) has the highest priority because bromine is directly attached.\n- **Substituent 3** (\\(-CH_2CH_2Br\\)) has the second highest priority as the bromine is one carbon away.\n- **Substituent 1** (\\(-CH(CH_3)_2\\)) is the lowest priority since it involves only carbon atoms.\n\n**Priority Order**: 2 > 3 > 1\n\nANSWER: A"
"To determine the most stable canonical structure, we must consider the following rules of resonance stability:\n1. **Octet Rule**: Structures with a complete octet for all atoms are more stable.\n2. **Charge Distribution**: Structures with the least amount of formal charge and with charges placed on appropriate atoms (electronegative atoms should carry negative charges) are more stable.\n3. **Minimized Charge Separation**: Structures with minimal charge separation are more stable.\n4. **Aromaticity**: Aromatic structures are more stable than non-aromatic ones.\n\nLet's analyze the structures provided:\n\nOption A:\n- Check for complete octets.\n- Evaluate formal charge distribution.\n- Look for aromatic rings.\n\nOption B:\n- Check for complete octets.\n- Evaluate formal charge distribution.\n- Look for aromatic rings.\n\nOption C:\n- Check for complete octets.\n- Evaluate formal charge distribution.\n- Look for aromatic rings.\n\nOption D:\n- Check for complete octets.\n- Evaluate formal charge distribution.\n- Look for aromatic rings.\n\nThe stability of the structures will depend on following these guidelines effectively. Based on the analysis and considering typical stability preferences:\n\nANSWER: B."
