"To determine the number of moles of ammonia formed, we'll use stoichiometry based on the balanced chemical equation:\n\n\\[\nN_2(g) + 3H_2(g) \\rightarrow 2NH_3(g)\n\\]\n\n1. **Identify the limiting reactant:**\n   - According to the equation, 1 mole of \\(N_2\\) reacts with 3 moles of \\(H_2\\) to form 2 moles of \\(NH_3\\).\n   - We start with 6 moles of \\(N_2\\) and 6 moles of \\(H_2\\).\n\n2. **Determine how much \\(H_2\\) is needed for 6 moles of \\(N_2\\):**\n   - For 6 moles of \\(N_2\\), we need \\(6 \\times 3 = 18\\) moles of \\(H_2\\).\n   - We only have 6 moles of \\(H_2\\), making \\(H_2\\) the limiting reactant.\n\n3. **Calculate moles of \\(NH_3\\) produced:**\n   - Using \\(H_2\\) as the limiting reactant:\n     - 3 moles of \\(H_2\\) produce 2 moles of \\(NH_3\\).\n     - Thus, 6 moles of \\(H_2\\) will produce \\(\\frac{6}{3} \\times 2 = 4\\) moles of \\(NH_3\\).\n\nANSWER: C."
