"To find the equilibrium constant, \\( K_{eq} \\), we use the relationship between the standard free energy change and the equilibrium constant:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta G\u00b0 = -RT \\ln K_{eq}\n\\]\n\nWhere:\n- \\( \\Delta G\u00b0 = -801 \\, \\text{kJ/mol} = -801,000 \\, \\text{J/mol} \\)\n- \\( R = 8.314 \\, \\text{J/mol K} \\) (universal gas constant)\n- \\( T = 298 \\, \\text{K} \\)\n\nFirst, solve for \\( \\ln K_{eq} \\):\n\n\\[\n-801,000 = -(8.314)(298) \\ln K_{eq}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\ln K_{eq} = \\frac{801,000}{8.314 \\times 298}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\ln K_{eq} = \\frac{801,000}{2477.372} \\approx 323.37\n\\]\n\nNow, convert to \\( K_{eq} \\) by taking the exponential:\n\n\\[\nK_{eq} = e^{323.37}\n\\]\n\nThis gives:\n\n\\[\nK_{eq} \\approx 1.12 \\times 10^{140}\n\\]\n\nANSWER: 1.12e140"
