"To determine whether the standard free energy of hydrolysis of phosphoarginine is more similar to that of glucose 6-phosphate or ATP, let's consider the typical values for these molecules.\n\n1. **Glucose 6-phosphate**: The standard free energy of hydrolysis is around -13.8 kJ/mol.\n\n2. **ATP (Adenosine triphosphate)**: The standard free energy of hydrolysis (for ATP to ADP + Pi) is about -30.5 kJ/mol under standard conditions.\n\nPhosphoarginine, like phosphocreatine, is a high-energy phosphate compound found in muscle tissues of invertebrates, serving a similar energy-buffering role as phosphocreatine in vertebrates. It has a high standard free energy of hydrolysis, more comparable to ATP than to glucose 6-phosphate.\n\nTherefore, the standard free energy of hydrolysis of phosphoarginine is more similar to that of ATP.\n\nANSWER: B."
