"To find the standard entropy change, \\(\\Delta S^\\circ\\), for the reaction, we'll use the following relationship between Gibbs free energy, enthalpy, and entropy:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta G^\\circ = \\Delta H^\\circ - T \\Delta S^\\circ\n\\]\n\nGiven:\n- \\(\\Delta G^\\circ = -801 \\text{ kJ/mol}\\)\n- \\(T = 298 \\text{ K}\\)\n\nWe can calculate \\(\\Delta H^\\circ\\) using bond energies.\n\n1. **Calculate \\(\\Delta H^\\circ\\) using bond energies:**\n\n   - **Bonds broken (reactants):**\n     - 4 C\u2013H bonds in \\(\\text{CH}_4\\): \\(4 \\times 415 = 1660\\) kJ/mol\n     - 2 O=O bonds in \\(2 \\text{O}_2\\): \\(2 \\times 495 = 990\\) kJ/mol\n\n   - **Bonds formed (products):**\n     - 2 C=O bonds in \\(\\text{CO}_2\\): \\(2 \\times 799 = 1598\\) kJ/mol\n     - 4 O\u2013H bonds in \\(2 \\text{H}_2\\text{O}\\): \\(4 \\times 463 = 1852\\) kJ/mol\n\n   - \\(\\Delta H^\\circ = \\) Bonds broken \u2013 Bonds formed\n   \\[\n   = (1660 + 990) - (1598 + 1852)\n   = 2650 - 3450\n   = -800 \\text{ kJ/mol}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Use \\(\\Delta G^\\circ = \\Delta H^\\circ - T \\Delta S^\\circ\\) to find \\(\\Delta S^\\circ\\):**\n\n   - \\( \\Delta G^\\circ = -801 \\text{ kJ/mol}\\)\n   - \\(\\Delta H^\\circ = -800 \\text{ kJ/mol}\\)\n   - \\(T = 298 \\text{ K}\\)\n\n   Convert \\(\\Delta G^\\circ\\) and \\(\\Delta H^\\circ\\) to J/mol:\n   - \\(\\Delta G^\\circ = -801000 \\text{ J/mol}\\)\n   - \\(\\Delta H^\\circ = -800000 \\text{ J/mol}\\)\n\n   \\[\n   -801000 = -800000 - 298 \\Delta S^\\circ\n   \\]\n\n   Rearrange for \\(\\Delta S^\\circ\\):\n   \\[\n   -801000 + 800000 = -298 \\Delta S^\\circ\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   -1000 = -298 \\Delta S^\\circ\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\Delta S^\\circ = \\frac{-1000}{-298} \\approx 3.36 \\text{ J/K}\n   \\]\n\nANSWER: 3"
"To balance the given chemical equation, we need to identify the products generated when the reactants undergo a chemical reaction. Let's analyze the equation:\n\nReactants:\n- Propanoic acid (CH\u2083CH\u2082COOH)\n- Sodium hydroxide (NaOH)\n\nProducts:\n- Sodium propanoate (CH\u2083CH\u2082COO\u207b Na\u207a)\n- A missing compound\n\nThe reaction involved is a neutralization reaction between an acid and a base, producing a salt and water. The missing compound should therefore be water (H\u2082O), completing the balanced equation.\n\nBalancing equation:\n1. CH\u2083CH\u2082COOH + NaOH \u2192 CH\u2083CH\u2082COO\u207b Na\u207a + H\u2082O\n\nThe equation is now balanced with each element having the same number of atoms on both sides.\n\nANSWER: B. H\u2082O"
