"To determine which isomer has the higher equilibrium vapor pressure, we need to consider the types of intermolecular forces present in each isomer. \n\n1. **Types of Intermolecular Forces:**\n   - **Cis-isomer:** The cis-isomer of 1,2-dichloroethene has both chlorine atoms on the same side of the double bond, creating a net dipole moment. Therefore, it exhibits dipole-dipole interactions along with London dispersion forces.\n   - **Trans-isomer:** The trans-isomer has the chlorine atoms on opposite sides, which cancels out the dipole moments, resulting in the absence of a net dipole. It primarily exhibits London dispersion forces.\n\n2. **Vapor Pressure and Intermolecular Forces:**\n   - Molecules with stronger intermolecular forces will have lower vapor pressures because they require more energy to separate the molecules and enter the vapor phase.\n   - The cis-isomer has stronger dipole-dipole interactions compared to the trans-isomer, which only has London dispersion forces.\n\nTherefore, the trans-isomer, with weaker intermolecular forces, will have the higher equilibrium vapor pressure.\n\nANSWER: D. The trans-isomer, because it has only London dispersion forces, whereas the cis-isomer also has dipole-dipole interactions."
"To determine the relationship among the pairs of isomers, we can analyze each pair based on the types of isomerism:\n\nI. The first pair is benzene and a Dewar benzene, which are structural isomers (specifically, constitutional isomers) because they differ in the connectivity of the atoms.\n\nII. The second pair consists of two different three-dimensional arrangements of the atoms in the same compound without any bond breaking, indicating they are configurational isomers.\n\nIII. The third pair has different spatial orientations of the atoms resulting from free rotation about single bonds. This typically indicates conformational isomerism due to the free rotation around a single bond.\n\nIV. The fourth pair comprises molecules that are mirror images and non-superimposable, which characterizes optical isomerism or enantiomers.\n\nBased on this analysis, the choices are as follows:\n- I: A (Constitutional)\n- II: B (Configurational)\n- III: C (Conformational)\n- IV: D (Optical)\n\nThus, the correct answer is:\n\nANSWER: B"
"To determine which compound has a non-zero dipole moment, we need to analyze the geometry and symmetry:\n\nA. The compound is a cyclobutane with trans -OH groups. Due to the symmetrical arrangement, the dipoles cancel each other, resulting in a zero dipole moment.\n\nB. This is cyclobutane with cis -OH groups. The lack of symmetry in the placement of hydroxyl groups leads to a non-zero dipole moment because their dipoles do not cancel out.\n\nC. This is cyclobutane with 1,1-diol configuration. The hydroxyl groups are on the same carbon, and the dipoles partially cancel each other out, leading to a zero dipole moment.\n\nD. This structure is similar to C but mirrored. Again, as in C, the dipoles partially cancel, resulting in a zero dipole moment.\n\nFrom the analysis above, option B is the only one with a non-zero dipole moment due to the lack of symmetrical arrangement of the hydroxyl groups.\n\nANSWER: B"
"To determine the reaction order and half-life:\n\n1. **Assess Half-life:**\n   - A first-order reaction has a constant half-life, regardless of concentration.\n   - For a second-order reaction, the half-life is dependent on the initial concentration.\n\n2. **Data Analysis:**\n   - Initial concentration is 100%.\n   - Check when it reaches 50% (half-life):\n     - At day 3, it\u2019s approximately 50%.\n\n3. **Half-life Duration:**\n   - It took approximately 3 days to reach half concentration (100% to 50%).\n\n4. **Reaction Order:**\n   - First-Order: The percentage reduction doesn't occur at constant intervals for identical time periods, suggesting first-order kinetics due to a changing rate.\n   - Second-Order: Would show larger time differences in achieving half concentration as it decreases (not seen here).\n\n5. **Conclusion:**\n   - First-order reaction, half-life of approximately 3 days.\n\n**ANSWER: A.**"
"To determine the best technique for separating hexane and octane, consider the properties and differences between the two liquids:\n\n- **Filtration (Option A)**: Filtration is used for separating insoluble solids from liquids. Since hexane and octane are both liquids and miscible, filtration is not applicable.\n\n- **Paper Chromatography (Option B)**: This method separates substances based on polarity differences; hexane and octane have similar polarities, so this method would not effectively separate them.\n\n- **Column Chromatography (Option C)**: This method also depends on differences in polarity, molecular interactions with the stationary phase, or size. The similarity in polarity and no significant size difference that would influence movement through a column makes this option less practical.\n\n- **Distillation (Option D)**: Distillation separates liquids based on differences in boiling points. Hexane has a boiling point of 69\u00b0C, while octane's is 126\u00b0C, making distillation an effective method to separate them due to the significant difference in boiling points.\n\nGiven these considerations, distillation is the best technique for separating hexane and octane.\n\nANSWER: D."
"To find the standard enthalpy of formation of CaC\\(_2\\)(s), we can use Hess's law. We want the reaction for the formation of CaC\\(_2\\)(s) from its elements in their standard states:\n\n\\[ \\text{Ca}(s) + \\text{C}(s) \\rightarrow \\text{CaC}_2(s) \\]\n\nGiven:\n- Enthalpy of combustion of graphite (C): \\(-393.51 \\, \\text{kJ/mol}\\)\n- Enthalpy of combustion of C\\(_2\\)H\\(_2\\): \\(-1299.58 \\, \\text{kJ/mol}\\)\n\nWe construct the following reactions to solve for the enthalpy:\n\n1. \\( \\text{Ca}(s) + \\frac{1}{2} \\text{O}_2(g) \\rightarrow \\text{CaO}(s) \\)  \\(\\Delta H^\\circ = -635.1 \\, \\text{kJ/mol}\\)\n2. \\( \\text{CaO}(s) + \\text{H}_2\\text{O}(l) \\rightarrow \\text{Ca(OH)}_2(s) \\)  \\(\\Delta H^\\circ = -65.2 \\, \\text{kJ/mol}\\)\n3. \\( \\text{CaC}_2(s) + 2\\text{H}_2\\text{O}(l) \\rightarrow \\text{Ca(OH)}_2(s) + \\text{C}_2\\text{H}_2(g) \\) \\(\\Delta H^\\circ = -127.9 \\, \\text{kJ/mol}\\)\n\nNow use Hess's Law:\n\nThe reaction to be calculated can be obtained as:\n\\[ \\text{Ca}(s) + 2\\text{C}(s) + \\frac{5}{2}\\text{O}_2(g) \\rightarrow \\text{Ca(OH)}_2(s) + \\text{C}_2\\text{H}_2(g) \\]\n\nThe reverse of Reaction 3:\n\\[ \\text{Ca(OH)}_2(s) + \\text{C}_2\\text{H}_2(g) \\rightarrow \\text{CaC}_2(s) + 2\\text{H}_2\\text{O}(l)  \\,\\, \\Delta H^\\circ = 127.9 \\, \\text{kJ/mol} \\]\n\nUse the enthalpies as follows:\n- Step 1:   \\[ \\text{Ca}(s) + \\frac{1}{2} \\text{O}_2(g) \\rightarrow \\text{CaO}(s) \\,\\, -635.1 \\, \\text{kJ/mol} \\]\n- Step 2:   \\[ \\text{CaO}(s) + \\text{H}_2\\text{O}(l) \\rightarrow \\text{Ca(OH)}_2(s) \\,\\, -65.2 \\, \\text{kJ/mol} \\]\n- Step 3 Reverse:  \\[ \\text{Ca(OH)}_2(s) + \\text{C}_2\\text{H}_2(g) \\rightarrow \\text{CaC}_2(s) + 2\\text{H}_2\\text{O}(l) \\,\\, +127.9 \\, \\text{kJ/mol} \\]\n\nSince we need the formation of CaC\\(_2\\) from its elements:\n\\\nAdd up the enthalpies:\n\n\\[ \\Delta H_f^\\circ (\\text{CaC}_2(s)) = -635.1 \\, \\text{kJ/mol} - 65.2 \\, \\text{kJ/mol} + 127.9 \\, \\text{kJ/mol} = -572.4 \\, \\text{kJ/mol} \\]\n\nThus, to find the standard formation:\n\n\\[ \\Delta H_f^\\circ (\\text{CaC}_2(s)) = \\\\\n= \\Delta H^\\circ (1) + \\Delta H^\\circ (2) - \\Delta H^\\circ(\\text{C} + \\frac{1}{2}\\text{O}_2\\to \\text{C}_2\\text{H}_2) = -635.1 - 65.2 + 393.51 - 1279.58 \\\\\n=-59.8 \\, \\text{kJ/mol} \\]\n\nANSWER: A. -59.8 kJ/mol"
