"To solve this, we need to determine how many moles of magnesium oxide can be formed based on the amount of magnesium and oxygen depicted in the image.\n\nThe balanced equation is:\n\\[ 2 \\text{Mg (s)} + \\text{O}_2 \\text{(g)} \\rightarrow 2 \\text{MgO (s)} \\]\n\nThis tells us that 2 moles of magnesium react with 1 mole of oxygen to produce 2 moles of magnesium oxide.\n\n**Step-by-step analysis:**\n\n1. **Count the moles of magnesium and oxygen in the image:**\n   - Moles of magnesium (white circles): 6\n   - Moles of oxygen (black circles): 5\n\n2. **Determine the limiting reactant:**\n   - According to the reaction, 2 moles of Mg react with 1 mole of O\\(_2\\). Therefore, for 5 moles of O\\(_2\\), we would need 10 moles of Mg.\n   - Since we only have 6 moles of Mg, magnesium is the limiting reactant.\n\n3. **Calculate moles of magnesium oxide formed:**\n   - For the 6 moles of Mg available, 6/2 = 3 moles of O\\(_2\\) are needed.\n   - Since we have 5 moles of oxygen available, we are limited to 6 moles of Mg being used entirely.\n   - Therefore, 6 moles of Mg will produce 6 moles of MgO (because the reaction produces 2 moles of MgO for every 2 moles of Mg used).\n\nThus, the reaction will form 6 moles of magnesium oxide.\n\nANSWER: C. 6 moles."
