"To rank the alkenes by decreasing heat of hydrogenation, consider the stability of each alkene. The less stable the alkene, the higher the heat of hydrogenation.\n\n1. **Alkene 2**: This is a trisubstituted alkene. Trisubstituted alkenes are generally more stable than disubstituted or monosubstituted ones, but it appears less stable due to steric factors from methyl groups.\n   \n2. **Alkene 3**: This is a disubstituted alkene. It is more stable than a monosubstituted alkene due to hyperconjugation and inductive effects.\n\n3. **Alkene 4**: This is a tetrasubstituted alkene, which is generally the most stable due to hyperconjugation and inductive effects.\n   \n4. **Alkene 1**: This is a monosubstituted alkene, which is the least stable of the group.\n\nBased on the stability, the order from largest to smallest heat of hydrogenation is the least stable to the most stable: 2 > 3 > 4 > 1.\n\nANSWER: A. 2 > 3 > 4 > 1."
