"To determine the number of chiral centers in isoleucine, look for carbon atoms that are attached to four different groups. \n\n1. Start at the central carbon (the alpha carbon) which is bonded to:\n   - An amino group (\\(NH_3^+\\))\n   - A hydrogen atom (H)\n   - A carboxylate group (\\(COO^-\\))\n   - Another carbon atom in the side chain\n\n   This central carbon is a chiral center because all four groups are different.\n\n2. Move to the first carbon in the side chain. It is bonded to:\n   - The rest of the side chain\n   - A methyl group (\\(CH_3\\))\n   - A hydrogen atom (H)\n   - The alpha carbon\n\n   This carbon is also chiral.\n\nNo other carbons in the structure can be chiral, as they either don't have four distinguishable substituents or they are part of a methyl group which cannot be chiral.\n\nTherefore, isoleucine has two chiral centers.\n\nANSWER: B."
"To determine how the mass of the \\(Pt(s)\\) electrode changes, we need to consider the function of each electrode and the direction of the redox reactions in the cell.\n\n### Reaction at Each Electrode:\n\n1. **Anode (Oxidation):**  \n   At the Pt electrode, copper is oxidized from \\(Cu^+(aq)\\) (from \\(CuNO_3\\)) and into \\(Cu^{2+}(aq)\\) (provided by \\(Cu(NO_3)_2\\)).  \n   \\[\n   \\text{Cu}^+(aq) \\rightarrow \\text{Cu}^{2+}(aq) + e^-\n   \\]\n   This reaction occurs at the anode.\n\n2. **Cathode (Reduction):**  \n   At the Au electrode, gold ions are reduced and deposited as gold solid.  \n   \\[\n   \\text{Au}^{3+}(aq) + 3e^- \\rightarrow \\text{Au}(s)\n   \\]\n   This takes place at the cathode.\n\n### Role of Pt Electrode:\n\n- The Pt electrode serves as an inert electrode to provide a surface for the oxidation reaction but does not participate chemically in the reaction. As a result, its mass doesn't change during the reaction.\n\n### Conclusion:\n\nThe mass of the \\(Pt(s)\\) electrode remains the same because it is an inert electrode and does not undergo any reactions that would add or remove material.\n\nANSWER: C. Remain the same."
"To determine which atom has the highest first ionization energy, we need to look at the atomic orbitals provided in the options. The first ionization energy generally increases across a period (left to right on the periodic table) and decreases down a group (top to bottom).\n\nLet's consider the electron configuration of the options:\n\nA. 1s\u00b9\nB. 2s\u00b9 2p\u00b2\nC. 2s\u00b2 2p\u00b3\nD. 2s\u00b2 2p\u2076 3s\u00b9\nE. 2s\u00b2 2p\u2076 3s\u00b2\n\nNow, analyze the given configurations:\n- Option A corresponds to Hydrogen.\n- Option B resembles Boron.\n- Option C resembles Nitrogen.\n- Option D resembles Sodium.\n- Option E resembles Magnesium.\n\nNitrogen (Option C) is located to the right of Boron (Option B) in the same period, hence it has a higher ionization energy. Hydrogen (Option A) has a lower ionization energy than both Nitrogen and Boron due to its position in the first period. Sodium (Option D) and Magnesium (Option E), being in the third period, will generally have lower ionization energies than these second period elements due to their larger atomic size and more shielding.\n\nTherefore, Nitrogen, with a half-filled 2p orbital, has the highest ionization energy of the given choices due to its stable electron configuration.\n\nANSWER: C."
"To find the equilibrium constant \\( K_{eq} \\) for the reaction, we need to use the relationship between the Gibbs free energy change (\\(\\Delta G^\\circ\\)) and \\( K_{eq} \\):\n\n\\[ \\Delta G^\\circ = -RT \\ln K_{eq} \\]\n\nFirst, we calculate the standard Gibbs free energy change \\(\\Delta G^\\circ\\) using the equation:\n\n\\[ \\Delta G^\\circ = \\Delta H^\\circ - T \\Delta S^\\circ \\]\n\nwhere:\n- \\(\\Delta H^\\circ = -2801 \\, \\text{kJ}\\)\n- \\(T = 298 \\, \\text{K}\\)\n\nNext, we need to find the standard entropy change \\(\\Delta S^\\circ\\):\n\n\\[ \\Delta S^\\circ = \\sum nS^\\circ (\\text{products}) - \\sum mS^\\circ (\\text{reactants}) \\]\n\nPlug in the entropy values given:\n\n\\[ \n\\Delta S^\\circ = [6 \\times 213.6 + 6 \\times 69.9] - [212.13 + 6 \\times 205] \n\\]\n\n\\[ \n\\Delta S^\\circ = [1281.6 + 419.4] - [212.13 + 1230] \n\\]\n\n\\[ \n\\Delta S^\\circ = 1701 - 1442.13 \n\\]\n\n\\[ \n\\Delta S^\\circ = 258.87 \\, \\text{J/mol-K} \n\\]\n\nNow, convert \\(\\Delta H^\\circ\\) to \\(\\text{J}\\):\n\n\\[ \\Delta H^\\circ = -2801 \\times 10^3 \\, \\text{J} \\]\n\nCalculate \\(\\Delta G^\\circ\\):\n\n\\[ \n\\Delta G^\\circ = -2801 \\times 10^3 - 298 \\times 258.87 \n\\]\n\n\\[ \n\\Delta G^\\circ = -2801000 - 77144.26 \n\\]\n\n\\[ \n\\Delta G^\\circ = -2878144.26 \\, \\text{J/mol} \n\\]\n\nNow calculate \\( K_{eq} \\):\n\n\\[ \n-2878144.26 = -8.314 \\times 298 \\ln K_{eq} \n\\]\n\n\\[ \n\\ln K_{eq} = \\frac{2878144.26}{8.314 \\times 298} \n\\]\n\n\\[ \n\\ln K_{eq} = \\frac{2878144.26}{2477.572} \n\\]\n\n\\[ \n\\ln K_{eq} = 1161.13 \n\\]\n\n\\[ \nK_{eq} = e^{1161.13} \n\\]\n\nThis value is extremely large, so we represent it in scientific notation.\n\nANSWER: 1e505"
"To determine the reason for the high basic strength of 1,8-bis(dimethylamino)naphthalene (also known as \"proton sponge\"), let's evaluate each option:\n\nA. **Resonance:** While resonance is a stabilizing factor for many compounds, it would generally lower basicity because the lone pair on nitrogen is delocalized, making it less available to accept protons.\n\nB. **Steric inhibition of resonance:** The proton sponge has dimethylamino groups that are orthogonal to the naphthalene ring. This steric hindrance prevents the lone pairs on nitrogen from participating in resonance with the ring, making them more available to accept protons and increasing basicity.\n\nC. **Ortho effect:** Generally refers to effects due to substituents at the ortho position in benzenoid compounds, but in this context, it isn't the primary reason the basicity is enhanced.\n\nD. **Hyperconjugation:** This phenomenon generally involves sigma bonds, and it doesn't significantly contribute to enhancing basicity in this scenario.\n\nThe correct explanation for the extraordinary basicity of the proton sponge is the steric inhibition of resonance (B), which leaves the lone pairs on the nitrogen atoms more available to accept protons.\n\nANSWER: B"
