"To prepare bromocyclopentane, you would typically start with cyclopentene and use an addition reaction with HBr (Hydrobromic acid). The double bond in cyclopentene would facilitate the formation of the bromine-substituted product through a Markovnikov addition mechanism.\n\nConsidering the mechanism, HBr adds across the double bond where the hydrogen attaches to the carbon with more hydrogens, and the bromide ion attaches to the other carbon. This results in the formation of bromocyclopentane.\n\nTherefore, the alkene you need is cyclopentene.\n\nANSWER: C. Cyclopentene."
"To determine the relative acidity (pKa values) of the hydrocarbons, we focus on the hybridization and bond angles:\n\n1. Lower pKa values indicate stronger acids.\n2. More s-character in hybridization increases acidity because s orbitals are closer to the nucleus and can stabilize the negative charge of the conjugate base.\n\nAnalyzing each compound:\n- **Compound V** has an sp hybridized carbon (180\u00b0 bond angle), indicating the most s-character, thus it's the strongest acid (lowest pKa).\n- **Compound VI** is next. It has an sp\u00b2 (120\u00b0 bond angle) and a high-angle strain, making it fairly acidic.\n- **Compound I** is sp\u00b3 hybridized (109.5\u00b0 bond angle) and is next due to typical sp\u00b3 hybridized protons.\n- **Compound II** and **IV** both have increased bond angles due to ring strain, with bond angles of 115\u00b0 and 108\u00b0, respectively, making them similar but slightly less acidic compared to typical alkanes.\n- **Compound III** has bond angles of 117\u00b0 in a typical alkene setting, making it less acidic compared to the previous ones.\n\nArranging by decreasing acidity (increasing pKa): V < VI < I < II < IV < III\n\nThe order that matches this sequence is option **A**: V < I < VI < II < III < IV.\n\nANSWER: A"
