"To determine if there are any potential outliers in the samples, we can use the Interquartile Range (IQR) method. Here are the steps:\n\n### Step 1: Calculate the IQR for each sample\n1. **Sample 1:**\n   - Order the data: 21.3, 21.5, 21.7, 21.7, 21.9, 22.1, 22.2, 22.6, 23.0, 23.1\n   - Median (Q2): \\(21.95\\) (average of 21.9 and 22.0)\n   - Q1: \\(21.6\\) (average of 21.5 and 21.7)\n   - Q3: \\(22.3\\) (average of 22.2 and 22.6)\n   - IQR = \\(22.3 - 21.6 = 0.7\\)\n\n2. **Sample 2:**\n   - Order the data: 12.9, 13.5, 13.5, 13.6, 13.8, 13.9, 13.9, 13.9, 14.2\n   - Median (Q2): \\(13.85\\) (average of 13.8 and 13.9)\n   - Q1: \\(13.5\\)\n   - Q3: \\(13.9\\)\n   - IQR = \\(13.9 - 13.5 = 0.4\\)\n\n3. **Sample 3:**\n   - Order the data: 15.9, 16.0, 16.3, 16.7, 16.7, 16.8, 16.9, 17.2, 17.4, 17.5\n   - Median (Q2): \\(16.75\\) (average of 16.7 and 16.8)\n   - Q1: \\(16.3\\) (average of 16.3 and 16.7)\n   - Q3: \\(17.0\\) (average of 16.9 and 17.2)\n   - IQR = \\(17.0 - 16.3 = 0.7\\)\n\n### Step 2: Determine potential outliers\nFor each sample, potential outliers are any values below \\(Q1 - 1.5 \\times \\text{IQR}\\) or above \\(Q3 + 1.5 \\times \\text{IQR}\\).\n\n1. **Sample 1:**\n   - Lower bound: \\(21.6 - 1.5 \\times 0.7 = 20.55\\)\n   - Upper bound: \\(22.3 + 1.5 \\times 0.7 = 23.35\\)\n   - No data points outside this range.\n\n2. **Sample 2:**\n   - Lower bound: \\(13.5 - 1.5 \\times 0.4 = 12.9\\)\n   - Upper bound: \\(13.9 + 1.5 \\times 0.4 = 14.5\\)\n   - No data points outside this range.\n\n3. **Sample 3:**\n   - Lower bound: \\(16.3 - 1.5 \\times 0.7 = 15.25\\)\n   - Upper bound: \\(17.0 + 1.5 \\times 0.7 = 18.05\\)\n   - No data points outside this range.\n\n### Conclusion\nNo values in any of the samples are outside the calculated bounds, indicating there are no potential outliers.\n\nANSWER: B. No, no evidence for any outliers."
"To calculate the amount of heat energy needed, we use the formula:\n\n\\[ q = m \\cdot c \\cdot \\Delta T \\]\n\nwhere:\n- \\( q \\) is the heat energy in joules,\n- \\( m \\) is the mass in grams,\n- \\( c \\) is the specific heat capacity in J/(g\u00b7\u00b0C),\n- \\( \\Delta T \\) is the change in temperature in \u00b0C.\n\nGiven:\n- \\( m = 1650 \\, \\text{g} \\)\n- \\( c = 0.810 \\, \\text{J/(g\u00b7\u00b0C)} \\)\n- \\( \\Delta T = 180 \\, \\text{\u00b0C} \\)\n\nSubstituting the values into the formula:\n\n\\[ \nq = 1650 \\, \\text{g} \\times 0.810 \\, \\text{J/(g\u00b7\u00b0C)} \\times 180 \\, \\text{\u00b0C} \n\\]\n\n\\[ \nq = 240570 \\, \\text{J} \n\\]\n\nConvert joules to kilojoules:\n\n\\[ \nq = \\frac{240570}{1000} = 240.57 \\, \\text{kJ} \n\\]\n\nThus, the heat energy needed is:\n\nANSWER: 240.57"
