"To determine the relationship between \\( K_1 \\) and \\( K_2 \\), let's analyze the given reactions and their equilibrium constants.\n\n1. **First Reaction:**\n   \\[\n   \\text{SO}_2(g) + \\frac{1}{2} \\text{O}_2(g) \\rightleftharpoons \\text{SO}_3(g)\n   \\]\n   Equilibrium constant: \\( K_1 \\)\n\n2. **Second Reaction:**\n   \\[\n   2 \\text{SO}_3(g) \\rightleftharpoons 2 \\text{SO}_2(g) + \\text{O}_2(g)\n   \\]\n   Equilibrium constant: \\( K_2 \\)\n\n### Steps to Determine the Relationship:\n\n- **Reverse the first reaction**: This changes the equilibrium constant to the reciprocal.\n  \\[\n  \\text{SO}_3(g) \\rightleftharpoons \\text{SO}_2(g) + \\frac{1}{2} \\text{O}_2(g)\n  \\]\n  New equilibrium constant: \\( \\frac{1}{K_1} \\)\n\n- **Multiply the balanced reverse reaction by 2**: This squares the equilibrium constant.\n  \\[\n  2 \\text{SO}_3(g) \\rightleftharpoons 2 \\text{SO}_2(g) + \\text{O}_2(g)\n  \\]\n  New equilibrium constant: \\( \\left(\\frac{1}{K_1}\\right)^2 = \\frac{1}{(K_1)^2} \\)\n\nTherefore, \\( K_2 = \\frac{1}{(K_1)^2} \\).\n\nANSWER: C"
