"To determine the pressure of the gas in the manometer, we need to consider the difference in mercury levels between the gas side and the open end. Given:\n\n- Height difference = 13.7 cm = 137.0 mm Hg\n- Atmospheric pressure = 760.0 mm Hg\n\nSince the mercury level is higher on the open end, the gas pressure is greater than atmospheric pressure.\n\nThe pressure of the gas is:\n\n\\[ P_{\\text{gas}} = P_{\\text{atm}} + \\Delta h \\]\n\\[ P_{\\text{gas}} = 760.0 \\, \\text{mm Hg} + 137.0 \\, \\text{mm Hg} = 897.0 \\, \\text{mm Hg} \\]\n\nNow, we convert mm Hg to kPa:\n\n1 mm Hg = 0.133322 kPa\n\n\\[ P_{\\text{gas}} = 897.0 \\, \\text{mm Hg} \\times 0.133322 \\, \\text{kPa/mm Hg} \\]\n\\[ P_{\\text{gas}} \\approx 119.60 \\, \\text{kPa} \\]\n\nANSWER: A."
