"To solve this problem, we need to analyze the electrochemical cell diagram and the context provided.\n\n1. **Type of Cell:** This is an electrolytic cell, indicated by the connection to a voltage source. This implies that non-spontaneous reactions are driven by an external voltage.\n\n2. **Electrodes:** The description mentions electrodes labeled as 1 and 2, both made of carbon (C(s)). They are immersed in a ZnSO\u2084 (aq) solution.\n\n3. **Process at Electrodes:**\n   - **Anode:** Oxidation occurs at the anode. Since the electrons flow from the anode, and one of the options (D) says zinc metal donates electrons, this suggests electrode #1 could be where Zn(s) forms Zn\u00b2\u207a ions by losing electrons.\n   - **Cathode:** Reduction happens at the cathode. Electrons travel towards the cathode, suggesting that a possible plating or some reduction reaction could happen there.\n\n4. **Direction of Electron Flow:**\n   - Electrode #1 likely connects to the negative terminal of the battery (since oxidation releases electrons), meaning electrons travel from the electrode to the circuit, not towards Zn\u00b2\u207a ions or other entities in the flask.\n\n5. **Analyzing the Options:**\n   - **A. Electrons travel out of electrode toward voltage source.** This is consistent with the role of the anode in an electrolytic cell.\n   - **B. Electrons travel out of electrode toward aqueous sulfate ions.** Sulfate ions are not directly involved in electron movement in this context.\n   - **C. Zinc metal electroplates onto electrode.** This happens at the cathode, not at electrode #1 (likely the anode).\n   - **D. Zinc metal donates electrons to electrode.** While zinc losing electrons is part of the process, this happens inherently during oxidation; thus misleading in context.\n   - **E. Oxygen gas bubbles from electrode.** Likely irrelevant here, unless there was an aqueous solution that provides oxygen evolution.\n\nBased on the above reasoning and knowing the role of the anode in an electrolytic cell:\n\nANSWER: A. Electrons travel out of electrode toward voltage source."
