"To determine the equilibrium vapor pressure of \\(C_2H_5OH\\) relative to \\(CH_3OH\\), we consider intermolecular forces:\n\n1. **Hydrogen Bonding:** Both \\(CH_3OH\\) and \\(C_2H_5OH\\) can form hydrogen bonds. However, the strength and number of hydrogen bonds can vary due to molecular size and structure.\n\n2. **London Dispersion Forces:** These forces are generally stronger in larger molecules. \\(C_2H_5OH\\), being larger than \\(CH_3OH\\), will experience stronger dispersion forces.\n\n3. **Boiling Points:** A higher boiling point generally indicates stronger intermolecular forces. \\(C_2H_5OH\\) has a higher boiling point (351 K) than \\(CH_3OH\\) (338 K), suggesting stronger intermolecular forces in \\(C_2H_5OH\\).\n\nStronger intermolecular forces in \\(C_2H_5OH\\) indicate it will have a lower equilibrium vapor pressure compared to \\(CH_3OH\\), which aligns with \\(C_2H_5OH\\)'s ability to form more or stronger hydrogen bonds and have greater London dispersion forces due to its larger size.\n\nThus, the most appropriate choice is:\n\nANSWER: C."
"The reactions shown involve hydrogenation steps:\n\n1. The first reaction involves the hydrogenation of benzene to form cyclohexene, which requires overcoming the aromatic stability of benzene. This typically has a high activation energy (\\(E_1\\)).\n\n2. The second reaction is the hydrogenation of cyclohexene (an alkene) to form cyclohexane. This reaction generally has a lower activation energy (\\(E_2\\)) than the aromatic hydrogenation of benzene because alkenes are less stable than aromatic systems.\n\n3. The third reaction is the hydrogenation of cyclohexane (an alkane) to form a saturated system, which generally requires minimal activation energy (\\(E_3\\)).\n\nThus, the most likely order is based on stability considerations: aromatic (benzene) > alkene (cyclohexene) > alkane (cyclohexane).\n\nTherefore, the relation should be \\(E_3 < E_2 < E_1\\).\n\nANSWER: D."
