"To determine the number of lone pairs on each sulfur atom in S\u2088:\n\n1. **Total Valence Electrons per Sulfur Atom**: \n   - Sulfur is in group 16, so it has 6 valence electrons.\n\n2. **Bonding in S\u2088**:\n   - Each sulfur atom forms two single bonds (one with each neighboring sulfur atom). Each bond uses up 1 electron from sulfur's 6 valence electrons, totaling 2 electrons for bonding.\n\n3. **Remaining Electrons as Lone Pairs**:\n   - Total valence electrons on a sulfur atom = 6.\n   - Electrons used in bonding = 2.\n   - Therefore, electrons available for lone pairs = 6 - 2 = 4.\n   - This gives 2 lone pairs (4 electrons) per sulfur atom, as each lone pair consists of 2 electrons.\n\nANSWER: B"
"To calculate the standard free-energy change (\\( \\Delta G^\\circ' \\)) for the given reaction, we use the relationship between the equilibrium constant (\\( K'_{eq} \\)) and \\( \\Delta G^\\circ' \\):\n\n\\[ \\Delta G^\\circ' = -RT \\ln K'_{eq} \\]\n\nWhere:\n- \\( R \\) is the gas constant, \\( 8.314 \\, \\text{J/mol} \\cdot \\text{K} \\).\n- \\( T \\) is the temperature in Kelvin. At 25 \u00b0C, \\( T = 298 \\, \\text{K} \\).\n- \\( K'_{eq} \\) is the equilibrium constant, given as 6.8.\n\nSteps:\n1. Convert units for \\( R \\) from J to kJ: \\( R = 0.008314 \\, \\text{kJ/mol} \\cdot \\text{K} \\).\n2. Substitute the given values into the formula:\n\n\\[ \\Delta G^\\circ' = -0.008314 \\, \\text{kJ/mol} \\cdot \\text{K} \\times 298 \\, \\text{K} \\times \\ln(6.8) \\]\n\n3. Calculate \\( \\ln(6.8) \\): \n\n\\[ \\ln(6.8) \\approx 1.918 \\]\n\n4. Calculate \\( \\Delta G^\\circ' \\):\n\n\\[ \\Delta G^\\circ' = -0.008314 \\times 298 \\times 1.918 \\]\n\n\\[ \\Delta G^\\circ' \\approx -4.74 \\, \\text{kJ/mol} \\]\n\nThus, the standard free-energy change for the reaction is approximately \\(-4.74 \\, \\text{kJ/mol} \\).\n\nANSWER: C"
