"In order to rank the solutions by increasing pH, we need to analyze each mixture:\n\n### Solution X:\n- 100 mL of 0.10 M NaOH is mixed with 100 mL of 0.10 M HBr.\n- NaOH is a strong base, and HBr is a strong acid.\n- Equal moles of NaOH and HBr react to form water and neutralize each other.\n- Resulting solution is neutral or has a pH of around 7.\n\n### Solution Y:\n- 100 mL of 0.10 M NaBr is mixed with 100 mL of 0.10 M HBr.\n- NaBr is neutral (resulting from a strong acid and strong base) and doesn't affect pH.\n- The solution will still have the acidic pH due to HBr.\n- The concentration of remaining HBr is reduced (0.05 M after dilution), making it still acidic.\n\n### Solution Z:\n- 100 mL of 0.10 M HC\u2082H\u2083O\u2082 (acetic acid) is mixed with 100 mL of 0.10 M NaC\u2082H\u2083O\u2082.\n- This creates a buffer solution (acetic acid/sodium acetate).\n- Buffers have a pH determined by the acetic acid and its conjugate base.\n- The pH of this buffer will be higher than the pH of the acidic solution Y.\n\n### Ordering by pH:\n- Solution Y (most acidic, due to HBr).\n- Solution Z (buffer around acetic acid, less acidic than HBr).\n- Solution X (neutral due to strong acid/strong base neutralization).\n\n**ANSWER: D. Z < Y < X**."
