"To find the percent by weight of copper in \\(Cu_2S\\), follow these steps:\n\n1. Calculate the molar mass of \\(Cu_2S\\):\n   - Copper (\\(Cu\\)) has an atomic mass of approximately 63.55 g/mol.\n   - Sulfur (\\(S\\)) has an atomic mass of approximately 32.07 g/mol.\n   \n   \\[\n   Molar \\, mass \\, of \\, Cu_2S = 2 \\times 63.55 \\, (Cu) + 32.07 \\, (S) = 159.17 \\, g/mol\n   \\]\n\n2. Calculate the total mass of copper in \\(Cu_2S\\):\n   \\[\n   Total \\, mass \\, of \\, Cu = 2 \\times 63.55 = 127.10 \\, g/mol\n   \\]\n\n3. Calculate the percent by weight of copper in \\(Cu_2S\\):\n   \\[\n   \\text{Percent by weight of copper} = \\left(\\frac{127.10}{159.17}\\right) \\times 100 \\approx 79.85\n   \\]\n\nANSWER: D"
"To determine how many stereoisomers may have the constitution shown, we need to identify the stereocenters in the molecule. \n\n1. **Identify Stereocenters:**\n   - The compound is a hydroxyaldehyde with an alkene chain.\n   - The 1,4-dihydroxy ketone moiety has two chiral carbons (adjacent to the hydroxyl groups).\n\n2. **Double Bonds (Cis/Trans Isomerism):**\n   - The chain has two double bonds, which can each lead to cis/trans (E/Z) isomerism.\n\n3. **Number of Stereoisomers:**\n   - For each chiral center, there are 2 configurations (R or S).\n   - The number of stereoisomers = \\(2^n\\) where \\(n\\) is the total number of stereocenters and double bonds with E/Z possibilities.\n   - In this case: 2 chiral centers (2 options each) and 2 double bonds (2 options each).\n\n\\[\n2^{2\\text{ (chiral centers)}} \\times 2^{2\\text{ (double bonds)}} = 2^4 = 16\n\\]\n\nANSWER: C. 16."
"To determine which structure is not a valid canonical structure for formaldehyde, let's analyze each option:\n\n1. **Structure I (CH\u2082=O)**:\n   - This is the standard Lewis structure for formaldehyde showing the typical double bonded oxygen. It is a valid and primary structure.\n\n2. **Structure II (\u2295CH\u2082\u2013O\u2296)**:\n   - This structure shows one oxygen with a negative charge and carbon with a positive charge. This could be a resonance form where oxygen is more electronegative and can bear the negative charge, which is reasonable.\n\n3. **Structure III (\u2296CH\u2082\u2013O\u2295)**:\n   - In this structure, carbon has a negative charge and oxygen has a positive charge. \n   - This distribution of charges is not feasible since carbon is less electronegative compared to oxygen, making it unfavorable for carbon to bear a negative charge, especially in such a simple molecule.\n\nBased on resonance theory and electronegativity considerations, structure III is not a plausible canonical form because it has an unfavorable charge distribution.\n\nANSWER: C"
"To identify which compounds yield the same carbocation upon ionization, let's analyze each structure in terms of where the carbocation would form after ionization (loss of the bromide ion):\n\n- **(A)**: Upon losing the Br\u207b ion, a carbocation forms at the allylic position. This will be a secondary carbocation adjacent to the double bond, allowing some stabilization due to resonance.\n  \n- **(B)**: On ionization, a secondary carbocation forms at the benzylic position. Due to the proximity to the pi bond, a rearrangement occurs leading to a tertiary carbocation formation featuring resonance stabilization.\n  \n- **(C)**: Ionization here would also lead to a benzylic secondary carbocation. This structure can be rearranged to offer resonance stabilization, which results in the same tertiary carbocation as option (B).\n\n- **(D)**: Losing Br\u2212 results in a different carbocation, but because of the position, it doesn't convert to match the others due to its non-allylic nature.\n\nThus, compounds **(B)** and **(C)** generate the same carbocation due to resonance and rearrangement abilities related to their shared structural features.\n\nANSWER: B, C."
"To determine the molecular mass of the compound depicted, we need to identify the atoms and their quantities within the molecule.\n\nThe structure shows:\n- 2 Carbon (C) atoms\n- 2 Bromine (Br) atoms\n- 2 Hydrogen (H) atoms\n\nThe atomic masses are approximately:\n- Carbon: 12 amu\n- Bromine: 80 amu\n- Hydrogen: 1 amu\n\nNow, calculate the total molecular mass:\n- Carbon: \\(2 \\times 12 = 24\\) amu\n- Bromine: \\(2 \\times 80 = 160\\) amu\n- Hydrogen: \\(2 \\times 1 = 2\\) amu\n\nAdd these values to find the total molecular mass:\n\\[ 24 + 160 + 2 = 186 \\, \\text{amu} \\]\n\nANSWER: 186"
