"To calculate the enthalpy change for the reaction \\( \\text{OH(g)} \\rightarrow \\text{H(g)} + \\text{O(g)} \\), we can use Hess's Law and the given reactions.\n\nGiven reactions with their enthalpies:\n\n1. \\( \\frac{1}{2} \\text{H}_2(g) + \\frac{1}{2} \\text{O}_2(g) \\rightarrow \\text{OH(g)} \\), \\( \\Delta H^\\circ = 38.95 \\, \\text{kJ/mol} \\)\n2. \\( \\text{H}_2(g) + \\frac{1}{2} \\text{O}_2(g) \\rightarrow \\text{H}_2\\text{O(g)} \\), \\( \\Delta H^\\circ = -241.814 \\, \\text{kJ/mol} \\)\n3. \\( \\text{H}_2(g) \\rightarrow 2\\text{H(g)} \\), \\( \\Delta H^\\circ = 435.994 \\, \\text{kJ/mol} \\)\n4. \\( \\text{O}_2(g) \\rightarrow 2\\text{O(g)} \\), \\( \\Delta H^\\circ = 498.34 \\, \\text{kJ/mol} \\)\n\nWe need to express the target reaction in terms of the given reactions.\n\n### Steps:\n\n1. Reverse Reaction 1: \n   \\[ \\text{OH(g)} \\rightarrow \\frac{1}{2} \\text{H}_2(g) + \\frac{1}{2} \\text{O}_2(g) \\]\n   \\[ \\Delta H^\\circ = -38.95 \\, \\text{kJ/mol} \\]\n\n2. Use Reaction 3 to break \\( \\frac{1}{2} \\text{H}_2(g) \\) into H(g):\n   \\[ \\frac{1}{2} \\text{H}_2(g) \\rightarrow \\text{H(g)} \\]\n   \\[ \\Delta H^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 435.994 \\, \\text{kJ/mol} = 217.997 \\, \\text{kJ/mol} \\]\n\n3. Use Reaction 4 to break \\( \\frac{1}{2} \\text{O}_2(g) \\) into O(g):\n   \\[ \\frac{1}{2} \\text{O}_2(g) \\rightarrow \\text{O(g)} \\]\n   \\[ \\Delta H^\\circ = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 498.34 \\, \\text{kJ/mol} = 249.17 \\, \\text{kJ/mol} \\]\n\n### Sum of enthalpies:\n\n\\[ \\Delta H^\\circ = (-38.95) + 217.997 + 249.17 \\]\n\n\\[ \\Delta H^\\circ = 428.217 \\, \\text{kJ/mol} \\]\n\nRounding to significant figures, it matches with:\n\nANSWER: C."
"To determine the most acidic hydrogen, we need to consider factors such as the stability of the conjugate base and the electronegative environment around the hydrogen.\n\n1. **Ha**: This hydrogen is alpha to a carbonyl group, which can stabilize the conjugate base through resonance.\n\n2. **Hb, Hc**: These hydrogens are in an alkyl position, not notably close to any electron-withdrawing groups, which typically makes them less acidic.\n\n3. **Hd**: This hydrogen is bonded to nitrogen. Generally, N-H hydrogens can be acidic, especially in an amide system like this, but less so than O-H.\n\n4. **He**: Similar to Hd, but in terms of positioning, it is part of the heterocycle where Hd might be involved in intramolecular hydrogen bonding or resonance which could enhance acidity.\n\nGiven the presence of a carbonyl group next to Ha, this hydrogen is the most likely to be acidic due to resonance stabilization of its conjugate base.\n\nANSWER: A."
"To determine which neutral unsaturated planar hydrocarbon will experience a greater change in bond order upon adding a $\\pi$ electron to form the anion, we should consider the resonance stability and aromaticity of the resulting anion.\n\n1. **Molecule A:**\n   - Structure: Cyclopentadienyl cation.\n   - If a $\\pi$ electron is added, it will form the cyclopentadienyl anion, which has 6 $\\pi$ electrons.\n   - According to H\u00fcckel\u2019s rule, having 4n+2 (where n is an integer) $\\pi$ electrons makes a compound aromatic.\n   - The cyclopentadienyl anion is aromatic (n=1), and this stabilization will lead to a significant change in bond order due to increased delocalization.\n\n2. **Molecule B:**\n   - Structure: Cycloheptatrienyl cation (tropylium ion).\n   - Adding a $\\pi$ electron will form the cycloheptatrienyl anion, which will have 8 $\\pi$ electrons.\n   - 8 $\\pi$ electrons do not satisfy H\u00fcckel\u2019s rule (4n+2), so the compound is not aromatic, and the change in bond order is less pronounced compared to the stabilization seen in an aromatic system.\n\nComparing both:\n- Adding a $\\pi$ electron to the cyclopentadienyl cation leads to the formation of an aromatic anion, which results in a significant change in bond order. \n- The cycloheptatrienyl cation becomes non-aromatic upon the addition of the $\\pi$ electron, so the change is less significant.\n\nTherefore, Molecule A experiences a greater change in bond order due to the aromatic stabilization.\n\nANSWER: A"
