"To determine which statements are true about the galvanic cell, let's analyze the given information and the standard reduction potentials:\n\n1. **Reduction Potentials:**\n   - \\( Ag^+ + e^- \\rightarrow Ag \\), \\( E^\\circ_{\\text{red}} = +0.80 \\, \\text{V} \\)\n   - \\( Ni^{2+} + 2e^- \\rightarrow Ni \\), \\( E^\\circ_{\\text{red}} = -0.28 \\, \\text{V} \\)\n\n2. **Determine the Anode and Cathode:**\n   - The half-cell with the higher reduction potential will act as the cathode (reduction occurs here).\n   - The silver ion has a higher reduction potential (+0.80 V) compared to nickel (-0.28 V), so the silver electrode is the cathode.\n\n3. **Determine the Anode:**\n   - The anode is where oxidation occurs and has the lower reduction potential.\n   - Therefore, the nickel electrode is the anode.\n\n4. **Direction of Electron Flow:**\n   - Electrons flow from the anode to the cathode in a galvanic cell.\n   - Thus, electrons flow from nickel (anode) to silver (cathode).\n\nNow, let's evaluate each statement:\n\nI: Nickel is the cathode. (False, nickel is the anode.)\nII: Silver is the anode. (False, silver is the cathode.)\nIII: Electrons flow from nickel to silver. (True, as electrons flow from anode to cathode.)\n\nANSWER: C"
