"To determine the correct answer, let's analyze the scenario provided in conjunction with the microscopic appearance of the kidney.\n\n1. **Clinical Presentation:**\n   - The patient is a 45-year-old woman with severe hypertension (300/150 mm Hg).\n   - She presents with kidney impairment (serum creatinine is 3.7 mg/dL).\n   - These symptoms suggest a hypertensive emergency affecting the kidneys.\n\n2. **Microscopic Appearance:**\n   - The image shows an \"onion-skin\" appearance with concentric thickening of the vessel wall and narrowing of the lumen.\n   - This pattern is characteristic of hyperplastic arteriolosclerosis, often associated with malignant hypertension.\n\n3. **Options Analysis:**\n   - **A. Benign nephrosclerosis:** This condition is associated with long-standing mild to moderate hypertension and exhibits hyaline change in arterioles, not the \"onion-skin\" pattern.\n   - **B. Hyperplastic arteriolosclerosis:** Typically caused by severe hypertension and shows an \"onion-skin\" concentric thickening, matching the description and image.\n   - **C. Nodular glomerulosclerosis:** Associated with diabetes mellitus, showing Kimmelstiel-Wilson nodules, not the pattern seen here.\n   - **D. Hyaline arteriolosclerosis:** Usually seen in benign hypertension and diabetes, characterized by homogeneous, pink, hyaline walls, not the \"onion-skin\" pattern.\n\nBased on the microscopic features and clinical context, the changes in the kidney are most consistent with **hyperplastic arteriolosclerosis**.\n\nANSWER: B."
