"To determine which structures are most closely associated with the transport of deoxygenated blood, we need to identify the parts of the heart responsible for receiving and transporting blood that has not yet been oxygenated in the lungs. In the human heart:\n\n1. **Deoxygenated blood** is brought to the heart via the superior and inferior vena cava.\n2. It enters the **right atrium (usually D)**.\n3. From the right atrium, blood moves through the **tricuspid valve and into the right ventricle (F)**.\n4. Then it is pumped through the **pulmonary artery (H or I)** towards the lungs. \n\nNow, let's match these to the given structures in the diagram:\n\n- D is the right atrium,\n- H is the pulmonary artery,\n- I is part of the right atrium or right ventricle, as indicated by its placement.\n\nThus, the structures associated with the transport of deoxygenated blood are D, H, and I.\n\nANSWER: D."
