"To identify the underlying condition consistent with the gross appearance of the heart, I will evaluate each option:\n\nA. Amyloidosis: This typically results in a restrictive cardiomyopathy, characterized by deposition of amyloid protein in the heart tissue. The heart may appear firm and rubbery, often without significant enlargement.\n\nB. Systemic hypertension: This usually causes left ventricular hypertrophy due to increased workload against high systemic pressures. The heart is often enlarged and may show thickened walls.\n\nC. Diffuse scleroderma: Can lead to restrictive cardiomyopathy, but the heart changes are not as distinct or specific as seen in amyloid deposition.\n\nD. Atherosclerosis: Typically affects coronary arteries with potential for ischemic damage, but gross changes are often focused in the vessels themselves; may lead to myocardial infarctions without distinct gross uniform thickening.\n\nBased on the provided heart image, particularly if there is a uniform, possibly thickened appearance of the ventricular walls, that can point toward changes from systemic hypertension due to pressure overload.\n\nANSWER: B. Systemic hypertension."
