"To determine if a deadlock is present, we need to analyze the graphs provided.\n\n### Resource Allocation Graph (Image 1):\n- **Processes** (P1, P2, etc.) send edges to **Resources** (R1, R2, etc.) representing requests.\n- **Resources** send edges to **Processes** denoting allocation.\n\n**Observations:**\n1. **P1** requests **R1**.\n2. **R1** allocated to **P2**.\n3. **P2** requests **R3**.\n4. **R3** allocated to **P5**.\n5. **P3** requests **R4**.\n6. **R4** allocated to **P2**.\n7. **P4** requests **R5**.\n8. **R5** allocated to **P4**.\n9. **R2** allocated to **P1**.\n\nWe look for cycles, indicating a deadlock:\n- **P1 -> R1 -> P2 -> R3 -> P5**: No cycle back to P1, no deadlock here.\n- **P4 -> R5 (Allocated)**: Since R5 is allocated back to P4, this is a standalone loop, not involving other processes, so no deadlock.\n  \nNo cycles involving other processes indicate a deadlock.\n\n### Wait-for Graph (Image 2):\nTransform the resource allocation to show direct waits:\n- If Process A is requesting a Resource allocated to Process B, A waits for B.\n\n**Observations:**\n1. **P1 waits for P2** (due to R1).\n2. **P2 waits for P5** (due to R3).\n3. **P3 waits for P2** (due to R4).\n4. **P4 waits for itself** effectively (due to R5).\n\nCheck for cycles:\n- **P1 -> P2 -> P5**: Ends without circular wait.\n- **P3 -> P2 -> P5**: Ends without circular wait.\n- **P4** holds its own resource.\n\nNo cycles indicate a complete deadlock in the wait-for graph.\n\n### Conclusion:\nThe Resource Allocation Graph and Wait-for Graph do not reveal any interdependencies resulting in cyclic waiting that could lead to deadlock.\n\nANSWER: B"
