"To determine the value of \\( k \\) that generates the solid decision boundary in the k-nearest neighbors (k-NN) algorithm, we need to consider how different \\( k \\) values affect the boundary:\n\n1. **k=1:** The decision boundary is highly flexible and closely follows the data points. It can be very jagged because each point affects the decision boundary, and it may not generalize well.\n\n2. **k=2:** The boundary is still relatively flexible but slightly smoother than \\( k=1 \\).\n\n3. **k=10:** The boundary becomes smoother and is less sensitive to noise or individual data points compared to lower \\( k \\) values.\n\n4. **k=100:** The boundary is much smoother and more generalized. It will largely ignore local variations and individual outliers, following the overall trend of the data distribution.\n\nIn the image, the solid decision boundary appears to be very jagged and closely follows the clusters of data points, particularly focusing on localized variations and showing high detail. This characteristic is typical of a very low \\( k \\) value.\n\nANSWER: A"
