"To calculate the average waiting time, we first need to determine the waiting time for each process. The waiting time is the total time a process spends in the ready queue before it gets executed.\n\nFrom the Gantt chart, we have the execution timeline as follows:\n\n- \\( P_1 \\) runs from 0 to 10\n- \\( P_2 \\) runs from 10 to 20, then again from 40 to 50, and finally from 52 to 61\n- \\( P_3 \\) runs from 20 to 23\n- \\( P_4 \\) runs from 23 to 30\n- \\( P_5 \\) runs from 30 to 40, then again from 50 to 52\n\nLet's calculate the waiting time for each process:\n\n1. **\\( P_1 \\):** \n   - Begins at 0 with no waiting.\n   - **Waiting Time = 0**\n\n2. **\\( P_2 \\):** \n   - First runs at 10 (after waiting 10 ms).\n   - Then waits from 20 to 40 (20 ms) before running again.\n   - Waits from 50 to 52 (2 ms) before the final execution.\n   - **Total Waiting Time = 10 + 20 + 2 = 32**\n\n3. **\\( P_3 \\):** \n   - First runs at 20 (after waiting 20 ms).\n   - **Waiting Time = 20**\n\n4. **\\( P_4 \\):** \n   - First runs at 23 (after waiting 23 ms).\n   - **Waiting Time = 23**\n\n5. **\\( P_5 \\):** \n   - First runs at 30 (after waiting 30 ms), and again at 50.\n   - The waiting time for the second part only counts from when the previous process starts so it's 0.\n   - **Waiting Time = 30**\n\nNow, calculate the average waiting time:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Average Waiting Time} = \\frac{0 + 32 + 20 + 23 + 30}{5} = \\frac{105}{5} = 21 \\text{ ms}\n\\]\n\nANSWER: A"
