"To determine what one step of gradient descent will do at a local optimum, consider the following steps:\n\n1. **Gradient Calculation**: At a local minimum or maximum, the gradient (derivative) of the function, \\(\\frac{d}{d\\theta_1} J(\\theta_1)\\), is zero.\n\n2. **Gradient Descent Step**: The update rule for gradient descent is:\n   \\[\n   \\theta_1 := \\theta_1 - \\alpha \\frac{d}{d\\theta_1} J(\\theta_1)\n   \\]\n   If the gradient is zero, the update becomes:\n   \\[\n   \\theta_1 := \\theta_1 - \\alpha \\times 0 = \\theta_1\n   \\]\n\n3. **Conclusion**: Therefore, at a local optimum, \\(\\theta_1\\) remains unchanged because the gradient is zero.\n\nANSWER: A."
