"To analyze the structure:\n\n1. The diagram is a finite automaton with a start state on the left and an accepting state on the right.\n2. There are epsilon (\u03b5) transitions from the start state to loops labeled \"s\" and \"t\" indicating zero-length moves.\n3. After the transition through either \"s\" or \"t,\" the automaton moves to the accepting state.\n4. The automaton accepts either the string \"s\" or the string \"t,\" as indicated by the paths available.\n\nGiven the options, we need to check which expression matches this behavior:\n\n- **A. s|t**: This represents a choice between \"s\" and \"t,\" which corresponds to the automaton's structure, accepting either \"s\" or \"t.\"\n- **B. st**: This implies \"s\" followed by \"t,\" which doesn't match since there is no enforced order.\n- **C. s\\*t\\***: This represents zero or more \"s\" followed by zero or more \"t,\" which doesn't fit.\n- **D. s+t+**: This requires one or more \"s\" and one or more \"t,\" which also doesn't fit.\n\nThe correct choice is the expression representing a choice between \"s\" and \"t\":\n\nANSWER: A"
"To determine which processor has better peak performance, we need to calculate the maximum possible instructions per second for each processor. This is derived using the formula:\n\n\\[ \\text{Peak Performance} = \\frac{\\text{Clock Speed}}{\\text{Minimum CPI}} \\]\n\n**For P1:**\n- Clock Speed = 4 GHz = \\(4 \\times 10^9\\) Hz\n- Minimum CPI from the table = 1 (Class A)\n\nPeak Performance for P1:\n\\[ \\text{Peak Performance of P1} = \\frac{4 \\times 10^9}{1} = 4 \\times 10^9 \\text{ instructions per second} \\]\n\n**For P2:**\n- Clock Speed = 6 GHz = \\(6 \\times 10^9\\) Hz\n- Minimum CPI from the table = 2 (Classes A, B, C)\n\nPeak Performance for P2:\n\\[ \\text{Peak Performance of P2} = \\frac{6 \\times 10^9}{2} = 3 \\times 10^9 \\text{ instructions per second} \\]\n\nComparing the peak performances:\n- P1: \\(4 \\times 10^9\\) instructions per second\n- P2: \\(3 \\times 10^9\\) instructions per second\n\nP1 has a higher peak performance compared to P2.\n\nANSWER: A"
