"To solve this problem using preemptive Shortest Job First (SJF) scheduling, we follow these steps:\n\n1. **At time 0:** \n   - Process P1 arrives with a burst time of 6.\n   - Since it's the only process, it starts executing.\n\n2. **At time 1:**\n   - P2 arrives with a burst time of 8.\n   - P1 (remaining time 5) is shorter than P2, so P1 continues.\n\n3. **At time 2:**\n   - P3 arrives with a burst time of 7.\n   - P1 (remaining time 4) is still the shortest, so P1 continues.\n\n4. **At time 3:**\n   - P4 arrives with a burst time of 3.\n   - P4 is the shortest, so it preempts P1 and starts executing.\n\n5. **At time 6:**\n   - P4 finishes. The remaining times are:\n     - P1: 4\n     - P2: 8\n     - P3: 7\n   - P1 is the shortest, so P1 continues execution.\n\n6. **At time 10:**\n   - P1 finishes. The remaining times are:\n     - P2: 8\n     - P3: 7\n   - P3 is the shortest, so P3 starts.\n\n7. **At time 17:**\n   - P3 finishes. Only P2 is left.\n   - P2 starts.\n\n8. **At time 25:**\n   - P2 finishes.\n\nThe execution order is as follows:\n- 0 to 1: P1\n- 1 to 3: P1\n- 3 to 6: P4\n- 6 to 10: P1\n- 10 to 17: P3\n- 17 to 25: P2\n\nThis corresponds with the execution in option:\n\nANSWER: F (None of the other answers)"
