"To keep the link busy, the combined data rate from both senders must equal the link's bandwidth. Here's how you determine the smallest value of \\( \\text{cwnd}_1 + \\text{cwnd}_2 \\):\n\n1. **Calculate the bandwidth-delay product (BDP):**\n   \\[\n   \\text{BDP} = \\text{bandwidth} \\times \\text{round-trip time (RTT)}\n   \\]\n   Bandwidth = 6 Mb/s = 0.75 MB/s\n\n   RTT = \\( 2 \\times 50 \\text{ ms} = 100 \\text{ ms} = 0.1 \\text{ s} \\)\n\n   \\[\n   \\text{BDP} = 0.75 \\text{ MB/s} \\times 0.1 \\text{ s} = 0.075 \\text{ MB} = 75 \\text{ KB}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Link Utilization:**\n   - To keep the link busy, the sum of the congestion windows needs to equal the BDP.\n   - So, \\( \\text{cwnd}_1 + \\text{cwnd}_2 = 75 \\text{ KB} \\).\n\nTherefore, the smallest value of \\( \\text{cwnd}_1 + \\text{cwnd}_2 \\) for which the link stays busy all the time is:\n\nANSWER: 75"
