"To determine the type of grammar, we can test for LL(1) and SLR(1) by analyzing its properties.\n\n**Testing for LL(1):**\n\nAn LL(1) grammar requires that:\n1. No production has common prefixes (FIRST sets should not intersect).\n2. It should be left-factored.\n3. There should not be any left recursion.\n\nFor the given grammar, check the FIRST sets:\n\n- \\( S \\to A a A b \\) and \\( S \\to B b B a \\):\n  - \\( A \\to \\epsilon \\), so \\( A a A b \\) yields \\( a \\).\n  - \\( B \\to \\epsilon \\), so \\( B b B a \\) yields \\( b \\).\n  \nTherefore, the FIRST sets don't have intersections (FIRST(A a A b) = {a}, FIRST(B b B a) = {b}), satisfying condition 1.\n\nAdditionally:\n- No left recursion is present as no non-terminal derives itself directly or indirectly.\n- The grammar is simple enough (~left factoring isn\u2019t needed).\n\n**Testing for SLR(1):**\n\nAn SLR(1) grammar requires that any conflicts (shift-reduce or reduce-reduce) should not occur in the parsing table. Each grammar state must have a unique action for each symbol:\n\nFor states:\n- \\( S \\to A a A b \\) or \\( S \\to B b B a \\), there's no ambiguity or conflicts upon examination since the follow sets will not overlap actions.\n\nGiven all checks, both LL(1) and SLR(1) conditions are satisfied, but usually, SLR(1) is less restrictive than LL(1).\n\nSince the given grammar satisfies both conditions, it is more efficiently described as an SLR(1) grammar.\n\nANSWER: B."
