"To identify the NOT AND (NAND) gate using transistor logic, we need to understand the arrangement of transistors in each figure:\n\n1. **Figure (a):** \n   - This is a single transistor configuration. It acts as an inverter, which is a NOT gate.\n\n2. **Figure (b):**\n   - This is a series arrangement of two transistors. For the output to be high, both inputs V1 and V2 need to be high. This is the behavior of an AND gate.\n\n3. **Figure (c):**\n   - This is a parallel arrangement of two transistors. For the output to be low, both inputs V1 and V2 need to be high. This is the behavior of a NAND gate, as it is the opposite of AND.\n\nThe NAND gate in transistor logic is represented by an arrangement where two transistors are in parallel and output high when at least one input is low.\n\nANSWER: C."
