"To determine the cache block size in L1, we need to analyze how the latency values correspond to the data access pattern.\n\nEach cache block holds a certain number of cache lines. In code (1), you are accessing memory locations sequentially (i.e., stride = 1).\n\nGiven:\n- Each array element is a 32-bit integer, or 4 bytes.\n- L1 cache is 4kB, set associative.\n\nThe graph shows the latency for the first 64 reads, and we need to analyze the pattern in latency which will hint us about the block size:\n\n1. **Latency Behavior**: If the first few accesses show higher latency and the subsequent accesses show lower latency, it indicates cache hits after the initial cache misses.\n\n2. **Cache Block Size**: The latency drop (indicating a cache hit rather than a miss) will occur every time a new cache block is loaded. So, if the latency lowers after every X accesses, the cache block size can be inferred as X times the size of each data element, which is 4 bytes.\n\nIf you inspect the graph (not shown here), look for when the latency becomes consistent. The point at which this change occurs will indicate the number of sequentially read elements that fit in one cache block.\n\nANSWER: B."
"To determine the maximum number of packets per second that can be transmitted, we first calculate the time required to transmit a single packet.\n\n1. **Calculate the time to transmit one packet:**\n   - Packet length \\( L = 16,000 \\) bits.\n   - Transmission rate \\( R = 10 \\) Mbps \\( = 10 \\times 10^6 \\) bps.\n\n   Transmission time for one packet \\( T = \\frac{L}{R} = \\frac{16,000 \\text{ bits}}{10 \\times 10^6 \\text{ bps}} = \\frac{16,000}{10,000,000} \\) seconds\n\n   \\( T = 0.0016 \\) seconds\n\n2. **Calculate the maximum number of packets per second:**\n   - Number of packets per second \\( = \\frac{1}{T} = \\frac{1}{0.0016} \\approx 625 \\) packets per second.\n\nANSWER: C"
