"To determine the joint probability \\( P(H, U, P, W) \\) given the Bayesian network, we need to consider the dependencies indicated by the arrows:\n\n1. \\( H \\) has no parents.\n2. \\( U \\) depends on \\( H \\).\n3. \\( P \\) depends on \\( U \\).\n4. \\( W \\) depends on \\( P \\).\n\nThe joint probability based on these dependencies is:\n\n\\[ P(H, U, P, W) = P(H) \\times P(U | H) \\times P(P | U) \\times P(W | P) \\]\n\nGiven the options:\n\nA. \\( P(H, U, P, W) = P(H) \\times P(W) \\times P(P) \\times P(U) \\) - Incorrect, does not reflect dependencies.\n\nB. \\( P(H, U, P, W) = P(H) \\times P(W) \\times P(P | W) \\times P(W | H, P) \\) - Incorrect, dependencies are not accurate.\n\nC. \\( P(H, U, P, W) = P(H) \\times P(W) \\times P(P | W) \\times P(U | H, P) \\) - Incorrect, dependencies are not accurate.\n\nD. None of the above.\n\nThe correct expression should reflect the dependencies as \\( P(H) \\times P(U | H) \\times P(P | U) \\times P(W | P) \\).\n\nANSWER: D."
