"To determine which transaction does not execute correctly due to uncontrolled concurrent execution, let's analyze both transactions:\n\n**Transaction T1:**\n1. Reads the value of `X`.\n2. Subtracts `N` from `X`.\n3. Writes the new value back to `X`.\n4. Reads the value of `Y`.\n5. Adds `N` to `Y`.\n6. Writes the new value back to `Y`.\n\n**Transaction T3:**\n1. Initializes `sum` to 0.\n2. Reads a series of items, including `X` and `Y`.\n3. Adds these values to `sum`.\n\n**Concurrent Execution Issue:**\n- The main issue arises from both transactions accessing and modifying the same items, `X` and `Y`.\n\n**Conflict Analysis:**\n- Suppose T1 begins by reading `X`, modifies it, and writes it back.\n- If during this time, T3 reads `X` before T1 writes the updated value back, T3 will read the unmodified `X`.\n- Similarly, if T3 reads `Y` before T1 updates it, T3 will have the unmodified value of `Y`.\n\nThis conflict can result in incorrect execution due to T3 potentially summing the old values of `X` and `Y` instead of the updated ones by T1.\n\n**Conclusion:**\nSince both transactions could potentially produce incorrect results when executed concurrently without proper isolation, both T1 and T3 are affected by uncontrolled concurrent execution.\n\nANSWER: C"
