"To determine the type of cache mapping:\n\n1. **Fully Associative**: Any block of memory can be stored in any cache line. This requires a tag for each block and tends to allow greater flexibility but higher complexity for searching.\n\n2. **Direct Mapped**: Each memory block is mapped to exactly one cache line. This is identified using the lower bits of the block's address. It's simple and fast to search, but can lead to more conflicts as multiple block addresses might map to the same cache line.\n\n3. **Set Associative**: It\u2019s a combination of the above two. Memory blocks map to a set of lines in the cache. Each set contains multiple lines. This allows some flexibility while reducing complexity compared to a fully associative cache.\n\nIn the given diagram:\n- The block frame address is sequential, meaning each block is directly mapped to one cache location as there is no indication of multiple lines per set.\n\nThis indicates a **Direct Mapped** cache mapping.\n\nANSWER: B. Direct mapped."
