"To determine which statement is TRUE, let's analyze the algorithm:\n\n1. **Understanding the Algorithm**:\n   - The algorithm initializes \\( L_{n-1} = 1 \\).\n   - It iterates backward from the second last element to the first element.\n   - For each element at index \\( i \\), it compares the current element \\( A[i] \\) with the next element \\( A[i+1] \\).\n   - If \\( A[i] < A[i+1] \\), it sets \\( L_i = 1 + L_{i+1} \\); otherwise, \\( L_i = 1 \\).\n   - The final result is obtained by taking the maximum among all \\( L_i \\).\n\n2. **Dynamic Programming**:\n   - The algorithm clearly uses previously computed values, \\( L_{i+1} \\), to compute \\( L_i \\).\n   - This is characteristic of dynamic programming, where overlapping subproblems are solved, and results are stored to avoid redundant calculations.\n\n3. **Complexity Analysis**:\n   - The algorithm processes each element of the array exactly once in a single loop, making the complexity \\( O(n) \\).\n\n4. **Algorithm Paradigm**:\n   - The algorithm fits the dynamic programming paradigm due to the use of previously calculated results to build up a solution.\n\nGiven this analysis, the correct statement is:\n\nANSWER: A. The algorithm uses dynamic programming paradigm."
"To perform an in-order traversal on a binary tree, follow these steps:\n\n1. Traverse the left subtree.\n2. Visit the root node.\n3. Traverse the right subtree.\n\nLet's apply these steps to the given tree:\n\n1. Start at the root, \\( P \\).\n2. Traverse the left subtree of \\( P \\), which is rooted at \\( Q \\):\n   - Traverse the left subtree of \\( Q \\), which is \\( S \\). Since \\( S \\) has no children, visit \\( S \\).\n   - Visit \\( Q \\).\n3. Visit \\( P \\).\n4. Traverse the right subtree of \\( P \\), which is rooted at \\( R \\):\n   - Traverse the left subtree of \\( R \\), which is \\( T \\). Since \\( T \\) has a left child \\( W \\):\n     - Visit \\( W \\) (as it has no children).\n     - Visit \\( T \\).\n   - Visit \\( R \\).\n   - Traverse the right subtree of \\( R \\), which is rooted at \\( U \\):\n     - Visit \\( U \\).\n     - Visit \\( V \\) (since it has no children).\n\nFollowing this order, the nodes are visited as: \\( S, Q, P, T, W, R, U, V \\).\n\nThe correct sequence is: **SQPTWRUV**.\n\nThus, the correct option is:\n\nANSWER: C."
