"To analyze uncontrolled concurrent execution, we must assess how the transactions \\(T_1\\) and \\(T_2\\) read and update the same data item \\(X\\).\n\n1. **\\(T_1\\) Execution:**\n   - Reads \\(X\\), subtracts \\(N\\), then writes \\(X - N\\).\n   - Reads \\(Y\\) and then writes \\(Y + N\\).\n\n2. **\\(T_2\\) Execution:**\n   - Reads \\(X\\), adds \\(M\\), then writes \\(X + M\\).\n\n**Possible Interleaving:**\n\n- \\(T_1\\) reads \\(X\\).\n- \\(T_2\\) reads \\(X\\).\n- \\(T_2\\) writes \\(X\\) (as \\(X + M\\)).\n- \\(T_1\\) writes \\(X\\) (as \\(X - N\\)).\n\nIn this sequence, the write of \\(X\\) by \\(T_1\\) overwrites the update made by \\(T_2\\), causing \\(T_2\\)'s addition to be lost.\n\n**Conclusion:**\n\n- **Data Loss:** Transaction \\(T_2\\) loses its data change due to the interleaving problem.\n\nANSWER: B"
