"To determine which value of \\( K \\) will have the least leave-one-out cross-validation accuracy, we need to examine the data distribution and how \\( K \\)-nearest neighbors (KNN) would classify the central minus (-) class when one point is left out at a time.\n\n1. **1NN:**  \n   - For leave-one-out cross-validation, each sample is classified based on its nearest neighbor.\n   - The minus (-) sample will be misclassified because its nearest neighbors are all plus (+).\n   - Misclassification count = 1.\n\n2. **3NN:**\n   - Here, the classification is done using the three nearest neighbors.\n   - The three nearest neighbors to the minus (-) are plus (+), so the minus (-) will be misclassified.\n   - Misclassification count = 1.\n\n3. **4NN:**\n   - In this scenario, the four nearest neighbors around the minus (-) are equidistant and split evenly (+ and -).\n   - A tie-breaking rule (often favoring the majority class or random) will likely classify it as plus (+), misclassifying the minus (-).\n   - Misclassification count = 1.\n\nSince all options have a misclassification count of 1, in leave-one-out cross-validation terms, these options will yield the same error. Therefore, all options have the same result.\n\nANSWER: D."
"To find the minimum spanning tree (MST) using Kruskal's algorithm, follow these steps:\n\n1. **Sort all edges in non-decreasing order of their weight.**  \n   The sorted edges are:  \n   (v2, v3) - 1  \n   (v3, v5) - 2  \n   (v3, v4) - 3  \n   (v2, v5) - 4  \n   (v5, v6) - 4  \n   (v3, v6) - 5  \n   (v1, v2) - 6  \n   (v4, v6) - 6  \n   (v1, v4) - 7\n\n2. **Pick the smallest edge. Check if it forms a cycle with the spanning tree formed so far. If a cycle is not formed, include it; otherwise, discard it.**\n\n   - Add (v2, v3) (weight 1).\n   - Add (v3, v5) (weight 2).\n   - Add (v3, v4) (weight 3).\n   - Add (v2, v5) (weight 4) (discarded, forms a cycle).\n   - Add (v5, v6) (weight 4).\n\n3. **Continue picking the smallest weight edge that doesn\u2019t cause a cycle.**\n\n   - Add (v3, v6) (weight 5) (discarded, forms a cycle).\n   - Add (v1, v2) (weight 6).\n\nNow, we have 5 edges, which is enough for a MST in a graph with 6 vertices (n-1 edges for n vertices).\n\nThus, the last edge added is (v1, v2).\n\nANSWER: C"
