"To solve this problem using Breadth First Search (BFS), we need to traverse the graph starting from a node and explore all its neighbors at the current depth prior to moving on to nodes at the next depth level. \n\nLet's assume the BFS starts from node N:\n\n1. Start from N, enqueue it: Queue: [N]\n2. Dequeue N, visit it. Explore its neighbors M, O, and Q. Enqueue them: Queue: [M, O, Q]\n3. Dequeue M, visit it. Explore its neighbor R. Enqueue it: Queue: [O, Q, R]\n4. Dequeue O, visit it. Node O's neighbors are already visited or in the queue: Queue: [Q, R]\n5. Dequeue Q, visit it. Explore its neighbor P. Enqueue it: Queue: [R, P]\n6. Dequeue R, visit it. Node R's neighbors are already visited: Queue: [P]\n7. Dequeue P, visit it. Node P's neighbors are already visited: Queue: []\n\nOrder of visitation: N, M, O, Q, R, P\n\nMatching this order with the options:\n\n- Option A: MNOPQR\n- Option B: NQMPOR\n- Option C: NQMPOR\n- Option D: NQMPOR\n\nSo, the correct order of visiting the nodes based on the described BFS traversal would be option A, which is MNOPQR.\n\nANSWER: A"
