"To identify the abnormality on a Bielschowsky stain, we first need to understand the characteristics of each option:\n\nA. Diffuse plaques: These are loosely arranged amyloid deposits that lack dense neuritic components. They appear as lighter, less dense areas on a stain.\n\nB. Neuritic plaques: These are amyloid deposits with associated dystrophic neurites, appearing as denser, complex structures with darker central areas on a stain.\n\nC. Prion plaques: These are deposits seen in prion diseases, and they typically have a more granular appearance, different from the plaques in Alzheimer's disease.\n\nD. Neuropil threads: These are not plaques but are tau-protein inclusions within the neuronal processes, appearing as thin, elongated threads.\n\nThe image shows a dense core with a complex structure, indicating it is likely a neuritic plaque, which is representative of Alzheimer's disease pathology. It does not have the diffuse appearance of diffuse plaques, the granular look of prion plaques, or the thin elongated appearance of neuropil threads.\n\nANSWER: B. neuritic plaques."
