"To determine the type of scalp hemorrhage depicted in the image, let's analyze the layers of the scalp where different types of hemorrhages can occur:\n\n1. **Pericranial hemorrhage**: This would be located beneath the periosteum of the skull. It\u2019s generally less extensive.\n\n2. **Epicranial hemorrhage**: This would be on-top of the skull but beneath the skin, possibly confused with subcutaneous.\n\n3. **Subcutaneous hemorrhage**: Found in the layer directly beneath the skin.\n\n4. **Sub-galeal hemorrhage**: Occurs beneath the galea aponeurotica, a tough layer of fibrous tissue which can be seen over the skull. This type tends to be quite extensive because it can spread across broad regions of the scalp.\n\n5. **Intramysial hemorrhage**: Occurs within the muscle tissue.\n\nIn the image, the hemorrhage appears to be extensive and located deep to the skin but outside of the skull, suggesting a location beneath the galea aponeurotica. It doesn\u2019t seem restricted to smaller, specific areas typical of subcutaneous bleeding.\n\nHence, the most likely type of hemorrhage depicted is sub-galeal. \n\nANSWER: D. Sub-galeal scalp hemorrhage."
