"To calculate the price elasticity of demand using the midpoint method, use the following formula:\n\n\\[ \\text{Price Elasticity of Demand} = \\frac{\\left( \\frac{Q_2 - Q_1}{(Q_2 + Q_1)/2} \\right)}{\\left( \\frac{P_2 - P_1}{(P_2 + P_1)/2} \\right)} \\]\n\nWhere:\n\n- \\( P_1 = 25 \\), \\( P_2 = 30 \\)\n- \\( Q_1 = 305 \\), \\( Q_2 = 285 \\)\n\n1. Calculate the percentage change in quantity demanded:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{Q_2 - Q_1}{(Q_2 + Q_1)/2} = \\frac{285 - 305}{(285 + 305)/2} = \\frac{-20}{295} = -0.0678\n   \\]\n\n2. Calculate the percentage change in price:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{P_2 - P_1}{(P_2 + P_1)/2} = \\frac{30 - 25}{(30 + 25)/2} = \\frac{5}{27.5} = 0.1818\n   \\]\n\n3. Calculate the price elasticity of demand:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Elasticity} = \\frac{-0.0678}{0.1818} = -0.37\n   \\]\n\nSince elasticity is typically expressed as a positive value when discussing magnitude:\n\nANSWER: B. 0.37"
