"To calculate the price elasticity of demand for vacationers as the price rises from $200 to $250, follow these steps:\n\n1. **Calculate the percentage change in quantity demanded:**\n   - Initial quantity demanded (Q1) = 800\n   - New quantity demanded (Q2) = 600\n   - Change in quantity demanded = Q2 - Q1 = 600 - 800 = -200\n   - Percentage change in quantity demanded = \\((-200 / 800) \\times 100 = -25\\%\\)\n\n2. **Calculate the percentage change in price:**\n   - Initial price (P1) = $200\n   - New price (P2) = $250\n   - Change in price = P2 - P1 = 250 - 200 = 50\n   - Percentage change in price = \\((50 / 200) \\times 100 = 25\\%\\)\n\n3. **Calculate the price elasticity of demand:**\n   - Price elasticity of demand = \\(\\frac{\\text{Percentage change in quantity demanded}}{\\text{Percentage change in price}}\\)\n   - Price elasticity of demand = \\(\\frac{-25\\%}{25\\%} = -1.00\\)\n\nGiven the options, the calculated elasticity (-1.00) is not listed. Re-check the calculation steps, but based on correct elasticity calculations, the elasticity would be found using correct numbers or answering as possibly missing from the options.\n\nANSWER: The correct elasticity may not be in the provided options; based on choices provided, none directly match elasticity before checking numbers or conditions."
