"To determine which city has a comparative advantage in producing red socks, we need to calculate the opportunity costs of producing red socks in both cities:\n\n- **Boston:** \n  - Opportunity cost of 1 pair of red socks = 1 pair of white socks (since they can produce 3 pairs of each per hour).\n\n- **Chicago:** \n  - Opportunity cost of 1 pair of red socks = 0.5 pairs of white socks (since they give up 1 pair of white socks to make 2 pairs of red socks).\n\nChicago has a lower opportunity cost for producing red socks (0.5 pairs of white socks) compared to Boston (1 pair of white socks).\n\nThus, Chicago has a comparative advantage in the production of red socks.\n\nANSWER: A."
"To determine which curve Curve 1 represents, consider the characteristics of the curves mentioned in the options:\n\n- **A. Short-run Phillips curve**: This typically shows a downward-sloping relationship between inflation and unemployment, indicating an inverse relationship in the short run.\n\n- **B. Long-run aggregate supply curve (LRAS)**: This is generally vertical, indicating that the total output is not affected by the price level in the long run.\n\n- **C. Short-run aggregate supply curve (SRAS)**: Usually upward sloping, indicating that as prices increase, quantity supplied also increases in the short run.\n\n- **D. Long-run Phillips curve**: This is vertical, reflecting the natural rate of unemployment and indicating no long-term trade-off between inflation and unemployment.\n\n**Analysis of Curve 1:**\n- Curve 1 is vertical at a specific unemployment rate, suggesting no relationship between inflation and unemployment at different inflation rates.\n\nGiven this analysis, Curve 1 is most consistent with:\n\n- **D. Long-run Phillips curve**, which is vertical and indicates the natural rate of unemployment.\n\nANSWER: D"
