"To calculate the price elasticity of demand (PED), we use the formula:\n\n\\[ \nPED = \\frac{\\%\\text{ Change in Quantity Demanded}}{\\%\\text{ Change in Price}} \n\\]\n\n### Step-by-Step Calculation\n1. **Determine the initial and new prices**:\n   - Initial Price (P1) = $8\n   - New Price (P2) = $10\n\n2. **Determine the initial and new quantities demanded**:\n   - Initial Quantity Demanded (Q1) = 40 DVDs\n   - New Quantity Demanded (Q2) = 32 DVDs\n\n3. **Calculate the percentage change in quantity demanded**:\n   \\[\n   \\%\\text{ Change in Quantity Demanded} = \\left(\\frac{Q2 - Q1}{Q1}\\right) \\times 100\\% = \\left(\\frac{32 - 40}{40}\\right) \\times 100\\% = \\left(\\frac{-8}{40}\\right) \\times 100\\% = -20\\%\n   \\]\n\n4. **Calculate the percentage change in price**:\n   \\[\n   \\%\\text{ Change in Price} = \\left(\\frac{P2 - P1}{P1}\\right) \\times 100\\% = \\left(\\frac{10 - 8}{8}\\right) \\times 100\\% = \\left(\\frac{2}{8}\\right) \\times 100\\% = 25\\%\n   \\]\n\n5. **Calculate the price elasticity of demand**:\n   \\[\n   PED = \\frac{-20\\%}{25\\%} = -0.8\n   \\]\n\nSince price elasticity of demand is usually expressed as a positive number for analysis purposes, we take the absolute value:\n\n\\[ \\text{Elasticity} = 0.8 \\]\n\nThis value is closest to the choice of 1, which is commonly considered an elastic demand scenario.\n\nANSWER: A"
