"To calculate the 95% confidence interval for \\(\\beta_1\\), we use the formula:\n\n\\[\n\\text{CI} = \\hat{\\beta}_1 \\pm t \\times \\text{SE}(\\hat{\\beta}_1)\n\\]\n\nGiven:\n- \\(\\hat{\\beta}_1 = -2.279808\\)\n- \\(\\text{SE}(\\hat{\\beta}_1) = 0.5194892\\)\n\nSince the sample size is large (\\(n = 420\\)), we can use the standard normal distribution for a 95% confidence interval, which has a critical value \\(t \\approx 1.96\\).\n\nSo, calculate the margin of error:\n\n\\[\n1.96 \\times 0.5194892 \\approx 1.0182\n\\]\n\nNow, apply this to find the confidence interval:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Lower bound} = -2.279808 - 1.0182 = -3.298\n\\]\n\\[\n\\text{Upper bound} = -2.279808 + 1.0182 = -1.2616\n\\]\n\nThus, the 95% confidence interval for \\(\\beta_1\\) is approximately \\([-3.30, -1.26]\\).\n\nANSWER: A."
"To determine who sells permits and how many, let's consider the initial pollution levels and costs of reduction for each firm:\n\n1. **Firm A**: Initial pollution = 70 units, Cost = $20/unit\n   - Permits allocated = 40\n   - Needs to reduce 30 units (70 - 40 = 30)\n   - It will cost 30 units * $20 = $600 to reduce\n\n2. **Firm B**: Initial pollution = 80 units, Cost = $25/unit\n   - Permits allocated = 40\n   - Needs to reduce 40 units (80 - 40 = 40)\n   - It will cost 40 units * $25 = $1000 to reduce\n\n3. **Firm C**: Initial pollution = 50 units, Cost = $10/unit\n   - Permits allocated = 40\n   - Needs to reduce 10 units (50 - 40 = 10)\n   - It will cost 10 units * $10 = $100 to reduce\n\nNext, check who may have spare permits to sell and the cost implications. Firm C can reduce pollution more cheaply, so it can potentially reduce more than required and sell permits.\n\n- If Firm C reduces an additional 40 units (total 50), it would incur costs of 50 * $10 = $500.\n- Firm C can sell 40 permits as their initial level was 50 and permits granted were 40.\n- For Firm C, 10 units reduction costs $100, leaving $400 for profit from selling 40 permits at a profitable price above $10 per unit.\n\nNow check the possible scenarios:\n- Firm A and Firm B both have higher unit reduction costs ($20 and $25 respectively) than Firm C ($10).\n\nTherefore, it makes sense for Firm C to sell permits. Both Firm A and Firm B would opt to buy permits from Firm C to avoid higher cost reductions themselves.\n\nThus, Firm C sells permits and would sell 40 units.\n\nANSWER: C."
