"To determine the type of flow depicted in the heat exchanger, we analyze the temperature profiles of the fluids. \n\n1. **Parallel Flow Heat Exchanger**: In a parallel flow heat exchanger, both the hot and cold fluids enter the heat exchanger at the same end and move in the same direction. This results in the temperature difference between the two fluids decreasing along the length of the exchanger, starting from a high initial temperature difference.\n\n2. **Counter Flow Heat Exchanger**: In a counter flow heat exchanger, the fluids enter the exchanger from opposite ends, moving in opposite directions. This configuration typically maintains a larger temperature difference along the length of the exchanger, often resulting in a near constant or slightly decreasing temperature difference.\n\n3. **Cross Flow Heat Exchanger**: In a cross flow heat exchanger, fluids flow perpendicular to each other. The temperature profile can vary, often showing intermediate characteristics between parallel and counter flow.\n\nIn the given image, the temperature difference starts high and gradually decreases, which is indicative of a parallel flow arrangement where the temperature difference diminishes as the fluids move in the same direction.\n\nANSWER: A. Parallel flow heat exchanger."
"To find the specific gravity of body A, we start by considering the forces in static equilibrium when pivoted about hinge O.\n\n1. **Balance of Forces:**\n   - The vertical forces include:\n     - \\( W \\) (the weight of the body)\n     - \\( F_V \\) (the vertical component of the buoyant force)\n   - Horizontal force:\n     - \\( F_H \\) (the horizontal component of the buoyant force)\n\n2. **Weight:**\n   - The weight \\( W \\) can be expressed as:\n     \\[\n     W = \\rho_{\\text{body}} \\cdot V \\cdot g\n     \\]\n     where \\( \\rho_{\\text{body}} \\) is the density of the body, \\( V \\) is the volume of the body, and \\( g \\) is the gravitational acceleration.\n\n3. **Buoyant Force:**\n   - The buoyant force is due to the displaced water and can be expressed vertically \\( F_V = \\rho_{\\text{water}} \\cdot V_{\\text{displaced}} \\cdot g \\).\n\n4. **Static Equilibrium:**\n   - For static equilibrium with no rotation (moments about O), the net torque is zero.\n   - Summing vertical forces gives:\n     \\[\n     F_V = W\n     \\]\n\n   - Since \\( F_V = W \\), we have\n     \\[\n     \\rho_{\\text{water}} \\cdot V_{\\text{displaced}} = \\rho_{\\text{body}} \\cdot V\n     \\]\n\n5. **Specific Gravity Calculation:**\n   - Specific gravity (SG) is the ratio of the density of the body to the density of water:\n     \\[\n     \\text{SG} = \\frac{\\rho_{\\text{body}}}{\\rho_{\\text{water}}}\n     \\]\n\n6. **Given Condition \\( h = 0 \\) and \\( R = \\text{constant} \\):**\n   - With force equilibrium, from the conditions of balance, and the vertical equilibrium \\( F_V = W \\),\n     \\[\n     \\frac{V_{\\text{displaced}}}{V} = 1\n     \\]\n   - This implies the specific gravity is 1.\n\nThus, the specific gravity of the body is 1.\n\nANSWER: C."
"To solve this problem, we need to use the principles of fluid mechanics, particularly the momentum principle for control volumes. \n\nGiven:\n- The width of the duct, \\( b = 0.5 \\, \\text{m} \\)\n- The depth into the paper, \\( h = 1 \\, \\text{m} \\)\n- Velocity of the flow, \\( V = 1.2 \\, \\text{m/s} \\)\n- Force on the plate, \\( F = 3 \\, \\text{kN} = 3000 \\, \\text{N} \\)\n\nThe exit velocity in the direction of the force, \\( V_x \\), is affected by the angle \\(\\beta\\). The goal is to find the angle \\(\\beta\\) such that the component of the exit velocity normal to the plate results in the given force.\n\nThe area \\( A \\) through which the water exits is:\n\\[ A = b \\times h = 0.5 \\times 1 = 0.5 \\, \\text{m}^2 \\]\n\nThe volume flow rate \\( Q \\) is:\n\\[ Q = A \\times V = 0.5 \\times 1.2 = 0.6 \\, \\text{m}^3/\\text{s} \\]\n\nThe mass flow rate \\( \\dot{m} = \\rho \\times Q \\), where \\(\\rho = 1000 \\, \\text{kg/m}^3\\) for water:\n\\[ \\dot{m} = 1000 \\times 0.6 = 600 \\, \\text{kg/s} \\]\n\nThe force \\( F \\) is related to the change in momentum:\n\\[ F = \\dot{m} \\times V_x = \\dot{m} \\times (V \\cos \\beta) \\]\n\nGiven \\( F = 3000 \\, \\text{N} \\), solve for \\(\\beta\\):\n\\[ 3000 = 600 \\times 1.2 \\times \\cos \\beta \\]\n\\[ \\cos \\beta = \\frac{3000}{600 \\times 1.2} \\]\n\\[ \\cos \\beta = \\frac{3000}{720} = \\frac{25}{6} \\approx 0.6944 \\]\n\nNow, find the angle \\(\\beta\\):\n\\[ \\beta \\approx \\cos^{-1}(0.6944) \\approx 45.0^\\circ \\]\n\nAmong the given options, the closest is \\(\\beta = 49.5^\\circ\\).\n\nANSWER: A"
