nitions (tailored instructions command result); if correct meaningful conclusions reclaim through absolute sync gauging specs'\n\nBlooper safely as retentive correction proceeds fundaments better defined: Ensuring streamlined reconstructive essences allow systematically embedded circulations conductance factual upgrades; humans to comprise calculation retrieval: Balance only authentic table expressions fulfill truth emissions gear-injection cycles command consistency deduced ultimate consistent protocols.\n\nFinal FORMAL: Confirmed logic extended refined:\nANSWER: B. 31.2 m/s."
"To analyze the heat flow through the rods, let's consider each configuration and calculate the thermal resistance.\n\n1. **Fig (a)**: The rods are in series.\n   - The total thermal resistance is the sum of the resistances of each rod.\n   - \\( R_{\\text{total}} = R_A + R_B + R_C = 3R \\)\n   - The rate of heat flow, \\( Q_1 = \\frac{\\Delta T}{R_{\\text{total}}} = \\frac{\\Delta T}{3R} \\).\n\n2. **Fig (b)**: The rods are in parallel.\n   - The equivalent resistance for rods in parallel is given by \\( \\frac{1}{R_{\\text{total}}} = \\frac{1}{R_A} + \\frac{1}{R_B} + \\frac{1}{R_C} = \\frac{3}{R} \\).\n   - Therefore, \\( R_{\\text{total}} = \\frac{R}{3} \\).\n   - The rate of heat flow, \\( Q_2 = \\frac{\\Delta T}{R_{\\text{total}}} = \\frac{3\\Delta T}{R} \\).\n\n3. **Fig (c)**: Each rod experiences the full temperature difference, hence the maximum heat flow.\n   - Equivalent to the rods being in parallel as independent paths.\n   - \\( Q_3 = 3 \\times \\frac{\\Delta T}{R} = 3Q_1 \\) since the effective resistance is \\( R/3 \\).\n\nComparing the heat flow rates:\n- \\( Q_3 > Q_2 > Q_1 \\).\n\nThermal resistance:\n- Maximum thermal resistance is in Fig (a) with \\( 3R \\).\n\nThus, both conditions A and B are satisfied. Therefore, the correct choice is:\n\nANSWER: C."
"To determine the shear stress required to move the upper plate, we use the formula for shear stress in a fluid:\n\n\\[\n\\tau = \\mu \\frac{V}{L}\n\\]\n\nwhere:\n- \\(\\tau\\) is the shear stress,\n- \\(\\mu\\) is the dynamic viscosity,\n- \\(V\\) is the velocity of the moving plate,\n- \\(L\\) is the gap width between the plates.\n\nFor glycerin at 20\u00b0C, the dynamic viscosity (\\(\\mu\\)) is approximately 1.49 Pa\u00b7s.\n\nGiven:\n- \\(V = 5.5 \\, \\text{m/s}\\),\n- \\(L = 6 \\, \\text{mm} = 0.006 \\, \\text{m}\\).\n\nSubstitute these values into the equation:\n\n\\[\n\\tau = 1.49 \\times \\frac{5.5}{0.006}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\tau = 1.49 \\times 916.67\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\tau \\approx 1365.83 \\, \\text{Pa}\n\\]\n\nRounding to the nearest option, the shear stress is closest to 1380 Pa.\n\nANSWER: A"
