"To calculate the work input during the process, we can use the polytropic process relation for work done, since \\( PV^{1.4} = \\) constant. The work done, \\( W \\), during a polytropic process is given by:\n\n\\[\nW = \\frac{P_2 V_2 - P_1 V_1}{1-n}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\( n = 1.4 \\) is the polytropic index.\n\nFirst, let's use the ideal gas law to find \\( V_1 \\) and \\( V_2 \\):\n\n\\[\nP_1 V_1 = m R T_1\n\\]\n\\[\nP_2 V_2 = m R T_2\n\\]\n\nwhere:\n- \\( m = 5 \\) kg is the mass of nitrogen,\n- \\( R = 0.2968 \\) kJ/kg\u00b7K for nitrogen,\n- \\( T_1 = 250 \\) K,\n- \\( T_2 = 360 \\) K.\n\nSubstitute to find \\( V_1 \\) and solve for \\( V_2 \\) using:\n\\[\n\\frac{P_1 V_1}{T_1} = \\frac{P_2 V_2}{T_2}\n\\]\n\nNow find \\( V_1 \\):\n\\[\nV_1 = \\frac{m R T_1}{P_1} = \\frac{5 \\times 0.2968 \\times 250}{100} = 3.71 \\, \\text{m}^3\n\\]\n\nExpress \\( V_2 \\) in terms of \\( P_2 \\):\n\\[\nV_2 = \\frac{P_1 V_1 T_2}{P_2 T_1}\n\\]\n\nNext, use the relation \\( P_1 V_1^{1.4} = P_2 V_2^{1.4} \\) to find \\( P_2 \\):\n\\[\nP_2 = P_1 \\left(\\frac{V_1}{V_2}\\right)^{1.4}\n\\]\n\nSubstitute into the work formula:\n\\[\nW = \\frac{P_2 V_2 - P_1 V_1}{1-1.4}\n\\]\n\nAfter calculations:\n\n1. Find \\( P_2/P_1 \\) and \\( V_2/V_1 \\).\n2. Substitute into the work equation.\n\nFinally, this results in:\n\nANSWER: B. 408 kJ."
"To solve this problem, we apply the principle of conservation of momentum in a control volume around the pipe bend. We consider steady, incompressible flow alongside the Bernoulli principle and the momentum equation.\n\n**Given:**\n\n- \\( p_1 = 350 \\, \\text{kPa} \\)\n- \\( D_1 = 25 \\, \\text{cm} = 0.25 \\, \\text{m} \\)\n- \\( V_1 = 2.2 \\, \\text{m/s} \\)\n- \\( p_2 = 120 \\, \\text{kPa} \\)\n- \\( D_2 = 8 \\, \\text{cm} = 0.08 \\, \\text{m} \\)\n- \\( p_a = 100 \\, \\text{kPa} \\)\n\n**Steps:**\n\n1. **Calculate flow areas:**\n   \\[\n   A_1 = \\frac{\\pi D_1^2}{4} = \\frac{\\pi (0.25)^2}{4} = 0.049 \\, \\text{m}^2\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   A_2 = \\frac{\\pi D_2^2}{4} = \\frac{\\pi (0.08)^2}{4} = 0.005 \\, \\text{m}^2\n   \\]\n\n2. **Apply the continuity equation for incompressible flow to find \\( V_2 \\):**\n   \\[\n   Q = A_1 V_1 = A_2 V_2\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   V_2 = \\frac{A_1 V_1}{A_2} = \\frac{0.049 \\times 2.2}{0.005} = 21.56 \\, \\text{m/s}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Apply the conservation of momentum principle:**\n\n   - The net force exerted by the fluid is equal to the change in momentum of the fluid.\n   - The horizontal component of the force:\n   \\[\n   \\sum F_x = \\rho Q(V_2 - V_1) + (p_1 - p_2)A\n   \\]\n\n   Where \\( \\rho = 998 \\, \\text{kg/m}^3 \\) (density of water at \\( 20^\\circ \\text{C} \\)).\n\n4. **Calculate forces:**\n\n   - Calculate the pressure force difference:\n   \\[\n   (p_1 - p_a) A_1 - (p_2 - p_a) A_2 = (350 - 100) \\times 10^3 \\times 0.049 - (120 - 100) \\times 10^3 \\times 0.005\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   = 12250 \\, \\text{N}\n   \\]\n\n   - Calculate the momentum change force (convert area cubic flow):\n   \\[\n   \\rho Q = \\rho A_1 V_1 = 998 \\times 0.049 \\times 2.2 = 107.78 \\, \\text{kg/s}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\Delta V = V_2 - V_1 = 21.56 - 2.2 = 19.36 \\, \\text{m/s}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\rho Q \\Delta V = 107.78 \\times 19.36 = 2085.28 \\, \\text{N}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Total force:**\n\n   The total force that must be resisted by the flange bolts:\n   \\[\n   F_{\\text{total}} = 12250 + 2085.28 = 14335.28 \\, \\text{N}\n   \\]\n\nThus, considering rounding and friction losses not significant in this simplified model often used in fluid dynamics problems, the nearest option is:\n\nANSWER: B. 14900 N."
