"To find the required return using the Capital Asset Pricing Model (CAPM), use the formula:\n\n\\[ \\text{Required Return} (r) = R_F + \\beta (r_m - R_F) \\]\n\nWhere:\n- \\( R_F \\) is the risk-free rate.\n- \\( r_m \\) is the market return.\n- \\( \\beta \\) is the beta of the asset.\n\nLet's calculate for each state:\n\n**State A:**\n- \\( R_F = 6\\% \\)\n- \\( r_m = 22\\% \\)\n- \\( \\beta = 2.40 \\)\n\n\\[ r = 6\\% + 2.40 (22\\% - 6\\%) \\]\n\\[ r = 6\\% + 2.40 \\times 16\\% \\]\n\\[ r = 6\\% + 38.4\\% \\]\n\\[ r = 44.4\\% \\]\n\n**State B:**\n- \\( R_F = 3\\% \\)\n- \\( r_m = 8\\% \\)\n- \\( \\beta = -0.50 \\)\n\n\\[ r = 3\\% + (-0.50) \\times (8\\% - 3\\%) \\]\n\\[ r = 3\\% - 0.50 \\times 5\\% \\]\n\\[ r = 3\\% - 2.5\\% \\]\n\\[ r = 0.5\\% \\]\n\n**State C:**\n- \\( R_F = 10\\% \\)\n- \\( r_m = 15\\% \\)\n- \\( \\beta = 0.9 \\)\n\n\\[ r = 10\\% + 0.9 \\times (15\\% - 10\\%) \\]\n\\[ r = 10\\% + 0.9 \\times 5\\% \\]\n\\[ r = 10\\% + 4.5\\% \\]\n\\[ r = 14.5\\% \\]\n\n**State D:**\n- \\( R_F = 12\\% \\)\n- \\( r_m = 18\\% \\)\n- \\( \\beta = 1.00 \\)\n\n\\[ r = 12\\% + 1.00 \\times (18\\% - 12\\%) \\]\n\\[ r = 12\\% + 6\\% \\]\n\\[ r = 18\\% \\]\n\n**State E:**\n- \\( R_F = 5\\% \\)\n- \\( r_m = 10\\% \\)\n- \\( \\beta = 0.70 \\)\n\n\\[ r = 5\\% + 0.70 \\times (10\\% - 5\\%) \\]\n\\[ r = 5\\% + 0.70 \\times 5\\% \\]\n\\[ r = 5\\% + 3.5\\% \\]\n\\[ r = 8.5\\% \\]\n\nBased on these calculations, the required returns match option D.\n\nANSWER: D"
"To calculate the standard deviation of a portfolio with perfect negative correlation between the two investments, we use the following formula:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Portfolio Standard Deviation} = |w_1 \\sigma_1 - w_2 \\sigma_2|\n\\]\n\nWhere:\n- \\(w_1\\) and \\(w_2\\) are the weights of the investments in the portfolio.\n- \\(\\sigma_1\\) and \\(\\sigma_2\\) are the standard deviations of the investments.\n\nGiven:\n- \\(w_1 = 0.5\\), \\(\\sigma_1 = 28\\) (for Stock Q)\n- \\(w_2 = 0.5\\), \\(\\sigma_2 = 26\\) (for Stock R)\n\nPlug in the values into the formula:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Portfolio Standard Deviation} = |0.5 \\times 28 - 0.5 \\times 26|\n\\]\n\n\\[\n= |14 - 13|\n\\]\n\n\\[\n= |1|\n\\]\n\n\\[\n= 1\\%\n\\]\n\nANSWER: A."
"To determine the location of the maximum shear stress in the I-shaped section of the beam, we need to consider the distribution of shear stress across the section. In a typical I-shaped beam:\n\n1. **Shear Stress Distribution**: \n   - Shear stress is zero at the extreme fibers (i.e., top and bottom flanges) and maximum at the neutral axis. \n   - In an I-beam, the web carries most of the shear force, so the maximum shear stress generally occurs in the web.\n\n2. **Neutral Axis**: \n   - The neutral axis in this section is along the centerline of the web.\n   - The maximum shear stress in an I-section is usually located along the neutral axis through the web.\n\n3. **Options Analysis**:\n   - **A.** At the neutral axis of the web \u2014 This is the typical location for maximum shear stress in an I-beam, as the shear distribution is parabolic with the peak at the neutral axis.\n   - **B.** Intersection between the extension line of the upper and lower edges of the web plate and the flanges on both sides \u2014 Shear stress isn\u2019t maximal here.\n   - **C.** Upper and lower edges of the section \u2014 Shear stress is zero at these points.\n   - **D.** Upper and lower edges of web plate \u2014 Shear stress is not maximum here.\n\nConsidering these points, the maximum shear stress in an I-beam section is typically at the neutral axis of the web.\n\nANSWER: A."
