"To solve this problem, we need to determine how many interference fringes are shifted due to the introduction of the glass piece.\n\n1. **Path Difference with Glass:**\n   When the glass piece is introduced, the light traveling through it experiences a phase change due to the increased optical path length. The new optical path difference (\\(\\Delta\\)) introduced is given by:\n\n   \\[\n   \\Delta = (n - 1) \\cdot e\n   \\]\n\n   where \\(n = 1.58\\) is the refractive index of the glass, and \\(e = 6.6 \\times 10^{-6}\\) m is the thickness.\n\n   Substituting the given values:\n\n   \\[\n   \\Delta = (1.58 - 1) \\cdot 6.6 \\times 10^{-6} = 0.58 \\times 6.6 \\times 10^{-6} = 3.828 \\times 10^{-6} \\text{ m}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Determine the Wavelength Number of Fringes Shifted:**\n   To find out how many wavelengths this path difference corresponds to, we divide by the wavelength of light (\\(\\lambda\\)) used in the experiment:\n\n   \\[\n   k = \\frac{\\Delta}{\\lambda}\n   \\]\n\n   where \\(\\lambda = 550 \\times 10^{-9}\\) m.\n\n   Substituting the values:\n\n   \\[\n   k = \\frac{3.828 \\times 10^{-6}}{550 \\times 10^{-9}} = 6.96\n   \\]\n\n   Since \\(k\\) must be an integer (as it represents the shift of bright fringes), we round 6.96 to the nearest whole number, which is 7.\n\nThus, the k-level for the shift caused by the glass is **7**.\n\nANSWER: B."
"To determine the permeability coefficient (\\(k\\)) using a constant head permeability test, we apply the formula:\n\n\\[\nk = \\frac{Q \\times L}{A \\times h \\times t}\n\\]\n\nwhere:\n- \\(Q\\) is the volume of water collected (400 cm\u00b3),\n- \\(L\\) is the length of the sample (15 cm),\n- \\(A\\) is the cross-sectional area of the sample,\n- \\(h\\) is the height of the water head (40 cm),\n- \\(t\\) is the time taken (6.0 s).\n\nFirst, calculate the cross-sectional area (\\(A\\)) of the sample:\n\n\\[\nA = \\pi \\left( \\frac{d}{2} \\right)^2 = \\pi \\left( \\frac{5.5}{2} \\right)^2 = \\pi \\times 2.75^2 \\approx 23.76 \\text{ cm}^2\n\\]\n\nSubstituting values into the permeability coefficient formula:\n\n\\[\nk = \\frac{400 \\, \\text{cm}^3 \\times 15 \\, \\text{cm}}{23.76 \\, \\text{cm}^2 \\times 40 \\, \\text{cm} \\times 6.0 \\, \\text{s}}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nk = \\frac{6000}{5702.4} \\approx 1.05 \\, \\text{cm/s}\n\\]\n\nANSWER: C"
"To solve this problem, we can consider the relationships and kinematics of the mechanism.\n\n1. **Given data:**\n   - \\( \\omega = 3 \\, \\text{rad/s} \\)\n   - \\( O_1A = O_2B = 20 \\, \\text{cm} \\)\n   - \\( O_2C = O_3D = 40 \\, \\text{cm} \\)\n\n2. **Velocity of Point B:**\n   - Since \\( O_1A = O_2B \\) and \\( O_1A \\) rotates, point B will have the same linear velocity as point A.\n   - \\( V_A = \\omega \\times O_1A = 3 \\, \\text{rad/s} \\times 20 \\, \\text{cm} = 60 \\, \\text{cm/s} \\)\n\n3. **Velocity of Point M:**\n   - Because O2C and O3D are equal and connect directly to the translating rod O2B, the entire horizontal bar CD moves horizontally with point B.\n   - Therefore, \\( V_M = V_B = 60 \\, \\text{cm/s} \\) (same as \\( V_A \\)).\n\n4. **Acceleration of Point M:**\n   - Since A moves in a circle, its acceleration is centripetal.\n   - \\( a_A = \\omega^2 \\times O_1A = (3 \\, \\text{rad/s})^2 \\times 20 \\, \\text{cm} = 180 \\, \\text{cm/s}^2 \\)\n   - The acceleration of M, translating horizontally without changing direction, matches the acceleration of point B, which equals \\( a_A \\).\n\n5. **Conclusion:**\n   - The velocity of point M is \\( 60 \\, \\text{cm/s} \\).\n   - The acceleration of point M is \\( 180 \\, \\text{cm/s}^2 \\).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is:\n\nANSWER: A."
"To calculate the total water pressure \\( P_w \\) acting on the supporting wall above the pit bottom, we need to consider the hydrostatic pressure due to groundwater.\n\nGiven:\n- Depth of foundation pit, \\( H = 8.0 \\, \\text{m} \\)\n- Natural weight of the medium sand, \\( \\gamma = 18.0 \\, \\text{kN/m}^3 \\)\n- Fully saturated weight of sand, \\( \\gamma_{\\text{Sat}} = 20.0 \\, \\text{kN/m}^3 \\)\n- Distance from groundwater level to ground, 0 m (i.e., groundwater is at ground level)\n\nThe pressure due to water, or hydrostatic pressure, at a depth \\( h \\) is given by:\n\n\\[ P = \\gamma_w \\times h \\]\n\nwhere \\( \\gamma_w = 9.81 \\, \\text{kN/m}^3 \\) (unit weight of water).\n\nSince the groundwater is at the top of the pit, the entire 8.0 m depth of the pit is subjected to hydrostatic pressure.\n\n\\[ P_w = \\gamma_w \\times H = 9.81 \\, \\text{kN/m}^3 \\times 8.0 \\, \\text{m} = 78.48 \\, \\text{kN/m}^2 \\]\n\nThe total pressure will then be calculated over the total area, which typically involves the consideration of full width. However, in the context of standardized options or a simplified representation, we believe the given options have been rounded conveniently, choosing the nearest:\n\n\\[ P_w \\approx 80 \\, \\text{kN/m}^2 \\]\n\nHence, the closest and most appropriate choice is:\n\nANSWER: B."
"To determine the closest vertical pressure option, we need to calculate the maximum allowable load (P) on the foundation using the formula for the eccentric load:\n\n\\[ P = q \\times (B - 2e) \\]\n\nwhere:\n- \\( q \\) = corrected characteristic value of the bearing capacity = 200 kPa\n- \\( B \\) = width of the foundation = 3 m\n- \\( e \\) = eccentricity = 0.6 m\n\nSubstituting the values, we have:\n\n\\[ P = 200 \\, \\text{kPa} \\times (3 \\, \\text{m} - 2 \\times 0.6 \\, \\text{m}) \\]\n\\[ P = 200 \\, \\text{kPa} \\times (3 \\, \\text{m} - 1.2 \\, \\text{m}) \\]\n\\[ P = 200 \\, \\text{kPa} \\times 1.8 \\, \\text{m} \\]\n\\[ P = 360 \\, \\text{kN/m} \\]\n\nThe closest option to 360 kN/m is 324 kN/m.\n\nANSWER: C. 324kN/m."
"To determine the standard value of wood strength, it's important to adjust the moisture content. Here's the reasoning:\n\nA. **Equilibrium moisture content:** This varies based on the environment and wouldn't provide a consistent standard for measurement.\n\nB. **Fiber saturation point:** This is the point where the cell walls are saturated but no free water exists. It is useful for understanding wood properties but not standard for strength measures.\n\nC. **Absolute dry state:** Not realistic for real-world applications as wood isn't used in an absolute dry state.\n\nD. **15%:** This is a widely accepted standard for moisture content in wood when measuring strength, as it represents typical indoor conditions.\n\nANSWER: D."
