"To solve this problem, we need to understand the formation of bright and dark fringes in a single slit Fraunhofer diffraction pattern.\n\nIn single slit diffraction, the condition for a dark fringe (minima) is given by:\n\n\\[ a \\sin \\theta = m\\lambda \\]\n\nwhere \\( a \\) is the slit width, \\( \\theta \\) is the angle of diffraction, \\( m \\) is the order of the minima (m is a non-zero integer: \u00b11, \u00b12, \u00b13, ...), and \\( \\lambda \\) is the wavelength of the light.\n\nFor bright fringes in the single slit diffraction pattern, the condition is slightly different and tends to occur between the minima. The central maximum is the brightest, and subsequent maxima become weaker.\n\nTo find the number of half wave bands that correspond to a bright pattern order, we observe the following:\n\n1. A full wave corresponds to one full cycle, leading to complete constructive interference.\n2. As seen from the first minimum condition, more half wave bands imply more alternation between constructive and destructive interference leading to a bright fringe.\n\nFor the third order bright fringe, it can be found halfway between the second (m=2) and third (m=3) minima of the single slit diffraction. It is an \"approximation\" since the actual calculations might not exactly result in a half number.\n\nEach bright fringe corresponds to an overlap of half-wave bands that must be an even number, where the central maximum overlaps fully constructive. To calculate this accurately for the third bright fringe on either side of the central maximum, you use an overlap derived from the minima conditions:\n\n\\[ m = 3 \\rightarrow 2m \\]\n\nThus, plug 3 into this condition as it relates to the number of bands giving third bright pattern:\n\n\\[ 2m + 1 = 7 \\]\n\nTherefore, for this third maxima,\n\nThe number of half wave bands is 7.\n\nANSWER: C"
"To solve this problem, we need to consider the properties and equations related to ideal gases. According to the ideal gas law, which is \\( PV = nRT \\):\n\n- \\( P \\) is pressure,\n- \\( V \\) is volume,\n- \\( n \\) is the number of moles,\n- \\( R \\) is the universal gas constant,\n- \\( T \\) is temperature.\n\nGiven that two ideal gases have the same pressure, temperature, and volume, the equation simplifies to \\( nRT = n' RT \\) where \\( n \\) and \\( n' \\) are the moles of different gases. Since \\( P, V, \\) and \\( T \\) are the same for both gases, the moles of gas (\\( n \\)) per unit volume are the same. However, because the gases have different molar masses, their number of molecules per unit volume can be different due to the relation \\( n = \\frac{m}{M} \\), where \\( M \\) is molar mass.\n\nNow consider the options:\n\nA. Different number of molecules per unit volume.\n- This is true due to the different molar masses. The same number of moles will have different numbers of molecules.\n\nB. The mass of gas per unit volume is the same.\n- Not true, as mass depends on molar mass, which is different for each gas.\n\nC. The total average translational kinetic energy of gas molecules per unit volume is the same.\n- True for individual molecules at the same temperature (using \\( \\frac{3}{2}kT \\)), but not for the number of molecules since they differ.\n\nD. The internal energy of gases per unit volume is the same.\n- Internal energy depends on the number of molecules and their kinetic energy, which will be different due to different numbers of molecules.\n\nANSWER: A"
