"To determine the relationship between the bending moment design values for the three foundations, we should consider how the distribution of reaction forces affects the bending moments.\n\n1. **Saddle Shaped Distribution**: In this case, the reaction forces are higher at the edges and lower in the middle. This can create larger bending moments because the forces are further from the center of the foundation, leading to larger lever arms and thus larger moments.\n\n2. **Uniform Distribution**: The forces are evenly distributed, which results in an even and predictable bending moment along the length of the foundation. The moment is generally less than that of a saddle distribution since the forces are concentrated towards the center.\n\n3. **Inverted Bell Shaped Distribution**: Here, the forces are concentrated more in the middle, which typically results in smaller bending moments compared to the saddle and uniform distributions. This is because the forces near the edges, which contribute to larger moments, are smaller.\n\nBased on these observations, the expected order for the bending moment design values would be:\n1. Saddle Shaped Distribution (highest moment)\n2. Uniform Distribution\n3. Inverted Bell Shaped Distribution (lowest moment)\n\nThus, the relationship is 1 > 2 > 3.\n\nANSWER: B."
"To calculate the water level drop \\( S \\) of the foundation pit, we will use the Thiem equation for confined aquifers:\n\n\\[ S = \\frac{Q}{2 \\pi k H} \\cdot \\ln \\left( \\frac{R_0}{r_w} \\right) \\]\n\nwhere:\n- \\( Q = 500 \\, \\text{m}^3/\\text{d} \\) (average water yield of a single well)\n- \\( k = 4 \\, \\text{m/d} \\) (permeability coefficient)\n- \\( H = 20 \\, \\text{m} \\) (thickness of the aquifer)\n- \\( R_0 = 130 \\, \\text{m} \\) (reference influence radius of the well group)\n- \\( r_w = 30 \\, \\text{m} \\) (approximate well spacing, based on the geometry)\n\nLet's calculate:\n\n\\[ \\frac{Q}{2 \\pi k H} = \\frac{500}{2 \\pi \\times 4 \\times 20} \\approx \\frac{500}{502.65} \\approx 0.995 \\]\n\nNow, calculate the natural log part:\n\n\\[ \\ln \\left( \\frac{130}{30} \\right) = \\ln \\left( \\frac{13}{3} \\right) \\approx \\ln(4.333) \\approx 1.464 \\]\n\nNow calculate \\( S \\):\n\n\\[ S = 0.995 \\times 1.464 \\approx 1.456 \\]\n\nTherefore, the closest calculation to one of the options given is approximately 1.5.\n\nANSWER: A. 1.5"
"To determine the vertical consolidation coefficient (\\(C_v\\)) of the clay layer, we need to consider the following parameters and formula:\n\n1. The coefficient of permeability (\\(k\\)) is given as 1.6 cm/year.\n2. The clay layer is 10 meters thick.\n3. \\(\\gamma_w\\) (unit weight of water) is assumed to be approximately 9.81 kN/m\u00b3, but we need to convert it to consistent units with other parameters if needed.\n\nThe formula used for calculating the vertical consolidation coefficient is:\n\\[ \nC_v = \\frac{k \\cdot \\gamma_w}{E_s \\cdot (1 - \\nu^2)}\n\\]\n\nWhile \\(\\nu\\) (Poisson's ratio) and \\(E_s\\) (modulus of elasticity) are not directly given in the problem, a typical value for normally consolidated clays can be assumed to effectively solve the problem.\n\nHowever, given only the thickness of the clay layer, the permeability coefficient, and without additional geotechnical parameters explicitly provided, we are typically asked to find a close match based on common practice or assumptions. \n\nTo simplify:\nFor this setup, we look at the value of \\(k\\) given and typical \\(C_v\\) ranges for saturated clay. Assuming the problem relies on typical domain knowledge, the calculated or expected value can often match up with values based on empirical data or standard ranges.\n\nBased on frequent values in typical scenarios:\n- Common \\(C_v\\) values for clays (empirical knowledge) can range from about 1 cm\u00b2/year to around 25 cm\u00b2/year depending on various geotechnical conditions.\n\nSince the value of permeability is low, it implies less consolidation and a lower \\(C_v\\).\n\nMatching the choices given:\n- The closest value that suits a low permeability and possible clay consolidation situation is 9.3 cm\u00b2/year.\n\nANSWER: B"
