"To solve this problem, we are dealing with a buffer solution, comprised of acetic acid (HAc) and sodium acetate (NaAc). \n\n### Initial Understanding:\n1. **Buffer Solution**: Consists of a weak acid (HAc) and its conjugate base (Ac\u207b from NaAc).\n2. **Henderson-Hasselbalch Equation** for buffer pH: \n   \\[\n   \\text{pH} = \\text{pKa} + \\log \\left( \\frac{[\\text{Ac}^-]}{[\\text{HAc}]} \\right)\n   \\]\n   - Since initial concentrations of HAc and NaAc are equal (0.1 mol/L), the initial pH comes from \\(\\text{pH} = \\text{pKa} \\).\n\n### Dilution:\n- **Diluting to 2%** means reducing the concentration of both [HAc] and [Ac\u207b] to 2% of their original values. \n- **Effect on [H\u207a]**: For a buffer, the [H\u207a] concentration is determined by the ratio of \\([\\text{Ac}^-]/[\\text{HAc}]\\), which remains unchanged with dilution.\n- **Effect on pH**: pH remains constant in an ideal buffer system because both components are diluted equally, keeping the ratio constant.\n\n### Conclusion:\n- **H\u207a concentration is invariant**, and the **pH does not change** upon dilution in an ideal buffer.\n\nThus, the correct choice is:\n\nANSWER: D. Invariant and unchanging."
