"To determine the hydraulic radius \\( R \\), we need to understand the definition:\n\nThe hydraulic radius \\( R \\) is defined as the ratio of the cross-sectional area of flow \\( A \\) to the wetted perimeter \\( P \\). Thus:\n\n\\[ R = \\frac{A}{P} \\]\n\nNow, let's evaluate the given options:\n\nA. The radius of the channel cross-section.\n   - This is not the definition of hydraulic radius. It specifically refers to actual circular radius, not a calculated ratio.\n\nB. Ratio of cross-sectional area to wet circumference.\n   - This matches the definition of hydraulic radius. Wetted perimeter can be interchanged with wet circumference in this context.\n\nC. Ratio of cross-sectional area to cross-sectional circumference.\n   - The term \"cross-sectional circumference\" typically refers to the perimeter of the entire cross-section, not just the wetted part.\n\nD. Ratio of cross-sectional area to water depth.\n   - This is not the definition of hydraulic radius.\n\nTherefore, the correct choice according to the standard definition of hydraulic radius is option B.\n\nANSWER: B"
"To solve for the plastic limit \\( w_P \\) of the soil, we first need to calculate the moisture content \\( w \\), using the natural and dry unit weights.\n\nThe formula for moisture content \\( w \\) is given by:\n\n\\[\nw = \\frac{y - y_d}{y_d} \\times 100\n\\]\n\nSubstitute the given values:\n\n\\[\nw = \\frac{17 - 14.5}{14.5} \\times 100 = \\frac{2.5}{14.5} \\times 100 \\approx 17.24\\%\n\\]\n\nGiven that the liquid index \\( I_L = 0 \\), we use the relation:\n\n\\[\nI_L = \\frac{w - w_P}{w_L - w_P}\n\\]\n\nSince \\( I_L = 0 \\), this implies:\n\n\\[\nw = w_P\n\\]\n\nTherefore, \\( w_P = 17.24\\% \\).\n\nComparing this with the given options, the closest value is:\n\nB. 17.2%\n\nANSWER: B"
"In debris flow investigation, the density of debris flow fluid is typically defined as the total mass of the debris flow mixture (solids and fluid) divided by the total volume of that mixture. It's essentially the mass per unit volume of the entire flow.\n\nLet's evaluate the options:\n\nA. This option defines density as the mass of debris flow fluid to the volume of the solid part, which does not represent the density of the entire flow.\n\nB. This option describes density as the debris flow fluid mass divided by debris flow volume, which correctly encompasses the entire flow (solids and fluids). \n\nC. This option mentions the solid mass to the total volume, which relates to the solid concentration but not the full density.\n\nD. This option considers the mass of debris flow solids to the volume of solids, akin to solid density, not total flow density.\n\nTherefore, the appropriate definition of the density of debris flow fluid, considering the whole mixture, is:\n\nANSWER: B."
