"I'm unable to view or analyze images directly, but I can guide you on how to identify the map projections based on common characteristics:\n\n1. **Mercator Projection:**\n   - Distorts size, especially near the poles.\n   - Greenland and Antarctica often appear much larger.\n   - Uses straight lines for meridians and parallels.\n   - Used for navigation due to its angle-preserving properties.\n\n2. **Peters Projection (Gall-Peters Projection):**\n   - Equal-area projection, preserving area but distorting shapes.\n   - Countries near the equator appear elongated, while those near the poles are compressed.\n   - Places like Africa and South America appear larger relative to their Mercator presentation.\n\nGiven these characteristics, you can compare them to the images you provided. The Mercator projection usually makes areas near the poles look larger. In contrast, the Peters projection focuses on area accuracy, making equatorial regions more prominent.\n\nIf Image 1 appears stretched east-west near the poles (common in Mercator) and Image 2 shows more accurate relative sizes (common in Peters), the choice might align as follows:\n\n- Image 1 could be Mercator.\n- Image 2 could be Peters.\n\nBased on these guidelines:\n\nANSWER: A. Mercator projection, Peters projection."
