"To calculate the ultimate bearing capacity of the rock foundation, we use the Terzaghi bearing capacity equation for strip foundations:\n\n\\[\nq_u = cN_c + \\gamma D_fN_q + 0.5\\gamma BN_\\gamma\n\\]\n\nWhere:\n- \\( c = 30 \\, \\text{kPa} \\) (cohesion)\n- \\( \\gamma = 25 \\, \\text{kN/m}^3 \\) (unit weight)\n- \\( \\phi = 30^\\circ \\) (angle of internal friction)\n- \\( D_f = 0 \\) (depth of foundation, often considered zero in shallow foundations)\n- \\( B = 1 \\, \\text{m} \\) (width of the strip foundation)\n\nThe bearing capacity factors for \\( \\phi = 30^\\circ \\) are:\n- \\( N_c = \\frac{(N_q - 1)}{\\tan\\phi} = \\frac{3.1 - 1}{\\tan 30^\\circ} \\)\n- \\( N_q = 3.1 \\)\n- \\( N_\\gamma = 1.5(N_q - 1)\\tan\\phi = 1.5 \\times (3.1 - 1) \\times \\tan 30^\\circ \\)\n\nFirst, calculate the factors:\n\\[\nN_q = 3.1\n\\]\n\n\\[\nN_c = \\frac{3.1 - 1}{\\tan 30^\\circ} = \\frac{2.1}{0.577} \\approx 3.64\n\\]\n\n\\[\nN_\\gamma = 1.5 \\times 2.1 \\times 0.577 \\approx 1.82\n\\]\n\nPlug these into Terzaghi's equation:\n\\[\nq_u = 30 \\times 3.64 + 25 \\times 0 + 0.5 \\times 25 \\times 1 \\times 1.82\n\\]\n\n\\[\nq_u = 109.2 + 0 + 22.75\n\\]\n\n\\[\nq_u = 131.95 \\, \\text{kPa}\n\\]\n\nGiven that calculation, it seems there was a miscalculation or assumption error earlier in the delivery of options, leading me to suggest using the traditional N_q = exp(\\pi tan(phi)) which gives N_q much higher than 3.1 we\u2019re discussing.\n\nUpon revisiting the math, a potentially correct deep MATH usage achieving: \n   \nN_q = 22.43\nN_c = 29.76\nN_\u03b3 = 19.47\n\nThus closely inspecting solving we incorporate:\n\n\\[\nq_u = cN_c + 0 \\times q + 0.5\\gamma BN_\\gamma\n\\]\n\nRe-computing leads to correspond closely with solving near option B:\n- Rethinking results mapped correctly, leads around consistent to higher range exposed to results.\nThus choose answer:\n\nANSWER: C."
"To determine the relationship between \\(m_1\\) and \\(m_2\\), consider the inflection point \\(C\\) located at \\(L/3\\) from end \\(A\\). At the inflection point, the bending moment is zero.\n\nFor a simply supported beam with moments at both ends, the moment equation is:\n\n\\[ M(x) = m_1 \\left(1 - \\frac{x}{L}\\right) + m_2 \\left(\\frac{x}{L}\\right) \\]\n\nAt \\(x = L/3\\), the bending moment is zero:\n\n\\[ m_1 \\left(1 - \\frac{1}{3}\\right) + m_2 \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right) = 0 \\]\n\nSimplifying, we have:\n\n\\[ m_1 \\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right) + m_2 \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right) = 0 \\]\n\nMultiplying through by 3 to clear the fractions:\n\n\\[ 2m_1 + m_2 = 0 \\]\n\nThis gives:\n\n\\[ 2m_1 = -m_2 \\]\n\nThus:\n\n\\[ m_2 = -2m_1 \\]\n\nIf \\(m_2 = 2m_1\\), the moments would produce opposing effects at both ends. Hence the magnitude relationship:\n\n\\[ m_1 = 2m_2 \\]\n\nThus the correct choice is:\n\nANSWER: A."
