"To determine which part of the Earth was formed millions of years ago through interactions with the other three spheres, let's analyze each option:\n\nA. **Biosphere** - Consists of all living organisms and was formed through interactions over time, but life evolved on Earth due to pre-existing conditions in the other spheres.\n\nB. **Geosphere** - This is the solid part of the Earth (land, rocks, etc.) and was formed from the cooling of molten material millions of years ago. It provides the structure on which other spheres interact, suggesting it was formed independently rather than through interactions.\n\nC. **Hydrosphere** - Consists of all water on Earth. Water has contributed to the formation and weathering of the geosphere but was likely not formed directly from interactions with the other spheres.\n\nD. **Atmosphere** - The gases surrounding the Earth. It interacts with the other spheres but was formed early on through volcanic outgassing and other geophysical processes.\n\nThe **geosphere** is the part of the Earth that was formed from the initial cooling and solidification of the planet and is not a result of interactions between the other spheres, as it existed early in Earth's history.\n\nANSWER: B"
"To solve the problem, we'll use the diffraction grating formula:\n\n\\[ d \\sin \\theta = m \\lambda \\]\n\nWhere:\n- \\( d \\) is the grating spacing (distance between adjacent lines),\n- \\( \\theta \\) is the diffraction angle,\n- \\( m \\) is the diffraction order,\n- \\( \\lambda \\) is the wavelength of light.\n\nGiven:\n- \\( 800 \\) lines/mm, so \\( d = \\frac{1}{800} \\) mm (convert to meters for consistency: \\( d = \\frac{1}{800,000} \\) m)\n- First-order spectrum: \\( m = 1 \\)\n- \\( \\theta = 30^\\circ \\)\n\nLet's plug the values into the formula and solve for \\( \\lambda \\):\n\n1. Calculate \\( d \\):\n   \\[ d = \\frac{1}{800,000} \\, \\text{m} = 1.25 \\times 10^{-6} \\, \\text{m} \\]\n\n2. Use the formula:\n   \\[ 1.25 \\times 10^{-6} \\sin(30^\\circ) = 1 \\cdot \\lambda \\]\n\n3. Solve for \\( \\lambda \\):\n   \\[ \\lambda = 1.25 \\times 10^{-6} \\cdot 0.5 = 0.625 \\times 10^{-6} \\, \\text{m} = 0.625 \\, \\mu \\text{m} \\]\n\nANSWER: A."
"To determine the load capacity of the composite foundation, we use the following formula:\n\n\\[ q_{c} = q_{sk} \\cdot (1 - \\eta) + n \\cdot q_{sk} \\cdot \\eta \\]\n\nWhere:\n- \\( q_{c} \\) is the bearing capacity of the composite foundation.\n- \\( q_{sk} \\) is the characteristic value of the bearing capacity of the soft soil foundation, \\( F_{sk} = 60 \\, \\text{kPa} \\).\n- \\( n \\) is the pile-soil stress ratio, \\( n = 8.5 \\).\n- \\( \\eta \\) is the displacement ratio, \\( \\eta = 0.20 \\).\n\nPlugging in the values:\n\n\\[ q_{c} = 60 \\cdot (1 - 0.20) + 8.5 \\cdot 60 \\cdot 0.20 \\]\n\n\\[ q_{c} = 60 \\cdot 0.80 + 8.5 \\cdot 60 \\cdot 0.20 \\]\n\n\\[ q_{c} = 48 + 102 \\]\n\n\\[ q_{c} = 150 \\, \\text{kPa} \\]\n\nTherefore, the closest value for the load capacity of the composite foundation is 150 kPa.\n\nANSWER: B."
"To determine the melting settlement category, we compare the plastic limit moisture content (\\(w_p\\)) and the total moisture content (\\(W_0\\)).\n\nGiven:\n- Plastic limit moisture content (\\(w_p\\)) = 18.2%\n- Total moisture content (\\(W_0\\)) = 26.8%\n\nSteps:\n1. Calculate moisture content difference: \\( \\Delta W = W_0 - w_p = 26.8\\% - 18.2\\% = 8.6\\%\\).\n2. Compare \\( \\Delta W\\) against typical thresholds for permafrost regions:\n   - If \\(\\Delta W\\) is small, sedimentation is less likely.\n   - Larger \\(\\Delta W\\) indicates higher potential for settlement upon thawing.\n\nGiven that \\(\\Delta W = 8.6\\%\\), this is a moderate value, suggesting some degree of settlement but not extremely high.\n\n- \\( \\Delta W \\leq 5\\%\\): Typically incoherent sinking or weak sedimentation.\n- \\( \\Delta W \\approx 5\\% - 10\\%\\): Melting and sinking.\n- \\( \\Delta W > 10\\%\\): Strong melting sedimentation.\n\nConclusion:\n\\(\\Delta W = 8.6\\%\\) fits within the \"Melting and sinking\" range.\n\nANSWER: C."
