"To find the actual horizontal distance, we first need to adjust for the thermal expansion of the steel ruler due to the temperature difference. The length equation for the steel ruler is given as:\n\n\\[ \nl(t) = 50.0044m + 1.25 \\times 10^{-5} \\times (t - 20) \\times 50m.\n\\]\n\nGiven \\( t = 31.4^\\circ C \\),\n\n\\[\nl(t) = 50.0044m + 1.25 \\times 10^{-5} \\times (31.4 - 20) \\times 50m.\n\\]\n\n\\[\n= 50.0044m + 1.25 \\times 10^{-5} \\times 11.4 \\times 50m.\n\\]\n\n\\[\n= 50.0044m + 0.007125m.\n\\]\n\n\\[\n= 50.011525m.\n\\]\n\nThe ruler measures 49.9062m, which corresponds to the adjusted length:\n\n\\[ \n\\text{Adjusted measured length} = 49.9062m \\times \\frac{50.0044m}{50.011525m}.\n\\]\n\nCalculate this:\n\n\\[\n= 49.9062m \\times \\frac{50.0044}{50.011525}.\n\\]\n\n\\[\n= 49.9062m \\times 0.999856.\n\\]\n\n\\[\n= 49.8998m.\n\\]\n\nNow adjust for the elevation difference to find the horizontal distance using the formula:\n\n\\[ \n\\text{Horizontal distance} = \\sqrt{(49.8998m)^2 - (0.705m)^2}.\n\\]\n\nCalculate:\n\n\\[\n= \\sqrt{2490.04m - 0.497025m}.\n\\]\n\n\\[\n= \\sqrt{2489.543m}.\n\\]\n\n\\[\n= 49.904m.\n\\]\n\nThus, the actual horizontal distance between the two points is approximately 49.904m.\n\nANSWER: A."
"To determine the correct requirement for ground resistance, consider the general principles for grounding in high-voltage direct current (HVDC) systems. Ground resistance typically depends on soil resistivity and is crucial for safe and effective operation.\n\n1. **Soil Resistivity and Grounding Resistance Relationship:**\n   - Lower soil resistivity generally allows for lower grounding resistance.\n   - Higher soil resistivity might lead to higher acceptable grounding resistance due to the innate difficulty of obtaining low resistance in high-resistivity soils.\n\n2. **Options Analysis:**\n   - **A.** Soil resistivity 100 \u03a9\u22c5m, grounding resistance \u2264 15 \u03a9: Feasible for relatively low resistivity.\n   - **B.** Soil resistivity 100-500 \u03a9\u22c5m, grounding resistance \u2264 20 \u03a9: Reasonable increase in resistance with increased resistivity.\n   - **C.** Soil resistivity 500-1000 \u03a9\u22c5m, grounding resistance \u2264 20 \u03a9: Potentially feasible, but maintaining a \u2264 20 \u03a9 resistance might be challenging in higher resistivity.\n   - **D.** Soil resistivity 1000-2000 \u03a9\u22c5m, grounding resistance \u2264 25 \u03a9: Likely adjustment to accommodate very high resistivity soils.\n\nGiven these points, the most reasonable match considering typical electrical engineering standards and practices is option B. The increase in acceptable grounding resistance is justified as soil resistivity rises in the range of 100-500 \u03a9\u22c5m.\n\nANSWER: B."
