"To determine how the electrode potential changes with concentration, we can use the Nernst equation for each electrode reaction:\n\nFor reaction (1): \\( \\text{Cu}^{2+} + 2e^- = \\text{Cu} \\)\nThe Nernst equation is:\n\\[ E = E^\\circ - \\frac{RT}{nF} \\ln \\left( \\frac{1}{[\\text{Cu}^{2+}]} \\right) \\]\nThis can be simplified to:\n\\[ E = E^\\circ + \\frac{RT}{nF} \\ln [\\text{Cu}^{2+}] \\]\nAs the concentration of \\(\\text{Cu}^{2+}\\) increases, the electrode potential \\(E\\) increases.\n\nFor reaction (2): \\( \\text{I}_2 + 2e^- = 2\\text{I}^- \\)\nThe Nernst equation is:\n\\[ E = E^\\circ - \\frac{RT}{nF} \\ln \\left( \\frac{[\\text{I}^-]^2}{[\\text{I}_2]} \\right) \\]\nAs the concentration of \\([\\text{I}^-]\\) increases, the term \\(\\ln \\left( \\frac{[\\text{I}^-]^2}{[\\text{I}_2]} \\right)\\) becomes larger, making \\(E\\) decrease.\n\nBased on these observations:\n- For reaction (1), as the concentration increases, the potential grows.\n- For reaction (2), as the concentration increases, the potential reduces.\n\nANSWER: C"
