"To determine the pH of the solution, we need to consider the acetic acid (HAc) and sodium acetate (NaAc) system, which forms a buffer. The pH of a buffer solution is calculated using the Henderson-Hasselbalch equation:\n\n\\[ \\text{pH} = \\text{pK}_a + \\log \\left( \\frac{[\\text{A}^-]}{[\\text{HA}]} \\right) \\]\n\nwhere:\n- \\([\\text{A}^-]\\) is the concentration of the acetate ion (from NaAc).\n- \\([\\text{HA}]\\) is the concentration of acetic acid (HAc).\n- \\(\\text{pK}_a\\) of acetic acid is 4.75.\n\n**Step 1: Determine the moles of each component.**\n\n- Moles of NaAc: \n  \\[ 0.10 \\, \\text{L} \\times 0.10 \\, \\text{mol/L} = 0.010 \\, \\text{mol} \\]\n\n- Moles of HAc: \n  \\[ 0.10 \\, \\text{L} \\times 0.14 \\, \\text{mol/L} = 0.014 \\, \\text{mol} \\]\n\n**Step 2: Calculate concentrations after mixing.**\n\nThe total volume of the solution is \\(100 \\, \\text{mL} + 100 \\, \\text{mL} = 200 \\, \\text{mL} = 0.20 \\, \\text{L}\\).\n\n- Concentration of acetate ion (\\([\\text{A}^-]\\)):\n  \\[ \\frac{0.010 \\, \\text{mol}}{0.20 \\, \\text{L}} = 0.050 \\, \\text{mol/L} \\]\n\n- Concentration of acetic acid (\\([\\text{HA}]\\)):\n  \\[ \\frac{0.014 \\, \\text{mol}}{0.20 \\, \\text{L}} = 0.070 \\, \\text{mol/L} \\]\n\n**Step 3: Apply the Henderson-Hasselbalch equation.**\n\n\\[ \\text{pH} = 4.75 + \\log \\left( \\frac{0.050}{0.070} \\right) \\]\n\n\\[ \\text{pH} = 4.75 + \\log \\left( \\frac{5}{7} \\right) \\]\n\n\\[ \\text{pH} = 4.75 + \\log(0.714) \\]\n\n\\[ \\text{pH} = 4.75 - 0.146 \\]\n\n\\[ \\text{pH} \\approx 4.60 \\]\n\nANSWER: C"
"To determine the correct option, we need to recall the Darcy-Weisbach equation, which describes frictional head loss (\\(h_f\\)) in a flow system:\n\n\\[\nh_f = \\frac{f \\cdot L \\cdot V^2}{2 \\cdot g \\cdot R_h}\n\\]\n\nWhere:\n- \\(f\\) is the friction factor,\n- \\(L\\) is the length of the flow path,\n- \\(V\\) is the flow velocity,\n- \\(g\\) is the acceleration due to gravity,\n- \\(R_h\\) is the hydraulic radius.\n\nThe average shear stress (\\(\\tau\\)) on the wall is related to the friction factor and the hydraulic radius by the equation:\n\n\\[\n\\tau = \\frac{f \\cdot \\rho \\cdot V^2}{8}\n\\]\n\nWhere \\(\\rho\\) is the fluid density.\n\nFrom the above relationships, \\(h_f\\) can be understood as being:\n- Directly proportional to the length of the flow path (\\(L\\)),\n- Directly proportional to the average shear stress (\\(\\tau\\)),\n- Inversely proportional to the hydraulic radius (\\(R_h\\)).\n\nGiven these considerations, the correct choice aligns with option D.\n\nANSWER: D."
