"To determine the sliding surface with the smallest safety factor, we should consider the mechanics of slope stability and the concept of the critical slip surface. The critical slip surface is the one with the lowest factor of safety against sliding, which typically occurs when the angle of the slip surface aligns with the angle of internal friction of the soil.\n\nGiven:\n- Internal friction angle of sandy soil, $\\phi = 35^\\circ$.\n- Slope ratio = 1:1.5.\n\nThe safety factor (SF) depends on the alignment between the slip surface angle $\\alpha$ and the internal friction angle $\\phi$. The critical condition often arises when $\\alpha$ approaches $\\phi$, but generally, the critical angle for a planar slip surface on a slope might slightly differ based on geometry and specific soil properties.\n\nOptions:\n- A. $\\alpha = 25^\\circ$\n- B. $\\alpha = 29^\\circ$\n- C. $\\alpha = 31^\\circ$\n- D. $\\alpha = 33^\\circ$\n\nConsidering the mechanics:\n1. A slip surface angle much less than $\\phi$ would result in a high safety factor, as the resisting shearing force would be much greater than the driving force.\n2. Ideally, the smallest safety factor would occur as the slip surface angle approaches the internal friction angle, reducing the resistance.\n\nBetween the options, $\\alpha = 33^\\circ$ is closest to $\\phi = 35^\\circ$, which would likely result in the smallest safety factor due to it being nearer to the angle where shearing resistance equals driving force, leading to critical conditions.\n\nANSWER: D"
