"To determine the expansion potential of the expansive soil, we need to calculate the percentage increase in volume when the soil is immersed in water.\n\n1. **Volume when dried:** 125 cm\u00b3\n2. **Volume after immersion:** 200 cm\u00b3\n\n**Percentage increase in volume:**\n\n\\[\n\\text{Percentage increase} = \\left( \\frac{\\text{Final Volume} - \\text{Initial Volume}}{\\text{Initial Volume}} \\right) \\times 100\n\\]\n\n\\[\n= \\left( \\frac{200 - 125}{125} \\right) \\times 100 = \\left( \\frac{75}{125} \\right) \\times 100 = 60\\%\n\\]\n\nAccording to the Technical Specification for Buildings in Expansive Soil Areas (GBJ 112-1987), we categorize the expansion potential as follows:\n\n- **Weak expansion potential:** typically for soils with a lower percentage increase.\n- **Medium expansion potential:** generally falls in the mid-range of percentage increase.\n- **Strong expansion potential:** higher percentage increase.\n\nGiven a 60% increase, the soil likely has a strong expansion potential.\n\nANSWER: C"
