"To determine the type of geological process that river erosion towards the source belongs to, let's analyze the options:\n\nA. Downward erosion: This process typically involves the deepening of valleys or riverbeds primarily by vertical cutting into the land through erosion, most often associated with river action over time.\n\nB. Lateral erosion: This refers to the sideways erosion that primarily widens the river valley by eroding the banks, often occurring in the middle and lower courses of the river.\n\nC. Chemical dissolution: This process involves the chemical breakdown and dissolution of rocks, typically involving chemical reactions with water or other chemicals in the environment.\n\nD. Mechanical erosion: This involves physical processes leading to the break down and transport of rock and sediment, usually including processes like abrasion, plucking, and physical breakage.\n\nGiven that river erosion towards the source often focuses on the removal of material from the bed of the river channel to cut downwards, it primarily involves downward erosion.\n\nANSWER: A."
"To find the length of the anchoring section, we can use the formula for the uplift resistance of the anchor:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Uplift Resistance} = \\text{Shear Strength} \\times \\text{Perimeter} \\times \\text{Length}\n\\]\n\nWhere:\n- The shear strength is given as \\(0.2 \\, \\text{MPa}\\).\n- The perimeter of the circular anchor is \\( \\pi \\times \\text{diameter} = \\pi \\times 0.13 \\, \\text{m} \\).\n- Let \\( L \\) be the length of the anchoring section.\n\nFirst, we need the uplift resistance to be equal to twice the anchoring force to meet the safety factor requirement:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Uplift Resistance} = 2 \\times 400 \\, \\text{kN} = 800 \\, \\text{kN}\n\\]\n\nConvert the uplift resistance into MPa considering the units (kN and meter):\n\n\\[\n800 \\, \\text{kN} = 800 \\times 10^3 \\, \\text{N}\n\\]\n\nNow, equate the uplift resistance to the expression for the resistance:\n\n\\[\n0.2 \\times \\pi \\times 0.13 \\times L = 800 \\times 10^3\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\( L \\):\n\n\\[\nL = \\frac{800 \\times 10^3}{0.2 \\times \\pi \\times 0.13}\n\\]\n\nCalculating \\( L \\):\n\n\\[\nL \\approx \\frac{800 \\times 10^3}{0.2 \\times 3.1416 \\times 0.13} \\]\n\n\\[\nL \\approx \\frac{800000}{0.08168}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nL \\approx 9800 \\, \\text{mm} = 9.8 \\, \\text{m}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the closest option is:\n\nANSWER: B. 9.8m"
"To calculate the freezing point of the solution, we use the formula for freezing point depression:\n\n\\[\n\\Delta T_f = i \\cdot K_f \\cdot m\n\\]\n\nWhere:\n- \\(\\Delta T_f\\) is the freezing point depression.\n- \\(i\\) is the van 't Hoff factor. For sugar (a non-electrolyte), \\(i = 1\\).\n- \\(K_f\\) is the freezing point depression constant, given as 1.86 \u00b0C kg/mol.\n- \\(m\\) is the molality of the solution.\n\nFirst, calculate the molality (\\(m\\)):\n\n1. Find the molar mass of sugar (C\\(_6\\)H\\(_{12}\\)O\\(_6\\)):\n   - C: 12.01 g/mol, H: 1.008 g/mol, O: 16.00 g/mol\n   - Molar mass = \\(6 \\times 12.01 + 12 \\times 1.008 + 6 \\times 16.00 = 180.18 \\, \\text{g/mol}\\)\n\n2. Calculate moles of sugar:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Moles} = \\frac{15.0 \\, \\text{g}}{180.18 \\, \\text{g/mol}} = 0.0832 \\, \\text{mol}\n   \\]\n\n3. Calculate molality (\\(m\\)):\n   \\[\n   m = \\frac{0.0832 \\, \\text{mol}}{0.200 \\, \\text{kg}} = 0.416 \\, \\text{mol/kg}\n   \\]\n\nNow, calculate \\(\\Delta T_f\\):\n\n\\[\n\\Delta T_f = 1 \\cdot 1.86 \\, \\text{\u00b0C kg/mol} \\cdot 0.416 \\, \\text{mol/kg} = 0.774 \\, \\text{\u00b0C}\n\\]\n\nSince the freezing point of pure water is 0 \u00b0C, the freezing point of the solution is:\n\n\\[\n0.0 \\, \\text{\u00b0C} - 0.774 \\, \\text{\u00b0C} = -0.774 \\, \\text{\u00b0C}\n\\]\n\nThus, the closest choice is:\n\nANSWER: B. -0.776 \u00b0C."
"To determine which coefficient corresponds to non-collapsible soil, we need to understand the threshold that differentiates collapsible from non-collapsible soils.\n\nThe coefficient of collapsibility ($\\delta_S$) generally indicates the degree to which soil will compress or settle when exposed to water. A commonly accepted limit is that soil is considered non-collapsible if $\\delta_S$ is less than or equal to 0.01.\n\nGiven the options:\n- A: $\\delta_S = 0.03$\n- B: $\\delta_S = 0.02$\n- C: $\\delta_S = 0.01$\n- D: $\\delta_S = 0.025$\n\nOnly option C has $\\delta_S$ equal to 0.01, which meets the condition for a non-collapsible soil.\n\nANSWER: C"
