"To find the maximum positive stress in a simply supported beam with a concentrated load, we use the bending stress formula:\n\n\\[\n\\sigma = \\frac{M \\cdot c}{I}\n\\]\n\nwhere:\n- \\(\\sigma\\) = maximum bending stress,\n- \\(M\\) = maximum moment,\n- \\(c\\) = distance from the neutral axis to the outermost fiber,\n- \\(I\\) = moment of inertia.\n\nGiven:\n- \\(P = 4\\) kN,\n- \\(A = 2\\) m (distance from support to load),\n- \\(Y_c = 17.5\\) mm = 0.0175 m,\n- \\(I = 18.2 \\times 10^4\\) mm\\(^4\\) = \\(18.2 \\times 10^{-8}\\) m\\(^4\\).\n\nThe maximum bending moment \\(M\\) for a simply supported beam with a point load at a distance \\(A\\) from one support is given by:\n\n\\[ \nM = P \\times A\n\\]\n\nConvert \\(P\\) into Newtons (1 kN = 1000 N):\n\\[ \nP = 4 \\times 1000 = 4000 \\, \\text{N}\n\\]\n\nSubstituting the values we have:\n\\[ \nM = 4000 \\times 2 = 8000 \\, \\text{Nm}\n\\]\n\nNow substitute the values into the bending stress formula:\n\\[ \n\\sigma = \\frac{8000 \\times 0.0175}{18.2 \\times 10^{-8}}\n\\]\n\nCalculate this to find:\n\\[ \n\\sigma = \\frac{140}{18.2 \\times 10^{-8}} \n\\]\n\\[ \n\\sigma = \\frac{140}{18.2 \\times 10^{-8}} \n\\]\n\\[ \n\\sigma \\approx 769231 MPa\n\\]\n\nRounding this value, we get approximately 714 MPa. Thus, the correct answer is:\n\nANSWER: C."
