"To determine the principal stress and its direction, we need to calculate the principal stresses based on the given stress components:\n\n1. **Given Stresses:**\n   - Normal Stress in x-direction, \\( \\sigma_x = 40 \\, \\text{MPa} \\)\n   - Normal Stress in y-direction, \\( \\sigma_y = 40 \\, \\text{MPa} \\)\n   - Shear Stress, \\( \\tau_{xy} = 40\\sqrt{3} \\, \\text{MPa} \\)\n\n2. **Principal Stresses Equations:**\n\nThe principal stresses \\( \\sigma_1 \\) and \\( \\sigma_2 \\) can be calculated using:\n\n\\[\n\\sigma_{1,2} = \\frac{\\sigma_x + \\sigma_y}{2} \\pm \\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{\\sigma_x - \\sigma_y}{2}\\right)^2 + \\tau_{xy}^2}\n\\]\n\nSubstitute the values:\n\n\\[\n\\sigma_{1,2} = \\frac{40 + 40}{2} \\pm \\sqrt{\\left(\\frac{40 - 40}{2}\\right)^2 + (40\\sqrt{3})^2}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\sigma_{1,2} = 40 \\pm \\sqrt{0 + 4800}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\sigma_{1,2} = 40 \\pm 69.28\n\\]\n\nThus,\n\\[\n\\sigma_1 = 109.28 \\, \\text{MPa} \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\sigma_2 = -29.28 \\, \\text{MPa}\n\\]\n\n3. **Principal Angle (\\(\\alpha\\)):**\n\nUse the equation for the angle \\( \\alpha \\) between the x-axis and the principal axis:\n\n\\[\n\\tan(2\\alpha) = \\frac{2\\tau_{xy}}{\\sigma_x - \\sigma_y}\n\\]\n\nSubstitute the values:\n\n\\[\n\\tan(2\\alpha) = \\frac{2 \\times 40\\sqrt{3}}{0} = \\infty\n\\]\n\nThus, \n\\[\n2\\alpha = 90^\\circ \\Rightarrow \\alpha = 45^\\circ\n\\]\n\nNow, checking provided options that closely match or round values, since none of these directly correspond to the calculated values, we select:\n\nANSWER: B."
