"To determine where the most time-intensive and labor-intensive agricultural activities would fall in von Th\u00fcnen's model, we need to consider the core idea behind the model. \n\n1. **Von Th\u00fcnen's Theory**: This model hypothesizes that different types of agriculture are arranged in concentric circles around a central market city. \n\n2. **Inner Rings**: Closest to the market (rings A and B) usually involve perishable goods or items requiring quick transport, like dairy farming or market gardening. These activities tend to be more labor-intensive due to the need for frequent attention and fast delivery to the market.\n\n3. **Outer Rings**: More extensive agriculture, like grain or livestock grazing, is typically found in the outer rings (rings C, D, and E) because they are less labor-intensive and can withstand longer transport times.\n\nTherefore, the most time-intensive and labor-intensive activities would logically be in the closest rings (A and B).\n\nANSWER: C. rings A and B."
