"To determine the wrong statement about land subsidence, let's analyze each option:\n\nA. Earthquake action can cause significant ground subsidence.\n- True: Earthquakes can cause land subsidence by shaking the ground, as well as through faulting and liquefaction.\n\nB. Neotectonic movements can cause land subsidence.\n- True: Neotectonic movements, which involve recent and ongoing crustal deformation, can cause land subsidence.\n\nC. Natural consolidation is not necessarily related to land subsidence.\n- False: Natural consolidation of soils, especially in soft or waterlogged areas, often leads to land subsidence as the ground settles and compacts over time.\n\nD. Large area ground loading can cause ground subsidence.\n- True: Large area ground loading, such as from heavy structures or accumulation of sediments, can cause subsidence as the ground compresses under the weight.\n\nANSWER: C."
"To find the active soil pressure behind the retaining wall, we can use Rankine\u2019s Earth Pressure Theory. This theory can help us estimate the lateral pressure exerted by the soil.\n\n### Given:\n- Wall height, \\(H = 5.5 \\, \\text{m}\\)\n- Weight of the soil, \\(\\gamma = 20 \\, \\text{kN/m}^3\\)\n- Internal friction angle of soil, \\(\\phi = 28^\\circ\\)\n- Back slope angle, \\(\\beta = 60^\\circ\\)\n- Wall back angle, \\(\\alpha = 75^\\circ\\)\n- Friction angle between soil and wall, \\(\\delta = 15^\\circ\\)\n  \n### Finding the Active Earth Pressure Coefficient (\\(K_a\\)):\n\n\\[ \nK_a = \\frac{\\cos^2(\\alpha)}{\\cos^2(\\alpha - \\phi) \\cdot \\cos(\\beta)} \\cdot \\frac{1 - \\sin(\\phi + \\delta) \\cdot \\sin(\\phi)}{1 + \\sin(\\phi - \\delta) \\cdot \\sin(\\beta)}\n\\]\n\n### Calculate \\(K_a\\):\n\n\\[\nK_a = \\frac{\\cos^2(75^\\circ)}{\\cos^2(75^\\circ - 28^\\circ) \\cdot \\cos(60^\\circ)} \\cdot \\frac{1 - \\sin(28^\\circ + 15^\\circ) \\cdot \\sin(28^\\circ)}{1 + \\sin(28^\\circ - 15^\\circ) \\cdot \\sin(60^\\circ)}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nK_a \\approx 0.33\n\\]\n\n### Active Earth Pressure (\\(P_a\\)):\n\nThe active earth pressure is given by:\n\n\\[ \nP_a = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot K_a \\cdot \\gamma \\cdot H^2\n\\]\n\n\\[\nP_a = \\frac{1}{2} \\cdot 0.33 \\cdot 20 \\cdot (5.5)^2 \n\\]\n\n\\[\nP_a = 99.825 \\, \\text{kN/m}^2\n\\]\n\nGiven the options, the closest pressure is:\n\nANSWER: A. 160 kN/m."
