"To find the reaction force on the nozzle, we can apply the principle of conservation of linear momentum. \n\n### Given:\n- Flow rate, \\( Q = 0.4 \\, \\text{m}^3/\\text{s} \\)\n- Velocity at nozzle outlet, \\( v_2 = 50.93 \\, \\text{m/s} \\)\n- Velocity in coarse pipe, \\( v_1 = 3.18 \\, \\text{m/s} \\)\n- Total pressure at the coarse pipe, \\( P_1 = 162.33 \\, \\text{kN} \\)\n\n### Steps:\n\n1. **Calculate the area of the cross-section at the inlet and outlet.**\n\n   Since \\( Q = A \\times v \\), we have:\n   \n   \\[\n   A_1 = \\frac{Q}{v_1} = \\frac{0.4}{3.18} = 0.1258 \\, \\text{m}^2\n   \\]\n   \n   \\[\n   A_2 = \\frac{Q}{v_2} = \\frac{0.4}{50.93} = 0.00785 \\, \\text{m}^2\n   \\]\n   \n2. **Determine the force due to pressure at the inlet.**\n\n   \\[\n   F_1 = P_1 \\times A_1 = 162,330 \\, \\text{N/m}^2 \\times 0.1258 \\, \\text{m}^2 = 20,416.74 \\, \\text{N}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the change in momentum.**\n\n   The momentum at the inlet and outlet can be written as:\n   \n   \\( \\text{Momentum change} = \\dot{m} (v_2 - v_1) \\)\n   \n   Using the relationship \\( \\dot{m} = \\rho Q \\) and assuming water (\\( \\rho = 1000 \\, \\text{kg/m}^3 \\)):\n\n   \\[\n   \\dot{m} = 1000 \\times 0.4 = 400 \\, \\text{kg/s}\n   \\]\n   \n   \\[\n   \\text{Momentum change} = 400 \\times (50.93 - 3.18) = 19,100 \\, \\text{N}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Calculate the resultant reaction force on the nozzle.**\n\n   Using the balance of force:\n   \n   \\[\n   F_{\\text{reaction}} = F_1 - \\text{Momentum change}\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   F_{\\text{reaction}} = 20,416.74 \\, \\text{N} - 19,100 \\, \\text{N} = 1,316.74 \\, \\text{N}\n   \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\n\nThe magnitude of the reaction force on the nozzle is approximately 1,316.74 N.\n\nANSWER: A"
