"To solve this problem, we need to determine the correct expression for the number of molecules (N) in an ideal gas, given its volume (V), pressure (p), and temperature (T).\n\nThe ideal gas law is given by:\n\\[ \nPV = nRT \n\\]\nwhere \\( n \\) is the number of moles and \\( R \\) is the molar gas constant.\n\nThe number of molecules is related to the number of moles by:\n\\[ \nn = \\frac{N}{N_A} \n\\]\nwhere \\( N_A \\) is Avogadro's number.\n\nSubstituting the expression for \\( n \\) in the ideal gas law:\n\\[ \nPV = \\frac{N}{N_A}RT \n\\]\n\nRearranging to find \\( N \\):\n\\[ \nN = \\frac{PVN_A}{RT} \n\\]\n\nUsing Boltzmann\u2019s constant, \\( k = \\frac{R}{N_A} \\), we can express \\( N_A \\) as:\n\\[ \nN_A = \\frac{R}{k} \n\\]\n\nSubstitute for \\( N_A \\) in the expression for \\( N \\):\n\\[ \nN = \\frac{PVR/(k)}{RT} \n= \\frac{PV}{kT} \n\\]\n\nThus, the expression for the number of molecules of the ideal gas is \\(\\frac{PV}{kT}\\).\n\nANSWER: B"
