"To solve the problem, we'll use the principles of soil mechanics to find the additional strip load \\( q \\) that makes the active earth pressure at point C equal to 1/3 of the passive earth pressure. Here's a step-by-step breakdown:\n\n1. **Active and Passive Earth Pressure Calculation:**\n\n   - **Active Earth Pressure (\\( P_a \\)):**  \n     The coefficient of active earth pressure (\\( K_a \\)) is calculated using the formula:\n     \\[\n     K_a = \\frac{{1 - \\sin \\phi}}{{1 + \\sin \\phi}}\n     \\]\n     Given \\( \\phi = 30\u00b0 \\), we find:\n     \\[\n     K_a = \\frac{{1 - \\sin 30\u00b0}}{{1 + \\sin 30\u00b0}} = \\frac{{1 - 0.5}}{{1 + 0.5}} = \\frac{0.5}{1.5} = \\frac{1}{3}\n     \\]\n\n   - **Passive Earth Pressure (\\( P_p \\)):**  \n     The coefficient of passive earth pressure (\\( K_p \\)) is:\n     \\[\n     K_p = \\frac{{1 + \\sin \\phi}}{{1 - \\sin \\phi}}\n     \\]\n     So,\n     \\[\n     K_p = \\frac{{1 + 0.5}}{{1 - 0.5}} = \\frac{1.5}{0.5} = 3\n     \\]\n\n2. **Relation between Earth Pressures:**\n\n   It's given that \\( P_a = \\frac{1}{3} P_p \\). We need to use this relation to determine \\( q \\).\n\n3. **Effective Calculation of Pressures:**\n\n   - Height difference \\( h = 6 \\, \\text{m} \\).\n   - Using the pressure-height relation, active pressure at depth h:\n     \\[\n     \\sigma_a = K_a \\cdot y \\cdot h = \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot 20 \\cdot 6 = 40 \\, \\text{kPa}\n     \\]\n   - Passive pressure:\n     \\[\n     \\sigma_p = K_p \\cdot y \\cdot h + q = 3 \\cdot 20 \\cdot 6 + q = 360 + q\n     \\]\n\n4. **Equating Pressures:**\n\n   \\[\n   \\sigma_a = \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot \\sigma_p \\implies 40 = \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot (360 + q)\n   \\]\n   Solving for \\( q \\):\n   \\[\n   40 = 120 + \\frac{q}{3}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   120 = \\frac{q}{3}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   q = 120 \\, \\text{kPa}\n   \\]\n\nThus, the additional strip load \\( q \\) that causes the active earth pressure value at point C to be 1/3 of the passive earth pressure value is \\( 120 \\, \\text{kPa} \\).\n\nANSWER: B."
