"To estimate the pile-soil stress ratio (\\(n\\)), we use the formula:\n\n\\[\nn = \\frac{q_p}{q_s}\n\\]\n\nWhere:\n- \\(q_p\\) is the bearing capacity of the piles,\n- \\(q_s\\) is the bearing capacity of the soil.\n\nGiven:\n- The characteristic value of the bearing capacity of the composite foundation: 200 kPa,\n- The characteristic value of the bearing capacity of the soil between piles: 150 kPa.\n\nTo find \\(q_p\\), use the formula for the total load on the composite foundation, assuming the load is shared between the piles and the soil:\n\n\\[\nq_c = \\frac{A_p \\cdot q_p + A_s \\cdot q_s}{A_p + A_s}\n\\]\n\nWhere:\n- \\(q_c\\) is the resultant bearing capacity (200 kPa),\n- \\(A_p\\) is the area of the pile,\n- \\(A_s\\) is the area of the soil.\n\nFor a square pile layout:\n- Pile diameter = 0.8 m,\n- Pile spacing = 2.0 m.\n\nCalculate the areas:\n- \\(A_p = \\pi \\left(\\frac{0.8}{2}\\right)^2 = 0.5024 \\, \\text{m}^2\\),\n- Total area of one pile section: \\((2.0 \\, \\text{m})^2 = 4.0 \\, \\text{m}^2\\),\n- \\(A_s = 4.0 \\, \\text{m}^2 - A_p = 4.0 - 0.5024 = 3.4976 \\, \\text{m}^2\\).\n\nRearrange the formula to solve for \\(q_p\\):\n\n\\[\n200 = \\frac{0.5024 \\cdot q_p + 3.4976 \\cdot 150}{4.0}\n\\]\n\nSolve for \\(q_p\\):\n\n\\[\n4.0 \\cdot 200 = 0.5024 \\cdot q_p + 3.4976 \\cdot 150\n\\]\n\n\\[\n800 = 0.5024 \\cdot q_p + 524.64\n\\]\n\n\\[\n800 - 524.64 = 0.5024 \\cdot q_p\n\\]\n\n\\[\n275.36 = 0.5024 \\cdot q_p\n\\]\n\n\\[\nq_p = \\frac{275.36}{0.5024} \\approx 548.065 \n\\]\n\nNow find the stress ratio:\n\n\\[\nn = \\frac{q_p}{q_s} = \\frac{548.065}{150} \\approx 3.65\n\\]\n\nThus, the closest value is:\n\nANSWER: D. 3.67."
