"To determine the tensile force on the wellbore at a depth of 5 meters, we start by understanding the setup and assumptions.\n\nThe caisson has a total height of \\( H = 12 \\) meters and a height of \\( h = 10 \\) meters submerged below. The tensile force at 5 meters below the surface depends on the weight of the caisson above that point.\n\n**Step-by-Step Calculation:**\n\n1. **Cross-sectional Area of the Caisson:**\n   - Outer diameter = 5.2 m\n   - Thickness = 0.4 m, so the inner diameter = 5.2 - (2 * 0.4) = 4.4 m\n   - Cross-sectional area, \\( A = \\frac{\\pi}{4} \\times (5.2^2 - 4.4^2) \\).\n\n2. **Calculate the Volume and Weight of the Caisson Segment:**\n   - Volume per meter = \\( A \\) (calculated above).\n   - Weight = Volume per meter \u00d7 9.81 m/s\u00b2 \u00d7 \\( \\text{density of material} \\).\n\n3. **Tensile Force:**\n   - The tensile force at 5 meters is due to the weight of the caisson segment from 5m to 12m (7m length).\n   - Tensile force = Weight per meter \u00d7 7 m (length of segment).\n\nWithout specific density values, this numerical answer emphasizes the conceptual framework. However, based on typical calculations:\n\n- The self-weight is evenly distributed, and thus the heaviest option indicates the highest tensile force value.\n\nConsidering the provided options and the setup, the closest likely answer with reasonable assumptions is:\n\nANSWER: C. 600kN."
