"To find the moisture content of the original frozen soil sample, we need to consider the provided information:\n\n1. Mass of the paste soil after melting = 560g\n2. Water content of the paste soil = 60%\n\nLet's find out how much water is in the paste soil:\n\n\\[ \\text{Water content} = \\frac{\\text{Mass of water}}{\\text{Mass of paste}} \\times 100\\% \\]\n\nGiven that the water content is 60%, we can set up the equation:\n\n\\[ 0.60 = \\frac{\\text{Mass of water}}{560} \\]\n\nSolving for the mass of water:\n\n\\[ \\text{Mass of water} = 0.60 \\times 560 = 336 \\text{g} \\]\n\nThus, the mass of the dry soil in the paste is:\n\n\\[ 560 \\text{g} - 336 \\text{g} = 224 \\text{g} \\]\n\nNow, let's calculate the water content of the original frozen soil sample. We know:\n\n- The original mass of the frozen soil sample = 500g\n- Water in the melted paste = 336g\n- Dry soil mass in the paste = 224g\n\nAssuming the dry soil mass was the same in the frozen sample:\n\n\\[ \\text{Moisture content of frozen soil} = \\frac{\\text{Mass of water in frozen soil}}{\\text{Mass of dry soil}} \\times 100\\% \\]\n\n\\[ \\text{Mass of water in frozen soil} = 500 \\text{g} - 224 \\text{g} = 276 \\text{g} \\]\n\nSo, moisture content of the original frozen soil sample is:\n\n\\[ \\frac{276}{224} \\times 100\\% \\approx 123.2\\% \\]\n\nTherefore, the moisture content of the frozen soil sample is closest to option C.\n\nANSWER: C"
