"To determine the load capacity of the composite foundation, we use the formula for the bearing capacity of a composite foundation:\n\n\\[\nq_c = q_{sk} + \\eta \\cdot (q_p - q_{sk})\n\\]\n\nwhere:\n- \\( q_c \\) is the load capacity of the composite foundation.\n- \\( q_{sk} \\) is the characteristic value of the bearing capacity of the soft soil foundation (\\( 60 \\, \\text{kPa} \\)).\n- \\(\\eta\\) is the area displacement ratio (20\\% or 0.2).\n- \\( q_p \\) is the bearing capacity of the pile (\\( 200 \\, \\text{kPa} \\)). \n\nSubstitute the known values into the formula:\n\n\\[\nq_c = 60 \\, \\text{kPa} + 0.2 \\cdot (200 \\, \\text{kPa} - 60 \\, \\text{kPa})\n\\]\n\nCalculate the difference:\n\n\\[\nq_p - q_{sk} = 200 \\, \\text{kPa} - 60 \\, \\text{kPa} = 140 \\, \\text{kPa}\n\\]\n\nThen calculate the contribution from the piles:\n\n\\[\n\\eta \\cdot (q_p - q_{sk}) = 0.2 \\times 140 \\, \\text{kPa} = 28 \\, \\text{kPa}\n\\]\n\nFinally, calculate the load capacity of the composite foundation:\n\n\\[\nq_c = 60 \\, \\text{kPa} + 28 \\, \\text{kPa} = 88 \\, \\text{kPa}\n\\]\n\nHowever, let's consider another perspective using the pile-soil stress ratio \\( n = 8.5 \\):\n\nThe stress ratio correction also affects the overall calculation which rearranges the pile-soil contribution to increase consistency:\n\nUsing factor values may adjust computations where:\n\nGiven, \\( f_{sk} = 60 \\, \\text{kPa} \\).\n\nThe \\( q_{c} \\approx (1 - \\eta) \\cdot q_{sk} + \\eta \\cdot n \\cdot q_{sk} \\).\n\nInsert the values:\n\n\\[\nq_{c} \\approx (1 - 0.2) \\cdot 60 \\, \\text{kPa} + 0.2 \\cdot 8.5 \\cdot 60 \\, \\text{kPa}\n\\]\n\nCalculate \\( q_{c} \\):\n\nFirst term:\n\\[\n(1 - 0.2) \\cdot 60 = 0.8 \\cdot 60 = 48 \\, \\text{kPa}\n\\]\nSecond term:\n\\[\n0.2 \\cdot 8.5 \\cdot 60 = 102 \\, \\text{kPa}\n\\]\n\nTotal:\n\\[\nq_{c} = 48 + 102 = 150 \\, \\text{kPa}\n\\]\n\nThus, the value closest is:\n\nANSWER: B."
