"To determine the relationship between the spontaneous periods of the three single-degree-of-freedom dynamical systems, let's consider the formula for the natural period \\( T \\) of a system:\n\n\\[ T = 2\\pi \\sqrt{\\frac{m}{k}} \\]\n\nwhere\n- \\( m \\) is the mass of the system,\n- \\( k \\) is the stiffness of the system.\n\nFor each case:\n\n1. **System a:**\n   - Mass \\( m \\)\n   - Stiffness \\( EI \\)\n\n   \\( T_a = 2\\pi \\sqrt{\\frac{m}{EI}} \\)\n\n2. **System b:**\n   - Mass \\( 2m \\) \n   - Stiffness \\( 2EI \\)\n\n   \\( T_b = 2\\pi \\sqrt{\\frac{2m}{2EI}} = 2\\pi \\sqrt{\\frac{m}{EI}} \\)\n\n3. **System c:**\n   - Mass \\( 2m \\)\n   - Stiffness \\( 2EI \\)\n   - The arrangement of stiffness and mass are the same as in b, thus:\n\n   \\( T_c = 2\\pi \\sqrt{\\frac{2m}{2EI}} = 2\\pi \\sqrt{\\frac{m}{EI}} \\)\n\nBy evaluating these expressions, we find:\n\n- \\( T_a = T_b = T_c \\)\n\nTherefore, the system periods \\( T_a \\), \\( T_b \\), and \\( T_c \\) are all equal.\n\nANSWER: D. are not equal."
