"To determine the permeability coefficient \\( k \\) of the rock mass, we'll use Darcy's Law as applied to the permeability test:\n\n1. **Reading and Adjustments:**\n   - Gauge reading: 0.05 MPa.\n   - Pressure loss in drill pipe and joints: 0.039 MPa.\n   - Corrected pressure: \\( 0.05 + 0.039 = 0.089 \\) MPa.\n\n2. **Pressure Head Calculation:**\n   - Convert pressure to head: \\( h = \\frac{0.089 \\times 10^6}{9.81 \\times 1000} = 9.07 \\) m.\n   - Adjust for gauge height: \\( h_{\\text{effective}} = 9.07 - 0.5 = 8.57 \\) m.\n\n3. **Calculation of Height Difference:**\n   - Depth of inspection section: from 6.0 m to 11.02 m.\n   - Midpoint of section: \\( \\frac{6.0 + 11.02}{2} = 8.51 \\) m.\n   - Height difference with groundwater level: \\( 8.5 - 8.51 = -0.01 \\) m (disregard because effective).\n\n4. **Flow Rate and Area:**\n   - Flow \\( Q = 88 \\) L/min = \\( \\frac{88}{60} \\) m\u00b3/s.\n   - Diameter \\( D = 0.15 \\) m, radius \\( r = 0.075 \\) m.\n   - Cross-sectional area \\( A = \\pi \\times (0.075)^2 = 0.01767 \\) m\u00b2.\n\n5. **Using Darcy\u2019s Law:**\n   \\[\n   k = \\frac{Q}{A \\cdot h_{\\text{effective}}}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   k = \\frac{\\frac{88}{60}}{0.01767 \\times 8.57} \\approx 1.38 \\text{ m/d}\n   \\]\n\nANSWER: B."
