"To determine the allowable load \\( P \\) for the structure, we need to consider two conditions: the stress in rod AB and the stress in rod BC.\n\nGiven:\n- Steel rod AB: Cross-section area \\( A_1 = 500 \\, \\text{mm}^2 \\), allowable stress \\([ \\sigma ]_1 = 160 \\, \\text{MPa}\\).\n- Copper rod BC: Cross-section area \\( A_2 = 700 \\, \\text{mm}^2 \\), allowable stress \\([ \\sigma ]_2 = 100 \\, \\text{MPa}\\).\n\n**Calculation Steps:**\n\n1. **For rod AB:**\n\n   The force in rod AB, \\( N_1 \\), must not exceed the allowable stress:\n   \\[\n   \\sigma_1 = \\frac{N_1}{A_1} \\leq [\\sigma]_1\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   N_1 \\leq [\\sigma]_1 \\times A_1 = 160 \\times 500 = 80000 \\, \\text{N}\n   \\]\n\n2. **For rod BC:**\n\n   The force in rod BC, \\( N_2 \\), must not exceed the allowable stress:\n   \\[\n   \\sigma_2 = \\frac{N_2}{A_2} \\leq [\\sigma]_2\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   N_2 \\leq [\\sigma]_2 \\times A_2 = 100 \\times 700 = 70000 \\, \\text{N}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Force equilibrium at point B:**\n\n   - Vertical equilibrium: \\( N_1 \\sin 30^\\circ + N_2 \\sin 60^\\circ = P \\)\n\n   Substituting \\( \\sin 30^\\circ = 0.5 \\) and \\( \\sin 60^\\circ = \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\):\n   \\[\n   P = N_1 \\times 0.5 + N_2 \\times \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n   \\]\n\n   Substitute the maximum values from conditions:\n   \\[\n   P = 80000 \\times 0.5 + 70000 \\times \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   P = 40000 + 70000 \\times 0.866 = 40000 + 60620 = 100620 \\, \\text{N}\n   \\]\n\nConvert \\( P \\) from \\( \\text{N} \\) to \\( \\text{kN} \\):\n\\[\nP = \\frac{100620}{1000} = 100.62 \\, \\text{kN}\n\\]\n\nOptions provided:\n- A. 92.4kN\n- B. 140kN\n- C. 104.3kN\n- D. 150kN\n\nThe closest option to 100.62 kN is 104.3 kN.\n\nANSWER: C."
"To solve this problem, we must determine the settlement remaining after 40% is completed during construction. Here\u2019s the step-by-step breakdown:\n\n1. **Identify Total Settlement:**\n   - Settlement under the foundation is influenced by the vertical stress distribution from the external load.\n   - The pressures at different layers after construction are given: 200 kPa at the depth of 2m and 100 kPa at the deeper layer.\n\n2. **Settlement Calculation Formula:**\n   - Settlement (\\( S \\)) can be estimated using the formula:\n     \\[\n     S = \\frac{\\sigma \\times H}{E_s}\n     \\]\n     where \\( \\sigma \\) is the additional stress, \\( H \\) is the layer thickness, and \\( E_s \\) is the modulus of elasticity.\n\n3. **Calculate Total Settlement for Each Layer:**\n\n   - **Layer 2:**\n     - \\( E_s = 6 \\) MPa = 6000 kPa\n     - Thickness (\\( H \\)) = 3000 mm\n     - Additional stress (\\( \\sigma \\)) = 200 kPa\n     - Settlement, \\( S_2 = \\frac{200 \\times 3000}{6000} = 100 \\) mm\n\n   - **Layer 3:**\n     - \\( E_s = 8 \\) MPa = 8000 kPa\n     - Thickness (\\( H \\)) = 4000 mm\n     - Additional stress (\\( \\sigma \\)) = 100 kPa\n     - Settlement, \\( S_3 = \\frac{100 \\times 4000}{8000} = 50 \\) mm\n\n4. **Total Initial Settlement Calculation:**\n   \\[\n   S_{\\text{total}} = S_2 + S_3 = 100 + 50 = 150 \\text{ mm}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Account for Construction Settlement:**\n   - 40% of the settlement occurs during construction:\n   \\[\n   S_{\\text{construction}} = 0.4 \\times 150 = 60 \\text{ mm}\n   \\]\n\n6. **Calculate Remaining Settlement:**\n   - Settlement remaining after construction:\n   \\[\n   S_{\\text{remaining}} = S_{\\text{total}} - S_{\\text{construction}} = 150 - 60 = 90 \\text{ mm}\n   \\]\n\nHowever, based on the given options, let's check if rounding or assumptions lead us to an allowable choice:\n\n7. **Adjust for Numerical Choices:**\n   - Closest permissible answer in the given options is 94 mm.\n\nANSWER: B."
"To determine which statement correctly describes a \"constant flow,\" we need to review each option:\n\nA. The flow velocity distribution at each section is the same.\n   - This implies that the velocity of the flow is uniform across different sections, which is characteristic of constant flow. This statement partly reflects constant flow if it's meant for an ideal situation but specifically doesn't capture it entirely in the context of time.\n\nB. All motion elements at each spatial point do not change over time.\n   - This is the classic definition of steady or constant flow, indicating that the flow properties (velocity, pressure, etc.) at any given point don't change with time.\n\nC. Streamlines are parallel straight lines.\n   - While parallel streamlines can indicate uniform flow, they are not exclusive to constant flow over time, as streamline shapes can vary depending on flow conditions.\n\nD. Flow varies according to a certain pattern over time.\n   - This describes unsteady flow, not constant flow, as constant flow involves no time variation.\n\nThe correct definition of constant flow is captured by option B: All motion elements at each spatial point do not change over time.\n\nANSWER: B"
"To determine the friction force \\( F \\) acting on block \\( A \\), we need to examine the forces acting on the block. Here's the step-by-step reasoning:\n\n1. **Weight of Block \\( A \\):** \n   - The weight \\( w = 60 \\, \\text{kN} \\) acts downward.\n\n2. **Tension \\( T \\):**\n   - The tension \\( T = 40 \\, \\text{kN} \\) acts at a \\( 30^\\circ \\) angle above the horizontal.\n\n3. **Normal Force \\( N \\):**\n   - The normal force counteracts the vertical components of the weight and tension.\n\n4. **Calculating Components:**\n   - Vertical component of tension: \\( T_v = T \\cdot \\sin(30^\\circ) = 40 \\times 0.5 = 20 \\, \\text{kN} \\)\n   - Hence, the effective downward force: \\( F_d = w - T_v = 60 - 20 = 40 \\, \\text{kN} \\)\n   - This is countered by the normal force \\( N \\).\n\n5. **Maximum Static Friction:**\n   - Max static friction \\( f_s = \\mu_s \\cdot N = 0.5 \\cdot 40 = 20 \\, \\text{kN} \\)\n\n6. **Horizontal Component of Tension:**\n   - \\( T_h = T \\cdot \\cos(30^\\circ) = 40 \\times \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} = 40 \\times 0.866 = 34.64 \\, \\text{kN} \\).\n\nSince the horizontal tension force \\( 34.64 \\, \\text{kN} \\) is greater than \\( 20 \\, \\text{kN} \\) (the static friction limit), the block will slide. Therefore, kinetic friction applies:\n\n7. **Kinetic Friction:**\n   - Kinetic friction \\( f_k = \\mu_k \\cdot N = 0.4 \\cdot 40 = 16 \\, \\text{kN} \\)\n\nTherefore, the friction force \\( F \\) when the block is sliding is:\n\n**ANSWER:** B. 16"
