"To determine the unit of the proportional coefficient for the elastic (foundation) coefficient of soil using static load tests, let's consider the relevant concepts.\n\n1. **Elastic (Foundation) Coefficient (k)**: Also known as the modulus of subgrade reaction, it is defined as the ratio between the pressure applied to the soil and the resulting displacement (settlement).\n\n2. **Formula**: \n   \\[\n   k = \\frac{P}{s}\n   \\]\n   where \\( P \\) is the pressure applied (usually in \\( \\text{KN/m}^2 \\)) and \\( s \\) is the displacement or settlement (usually in meters).\n\n3. **Units**:\n   - Pressure \\( P \\) is measured in \\( \\text{KN/m}^2 \\).\n   - Displacement \\( s \\) is measured in meters (m).\n\n4. **Coefficient of Subgrade Reaction (k)**:\n   - Therefore, the unit for \\( k \\) becomes:\n   \\[\n   k = \\frac{\\text{KN/m}^2}{\\text{m}} = \\text{KN/m}^3\n   \\]\n\nBased on the reasoning above, the correct unit for the elastic (foundation) coefficient of soil is \\( \\text{KN/m}^3 \\).\n\nANSWER: C."
