ownward.\n\nAnswer choice B mentions: \"2 m/s^2, up\", corresponding correctly to our result.\n\nANSWER: B."
"To design the model with the same dry resistance as the prototype and using the same medium, we need to consider the Reynolds number similarity. The Reynolds number (\\(Re\\)) is given by:\n\n\\[ Re = \\frac{\\rho u L}{\\mu} \\]\n\nwhere \\(\\rho\\) is the fluid density, \\(u\\) is the flow velocity, \\(L\\) is the characteristic length, and \\(\\mu\\) is the dynamic viscosity. When using the same medium, \\(\\rho\\) and \\(\\mu\\) remain constant.\n\nGiven the geometric scale (\\(L_r\\)) as 10, the characteristic length of the model is \\(L_m = \\frac{L_p}{10}\\), where \\(L_p\\) is the prototype length.\n\nThe flow velocity scale (\\(U_r\\)) can be found by ensuring the Reynolds number of the model (\\(Re_m\\)) and prototype (\\(Re_p\\)) are equal:\n\n\\[ Re_m = Re_p \\]\n\n\\[ \\frac{u_m \\cdot L_m}{\\nu} = \\frac{u_p \\cdot L_p}{\\nu} \\]\n\n\\[ u_m \\cdot \\frac{L_p}{10} = u_p \\cdot L_p \\]\n\n\\[ u_m = 10 \\cdot u_p \\]\n\nThe flow rate (\\(Q\\)) is related to velocity and cross-sectional area (\\(A\\)):\n\n\\[ Q = u \\cdot A \\]\n\nSince the area scales with the square of the length scale (\\(A \\sim L^2\\)), the area scale is \\(L_r^2 = 10^2 = 100\\). Thus, the flow rate scale (\\(Q_r\\)) considering both area and velocity scales is:\n\n\\[ Q_r = A_r \\cdot U_r = 100 \\cdot 10 = 1000 \\]\n\nGiven these choices:\nA. 10 \nB. 1 \nC. 0.1 \nD. 5\n\nThe correct choice for the flow rate scale is technically not listed; however, if considering the velocity alone before adjusting for area, it appears the flow rate in terms of flow volume relative simplicity is closest to scaling by \\(U_r\\), which is \\(10\\).\n\nANSWER: A"
