"To solve this problem, we need to calculate the coefficient of increase in seismic allowable bearing capacity. \n\nHere's a step-by-step breakdown:\n\n1. Given the ultimate bearing capacity under dynamic load is 380 kPa and the safety factor is 1.6.\n2. The dynamic allowable bearing capacity is calculated as: \n\n   \\[\n   \\text{Dynamic Allowable Bearing Capacity} = \\frac{\\text{Ultimate Bearing Capacity (Dynamic)}}{\\text{Safety Factor (Dynamic)}} = \\frac{380}{1.6}\n   \\]\n\n3. Given the ultimate bearing capacity under static load is 370 kPa and the safety factor is 2.0.\n4. The static allowable bearing capacity is calculated as: \n\n   \\[\n   \\text{Static Allowable Bearing Capacity} = \\frac{\\text{Ultimate Bearing Capacity (Static)}}{\\text{Safety Factor (Static)}} = \\frac{370}{2.0}\n   \\]\n\n5. The coefficient of increase in seismic allowable bearing capacity is determined by the ratio between dynamic and static allowable bearing capacity:\n\n   \\[\n   \\text{Coefficient of Increase} = \\frac{\\text{Dynamic Allowable Capacity}}{\\text{Static Allowable Capacity}}\n   \\]\n\n   Plug in the values:\n\n   \\[\n   \\text{Dynamic Allowable Capacity} = \\frac{380}{1.6} = 237.5\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   \\text{Static Allowable Capacity} = \\frac{370}{2.0} = 185\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   \\text{Coefficient of Increase} = \\frac{237.5}{185} \\approx 1.2838\n   \\]\n\nThe closest option to 1.2838 is 1.3.\n\nANSWER: C."
