"To determine the relationship between the bending moments that each beam section can withstand, we need to consider the geometry and the amount of reinforcement (As) in each section.\n\n1. **Section A**: Rectangular section, the most basic. It generally has the least capacity to resist bending because it lacks flanges that increase moment capacity by having more material further from the neutral axis.\n\n2. **Section B**: T-beam section. The flange at the top provides additional moment capacity as it places more material far from the neutral axis, improving its resistance to bending when compared to a rectangular section.\n\n3. **Section C**: I-beam section. This type of section is even more efficient in resisting bending moments because it has two large flanges far from the neutral axis, maximizing the section modulus. This section will generally have the highest bending moment capacity among those presented.\n\n4. **Section D**: Inverted T-beam section. It has a greater capacity than section B due to the additional bottom flange, resulting in more efficient use of material for bending moment resistance.\n\nConsidering these aspects, the order of bending moment capacity is:  \nMa < Mb < Md < Mc\n\nHowever, there is no option that strictly follows this order. The closest correct order available in the options is:\n\nANSWER: A. Ma < Mb < Mc < Md, which is not entirely accurate since D should be less than C, but it fits the options best considering typical characteristics."
