"To solve this problem using the third strength theory (also known as the maximum shear stress theory or Tresca criterion), we need to find the maximum shear stress in each state. The maximum shear stress (\\(\\tau_{\\text{max}}\\)) is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\tau_{\\text{max}} = \\frac{1}{2}(\\sigma_{\\text{max}} - \\sigma_{\\text{min}})\n\\]\n\nWhere \\(\\sigma_{\\text{max}}\\) and \\(\\sigma_{\\text{min}}\\) are the principal stresses.\n\n### State (1)\n- \\(\\sigma_1 = 120\\)\n- \\(\\sigma_2 = -120\\)\n- \\(\\sigma_3 = 0\\)\n\n\\[\n\\tau_{\\text{max}} = \\frac{1}{2}(120 - (-120)) = \\frac{1}{2}(240) = 120\n\\]\n\n### State (2)\n- \\(\\sigma_1 = 100\\)\n- \\(\\sigma_2 = 100\\)\n- \\(\\sigma_3 = 0\\)\n\n\\[\n\\tau_{\\text{max}} = \\frac{1}{2}(100 - 0) = 50\n\\]\n\n### State (3)\n- \\(\\sigma_1 = 150\\)\n- \\(\\sigma_2 = 100\\)\n- \\(\\sigma_3 = 60\\)\n\n\\[\n\\tau_{\\text{max}} = \\frac{1}{2}(150 - 60) = 45\n\\]\n\n### State (4)\n- \\(\\sigma_1 = 100\\)\n- \\(\\sigma_2 = 10\\)\n- \\(\\sigma_3 = 0\\)\n\n\\[\n\\tau_{\\text{max}} = \\frac{1}{2}(100 - 0) = 50\n\\]\n\n### Conclusion\nThe maximum shear stress occurs in State (1). \n\nANSWER: A"
