"To determine the hydraulic radius \\( R \\), we need to understand its definition in fluid mechanics. The hydraulic radius is defined as the ratio of the cross-sectional area of flow to the wetted perimeter. \n\nReviewing the options:\n- **A. The radius of the channel cross-section.** This is not correct. The hydraulic radius is not simply the radius of a circular cross-section.\n- **B. Ratio of cross-sectional area to wet circumference.** This matches the definition of the hydraulic radius, where the wetted perimeter is the same as the wetted circumference.\n- **C. Ratio of cross-sectional area to cross-sectional circumference.** This is incorrect because it refers to the total circumference, not just the wetted part.\n- **D. Ratio of cross-sectional area to water depth.** This is incorrect as it describes the hydraulic depth, not the hydraulic radius.\n\nANSWER: B"
"To determine the critical load \\( F_{cr} \\) for each column, we use Euler's formula for buckling:\n\n\\[\nF_{cr} = \\frac{\\pi^2 EI}{(KL)^2}\n\\]\n\nwhere:\n- \\( E \\) is the modulus of elasticity,\n- \\( I \\) is the moment of inertia,\n- \\( L \\) is the actual length of the column,\n- \\( K \\) is the effective length factor, depending on end conditions.\n\nThe given columns are:\n1. Column (a) is pinned-pinned:\n   - \\( K = 1 \\)\n   - \\( L = 5 \\, \\text{m} \\)\n\n2. Column (b) is fixed-free (cantilever):\n   - \\( K = 2 \\)\n   - \\( L = 3 \\, \\text{m} \\)\n\n3. Column (c) is fixed-fixed:\n   - \\( K = \\frac{1}{2} \\)\n   - \\( L = 6 \\, \\text{m} \\)\n\nCalculate the \\( (KL)^2 \\) for each column:\n- For (a): \\( (KL)^2 = (1 \\times 5)^2 = 25 \\)\n- For (b): \\( (KL)^2 = (2 \\times 3)^2 = 36 \\)\n- For (c): \\( (KL)^2 = \\left(\\frac{1}{2} \\times 6\\right)^2 = 9 \\)\n\nThus, the inverse order of critical loads is: Column (c), Column (a), Column (b).\n\nTherefore, the order of the critical loads from highest to lowest is:\n\n\\( F_{erc} > F_{era} > F_{erb} \\).\n\nANSWER: D."
