"To determine the thickness of the possible collapse, we can apply the concept of the collapse arch theory.\n\nThe collapse arch theory suggests that the shape of the collapse is an arch formed through the interaction of the forces acting on the rock mass. The key parameter here is the internal friction angle and the shape/dimensions of the karst cave.\n\nFor a square cave with side length \\(a = 6.0 \\, \\text{m} \\) and an internal friction angle \\( \\phi = 40^\\circ \\):\n\nThe potential thickness \\( h \\) of the collapse can be estimated using the following relationship considering an arch forming above the square cave:\n\n\\[ h = a \\cdot \\left(0.5 + \\tan(45^\\circ - \\phi/2)\\right) \\]\n\nFor \\( \\phi = 40^\\circ \\), \n\\[ h = 6.0 \\cdot \\left(0.5 + \\tan(45^\\circ - 20^\\circ)\\right) \\]\n\\[ h = 6.0 \\cdot \\left(0.5 + \\tan(25^\\circ)\\right) \\]\n\\[ h = 6.0 \\cdot \\left(0.5 + 0.4663\\right) \\]\n\\[ h \\approx 6.0 \\cdot 0.9663 \\]\n\\[ h \\approx 5.8 \\, \\text{m} \\]\n\nRounding to the nearest whole number gives 6m, which is not listed among the options.\n\nSince rounding discrepancies could occur given the multiple choice options, let's consider the closest practical option.\n\nThe nearest choice is:\n\nANSWER: A. 5m."
