"To determine which nations were created exclusively out of Russian land, we need to compare the maps of Europe before and after World War I.\n\n1. Identify territories under Russian control before WWI:\n   - Based on Map 1, regions directly part of Russia include Finland, Estonia, Latvia, Lithuania, and part of Poland.\n\n2. Examine new nations created after WWI:\n   - From Map 2, we can identify new cases such as Finland, Estonia, Latvia, Lithuania, and Poland being formed in the areas that were formerly Russian.\n\n3. Analyze the options provided:\n   - Option A: Finland, Estonia, Latvia, Lithuania. These were part of Russian territory before WWI.\n   - Option B: Czechoslovakia, Poland, East Prussia. Poland was partly from Russia, but Czechoslovakia and East Prussia were not exclusively from Russian land.\n   - Option C: Austria, Hungary. These were part of Austria-Hungary, not Russia.\n   - Option D: Estonia, Latvia, Lithuania, Poland. These nations were created in areas that include territory from Russia.\n\nSince the options are unclear whether to count Finland or Poland as \"exclusively,\" and given typical historical interpretations, Finland would also count due to its autonomous status as a Grand Duchy under Russia.\n\nIn conclusion, based on the typical historical understanding and given the map evidence, the nations carved exclusively out of Russian land primarily emphasizing this interpretation match:\n\nANSWER: A"
