"To determine which development most contributed to the popularity of the postal system in late nineteenth-century England, let's consider each option:\n\nA. Better living conditions: While this might have improved overall quality of life, it doesn't directly explain the postal system's popularity.\n\nB. Improved methods of steel production: This could have improved infrastructure but isn't directly linked to the use of postal services.\n\nC. Increased literacy rates: As more people learned to read and write, they were more likely to use the postal system to communicate, leading to its increased popularity.\n\nD. A decrease in leisure time for the middle class: This seems unlikely to promote postal system usage, as less leisure time would typically mean less time for writing letters.\n\nThe most likely factor for the increased popularity of the postal system is improved literacy, as this directly enabled more people to engage in written communication.\n\nANSWER: C"
