"To determine the truth of the statement, we need to calculate and compare the mean unemployment rate for October 2012 and the median rate for October 2011.\n\n**Step 1: Calculate the Mean Unemployment Rate for October 2012**\n\nUnemployment rates for October 2012:\n- California: 10.1\n- District of Columbia: 8.5\n- Florida: 8.5\n- Georgia: 8.7\n- Hawaii: 5.5\n- Idaho: 7.0\n- Illinois: 8.8\n- Indiana: 8.0\n- Kansas: 5.7\n- Mississippi: 8.9\n- Missouri: 6.9\n- Nebraska: 3.8\n- Nevada: 11.5\n- North Carolina: 9.3\n- Ohio: 6.9\n- Oklahoma: 5.3\n- South Carolina: 8.6\n- Texas: 6.6\n- Utah: 5.2\n\nSum = 134.3\nNumber of states = 19\nMean = 134.3 / 19 \u2248 7.06\n\n**Step 2: Calculate the Median Unemployment Rate for October 2011**\n\nSort the unemployment rates for October 2011:\n- Nebraska: 4.4\n- Utah: 6.2\n- Oklahoma: 6.3\n- Kansas: 6.6\n- Hawaii: 6.8\n- Missouri: 8.3\n- Ohio: 8.3\n- Idaho: 8.6\n- Indiana: 9.1\n- Georgia: 9.7\n- Illinois: 10.0\n- South Carolina: 10.0\n- Florida: 10.2\n- District of Columbia: 10.3\n- North Carolina: 10.6\n- Mississippi: 10.7\n- California: 11.5\n- Nevada: 13.4\n\nThere are 19 states. The 10th value in this ordered list is the median:\n9.7\n\n**Conclusion:**\n\n- Mean for October 2012: \u2248 7.06\n- Median for October 2011: 9.7\n\nSince 7.06 (mean for October 2012) is less than 9.7 (median for October 2011), the statement is true.\n\nANSWER: A. True."
"To identify the cost type for each department, we need to analyze the cost behavior with the change in production:\n\n1. **Department A:**\n   - Cost remains constant at $10,000 for both months despite production doubling.\n   - **Conclusion:** Fixed cost.\n\n2. **Department B:**\n   - Cost increases from $25,000 to $50,000 as production doubles.\n   - **Conclusion:** Variable cost.\n\n3. **Department C:**\n   - Cost increases from $35,000 to $45,000 with doubling production, but the increase is not directly proportional.\n   - **Conclusion:** Mixed cost (since there's both a fixed component and a variable component).\n\n4. **Department D:**\n   - Cost increases from $18,000 to $30,000 as production doubles, but not proportionally.\n   - **Conclusion:** Mixed cost.\n\n5. **Department E:**\n   - Cost doubles from $22,000 to $44,000 as production doubles.\n   - **Conclusion:** Variable cost.\n\nLet's match these findings to the available options.\n\n- **A:** Fixed; B: Mixed; C: Variable; D: Variable; E: Mixed\n- **B:** Mixed; B: Variable; C: Fixed; D: Mixed; E: Mixed\n- **C:** Mixed; B: Mixed; C: Fixed; D: Variable; E: Variable\n- **D:** Fixed; B: Variable; C: Mixed; D: Mixed; E: Variable\n\nANSWER: D"
