"To determine which option is not included in Kenichi Ohmae's 3C Model, we should consider the core elements of the model. The 3Cs are:\n\n1. **Company**: Focuses on the internal strengths and capabilities.\n2. **Customers**: Focuses on understanding and serving customer needs and desires.\n3. **Competition**: Focuses on analyzing and understanding competitors.\n\nThe options are:\n- A. Company (Who are we? What do we do well?).\n- B. Customers (Who do we serve? What do they want?).\n- C. Competition (Who are we competing against? What are their strengths and weaknesses?).\n- D. Innovation (Who should we compete with to innovate? In what aspect should we surpass them?).\n\nKenichi Ohmae's 3C Model emphasizes the balance between the company, customers, and competition as the key components of successful strategy. Innovation, while important, is not explicitly one of the three Cs in Ohmae's model.\n\nANSWER: D."
