"To calculate the number of equivalent units for conversion costs using the FIFO method, follow these steps:\n\n1. **Identify the beginning inventory:**\n   - Returns in process, March 1: 100 (30% complete)\n\n2. **Calculate the work done to complete beginning inventory:**\n   \\[\n   \\text{100 returns} \\times (100\\% - 30\\%) = 70 \\text{ equivalent units}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Identify new units started and completed in March:**\n   - Returns started in March: 1,600\n   - Ending inventory: 200 (90% complete)\n   - Units completed = Total units started + Beginning inventory - Ending inventory\n   \\[\n   = 1,600 + 100 - 200 = 1,500 \\text{ units}\n   \\]\n   - New units started and completed = Units completed - Beginning inventory completed\n   \\[\n   = 1,500 - 70 = 1,430 \\text{ units} \n   \\]\n\n4. **Calculate equivalent units for ending inventory:**\n   \\[\n   \\text{200 returns} \\times 90\\% = 180 \\text{ equivalent units}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Sum the equivalent units for conversion:**\n   \\[\n   \\text{Equivalent units} = 70 \\, (\\text{from beginning inventory}) + 1,430 \\, (\\text{new units}) + 180 \\, (\\text{ending inventory})\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   = 1,680 \\text{ equivalent units}\n   \\]\n\nANSWER: A. 1,680"
"To determine overapplied or underapplied overhead, we follow these steps:\n\n1. **Calculate the Predetermined Overhead Rate:**\n   \\[\n   \\text{Predetermined Overhead Rate} = \\frac{\\text{Budgeted Overhead}}{\\text{Budgeted Direct Labor Cost}}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\text{Predetermined Overhead Rate} = \\frac{300,000}{150,000} = 2.0\n   \\]\n\n2. **Calculate Applied Overhead:**\n   \\[\n   \\text{Applied Overhead} = \\text{Predetermined Overhead Rate} \\times \\text{Actual Direct Labor Cost}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\text{Applied Overhead} = 2.0 \\times 160,000 = 320,000\n   \\]\n\n3. **Determine Actual Overhead Incurred:**\n   \\[\n   \\text{Actual Overhead Incurred} = 310,000\n   \\]\n\n4. **Determine Overapplied or Underapplied Overhead:**\n   \\[\n   \\text{Overapplied/Underapplied Overhead} = \\text{Applied Overhead} - \\text{Actual Overhead Incurred}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\text{Overapplied/Underapplied Overhead} = 320,000 - 310,000 = 10,000 \\text{ (Overapplied)}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the overhead was overapplied by $10,000.\n\nANSWER: B."
"To calculate the budgeted cash disbursements for December, we need to consider payments for purchases in both November and December.\n\n### Step 1: Calculate Inventory Purchases\n- **Cost of Goods Sold (COGS)**: Since the gross profit rate is 30%, COGS is 70% of sales.\n- **Projected Sales for January** = $200,000\n- **December Purchases**: Since purchases in a month are for the next month's sales, December purchases = 70% of $200,000 = $140,000.\n\n### Step 2: Payment Structure\n- **November Purchases (for December sales)**: 70% of $170,000 (December sales) = $119,000\n  - 25% paid in November: 0.25 * $119,000 = $29,750\n  - 75% paid in December: 0.75 * $119,000 = $89,250\n\n- **December Purchases (for January sales)**: $140,000\n  - 25% paid in December: 0.25 * $140,000 = $35,000\n\n### Step 3: Total Cash Disbursements for December\n- Payments in December for November Purchases: $89,250\n- Payments in December for December Purchases: $35,000\n- **Total Cash Disbursements for December** = $89,250 + $35,000 = $124,250\n\nANSWER: D. $124,250"
