n.\n\nHence, the correct answer is:\nANSWER: B"
"To determine if the energy use values in the USA and the European Union (EU) come from the same distribution, we can use a chi-square test for independence.\n\n### Steps:\n\n1. **Construct the Contingency Table:**\n   - We have two categories: EU and USA energy use.\n   - Calculate the expected frequencies for each cell: \n     \\[\n     \\text{Expected frequency} = \\frac{(\\text{Row total} \\times \\text{Column total})}{\\text{Total total}}\n     \\]\n\n2. **Calculate the Chi-square Statistic:**\n   \\[\n   \\chi^2 = \\sum \\frac{(\\text{Observed frequency} - \\text{Expected frequency})^2}{\\text{Expected frequency}}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Determine Degrees of Freedom:**\n   - \\(df = (r - 1) \\times (c - 1)\\)\n   - Where \\(r\\) is the number of rows, and \\(c\\) is the number of columns.\n   - In this case, \\(r = 6\\) (for the years) and \\(c = 2\\) (for EU and USA).\n   - Thus, \\(df = (6-1) \\times (2-1) = 5\\).\n\n### Conclusion:\n\nUsing the chi-square test framework and the calculated degrees of freedom, we can proceed to compare the chi-square statistic with a critical value from the chi-square distribution table. The hypothesis being tested is whether the energy usage in the EU and the USA is independent (i.e., from the same distribution).\n\nANSWER: 5"
