"To determine whether the survey participants are a representative sample of the USA obese population, we will use a chi-square goodness-of-fit test. Here are the steps to perform the test:\n\n1. **Define Hypotheses:**\n   - Null Hypothesis (\\(H_0\\)): The sample distribution is consistent with the national distribution.\n   - Alternative Hypothesis (\\(H_a\\)): The sample distribution is not consistent with the national distribution.\n\n2. **Calculate Expected Frequencies:**\n   - For each age class, calculate the expected number of obese people based on the national averages and total sample size (499).\n\n3. **Calculate Test Statistic:**\n   - Use the formula: \n     \\[\n     \\chi^2 = \\sum \\frac{(O_i - E_i)^2}{E_i}\n     \\]\n     where \\(O_i\\) is the observed frequency and \\(E_i\\) is the expected frequency.\n\n4. **Degrees of Freedom:**\n   - The degrees of freedom (df) is the number of age classes minus 1. In this case, there are five age classes, so df = 5 - 1 = 4.\n\n5. **Significance Level:**\n   - Use a significance level (\\(\\alpha\\)) of 0.05.\n\n6. **Decision Rule:**\n   - Compare the calculated \\(\\chi^2\\) statistic to the critical value from the chi-square distribution table with df = 4 at \\(\\alpha = 0.05\\).\n\nTherefore, the degrees of freedom are 4.\n\nANSWER: B."
