"To determine whether the ages and net worth are independent, we need to calculate the degrees of freedom (df) for the chi-square test.\n\nThe formula for degrees of freedom in a contingency table is:\n\n\\[\n\\text{df} = (\\text{number of rows} - 1) \\times (\\text{number of columns} - 1)\n\\]\n\nHere, we have:\n\n- 2 age groups (rows): 17-25 and 26-30\n- 3 net worth categories (columns): 1-5 million, 6-24 million, \u226525 million\n\nApplying the formula:\n\n\\[\n\\text{df} = (2 - 1) \\times (3 - 1) = 1 \\times 2 = 2\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the degrees of freedom is 2, which corresponds to option A.\n\nANSWER: A"
"To find \\( P(\\text{musician is a female} \\cup \\text{is self-taught}) \\), we use the formula for the probability of a union of two events:\n\n\\[ P(A \\cup B) = P(A) + P(B) - P(A \\cap B) \\]\n\nWhere:\n- \\( A \\) is the event that the musician is female.\n- \\( B \\) is the event that the musician is self-taught.\n\nFirst, calculate \\( P(A) \\):\n- Total females = 72\n- Total musicians = 130\n- \\( P(A) = \\frac{72}{130} \\)\n\nNext, calculate \\( P(B) \\):\n- Total self-taught = 31\n- \\( P(B) = \\frac{31}{130} \\)\n\nThen, calculate \\( P(A \\cap B) \\):\n- Females who are self-taught = 12\n- \\( P(A \\cap B) = \\frac{12}{130} \\)\n\nNow, apply the formula:\n\\[ P(A \\cup B) = \\frac{72}{130} + \\frac{31}{130} - \\frac{12}{130} \\]\n\\[ P(A \\cup B) = \\frac{91}{130} \\]\n\nFinally, simplify:\n\\[ \\frac{91}{130} \\approx 0.7 \\]\n\nTherefore, the answer is:\n\nANSWER: D."
