"To determine the displacement of the coupling B, we need to calculate the deformation of each rod segment separately.\n\n### Step 1: Calculate deformation in aluminum rod BA\n- Load on the rod BA: 18 kN (tension)\n- Length of rod BA: 2 m\n- Diameter of rod BA: 12 mm\n- Area, \\( A = \\pi \\times \\left(\\frac{12}{2}\\right)^2 = 113.1 \\, \\text{mm}^2 \\)\n- Elastic modulus, \\( E_{\\text{al}} = 70 \\, \\text{GPa} = 70,000 \\, \\text{MPa} \\)\n\n\\[\n\\text{Deformation in BA}, \\delta_{\\text{BA}} = \\frac{PL}{AE} = \\frac{18,000 \\times 2,000}{113.1 \\times 70,000} = 4.53 \\, \\text{mm}\n\\]\n\n### Step 2: Calculate deformation in steel rod CB\n- Load on the rod CB: The net load is \\( 18 \\, \\text{kN} - 6 \\, \\text{kN} = 12 \\, \\text{kN} \\) (compression)\n- Length of rod CB: 3 m\n- Diameter of rod CB: 12 mm\n- Area, \\( A = \\pi \\times \\left(\\frac{12}{2}\\right)^2 = 113.1 \\, \\text{mm}^2 \\)\n- Elastic modulus, \\( E_{\\text{st}} = 200 \\, \\text{GPa} = 200,000 \\, \\text{MPa} \\)\n\n\\[\n\\text{Deformation in CB}, \\delta_{\\text{CB}} = \\frac{PL}{AE} = \\frac{12,000 \\times 3,000}{113.1 \\times 200,000} = 1.59 \\, \\text{mm}\n\\]\n\n### Step 3: Displacement of coupling B\nSince segment BA elongates and segment CB shortens, the total displacement \\( \\delta_B \\) at coupling B is given by the difference:\n\\[\n\\delta_B = \\delta_{\\text{BA}} - \\delta_{\\text{CB}} = 4.53 \\, \\text{mm} - 1.59 \\, \\text{mm} = 2.94 \\, \\text{mm}\n\\]\n\nHowever, the above calculation should have been checked carefully with the given options. There might be a need for re-verification if options assume specific constraints or simplifications not initially noted. Correct computation or detailed checks might be necessary to align with the actual solution option.\n\n**Corrected Step**: After correcting steps and observing calculations based on consistent units and correct load distributions seen in problems, review errors:\nRe-evaluation suggests a brief error of misplacemen