"To solve this problem, we need to determine the average normal strain developed in wire AC as the angle \\(\\theta\\) changes from \\(30^\\circ\\) to \\(30.2^\\circ\\).\n\n### Steps:\n\n1. **Initial and Final Lengths of AC:**\n\n   - Use trigonometry to determine the lengths of AC before and after the force is applied.\n   - The initial length of AC (\\(L_i\\)) when \\(\\theta = 30^\\circ\\):\n     \\[\n     L_i = 2 \\cdot 600 \\cdot \\sin(30^\\circ) = 2 \\cdot 600 \\cdot 0.5 = 600 \\text{ mm}\n     \\]\n   - The final length of AC (\\(L_f\\)) when \\(\\theta = 30.2^\\circ\\):\n     \\[\n     L_f = 2 \\cdot 600 \\cdot \\sin(30.2^\\circ)\n     \\]\n   - Calculate \\(\\sin(30.2^\\circ)\\) using a calculator:\n     \\[\n     \\sin(30.2^\\circ) \\approx 0.502\n     \\]\n   - Therefore:\n     \\[\n     L_f = 2 \\cdot 600 \\cdot 0.502 = 602.4 \\text{ mm}\n     \\]\n\n2. **Calculate the Strain:**\n\n   The average normal strain (\\(\\varepsilon\\)) is given by:\n   \\[\n   \\varepsilon = \\frac{L_f - L_i}{L_i} = \\frac{602.4 - 600}{600} = \\frac{2.4}{600} = 0.004\n   \\]\n\n3. **Express in Correct Form:**\n\n   The strain is \\(0.004\\), which is written as:\n   \\[ \n   4.00 \\times 10^{-3} \\text{ mm/mm}\n   \\]\n\nThe closest matching option to this result is:\n\nANSWER: D. 4.06*10^(-3) mm/mm."
