"To determine the modulus of elasticity, we first need to calculate the strain in the axial and circumferential directions and then use Hooke's Law for isotropic materials.\n\n### Axial Strain\nAxial strain (\\(\\varepsilon_{\\text{axial}}\\)) is given by the change in length divided by the original length:\n\n\\[\n\\varepsilon_{\\text{axial}} = \\frac{\\Delta L}{L_0} = \\frac{3 \\text{ mm}}{900 \\text{ mm}} = 0.00333\n\\]\n\n### Circumferential Strain\nCircumferential strain (\\(\\varepsilon_{\\text{circum}}\\)) is given by the change in circumference divided by the original circumference. The original circumference, \\(C_0\\), is given by:\n\n\\[\nC_0 = 2\\pi \\times 12.5 \\text{ mm} = 78.54 \\text{ mm}\n\\]\n\nThus, the circumferential strain is:\n\n\\[\n\\varepsilon_{\\text{circum}} = \\frac{-0.09 \\text{ mm}}{78.54 \\text{ mm}} = -0.00115\n\\]\n\nNegative because the circumference decreases.\n\n### Relating Strains and Modulus of Elasticity\nHooke\u2019s Law for axial and circumferential strains with Poisson's ratio (\\(\\nu\\)) for isotropic materials:\n- Axial strain: \\(\\varepsilon_{\\text{axial}} = \\frac{\\sigma}{E} - \\nu \\varepsilon_{\\text{circum}}\\)\n\nGiven:\n- Force \\(F = 40 \\text{ kN} = 40,000 \\text{ N}\\),\n- Cross-sectional area \\(A\\) (based on thickness), the approximate area for thin wall is:\n\n\\[\nt = 10 \\text{ mm}, \\quad A = \\pi \\times d \\times t = \\pi \\times 12.5 \\text{ mm} \\times 10 \\text{ mm} = 392.7 \\text{ mm}^2 = 392.7 \\times 10^{-6} \\text{ m}^2\n\\]\n\nThus, stress \\(\\sigma\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\sigma = \\frac{F}{A} = \\frac{40,000 \\text{ N}}{392.7 \\times 10^{-6} \\text{ m}^2} = 101.87 \\text{ MPa}\n\\]\n\nAssuming a typical Poisson's ratio \\(\\nu\\) around 0.3 for metals:\n\n\\[\n\\varepsilon_{\\text{axial}} = \\frac{\\sigma}{E} - \\nu \\varepsilon_{\\text{circum}}\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(E\\):\n\n\\[\n0.00333 = \\frac{101.87 \\times 10^6}{E} - 0.3(-0.00115)\n\\]\n\n\\[\n0.00333 + 0.000345 = \\frac{101.87 \\times 10^6}{E}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n0.003675 = \\frac{101.87 \\times 10^6}{E}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nE = \\frac{101.87 \\times 10^6}{0.003675} = 27.73 \\text{ GPa}\n\\]\n\nThe closest option to this calculation is 25.3 GPa.\n\n### Conclusion\nANSWER: A"
"To determine which polymer features the lowest tensile yield point, we can consider the following factors:\n\n1. **Degree of Polymerization**: Generally, the higher the molecular weight (degree of polymerization), the stronger the polymer, increasing the tensile yield point.\n\n2. **Architecture**: Linear polymers typically pack more closely than branched polymers, leading to higher strength and hence a higher tensile yield point.\n\n3. **% Crystalline**: Higher crystallinity generally leads to greater strength due to better packing in the crystalline regions and increased intermolecular forces.\n\nNow, let's analyze each option:\n\n- **(a)**: Low degree of polymerization, linear, low crystallinity.\n- **(b)**: Low degree of polymerization, branched, low crystallinity.\n- **(c)**: Medium degree of polymerization, linear, medium crystallinity.\n- **(d)**: High degree of polymerization, branched, medium crystallinity.\n- **(e)**: High degree of polymerization, linear, high crystallinity.\n\nGiven these properties, option (b) has the lowest degree of polymerization combined with a branched structure and low crystallinity, indicating it is the weakest and likely to have the lowest tensile yield point.\n\nANSWER: B"
