"To determine the ductile-to-brittle transition temperature at which the impact energy is 70 J, we need to find the temperature where this energy value falls between two recorded data points.\n\nFrom the table:\n\n- At \\(-50^\\circ\\text{C}\\), the impact energy is 73.1 J.\n- At \\(-65^\\circ\\text{C}\\), the impact energy is 66.0 J.\n\nThe target energy of 70 J lies between these two points. We'll perform a linear interpolation to estimate the temperature:\n\n1. **Data Points for Interpolation:**\n   - \\((-50, 73.1)\\)\n   - \\((-65, 66.0)\\)\n\n2. **Linear Interpolation Formula:**\n\n   \\[\n   T = T_1 + \\left( \\frac{E - E_1}{E_2 - E_1} \\right) (T_2 - T_1)\n   \\]\n\n   Where:\n   - \\( T \\) is the temperature we want to find,\n   - \\( T_1 = -50^\\circ\\text{C}, E_1 = 73.1 \\text{ J} \\),\n   - \\( T_2 = -65^\\circ\\text{C}, E_2 = 66.0 \\text{ J} \\),\n   - \\( E = 70 \\text{ J} \\).\n\n3. **Plugging in the Values:**\n\n   \\[\n   T = -50 + \\left( \\frac{70 - 73.1}{66.0 - 73.1} \\right) (-65 + 50)\n   \\]\n   \n   \\[\n   T = -50 + \\left( \\frac{-3.1}{-7.1} \\right) (-15)\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   T = -50 + (0.4366 \\times (-15))\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   T = -50 - 6.549\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   T \\approx -56.5^\\circ\\text{C}\n   \\]\n\nSince the closest option is \\(-55^\\circ\\text{C}\\), the answer is:\n\nANSWER: A"
"To determine the size of the square bearing plate \\(A'\\), we need to follow these steps:\n\n1. **Calculate the reactions at supports A and B:**\n\n   The beam is subject to a uniformly distributed load (UDL) and a point load \\(P\\).\n\n   - The total UDL is \\(40 \\, \\text{kN/m} \\times 3 \\, \\text{m} = 120 \\, \\text{kN}\\).\n   \n   - The total point load \\(P\\) is \\(100 \\, \\text{kN}\\).\n\n   - The sum of vertical forces:\n     \\[\n     R_A + R_B = 120 \\, \\text{kN} + 100 \\, \\text{kN} = 220 \\, \\text{kN}\n     \\]\n\n   - Taking moments about A to solve for \\(R_B\\):\n     \\[\n     R_B \\times 6 \\, \\text{m} = 120 \\, \\text{kN} \\times 1.5 \\, \\text{m} + 100 \\, \\text{kN} \\times 6 \\, \\text{m}\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     R_B \\times 6 \\, \\text{m} = 180 \\, \\text{kN} \\cdot \\text{m} + 600 \\, \\text{kN} \\cdot \\text{m}\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     R_B = \\frac{780}{6} = 130 \\, \\text{kN}\n     \\]\n\n   - Now, calculating \\(R_A\\):\n     \\[\n     R_A = 220 \\, \\text{kN} - 130 \\, \\text{kN} = 90 \\, \\text{kN}\n     \\]\n\n2. **Determine the required area of the bearing plate:**\n\n   The allowable bearing stress \\((\\sigma_b)_{\\text{allow}} = 1.5 \\, \\text{MPa} = 1.5 \\, \\text{kN/mm}^2\\).\n\n   Using the equation for bearing stress:\n   \\[\n   (\\sigma_b) = \\frac{R_A}{A} \\Rightarrow A = \\frac{R_A}{(\\sigma_b)_{\\text{allow}}}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   A = \\frac{90 \\, \\text{kN}}{1.5 \\, \\text{kN/mm}^2} = 60 \\, \\text{mm}^2\n   \\]\n\n3. **Determine the dimensions of the square plate:**\n\n   Let the side of the square plate be \\(x\\). Then,\n   \\[\n   x^2 = 60 \\times 10^3 \\, \\text{mm}^2 \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad x = \\sqrt{60 \\times 10^3} \\approx 245 \\, \\text{mm}\n   \\]\n\n   Rounding to the nearest mm, the possible option closest to this dimension is **300 mm**.\n\nANSWER: A"
"To determine whether a grain boundary is depicted in the image, let's consider the following:\n\n1. **Definition of a Grain Boundary**: A grain boundary is the interface between two grains, or crystallites, in a polycrystalline material. It is a region where the crystalline structure is disrupted.\n\n2. **Observation of the Image**: In the image, there is a distinct region where the orientation or arrangement of atoms changes. The green and gray dots represent atoms in different orientations, indicating two distinct crystalline regions meeting.\n\n3. **Detection**: The area where these two differently oriented crystal structures meet is characterized by a misalignment, which is indicative of a grain boundary.\n\nBased on the definition and the observation from the image that shows two differently oriented regions meeting, it is clear that a grain boundary is depicted.\n\nANSWER: A."
