"To determine the required diameter of the bolts, we need to calculate the shear force exerted on each bolt and the corresponding shear stress.\n\n### Given:\n- Total applied force, \\( F = 80 \\text{ kN} \\).\n- Failure shear stress, \\( \\tau_{\\text{fail}} = 350 \\text{ MPa} \\).\n- Factor of Safety, \\( \\text{F.S.} = 2.5 \\).\n\n### Steps:\n\n1. **Calculate Shear Force on One Bolt:**\n   Since the force is distributed equally between the two bolts, the shear force on each bolt is:\n   \\[\n   F_{\\text{bolt}} = \\frac{F}{2} = \\frac{80 \\times 10^3 \\text{ N}}{2} = 40 \\times 10^3 \\text{ N}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Determine Allowable Shear Stress:**\n   The allowable shear stress considering the factor of safety is:\n   \\[\n   \\tau_{\\text{allow}} = \\frac{\\tau_{\\text{fail}}}{\\text{F.S.}} = \\frac{350 \\text{ MPa}}{2.5} = 140 \\text{ MPa}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculate Required Bolt Diameter:**\n   The shear stress on each bolt can be given by:\n   \\[\n   \\tau = \\frac{F_{\\text{bolt}}}{A_{\\text{bolt}}}\n   \\]\n   where \\( A_{\\text{bolt}} = \\frac{\\pi d^2}{4} \\) is the cross-sectional area of the bolt.\n\n   Setting \\( \\tau = \\tau_{\\text{allow}} \\), we have:\n   \\[\n   140 \\times 10^6 = \\frac{40 \\times 10^3}{\\frac{\\pi d^2}{4}}\n   \\]\n\n   Solving for \\( d \\):\n   \\[\n   d^2 = \\frac{4 \\times 40 \\times 10^3}{140 \\times 10^6 \\times \\pi}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   d^2 = \\frac{160 \\times 10^3}{140 \\times 10^6 \\times \\pi}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   d = \\sqrt{\\frac{160 \\times 10^3}{140 \\times 10^6 \\times \\pi}}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   d \\approx 0.0153 \\text{ m} = 15.3 \\text{ mm}\n   \\]\n\nThe required diameter of the bolts is approximately 15.3 mm.\n\n### Thus, the answer is:\nANSWER: C. 15.3 mm."
