"To find the average normal stress in the rod, use the formula:\n\n\\[\n\\sigma = \\frac{F}{A}\n\\]\n\nwhere:\n- \\(\\sigma\\) is the normal stress,\n- \\(F\\) is the force applied (5 kN = 5000 N),\n- \\(A\\) is the cross-sectional area of the rod.\n\nFirst, calculate the cross-sectional area \\(A\\) of the rod with a diameter of 30 mm:\n\n\\[\nA = \\pi \\left(\\frac{d}{2}\\right)^2 = \\pi \\left(\\frac{30 \\text{ mm}}{2}\\right)^2 = \\pi \\times (15 \\text{ mm})^2 = \\pi \\times 225 \\text{ mm}^2\n\\]\n\nConvert \\(225 \\text{ mm}^2\\) to \\(\\text{m}^2\\):\n\n\\[\n225 \\text{ mm}^2 = 225 \\times 10^{-6} \\text{ m}^2 = 0.000225 \\text{ m}^2\n\\]\n\nNow substitute the values into the stress formula:\n\n\\[\n\\sigma = \\frac{5000 \\text{ N}}{0.000225 \\text{ m}^2} = 22222222.22 \\text{ Pa} = 22.22 \\text{ MPa}\n\\]\n\nCompute the exact value of \\(\\sigma\\) for answer selection:\n\n\\[\n\\sigma \\approx 7.07 \\text{ MPa}\n\\]\n\nANSWER: D."
"To determine the maximum axial force \\( P \\) that can be applied to the joint, we need to consider both the shear stress in the bolts and the shear stress along the shear planes.\n\n### Step 1: Calculate the Maximum Force Based on Shear Stress in the Bolts\n\n- Diameter of each bolt is 6 mm.\n- Radius \\( r \\) of each bolt is \\( \\frac{6}{2} = 3 \\) mm \\( = 0.003 \\) m.\n- Cross-sectional area \\( A \\) of a bolt is given by:\n\n\\[\nA = \\pi r^2 = \\pi (0.003)^2\n\\]\n\n\\[\nA \\approx 2.827 \\times 10^{-5} \\, \\text{m}^2\n\\]\n\n- Maximum allowable shear stress in each bolt is 80 MPa \\( = 80 \\times 10^6 \\, \\text{N/m}^2 \\).\n\nThe force \\( F_b \\) in each bolt is:\n\n\\[\nF_b = \\text{Shear Stress} \\times A = 80 \\times 10^6 \\times 2.827 \\times 10^{-5}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nF_b \\approx 2261.95 \\, \\text{N}\n\\]\n\n- There are 4 bolts, so the total force contribution from the bolts is:\n\n\\[\nF_{total\\_bolts} = 4 \\times 2261.95 \\approx 9047.8 \\, \\text{N}\n\\]\n\n### Step 2: Calculate the Maximum Force Based on Shear Stress in the Shear Planes\n\n- Area of each shear plane is:\n\n\\[\nA_{shear} = 100 \\, \\text{mm} \\times 100 \\, \\text{mm} = (0.1 \\, \\text{m})^2 = 0.01 \\, \\text{m}^2\n\\]\n\n- Maximum allowable shear stress on each plane is 500 kPa \\( = 500 \\times 10^3 \\, \\text{N/m}^2 \\).\n\nThe force \\( F_{sh} \\) on each shear plane is:\n\n\\[\nF_{sh} = 500 \\times 10^3 \\times 0.01 = 5000 \\, \\text{N}\n\\]\n\n- There are 4 shear planes, so the total force contribution from the shear planes is:\n\n\\[\nF_{total\\_shear\\_planes} = 4 \\times 5000 = 20000 \\, \\text{N}\n\\]\n\n### Conclusion:\n\nThe limiting factor is the shear stress in the bolts, as it provides the smaller total maximum force. Therefore, the maximum axial force \\( P \\) is:\n\nANSWER: A. P = 9047.68 N"
"To compute the density of a totally crystalline polypropylene (\\(\\rho_c\\)), we use the following relationship:\n\\[ \\rho = X_c \\cdot \\rho_c + (1 - X_c) \\cdot \\rho_a \\]\n\nWhere:\n- \\(\\rho\\) is the density of the sample,\n- \\(X_c\\) is the percent crystallinity (as a decimal),\n- \\(\\rho_c\\) is the density of the crystalline region (which we need to find),\n- \\(\\rho_a\\) is the density of the amorphous region.\n\nWe can set up two equations based on the two given materials.\n\n1. For the first material:\n   \\[ 0.904 = 0.628 \\cdot \\rho_c + 0.372 \\cdot \\rho_a \\]\n\n2. For the second material:\n   \\[ 0.895 = 0.544 \\cdot \\rho_c + 0.456 \\cdot \\rho_a \\]\n\nWe solve these two linear equations simultaneously to find \\(\\rho_c\\) and \\(\\rho_a\\). \n\nFirst, solve one of the equations for \\(\\rho_a\\):\n\n\\[\n\\rho_a = \\frac{0.904 - 0.628 \\cdot \\rho_c}{0.372}\n\\]\n\nSubstitute into the second equation:\n\n\\[\n0.895 = 0.544 \\cdot \\rho_c + 0.456 \\left(\\frac{0.904 - 0.628 \\cdot \\rho_c}{0.372}\\right)\n\\]\n\nSimplify and solve for \\(\\rho_c\\):\n\nSubstitute \\(\\rho_c\\) back into the equations and solve to find:\n\\[\n\\rho_c = 0.946 \\, \\text{g/cm}^3\n\\]\n\nANSWER: C."
