"To compute the minimum required thickness for the aluminum tube, we can use the thin-walled pressure vessel formula for cylindrical tubes:\n\n\\[\nt = \\frac{P \\cdot r}{\\sigma_y / \\text{FS}}\n\\]\n\nwhere:\n- \\( t \\) is the thickness of the tube,\n- \\( P \\) is the internal pressure difference,\n- \\( r \\) is the radius of the tube,\n- \\( \\sigma_y \\) is the yield strength,\n- FS is the factor of safety.\n\nGiven:\n- \\( P = 10.13 - 0.1013 = 10.0287 \\) MPa,\n- \\( r = 65 \\) mm \\( = 0.065 \\) m,\n- \\( \\sigma_y = 275 \\) MPa,\n- FS = 3.5.\n\nSubstitute the values into the formula:\n\n\\[\nt = \\frac{10.0287 \\times 0.065}{275 / 3.5}\n\\]\n\nCalculating \\( \\sigma_y / \\text{FS} \\):\n\n\\[\n\\sigma_y / \\text{FS} = \\frac{275}{3.5} = 78.57 \\text{ MPa}\n\\]\n\nNow, calculate \\( t \\):\n\n\\[\nt = \\frac{10.0287 \\times 0.065}{78.57}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nt = \\frac{0.6518655}{78.57}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nt \\approx 0.00830 \\text{ m} = 8.30 \\text{ mm}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the minimum required thickness is approximately 8.30 mm.\n\nANSWER: C. 8.30 mm."
"To determine the change in volume of the pipe, we need to consider how the axial force affects the pipe material.\n\n1. **Axial Deformation**: The axial deformation (change in length) of the pipe, \\(\\Delta L\\), due to the force \\(P\\) is given by:\n   \\[\n   \\Delta L = \\frac{PL}{AE}\n   \\]\n   where \\(A\\) is the cross-sectional area of the pipe.\n\n2. **Volume Change Due to Axial Strain**: The axial strain \\(\\varepsilon\\) is \\(\\Delta L/L = \\frac{P}{AE}\\), leading to a volume change:\n   \\[\n   \\Delta V_{\\text{axial}} = A \\cdot \\Delta L = \\frac{PL}{E}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Lateral Deformation**: Due to Poisson's effect, the diameter (and thus the cross-sectional area) also changes. Given Poisson's ratio \\(v\\), the lateral strain is \\(-v \\cdot \\varepsilon = -v \\cdot \\frac{P}{AE}\\). This changes both the inner and outer radii, causing a change in volume:\n   \\[\n   \\Delta A = A \\cdot (1 - 2v \\cdot \\frac{P}{AE}) - A\n   \\]\n   Since \\(\\Delta A/A = -2v \\cdot \\frac{P}{AE}\\), the total lateral volume change is:\n   \\[\n   \\Delta V_{\\text{lateral}} = -V \\cdot 2v \\cdot \\frac{P}{AE} = -2v \\frac{PL}{E}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Total Volume Change**:\n   Collectively, the changes are:\n   \\[\n   \\Delta V = \\Delta V_{\\text{axial}} + \\Delta V_{\\text{lateral}} = \\frac{PL}{E} - 2v \\frac{PL}{E} = \\frac{PL}{E} (1 - 2v)\n   \\]\n\nThus, the change in volume of the material due to the axial force \\(P\\) is captured by option C.\n\nANSWER: C"
