"To find the modulus of elasticity \\( E \\), we use the formula:\n\n\\[\nE = \\frac{\\sigma}{\\epsilon}\n\\]\n\nwhere \\( \\sigma \\) is the stress and \\( \\epsilon \\) is the strain.\n\nFirst, calculate the stress (\\( \\sigma \\)):\n\\[\n\\sigma = \\frac{P}{A} = \\frac{8000 \\, \\text{lb}}{0.7 \\, \\text{in}^2} = 11428.57 \\, \\text{psi}\n\\]\n\nNext, calculate the strain (\\( \\epsilon \\)):\n\\[\n\\epsilon = \\frac{\\Delta L}{L} = \\frac{0.002 \\, \\text{in}}{5 \\, \\text{in}} = 0.0004\n\\]\n\nNow, calculate the modulus of elasticity (\\( E \\)):\n\\[\nE = \\frac{\\sigma}{\\epsilon} = \\frac{11428.57 \\, \\text{psi}}{0.0004} = 28571.43 \\, \\text{ksi}\n\\]\n\nMatching with the given options, \\( E \\) corresponds to:\n\\[ \n\\text{B. } 28.6 \\times 10^3 \\, \\text{ksi}\n\\]\n\nANSWER: B."
"To determine the elastic elongation and decrease in diameter of rod BC, we will use the following formulas:\n\n1. **Elongation (\\(\\Delta L\\))**: \n   \\[\n   \\Delta L = \\frac{PL}{AE}\n   \\]\n   where:\n   - \\(P = 150 \\, \\text{kN} = 150,000 \\, \\text{N}\\)\n   - \\(L = 1 \\, \\text{m}\\)\n   - \\(A = \\frac{\\pi d^2}{4}\\), with \\(d = 40 \\, \\text{mm} = 0.04 \\, \\text{m}\\)\n   - \\(E = 200 \\, \\text{GPa} = 200 \\times 10^9 \\, \\text{Pa}\\) (modulus of elasticity for A-36 steel)\n\n2. **Decrease in diameter (\\(\\Delta d\\))** using Poisson's ratio (\\(\\nu\\)):\n   \\[\n   \\Delta d = -\\nu \\frac{P}{AE} \\times d\n   \\]\n   where \\(\\nu = 0.3\\) for A-36 steel.\n\n**Steps:**\n\n1. **Calculate the cross-sectional area \\(A\\)**:\n   \\[\n   A = \\frac{\\pi (0.04)^2}{4} = 1.25664 \\times 10^{-3} \\, \\text{m}^2\n   \\]\n\n2. **Calculate the elongation (\\(\\Delta L\\))**:\n   \\[\n   \\Delta L = \\frac{150,000 \\times 1}{1.25664 \\times 10^{-3} \\times 200 \\times 10^9} = 0.000933 \\, \\text{m} = 0.933 \\, \\text{mm}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the decrease in diameter (\\(\\Delta d\\))**:\n   \\[\n   \\Delta d = -0.3 \\times \\frac{150,000}{1.25664 \\times 10^{-3} \\times 200 \\times 10^9} \\times 0.04 = -9.55 \\times 10^{-6} \\, \\text{m} = -9.55 \\, \\times 10^{-3} \\, \\text{mm}\n   \\]\n\n**ANSWER: D**"
"To determine the shear strain \\(\\gamma_{xy}\\) at corner D, we need to calculate the change in the right angle at that corner due to the distortion.\n\nShear strain is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\gamma_{xy} = \\tan(\\theta) \\approx \\theta\n\\]\n\nfor small angles, where \\(\\theta\\) is the change in angle from 90 degrees.\n\n### Steps:\n\n1. **Original Right Angle at D:**\n   - Originally D was a right angle (90 degrees).\n\n2. **Horizontal Deformation:**\n   - Point A moves 3 mm to the right.\n   - Point B moves 5 mm to the right.\n   \n3. **Vertical Deformation:**\n   - Point A moves 2 mm down.\n   - Point B moves 4 mm up.\n\n4. **Calculate Shear Strain Components:**\n   - The change in angle \\(\\theta\\) is largely due to these movements.\n   - Consider the horizontal movement: \\(\\Delta x = AB - CD = 5 \\, \\text{mm} - 3 \\, \\text{mm} = 2 \\, \\text{mm}\\).\n   - Consider the vertical movement: \\(\\Delta y = 4 \\, \\text{mm} - 2 \\, \\text{mm} = 2 \\, \\text{mm}\\).\n\n5. **Calculate \\(\\theta\\):**\n   - The actual change around D can be approximated using the displacement assuming only shear effect:\n   \\[\n   \\theta \\approx \\frac{\\Delta x + \\Delta y}{Length} = \\frac{2 \\, \\text{mm} + 2 \\, \\text{mm}}{400 \\, \\text{mm}}\n   \\]\n   - Simplifying,\n   \\[\n   \\theta \\approx \\frac{4 \\, \\text{mm}}{400 \\, \\text{mm}} = 0.01 \\, \\text{rad} = 10 \\times 10^{-3} \\, \\text{rad}\n   \\]\n\nNote: Adding some approximate correction factors can offer better insight on practical strain measure:\n\n\\[\n\\gamma_{xy} = \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{\\Delta y_A + \\Delta y_B}{400}\\right) + \\tan^{-1}\\left(\\frac{\\Delta x_B - \\Delta x_A}{300}\\right)\n\\]\n\nCalculating simplifies greatly near \\(\\frac{4}{300} + \\frac{2}{400} \\approx .011666\\).\n\nAfter approximations and non-exact change preference over maximum strains,\n\nThe closest option choice, which adjusts to preference is:\n\n\\[ ANSWER: \\, C. \\]"
