"To find the required minimum dimension of the square bearing plate at C, we need to:\n\n1. **Calculate the total vertical reaction at A (C):**\n\n   Given forces on the truss:\n   \n   - Total vertical loads = 300 + 300 + 300 + 300 + 200 = 1400 kip. \n\n   Assume the sum of moments about B is zero (using counterclockwise as positive):\n\n   \\[\n   \\sum M_B = 150 \\times 48 + 6 \\times 300 \\left(\\frac{6 + 12 + 18 + 24}{4}\\right) + 200 \\times 6 - R_A \\times 36 = 0 \n   \\]\n\n   Solving for \\( R_A \\):\n\n   \\[\n   R_A \\times 36 = 150 \\times 48 + 300 \\times 54 + 200 \\times 6 \n   \\]\n   \n   \\[\n   R_A = \\frac{7200 + 16200 + 1200}{36} \n   \\]\n   \n   \\[\n   R_A = 670 \\text{ kip}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Determine the allowable bearing stress:**\n\n   - Given \\((\\sigma_\\text{fail})_b = 4 \\text{ ksi}\\).\n   - Factor of safety = 2. \n\n   \\[\n   \\sigma_\\text{allowable} = \\frac{4}{2} = 2 \\text{ ksi}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the required area of the bearing plate:**\n\n   \\[\n   \\text{Area required} = \\frac{R_A}{\\sigma_\\text{allowable}} = \\frac{670}{2} = 335 \\text{ in}^2\n   \\]\n\n4. **Determine the side length of the bearing plate:**\n\n   Since the plate is square:\n\n   \\[\n   \\text{Side length} = \\sqrt{335} \\approx 18.31 \\text{ in}\n   \\]\n\n5. **Select the nearest option:**\n\n   Looking at the options to the nearest 1/16 in, the closest side length greater than 18.31 in to ensure safe design is 19.0625 in.\n\nANSWER: D."
