"To find the maximum axial force \\( P \\) that can be applied to the turnbuckle without exceeding the allowable stress, we first need to calculate the cross-sectional area at section \\( a-a \\).\n\n### Step 1: Determine Cross-Sectional Area\n\n1. The minimum cross-section where the turnbuckle can fail is the net section at the bolts.\n2. The nominal area is reduced by the diameter of the bolt holes for two bolts. \n\nGiven:\n- Each bolt shank has a diameter of 0.5 in.\n- The body dimension perpendicular to the section is 1 in.\n- The thickness of the section is 0.25 in.\n\nThe net width = Total width - 2 \\(\\times\\) diameter of bolt holes:\n\\[ \\text{Net width} = 1 \\, \\text{in} - 2 \\times 0.5 \\, \\text{in} = 0 \\, \\text{in} \\]\n\n### Step 2: Calculate Maximum Force \\( P \\)\n\nThe maximum stress in section \\( a-a \\) is constrained to 15 ksi. The relationship between stress and axial load is given by:\n\n\\[ \\sigma = \\frac{P}{A} \\]\n\nWhere:\n- \\( \\sigma \\) is the stress (15 ksi),\n- \\( P \\) is the maximum force we want to find,\n- \\( A \\) is the cross-sectional area (0 square inches due to incorrect calculation).\n\nCorrection:\n- To calculate the correct net area, the section across the bolts has additional material: the total area \\( A_{\\text{section}} = (\\text{Width}) \\times (\\text{Thickness}) = 1 \\times 0.25 = 0.25 \\, \\text{sq in} \\).\n\nAssuming:\n\\[ A_{\\text{net}} = 0.25 \\, \\text{sq in} \\]\n\nSet the allowable stress and solve for \\( P \\):\n\n\\[ 15,000 \\, \\text{psi} = \\frac{P}{0.25 \\, \\text{in}^2} \\]\n\n\\[ P = 15,000 \\, \\text{psi} \\times 0.25 \\, \\text{in}^2 \\]\n\n\\[ P = 3,750 \\, \\text{lb} = 3.75 \\, \\text{kips} \\]\n\nThis calculation doesn\u2019t match any options; reevaluate the calculation process or problem constraints.\n\nAfter recalculation and a valid approach, based on the given question, the actual intended correct problem should yield the result appropriate to the options. If solving for incor