"To determine if it's possible to have a poly(vinyl chloride) homopolymer with a degree of polymerization of 1120, we can calculate the average molecular weight (\\( \\overline{M}_n \\)) using the mole fraction (\\( x_i \\)) and molecular weight range (\\( M_i \\)).\n\n1. **Calculate \\( \\overline{M}_n \\)**:\n\n\\[ \\overline{M}_n = \\sum (x_i \\cdot \\text{average } M_i) \\]\n\nEstimate the average molecular weight for each range:\n\n- \\(8,000-20,000 \\rightarrow 14,000\\)\n- \\(20,000-32,000 \\rightarrow 26,000\\)\n- \\(32,000-44,000 \\rightarrow 38,000\\)\n- \\(44,000-56,000 \\rightarrow 50,000\\)\n- \\(56,000-68,000 \\rightarrow 62,000\\)\n- \\(68,000-80,000 \\rightarrow 74,000\\)\n- \\(80,000-92,000 \\rightarrow 86,000\\)\n\n\\[ \\overline{M}_n = (0.05 \\times 14,000) + (0.15 \\times 26,000) + (0.21 \\times 38,000) + (0.28 \\times 50,000) + (0.18 \\times 62,000) + (0.10 \\times 74,000) + (0.03 \\times 86,000) \\]\n\n\\[ \\overline{M}_n = 700 + 3900 + 7980 + 14000 + 11160 + 7400 + 2580 \\]\n\n\\[ \\overline{M}_n = 47,720 \\, \\text{g/mol} \\]\n\n2. **Calculate degree of polymerization (DP)**:\n\nThe degree of polymerization (DP) is given by:\n\n\\[ DP = \\frac{\\overline{M}_n}{M_0} \\]\n\nwhere \\(M_0\\) is the molecular weight of the repeating unit of PVC (with empirical formula C2H3Cl), which is approximately 62.5 g/mol.\n\n\\[ DP = \\frac{47,720}{62.5} \\approx 763.52 \\]\n\nThis value is significantly less than the target degree of polymerization of 1120.\n\nTherefore, with the given molecular weight distribution, it is not possible to have a degree of polymerization of 1120.\n\nANSWER: B. No."
