"To determine the force \\( P \\) applied to the bolt, we can calculate the contributions from both the shear and tension failures:\n\n1. **Shear Failure at BC:**\n   - Shear stress (\\(\\tau\\)) at BC is given as 4.5 MPa.\n   - The cylinder diameter is 25 mm, and the depth is 30 mm.\n   - Surface area \\( A_{BC} \\) of the cylindrical surface is:\n     \\[\n     A_{BC} = \\pi \\times \\text{diameter} \\times \\text{height} = \\pi \\times 25 \\times 30 \\times 10^{-6} = 0.002356 \\, \\text{m}^2\n     \\]\n   - Shear force \\( F_{\\text{shear}} \\) is:\n     \\[\n     F_{\\text{shear}} = \\tau \\times A_{BC} = 4.5 \\times 10^6 \\times 0.002356 = 10.602 \\, \\text{kN}\n     \\]\n\n2. **Tension Failure at AB:**\n   - Tension stress (\\(\\sigma\\)) is 3 MPa.\n   - This surface is a frustum with a slant height (hypotenuse) of 50 mm.\n   - The base of the frustum is 25 mm, the top is 25 + 2 \\times 50 mm = 125 mm (total height is negligible due to the angles).\n   - The average circumference for the slant height can be approximated as:\n     \\[\n     \\text{Average circumference} = \\frac{\\pi \\times (25 + 125)}{2} = 75 \\, \\text{mm}\n     \\]\n   - The area \\( A_{AB} \\) of the frustum surface is:\n     \\[\n     A_{AB} = 75 \\times 50 \\times 10^{-6} = 0.00375 \\, \\text{m}^2\n     \\]\n   - Tension force \\( F_{\\text{tension}} \\) is:\n     \\[\n     F_{\\text{tension}} = \\sigma \\times A_{AB} = 3 \\times 10^6 \\times 0.00375 = 11.25 \\, \\text{kN}\n     \\]\n\n3. **Total Force \\( P \\):**\n   \\[\n   P = F_{\\text{shear}} + F_{\\text{tension}} = 10.602 + 11.25 = 21.852 \\, \\text{kN}\n   \\]\n\nHowever, the problem involves determining options given for failure surfaces that are misaligned with correct application. With an adjustment due to inconsistent or different surface or geometry failure assumption, and correct exploration of conditions:\n\n- Evaluate and cross-check from given options:\n\nUpon aligning screws, shanks, verify more tension or shear, usuall