"To find the maximum allowable load \\( P \\) that the frame can safely support, we need to consider both the normal and shear stress in the wood. The factor of safety is given as 2.\n\n**Step 1: Calculate the cross-sectional area.**\n\nThe cross-sectional area \\( A \\) of the wood is given by:\n\\[ A = b \\times t \\]\n\\[ A = 3.5 \\, \\text{in} \\times 1.25 \\, \\text{in} = 4.375 \\, \\text{in}^2 \\]\n\n**Step 2: Determine the allowable normal stress.**\n\nThe normal failure stress is given as \\( \\sigma_\\text{fail} = 6 \\, \\text{ksi} \\).\n\nWith a safety factor of 2, the allowable normal stress is:\n\\[ \\sigma_\\text{allow} = \\frac{\\sigma_\\text{fail}}{\\text{Factor of Safety}} = \\frac{6}{2} = 3 \\, \\text{ksi} \\]\n\n**Step 3: Determine the allowable shear stress.**\n\nThe shear failure stress is given as \\( \\tau_\\text{fail} = 1.5 \\, \\text{ksi} \\).\n\nWith a safety factor of 2, the allowable shear stress is:\n\\[ \\tau_\\text{allow} = \\frac{\\tau_\\text{fail}}{\\text{Factor of Safety}} = \\frac{1.5}{2} = 0.75 \\, \\text{ksi} \\]\n\n**Step 4: Calculate the maximum load \\( P \\).**\n\n- **Normal Stress Consideration:**\n\nThe member is likely subjected to axial stress along the length due to load \\( P \\), especially in member \\( AB \\), where:\n\\[ \\sigma = \\frac{P}{A} \\Rightarrow P = \\sigma \\times A \\]\n\\[ P = 3 \\, \\text{ksi} \\times 4.375 \\, \\text{in}^2 = 13.125 \\, \\text{kips} \\]\n\n- **Shear Stress Consideration:**\n\nFor member \\( AB \\) under shear:\n\\[ \\tau = \\frac{P}{A} \\Rightarrow P = \\tau \\times A \\]\n\\[ P = 0.75 \\, \\text{ksi} \\times 4.375 \\, \\text{in}^2 = 3.28 \\, \\text{kips} \\]\n\nThe more restrictive condition is governed by the shear stress, so:\n\n**ANSWER: B. 0.75 ksi.**"
"To determine which polymer has the lowest elastic modulus, consider the following factors:\n\n1. **Degree of Polymerization**: Higher molecular weight (degree of polymerization) generally increases the elastic modulus due to enhanced chain entanglement and interaction.\n\n2. **Architecture**: Linear polymers tend to have higher elastic modulus compared to branched polymers because they pack more efficiently, leading to greater intermolecular forces.\n\n3. **% Crystallinity**: Higher crystallinity typically results in a higher elastic modulus because of the ordered structure and stronger intermolecular forces.\n\nNow, analyze the given options:\n\n- (a) Linear, 20% crystalline, degree 3,000\n- (b) Branched, 20% crystalline, degree 3,000\n- (c) Linear, 45% crystalline, degree 150,000\n- (d) Branched, 45% crystalline, degree 275,000\n- (e) Linear, 65% crystalline, degree 275,000\n\n**Option (b)** has the following properties:\n- It is **branched**, lowering the elastic modulus compared to linear polymers.\n- It has the lowest **degree of polymerization/lowest molecular weight** (3,000, same as (a)).\n- It has **20% crystallinity**, lowest in the list (same as (a)).\n\nThus, **option (b)** would feature the lowest elastic modulus as it combines low crystallinity, low degree of polymerization, and branched architecture.\n\nANSWER: B."
