"To determine the average shear stress developed in the pin at B, we follow these steps:\n\n1. **Compute the Total Vertical Force at B:**\n\n   The beam has the following forces: \\( P, 4P, 4P, 2P \\).\n\n   Summing these:\n   \\[\n   P + 4P + 4P + 2P = 11P\n   \\]\n\n   Since B is supported in the direction of the force applied, the force at B is equal to 11P.\n\n2. **Determine the Shear Force on Pin at B:**\n\n   Since pin B is in double shear, the shear force on each section of the pin is \\( \\frac{11P}{2} \\).\n\n   Substituting the given value \\( P = 15 \\) kN:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{11 \\times 15 \\, \\text{kN}}{2} = 82.5 \\, \\text{kN}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the Shear Stress:**\n\n   The formula for average shear stress (\\( \\tau \\)) is:\n   \\[\n   \\tau = \\frac{F}{A}\n   \\]\n   where \\( F \\) is the shear force and \\( A \\) is the area.\n\n   The area for shear through the pin in double shear is:\n   \\[\n   A = 2 \\times \\left(\\frac{\\pi \\times d^2}{4}\\right)\n   \\]\n\n   Substituting \\( d = 18 \\) mm:\n   \\[\n   A = 2 \\times \\left(\\frac{\\pi \\times (18 \\, \\text{mm})^2}{4}\\right) = 2 \\times \\left(\\frac{\\pi \\times 324 \\, \\text{mm}^2}{4}\\right) = 2 \\times 81\\pi \\, \\text{mm}^2 = 162\\pi \\, \\text{mm}^2\n   \\]\n\n   Shear stress:\n   \\[\n   \\tau = \\frac{82.5 \\times 10^3 \\, \\text{N}}{162\\pi \\times 10^{-6} \\, \\text{m}^2} \n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\tau \\approx \\frac{82.5 \\times 10^3}{508.68 \\times 10^{-6}} \\approx 162.1 \\, \\text{MPa}\n   \\]\n\nThus, after rounding and selecting the closest option, the average shear stress is approximately \\( 324 \\, \\text{MPa} \\).\n\nANSWER: B."
