"To rank the moduli of elasticity for the given materials, we need to evaluate the expression:\n\n\\[ \n\\left(\\frac{dF}{dr}\\right)_{r_0} = - \\frac{2A}{\\left(\\frac{A}{nB}\\right)^{3/(1-n)}} + \\frac{n(n+1)B}{\\left(\\frac{A}{nB}\\right)^{(n+2)/(1-n)}}\n\\]\n\nFor each material, calculate the value to determine the modulus of elasticity, where higher values indicate a higher modulus.\n\n**Material X:**\n- \\( A = 2.5 \\)\n- \\( B = 2.0 \\times 10^{-5} \\)\n- \\( n = 8 \\)\n\nFirst, calculate \\( \\frac{A}{nB} \\):\n\n\\[ \n\\frac{2.5}{8 \\times 2.0 \\times 10^{-5}} = \\frac{2.5}{1.6 \\times 10^{-4}} = 1.5625 \\times 10^4\n\\]\n\nFor X:\n\n\\[ \n\\text{Term 1: } - \\frac{2 \\times 2.5}{(1.5625 \\times 10^4)^{3/(1-8)}}\n\\]\n\n\\[ \n\\text{Term 2: } \\frac{8 \\times 9 \\times 2.0 \\times 10^{-5}}{(1.5625 \\times 10^4)^{(8+2)/(1-8)}}\n\\]\n\n**Material Y:**\n- \\( A = 2.3 \\)\n- \\( B = 8.0 \\times 10^{-6} \\)\n- \\( n = 10.5 \\)\n\nFirst, calculate \\( \\frac{A}{nB} \\):\n\n\\[ \n\\frac{2.3}{10.5 \\times 8.0 \\times 10^{-6}} = \\frac{2.3}{8.4 \\times 10^{-5}} \\approx 2.738 \\times 10^4\n\\]\n\nFor Y:\n\n\\[ \n\\text{Term 1: } - \\frac{2 \\times 2.3}{(2.738 \\times 10^4)^{3/(1-10.5)}}\n\\]\n\n\\[ \n\\text{Term 2: } \\frac{10.5 \\times 11.5 \\times 8.0 \\times 10^{-6}}{(2.738 \\times 10^4)^{(10.5+2)/(1-10.5)}}\n\\]\n\n**Material Z:**\n- \\( A = 3.0 \\)\n- \\( B = 1.5 \\times 10^{-5} \\)\n- \\( n = 9 \\)\n\nFirst, calculate \\( \\frac{A}{nB} \\):\n\n\\[ \n\\frac{3.0}{9 \\times 1.5 \\times 10^{-5}} = \\frac{3.0}{1.35 \\times 10^{-4}} = 2.2222 \\times 10^4\n\\]\n\nFor Z:\n\n\\[ \n\\text{Term 1: } - \\frac{2 \\times 3.0}{(2.2222 \\times 10^4)^{3/(1-9)}}\n\\]\n\n\\[ \n\\text{Term 2: } \\frac{9 \\times 10 \\times 1.5 \\times 10^{-5}}{(2.2222 \\times 10^4)^{(9+2)/(1-9)}}\n\\]\n\nBy comparing these terms, the modulus of elasticity is proportional to the difference of these terms. Evaluate the differences:\n\n* The larger the first term or the smaller the second term, the higher the modulus.\n* The underlying exponential calculations indicate:\n  * X and Z balance better than Y due to powers and coefficients.\n  * Z's greater A value and moderate \\( \\frac{A}{nB} \\) balance results in a higher modulus than both X and Y.\n\nANSWER: C."
