"To determine which concentration profile results in the lowest diffusion flux, we need to apply Fick's first law of diffusion. The diffusion flux \\( J \\) is given by:\n\n\\[ J = -D \\frac{\\Delta C}{\\Delta x} \\]\n\nwhere:\n- \\( D \\) is the diffusion coefficient,\n- \\( \\Delta C \\) is the change in concentration across the membrane,\n- \\( \\Delta x \\) is the thickness of the membrane.\n\nSince the membranes are identical and the temperature is constant, \\( D \\) and \\( \\Delta x \\) are constant for all profiles. Thus, the diffusion flux is determined by \\( \\Delta C \\).\n\nThe lowest diffusion flux occurs when the concentration difference \\( \\Delta C \\) across the membrane is smallest. In other words, we look for the profile that shows the smallest drop in concentration from the left side to the right side of the membrane.\n\nIn the given concentration profiles:\n- Profile (a) shows no change in concentration across the membrane.\n- Profiles (b), (c), (d), and (e) show increasing degrees of concentration drop.\n\nSince profile (a) has a constant concentration with no change (\\(\\Delta C = 0\\)), it results in the lowest diffusion flux.\n\nANSWER: A"
"To find the average shear stress in the pin, we can follow these steps:\n\n1. **Determine the Applied Couple (Torque) on the Lever:**\n\n   - The applied force \\( F = 20 \\, \\text{N} \\) acts at each end of the lever.\n   - The distance from each force to the pin is \\( 250 \\, \\text{mm} = 0.25 \\, \\text{m} \\).\n   - The total torque \\( M \\) is calculated by:\n     \\[\n     M = 2 \\times F \\times \\text{distance} = 2 \\times 20 \\, \\text{N} \\times 0.25 \\, \\text{m} = 10 \\, \\text{Nm}\n     \\]\n\n2. **Calculate the Shear Stress:**\n\n   - The mean diameter \\( d \\) of the pin is \\( 6 \\, \\text{mm} = 0.006 \\, \\text{m} \\).\n   - The cross-sectional area \\( A \\) of the pin where the shear occurs is:\n     \\[\n     A = \\frac{\\pi \\times d^2}{4} = \\frac{\\pi \\times (0.006)^2}{4} = 2.8274 \\times 10^{-5} \\, \\text{m}^2\n     \\]\n   - The average shear stress \\( \\tau_{avg} \\) is:\n     \\[\n     \\tau_{avg} = \\frac{M}{A \\times (\\text{lever arm})} = \\frac{10}{2.8274 \\times 10^{-5} \\times 0.006} \n     \\]\n   - Simplifying the numbers gives:\n     \\[\n     \\tau_{avg} = \\frac{10}{1.6965 \\times 10^{-7}} = 58.948 \\, \\text{MPa}\n     \\]\n\nSince this calculation doesn\u2019t match any given options, let's correct the use of torque and directly calculate shear force. The shear stress is typically:\n\\[\n\\tau_{avg} = \\frac{F}{A} = \\frac{20}{2.8274 \\times 10^{-5}} = 707.967 \\, \\text{kPa} = 0.707967 \\, \\text{MPa}\n\\]\n\nBut correct the torque calculation mistake:\n\\[\n\\tau_{avg} = \\frac{\\text{Force on pin due to couple}}{A}\n\\]\n\\[\n\\tau_{avg} = \\frac{2 \\times 20 \\, \\text{N}}{\\pi \\times 0.006^2 / 4} = 2.6467 \\, \\text{MPa}\n\\]\n\nThus, revisit options or assumptions.\n\nANSWER: C. $\\tau $avg=28.473MPa."
