"To determine the largest moment \\( M \\) that can be applied to the bar before it fails, we need to consider the bending stress in the bar. The stress-strain curve given shows that the material will fail at approximately \\( 60 \\, \\text{MPa} \\) in tension.\n\nThe bending stress \\( \\sigma \\) in a beam is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\sigma = \\frac{M \\cdot c}{I}\n\\]\n\nWhere:\n- \\( M \\) is the moment.\n- \\( c \\) is the distance from the neutral axis to the outermost fiber (here \\( 20 \\, \\text{mm} \\)).\n- \\( I \\) is the moment of inertia.\n\nFor a rectangular cross-section:\n\n\\[\nI = \\frac{b \\cdot h^3}{12}\n\\]\n\nGiven:\n- Cross-section dimensions \\( b = 20 \\, \\text{mm} \\) and \\( h = 20 \\, \\text{mm} \\).\n\nCalculate the moment of inertia:\n\n\\[\nI = \\frac{20 \\, \\text{mm} \\cdot (20 \\, \\text{mm})^3}{12} = \\frac{20 \\cdot 8000}{12} = 13333.33 \\, \\text{mm}^4\n\\]\n\nConvert the moment of inertia to \\( \\text{m}^4 \\):\n\n\\[\nI = 13333.33 \\times 10^{-12} \\, \\text{m}^4\n\\]\n\nThe maximum allowable stress \\( \\sigma = 60 \\, \\text{MPa} \\) or \\( 60 \\times 10^{6} \\, \\text{N/m}^2 \\).\n\nSubstitute to find \\( M \\):\n\n\\[\n60 \\times 10^{6} = \\frac{M \\cdot 20 \\times 10^{-3}}{13333.33 \\times 10^{-12}}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nM = \\frac{60 \\times 10^{6} \\cdot 13333.33 \\times 10^{-12}}{20 \\times 10^{-3}}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nM = \\frac{60 \\cdot 13333.33}{20} \\times 10^{-3}\n\\]\n\n\\[\nM = 40000 \\times 10^{-3} = 40 \\, \\text{N\u00b7m}\n\\]\n\nSince this value does not match any choice and due to any misinterpretations, let's review calculations or compare with provided options. Here, a logical calculation suggests the need to approximate or analyze further details in diagram context to correct bounds or values.\n\nOn careful analysis, we're referencing typical textbook problem tensions within the curve's mechanical tolerances as per \\( \\pm \\) signs compensated. Re-examine data or correctly graph stresses alignment addressing such standar