"To find \\( h \\) in the parallelogram, we can use the Pythagorean theorem in the right triangle formed by the height, the base, and the diagonal.\n\n- We have a right triangle with:\n  - Hypotenuse (diagonal) = 36\n  - One side (base) = 24\n  - Other side (height \\( h \\))\n\nThe Pythagorean theorem states:\n\\[\na^2 + b^2 = c^2\n\\]\nwhere \\( c \\) is the hypotenuse, and \\( a \\) and \\( b \\) are the other two sides.\n\nPlug in the numbers:\n\\[\n24^2 + h^2 = 36^2\n\\]\n\nCalculate \\( 24^2 \\):\n\\[\n24^2 = 576\n\\]\n\nCalculate \\( 36^2 \\):\n\\[\n36^2 = 1296\n\\]\n\nSubstitute these values into the equation:\n\\[\n576 + h^2 = 1296\n\\]\n\nSolve for \\( h^2 \\):\n\\[\nh^2 = 1296 - 576\n\\]\n\\[\nh^2 = 720\n\\]\n\nFind \\( h \\) by taking the square root of both sides:\n\\[\nh = \\sqrt{720}\n\\]\n\nSimplify \\(\\sqrt{720}\\):\n\\[\n\\sqrt{720} = \\sqrt{36 \\times 20} = 6\\sqrt{20} = 6 \\times 4.5 = 27\n\\]\n\nThus, the height \\( h \\) is 27. \n\nBut the base we need for \\( h \\) is actually 27, which indicates the perpendicular relationship was already correct, implying this should equate directly with given height \\( h = 27 \\).\n\nThus re-checking, \\(\\sqrt{720} \\approx 26.83\\) approaches to the closest relation height but matches perfectly to the closest choice:\n\nANSWER: B. 28."
