"To find the points of intersection between the line \\( y = x + 1 \\) and the parabola \\( y = x^2 - 3x - 4 \\), we need to set the equations equal to each other:\n\n\\[ x + 1 = x^2 - 3x - 4. \\]\n\nRearrange the equation:\n\n\\[ x^2 - 3x - 4 = x + 1 \\]\n\n\\[ x^2 - 3x - 4 - x - 1 = 0 \\]\n\n\\[ x^2 - 4x - 5 = 0. \\]\n\nNow, solve for \\( x \\) using the quadratic formula, \\( x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a} \\), where \\( a = 1 \\), \\( b = -4 \\), and \\( c = -5 \\).\n\nCalculate the discriminant:\n\n\\[ b^2 - 4ac = (-4)^2 - 4(1)(-5) = 16 + 20 = 36. \\]\n\nNow plug into the quadratic formula:\n\n\\[ x = \\frac{-(-4) \\pm \\sqrt{36}}{2(1)} \\]\n\n\\[ x = \\frac{4 \\pm 6}{2}. \\]\n\nThe possible values for \\( x \\) are:\n\n\\[ x = \\frac{4 + 6}{2} = 5 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad x = \\frac{4 - 6}{2} = -1. \\]\n\nSubstitute these \\( x \\)-values back into the line equation \\( y = x + 1 \\) to find the corresponding \\( y \\)-coordinates:\n\nFor \\( x = 5 \\):\n\n\\[ y = 5 + 1 = 6. \\]\n\nFor \\( x = -1 \\):\n\n\\[ y = -1 + 1 = 0. \\]\n\nTherefore, the points of intersection are \\( (-1, 0) \\) and \\( (5, 6) \\). \n\nThe coordinates of point \\( Q \\) are \\((5, 6)\\).\n\nANSWER: D."
