"To find \\(\\frac{dr}{d\\theta}\\) for the function \\(r = \\sqrt{\\theta + \\cos(2\\theta)}\\), we'll use the chain rule. Let's differentiate:\n\n1. Set \\(u = \\theta + \\cos(2\\theta)\\), so \\(r = \\sqrt{u}\\).\n\n2. Differentiate \\(r\\) with respect to \\(u\\):\n   \\[\n   \\frac{dr}{du} = \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{u}}\n   \\]\n\n3. Differentiate \\(u = \\theta + \\cos(2\\theta)\\) with respect to \\(\\theta\\):\n   \\[\n   \\frac{du}{d\\theta} = 1 - 2\\sin(2\\theta)\n   \\]\n\n4. Apply the chain rule:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{dr}{d\\theta} = \\frac{dr}{du} \\cdot \\frac{du}{d\\theta} = \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{\\theta + \\cos(2\\theta)}} \\cdot (1 - 2\\sin(2\\theta))\n   \\]\n\nThus, \\(\\frac{dr}{d\\theta} = \\frac{1 - 2\\sin(2\\theta)}{2\\sqrt{\\theta + \\cos(2\\theta)}}\\), which matches the given expression.\n\nANSWER: A."
