"To find the area of the region inside the circle \\( r = 3\\cos\\theta \\) and outside the cardioid \\( r = 1 + \\cos\\theta \\), follow these steps:\n\n1. **Find Points of Intersection:**\n   Set the equations equal to find the points where the curves intersect:\n   \\[\n   3\\cos\\theta = 1 + \\cos\\theta\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   2\\cos\\theta = 1 \\implies \\cos\\theta = \\frac{1}{2}\n   \\]\n   The solutions for \\( \\theta \\) are \\( \\theta = \\pm \\frac{\\pi}{3} \\).\n\n2. **Set up the Integral for Area:**\n   The total area is determined by integrating the difference between the squares of the radii of the two curves from \\( \\theta = -\\frac{\\pi}{3} \\) to \\( \\theta = \\frac{\\pi}{3} \\).\n\n   The area \\( A \\) is given by:\n   \\[\n   A = \\frac{1}{2} \\int_{-\\frac{\\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{3}} \\left[(3\\cos\\theta)^2 - (1+\\cos\\theta)^2\\right] d\\theta\n   \\]\n\n3. **Simplify the Integral:**\n   \\[\n   (3\\cos\\theta)^2 = 9\\cos^2\\theta\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   (1 + \\cos\\theta)^2 = 1 + 2\\cos\\theta + \\cos^2\\theta\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   A = \\frac{1}{2} \\int_{-\\frac{\\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{3}} \\left[9\\cos^2\\theta - (1 + 2\\cos\\theta + \\cos^2\\theta)\\right] d\\theta\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   = \\frac{1}{2} \\int_{-\\frac{\\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{3}} \\left(8\\cos^2\\theta - 2\\cos\\theta - 1\\right) d\\theta\n   \\]\n\n4. **Evaluate the Integral:**\n   The integral can be split as follows:\n   \\[\n   = \\frac{1}{2} \\left[ 8\\int_{-\\frac{\\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{3}} \\cos^2\\theta \\, d\\theta - 2\\int_{-\\frac{\\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{3}} \\cos\\theta \\, d\\theta - \\int_{-\\frac{\\pi}{3}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{3}} 1 \\, d\\theta \\right]\n   \\]\n\n   Using the identities and integrating:\n   - \\( \\int \\cos^2\\theta \\, d\\theta = \\frac{1}{2}\\theta + \\frac{1}{4}\\sin(2\\theta) \\)\n   - \\( \\int \\cos\\theta \\, d\\theta = \\sin\\theta \\)\n\n   Evaluate each integral from \\( -\\frac{\\pi}{3} \\) to \\( \\frac{\\pi}{3} \\), combine and simplify.\n\