"To find the probability that the card selected is either a queen or a 3:\n\n1. Count the number of queens in a deck: There are 4 queens (one from each suit: clubs, diamonds, hearts, and spades).\n\n2. Count the number of 3s in a deck: There are 4 threes (one from each suit: clubs, diamonds, hearts, and spades).\n\n3. Calculate the total number of favorable outcomes: There are no overlapping cards between queens and 3s, so add the number of queens and number of 3s.\n   \\[\n   4 \\text{ (queens)} + 4 \\text{ (threes)} = 8\n   \\]\n\n4. Since a standard deck has 52 cards, the probability \\(P\\) is:\n   \\[\n   P(\\text{Queen or 3}) = \\frac{8}{52} = \\frac{2}{13}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the probability that the card selected is either a queen or a 3 is \\( \\frac{2}{13} \\).\n\nANSWER: C"
