"To find the shortest spanning tree using a greedy algorithm (Kruskal's or Prim's), we follow specific steps. I'll use Kruskal's algorithm here:\n\n**Kruskal\u2019s Algorithm Steps:**\n\n1. **Sort all the edges** in the graph in non-decreasing order of their weights.\n2. **Initialize a tree** with no edges.\n3. **Add edges one by one,** starting with the smallest:\n   - Add the next edge to the spanning tree if it doesn\u2019t form a cycle.\n4. Stop when there are (V-1) edges in the tree, where V is the number of vertices.\n\n**Applying Kruskal\u2019s:**\n\nEdges sorted by weight: \n\n1. \\( (C, D, -10) \\)\n2. \\( (D, B, -2) \\)\n3. \\( (G, K, -5) \\)\n4. \\( (F, D, 3) \\) \n5. \\( (C, F, 4) \\)\n6. \\( (E, D, 12) \\)\n7. \\( (B, E, 15) \\)\n8. \\( (E, G, 20) \\)\n\nProceed with adding the smallest edges (avoid cycles):\n\n1. **(C, D, -10)** \n2. **(D, B, -2)** \n3. **(G, K, -5)**\n4. **(F, D, 3)**\n5. **(C, F, 4)** \n6. **(E, D, 12)**\n7. **(B, E, 15)**\n\nThis completes the tree with 7 edges (since there are 8 vertices).\n\nChecking options, the edge set \\(EDBCFGKH_A\\) corresponds to the selection including \\(ED, B, C, F, G, K, H, A\\) and follows the edges mentioned above.\n\nANSWER: A"
