"To find the area of the region bounded by the curves \\( y = x^2 \\) and \\( y = x^3 \\), we first determine their points of intersection. We set the equations equal to each other:\n\n\\[ x^2 = x^3 \\]\n\nSolving for \\( x \\), we get:\n\n\\[ x^3 - x^2 = 0 \\]\n\\[ x^2(x - 1) = 0 \\]\n\nThe solutions are \\( x = 0 \\) and \\( x = 1 \\).\n\nNext, we set up the integral to find the area between the curves from \\( x = 0 \\) to \\( x = 1 \\). The area \\( A \\) is given by:\n\n\\[ A = \\int_0^1 (x^2 - x^3) \\, dx \\]\n\nEvaluating the integral:\n\n\\[ A = \\int_0^1 (x^2 - x^3) \\, dx \\]\n\\[ = \\int_0^1 x^2 \\, dx - \\int_0^1 x^3 \\, dx \\]\n\nCalculating each integral separately:\n\n\\[ \\int_0^1 x^2 \\, dx = \\left[ \\frac{x^3}{3} \\right]_0^1 = \\frac{1}{3} \\]\n\n\\[ \\int_0^1 x^3 \\, dx = \\left[ \\frac{x^4}{4} \\right]_0^1 = \\frac{1}{4} \\]\n\nSubtract to find the area:\n\n\\[ A = \\frac{1}{3} - \\frac{1}{4} \\]\n\\[ = \\frac{4}{12} - \\frac{3}{12} \\]\n\\[ = \\frac{1}{12} \\]\n\nThus, the area of the region is \\(\\frac{1}{12}\\).\n\nANSWER: C"
