"To find the length of \\(\\overline{P S}\\), we can use the Power of a Point theorem. The theorem states that if a secant segment and a tangent segment are drawn from an external point to a circle, then the square of the length of the tangent segment is equal to the product of the entire secant segment length and its external part. Therefore, in this scenario, \\((\\overline{P S})^2 = \\overline{P T} \\times \\overline{P R}\\).\n\nFirst, we need to determine \\(\\overline{P R}\\):\n\n1. **Find the lengths of \\(\\overline{R M}\\) and \\(\\overline{T M}\\):**\n\n   Given that \\(\\overline{R M}\\) is 2 more than \\(\\overline{T M}\\), we set \\( \\overline{T M} = x\\). Then \\(\\overline{R M} = x + 2\\).\n\n2. **Apply the formula for intersecting chords:**\n\n   The formula for intersecting chords states:\n   \\[\n   \\overline{R M} \\times \\overline{M T} = \\overline{Q M} \\times \\overline{M S}\n   \\]\n   Substitute the values:\n   \\[\n   (x + 2) \\times x = 2 \\times 12\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   x^2 + 2x = 24\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   x^2 + 2x - 24 = 0\n   \\]\n\n   **Solve this quadratic equation:**\n\n   \\[\n   (x + 6)(x - 4) = 0\n   \\]\n\n   So, \\(x = -6\\) or \\(x = 4\\). Since length cannot be negative, \\(x = 4\\).\n\n   Therefore, \\(\\overline{T M} = 4\\) and \\(\\overline{R M} = 6\\).\n\n   Thus, \\(\\overline{R T} = \\overline{R M} + \\overline{M T} = 6 + 4 = 10\\).\n\n3. **Use the Power of a Point theorem:**\n\n   \\[\n   (\\overline{P S})^2 = \\overline{P T} \\times \\overline{P R}\n   \\]\n\n   We know \\(\\overline{P T} = 8\\) and \\(\\overline{P R} = \\overline{P T} + \\overline{T R} = 8 + 10 = 18\\).\n\n4. **Calculate \\(\\overline{P S}\\):**\n\n   \\[\n   (\\overline{P S})^2 = 8 \\times 18 = 144\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   \\overline{P S} = \\sqrt{144} = 12\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the length of \\(\\overline{P S}\\) is 12.\n\nANSWER: D"
