"To solve for the transfer function \\( G(s) = \\frac{E_0(s)}{T(s)} \\), we need to analyze each component of the system.\n\n1. **Motor and Gear Train:**\n   - The torque \\( T(s) \\) is applied to a load with inertia \\( J \\) through a gear train with ratio \\( N_1/N_2 \\).\n   - The angular speed at the motor is transformed through the gear ratio: \\(\\omega_m = \\frac{N_1}{N_2} \\omega_L\\).\n   - The torque equation is \\( J s \\omega_L(s) = T(s) \\), so \\(\\omega_L(s) = \\frac{T(s)}{Js}\\).\n   - Therefore, \\(\\omega_m(s) = \\frac{N_1}{N_2} \\frac{T(s)}{Js}\\).\n\n2. **Electrical Section:**\n   - The angular velocity \\(\\omega_m(s)\\) is converted to an electrical signal \\( e_1(t) \\).\n   - \\( e_1(s) = K \\omega_m(s) \\) where \\( K = \\frac{10}{2\\pi} \\) is from the potentiometer and the conversion factor from radian to voltage.\n   - So, \\( e_1(s) = \\frac{10 N_1}{2\\pi N_2} \\frac{T(s)}{Js} \\).\n\n3. **RC Network:**\n   - The voltage \\( e_1(s) \\) is passed through an RC low-pass filter.\n   - The transfer function of the RC filter is \\( \\frac{E_0(s)}{E_1(s)} = \\frac{RCs}{RCs + 1} \\).\n\nCombining these components:\n\n\\[ E_0(s) = \\frac{RCs}{RCs + 1} \\frac{10 N_1}{2\\pi N_2} \\frac{T(s)}{Js} \\]\n\nThe overall transfer function is:\n\n\\[ G(s) = \\frac{E_0(s)}{T(s)} = \\frac{10 N_1 RCs}{Js^2 2\\pi N_2(RCs + 1)} \\]\n\nMultiplying numerator and denominator by 5 to simplify:\n\n\\[ G(s) = \\frac{5 N_1 RCs}{Js^2 \\pi N_2(RCs + 1)} \\]\n\nComparing this with the options:\n\nANSWER: C"
