"To find the torque applied by the belt on the fan pulley and the power transmitted, we can use the following formulas:\n\n1. **Torque:**  \n   \\[\n   \\text{Torque} (T) = (F_{\\text{tight}} - F_{\\text{slack}}) \\times r\n   \\]\n   where:\n   - \\( F_{\\text{tight}} = 2000 \\, \\text{N} \\) (tight side tension)\n   - \\( F_{\\text{slack}} = 200 \\, \\text{N} \\) (slack side tension)\n   - \\( r = \\frac{d}{2} = \\frac{0.3 \\, \\text{m}}{2} = 0.15 \\, \\text{m} \\) (radius of the pulley)\n   \n   \\[\n   T = (2000 - 200) \\, \\text{N} \\times 0.15 \\, \\text{m} = 270 \\, \\text{N} \\cdot \\text{m}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Power:**  \n   \\[\n   \\text{Power} (P) = T \\times \\omega\n   \\]\n   where:\n   - Angular speed \\( \\omega = 2\\pi \\times \\text{revolutions per second} \\)\n   - RPM is given as 1800, so revolutions per second = \\( \\frac{1800}{60} = 30 \\)\n   - \\( \\omega = 2\\pi \\times 30 = 60\\pi \\, \\text{rad/s} \\)\n\n   \\[\n   P = 270 \\, \\text{N} \\cdot \\text{m} \\times 60\\pi \\, \\text{rad/s}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   P \\approx 270 \\times 188.5 = 50900 \\, \\text{W} = 50.9 \\, \\text{kW}\n   \\]\n\nThus, the torque applied by the belt on the fan pulley is 270 N\u22c5m, and the power transmitted is 50.9 kW.\n\nANSWER: A."
"To solve this problem, we can apply the concept of fracture mechanics, particularly using the relationship for fracture toughness. For a central crack in a large plate, the critical stress \\(\\sigma_c\\) is related to the crack length \\(a\\) and the stress intensity factor \\(K_{IC}\\) as follows:\n\n\\[ \nK_{IC} = \\sigma_c \\sqrt{\\pi a}\n\\]\n\nFor the initial crack length \\(a_1 = 0.5 \\, \\text{in}\\) (since the total crack length is 1 inch), the fracture stress is \\(\\sigma_{c1} = 80 \\, \\text{ksi}\\).\n\n\\[ \nK_{IC} = 80 \\sqrt{\\pi \\times 0.5}\n\\]\n\nFor the situation with the new crack length \\(a_2 = 0.875 \\, \\text{in}\\) (since the total crack length is 1.75 inches), let the critical stress be \\(\\sigma_{c2}\\).\n\nSince \\(K_{IC}\\) is a property of the material and remains constant:\n\n\\[\n80 \\sqrt{\\pi \\times 0.5} = \\sigma_{c2} \\sqrt{\\pi \\times 0.875}\n\\]\n\nSolving for \\(\\sigma_{c2}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\sigma_{c2} = \\frac{80 \\sqrt{\\pi \\times 0.5}}{\\sqrt{\\pi \\times 0.875}}\n\\]\n\nSimplifying:\n\n\\[\n\\sigma_{c2} = 80 \\frac{\\sqrt{0.5}}{\\sqrt{0.875}}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\sigma_{c2} = 80 \\times \\sqrt{\\frac{0.5}{0.875}}\n\\]\n\nCalculating the numerical value:\n\n\\[\n\\sigma_{c2} \\approx 80 \\times 0.7559\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\sigma_{c2} \\approx 60.47 \\, \\text{ksi}\n\\]\n\nSo the fracture load for the sheet with a 1.75-inch crack is approximately 60.47 ksi.\n\nANSWER: A."
"To solve this problem, we need to use Hertzian contact theory for line contact between two parallel cylinders. \n\n### Given:\n- Diameter of smaller roller, \\( d_1 = 25 \\) mm\n- Diameter of larger roller, \\( d_2 = 75 \\) mm\n- Width of the rollers, \\( b = 25 \\) mm\n- Preload force, \\( F = 200 \\) N\n- Modulus of elasticity for steel, \\( E = 210 \\) GPa (typical value)\n- Poisson\u2019s ratio for steel, \\( \\nu = 0.3 \\) (typical value)\n\n### Formulas:\n\n1. **Contact Width (\\( 2b \\))**\n   \\[\n   b = \\sqrt{\\frac{2F}{{\\pi L E'}}}\n   \\]\n   where \\( E' \\) is the combined modulus of elasticity:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{E'} = \\frac{1-\\nu^2_1}{E_1} + \\frac{1-\\nu^2_2}{E_2}\n   \\]\n   Since both rollers are steel, \\( E_1 = E_2 = E \\) and \\( \\nu_1 = \\nu_2 = \\nu \\).\n\n2. **Maximum Contact Pressure (\\( p_0 \\))**\n   \\[\n   p_0 = \\frac{2F}{\\pi b L}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Area of Contact (\\( A \\))**\n   \\[\n   A = 2b \\times L\n   \\]\n\n4. **Subsurface Shear Stress (\\( \\tau_{\\text{max}} \\))**\n   \\[\n   \\tau_{\\text{max}} = 0.3 \\times p_0\n   \\]\n\n### Calculation Steps:\n\n1. **Calculate Combined Modulus of Elasticity \\( E' \\):**\n   \\[\n   E' = \\frac{E}{2(1-\\nu^2)} = \\frac{210}{2(1 - 0.3^2)} = 230.77 \\text{ GPa}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Calculate Contact Width \\( b \\):**\n   \\[\n   b = \\sqrt{\\frac{2 \\times 200}{\\pi \\times 25 \\times 230.77 \\times 10^3}} = \\sqrt{\\frac{400}{\\pi \\times 25 \\times 230.77 \\times 10^3}} \\approx 0.029 \\text{ mm}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculate Maximum Contact Pressure \\( p_0 \\):**\n   \\[\n   p_0 = \\frac{2 \\times 200}{\\pi \\times 0.029 \\times 25} \\approx 188 \\text{ MPa}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Calculate Area of Contact \\( A \\):**\n   \\[\n   A = 2 \\times 0.029 \\times 25 \\approx 1.45 \\text{ mm}^2\n   \\]\n\n5. **Subsurface Shear Stress \\( \\tau_{\\text{max}} \\):**\n   \\[\n   \\tau_{\\text{max}} = 0.3 \\times 188 \\approx 56.4 \\text{ MPa}\n   \\]\n\n### Choose the Closest Option:\nBased on these calculations, the closest option is:\n- \\( p_0 \\approx 188 \\) MPa\n- \\( b \\approx 0.029 \\) mm\n- \\( A \\approx 1.45 \\) mm\\(^2\\)\n- \\( \\tau_{\\text{max}} \\approx 56 \\) MPa (close to given)\n\nTherefore, the closest option is: \n\n**ANSWER: C**"
"To solve this problem, we'll go through each part step by step.\n\n### (a) Time Required to Accelerate\n\nThe torque \\( T \\) provided by the gear reducer is used to accelerate the machine.\n\nGiven:\n- Torque \\( T = 6 \\, \\text{N} \\cdot \\text{m} \\)\n- Inertia \\( I = 0.7 \\, \\text{N} \\cdot \\text{m} \\cdot \\text{s}^2 \\)\n- Final speed \\( \\omega = 600 \\, \\text{rpm} \\)\n\nConvert \\( 600 \\, \\text{rpm} \\) to rad/s:\n\\[\n\\omega = 600 \\times \\frac{2\\pi}{60} = 20\\pi \\, \\text{rad/s}\n\\]\n\nUsing the equation \\( T = I \\cdot \\alpha \\) where \\( \\alpha \\) is the angular acceleration, we have:\n\\[\n\\alpha = \\frac{T}{I} = \\frac{6}{0.7} \\approx 8.57 \\, \\text{rad/s}^2\n\\]\n\nThe time \\( t \\) to reach the final speed from rest is:\n\\[\n\\omega = \\alpha \\cdot t \\quad \\Rightarrow \\quad t = \\frac{\\omega}{\\alpha} = \\frac{20\\pi}{8.57} \\approx 7.33 \\, \\text{s}\n\\]\n\n### (b) Energy Delivered to the Machine\n\nThe kinetic energy \\( E \\) delivered to the machine is:\n\\[\nE = \\frac{1}{2} I \\omega^2 = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 0.7 \\times (20\\pi)^2 \\approx 1382 \\, \\text{J}\n\\]\n\n### (c) Heat Energy Generated in the Clutch\n\nThe total work done by the clutch is the torque times the angular displacement.\n\nFirst, find the angular displacement:\n\\[\n\\theta = \\frac{1}{2} \\alpha t^2 = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 8.57 \\times (7.33)^2 \\, \\text{rad}\n\\]\n\nThen, calculate the total work done:\n\\[\nW_{\\text{total}} = T \\times \\theta = 6 \\times \\theta\n\\]\n\nThe heat energy generated is the difference between the total work done and the kinetic energy:\n\\[\nQ = W_{\\text{total}} - E\n\\]\n\nCalculating \\( Q \\) with the obtained values:\n- \\( W_{\\text{total}} \\approx 2763 \\, \\text{J} \\)\n- \\( Q = 2763 - 1382 = 1381 \\, \\text{J} \\)\n\n### Conclusion\n\nComparing calculations with the options given, the closest match is:\n\nANSWER: A. 7.33s, 1382J, 1381J."
