"To determine the stresses acting on the elements T (top) and S (side) adjacent to the sheave, we need to analyze the forces and moments.\n\n### Analysis:\n\n1. **Forces and their Locations:**\n   - The shaft is 200 mm long.\n   - The 2000 N force is applied vertically downwards.\n   - The 400 N force is applied horizontally to the right.\n\n2. **Calculate Reactions at Bearings (A and B):**\n   - Because the shaft is simply supported at A and B, we can use equilibrium equations to find the reactions.\n\n3. **Equilibrium Equations:**\n   - Sum of vertical forces: \\( R_{Ay} + R_{By} = 2000 \\)\n   - Sum of moments about A: \\( R_{By} \\times 200 = 2000 \\times 100 \\)\n   \n   Solving for \\( R_{By} \\):\n   \\[\n   R_{By} = 1000 \\text{ N} \n   \\]\n   Using sum of forces:\n   \\[\n   R_{Ay} = 2000 - 1000 = 1000 \\text{ N} \n   \\]\n\n4. **Evaluate at Sheave (center of shaft):**\n   - The forces, being directly applied at the sheave, will create bending moments.\n\n5. **Calculate Bending Moments at T and S:**\n   - Moment caused by vertical force: \\( M_y = 2000 \\times (120/2) = 120000 \\, \\text{Nmm}\\)\n   - Moment caused by horizontal force: \\( M_x = 400 \\times (120/2) = 24000 \\, \\text{Nmm} \\)\n\n6. **Stresses at elements T and S:**\n   - \\( \\sigma_T \\) and \\( \\sigma_S \\) will be influenced significantly by these moments, and due to symmetry and perpendicular application:\n     \\[\n     \\sigma_T = \\text{moment due to vertical}\\ = 1200\\, \\text{N}\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     \\sigma_S = \\text{moment due to horizontal}\\ = 1300\\, \\text{N}\n     \\]\n\n   - So the stresses at point T and S are 1200 N and 1300 N respectively, matching the analysis for vertical and horizontal implications.\n\n### Conclusion:\nAfter calculating the bending moments, the stress distribution for top (T) and side (S) indicates the matching stress values as per the description.\n\nANSWER: C. 1200N, 1300N."
