"To determine the shear stress \\(\\tau\\) for a thin-walled tube with a hexagonal cross-section, we follow these steps:\n\n1. **Geometry of the Hexagon:**\n   - A regular hexagon can be divided into six equilateral triangles.\n   - The side length of each triangle is \\(b\\).\n\n2. **Perimeter (Mean Circumference) of the Cross-Section:**\n   - Since the hexagon is regular with each side of length \\(b\\), the total perimeter, \\(p\\), is:\n     \\[\n     p = 6b\n     \\]\n\n3. **Area Enclosed by the Midline Polygon:**\n   - The area, \\(A_m\\), of a regular hexagon with side \\(b\\) is given by:\n     \\[\n     A_m = \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}b^2\n     \\]\n\n4. **Shear Stress Formula:**\n   - For thin-walled sections, the shear stress \\(\\tau\\) is given by:\n     \\[\n     \\tau = \\frac{T}{2A_mt}\n     \\]\n   - Substituting \\(A_m = \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}b^2\\) into the formula:\n     \\[\n     \\tau = \\frac{T}{2 \\cdot \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2}b^2 \\cdot t} = \\frac{T}{3\\sqrt{3}b^2t}\n     \\]\n\n5. **Rearrange the Expression:**\n   - Multiply the numerator and denominator by \\(\\sqrt{3}\\):\n     \\[\n     \\tau = \\frac{T\\sqrt{3}}{9b^2t}\n     \\]\n\nThus, the correct choice is:\n\nANSWER: B"
