"To solve this problem, we'll use the principle of buoyancy, which states that the buoyant force on a submerged object is equal to the weight of the fluid displaced by the object.\n\n### Steps:\n\n1. **Calculate the Volume of Water Displaced:**\n   The submerged part of the can displaces water. The submerged height is 8 cm, and the diameter of the cylindrical can is 9 cm. \n   \n   \\[\n   \\text{Volume of water displaced} = \\text{Base Area} \\times \\text{Height}\n   \\]\n\n   The base area (A) of the cylinder is:\n\n   \\[\n   A = \\pi \\left(\\frac{D}{2}\\right)^2 = \\pi \\left(\\frac{9\\, \\text{cm}}{2}\\right)^2 = \\pi \\times 4.5^2\\, \\text{cm}^2\n   \\]\n\n   So, the volume of water displaced (V) is:\n\n   \\[\n   V = \\pi \\times 4.5^2 \\times 8 \\, \\text{cm}^3\n   \\]\n\n2. **Convert cm\\(^3\\) to m\\(^3\\):**\n\n   \\[\n   V = \\pi \\times (4.5 \\times 10^{-2})^2 \\times (8 \\times 10^{-2}) \\, \\text{m}^3\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the Buoyant Force:**\n\n   The buoyant force (which is equal to the weight of the can) is equal to the weight of the displaced water. The density of water is approximately \\(1000 \\, \\text{kg/m}^3\\), and gravitational acceleration \\(g\\) is \\(9.81 \\, \\text{m/s}^2\\).\n\n   \\[\n   \\text{Buoyant Force} = \\text{Density of water} \\times V \\times g = 1000 \\, \\times V \\times 9.81\n   \\]\n\n4. **Compute the Weight of the Can:**\n\n   Plug in the volume from step 2 into the buoyant force equation to find the weight of the can:\n\n   \\[\n   V = \\pi \\times (4.5 \\times 10^{-2})^2 \\times (8 \\times 10^{-2})\\, \\text{m}^3 \n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   = \\pi \\times 0.045^2 \\times 0.08 \\, \\text{m}^3\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   \\approx \\pi \\times 0.002025 \\times 0.08 \\, \\text{m}^3\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   \\approx 0.00050868 \\times \\pi \\, \\text{m}^3\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   \\text{Buoyant Force} = 1000 \\times 0.00050868 \\pi \\times 9.81\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   \\approx 4.983 \\, \\text{N}\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the weight of the can is approximately 4.983 N.\n\nANSWER: A"
