"To find the natural frequency of the system, we consider the effects of both the linear springs and the torsional spring. The uniform slender rod is hinged at point A and subjected to both linear and torsional restoring forces.\n\n### Step-by-step Approach:\n\n1. **Linear Springs Contribution**:\n   - Each spring, with spring constant \\( k \\), will provide a force restoring the system to equilibrium.\n   - Distance from point A to each spring point is \\( \\frac{l}{3} \\) on one side and \\( \\frac{2l}{3} \\) on the other side.\n   - The equivalent linear spring constant \\( k_{\\text{eq}} \\) for small oscillations around the hinge A:\n     \\[\n     k_{\\text{eq}} = 3k = 3(2000) = 6000 \\, \\text{N/m}\n     \\]\n   - Since there are two sets of parallel springs, we consider their effective spring constant \\( 2k_{\\text{eq}} \\).\n\n2. **Torsional Spring Contribution**:\n   - Given \\( k_t = 1000 \\, \\text{N-m/rad} \\), it adds torsional stiffness around point A.\n\n3. **Moment of Inertia of the Rod**:\n   - For a slender rod of mass \\( m \\) and length \\( l \\) about an axis through one end (A):\n     \\[\n     I = \\frac{1}{3} m l^2 = \\frac{1}{3} \\cdot 10 \\cdot (5)^2 = \\frac{250}{3} \\, \\text{kg}\\cdot\\text{m}^2\n     \\]\n\n4. **Equation of Motion & Natural Frequency**:\n   - The natural frequency \\( \\omega_n \\) of the system can be found by equating the restoring torques due to both the springs:\n     \\[\n     \\omega_n = \\sqrt{\\frac{k_{\\text{eq}} \\cdot l^2 + k_t}{I}}\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     \\omega_n = \\sqrt{\\frac{(6000 \\cdot (5)^2) + 1000}{250/3}}\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     \\omega_n = \\sqrt{\\frac{150000 + 1000}{\\frac{250}{3}}}\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     \\omega_n = \\sqrt{\\frac{151000 \\times 3}{250}}\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     \\omega_n = \\sqrt{\\frac{453000}{250}}\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     \\omega_n = \\sqrt{1812}\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     \\omega_n \\approx 42.57 \\, \\text{rad/s}\n     \\]\n\nThe closest option to this result is approximately \\( 45.154 \\, \\text{rad/s} \\).\n\nANSWER: B. \\( w_{n}=45.154 \\, \\text{rad/s} \\)."
