"To find the transfer function \\( G(s) = \\frac{\\Theta_2(s)}{T(s)} \\), we need to analyze the rotational mechanical system.\n\n### Step 1: Define System Components\n\n1. **Moment of Inertia**: \\( J = 1 \\, \\text{kg-m}^2 \\)\n2. **Damper Coefficients**: \n   - Damper 1: \\( b_1 = 1 \\, \\text{N-m-s/rad} \\)\n   - Damper 2: \\( b_2 = 1 \\, \\text{N-m-s/rad} \\)\n   - Damper 3: \\( b_3 = 1 \\, \\text{N-m-s/rad} \\)\n3. **Spring Stiffness**: \\( k = 1 \\, \\text{N-m/rad} \\)\n\n### Step 2: Derive Equations of Motion\n\nUsing the free-body diagram, apply the rotational form of Newton\u2019s second law:\n\n1. **For first disk:**\n   \\[\n   J\\ddot{\\theta_1} = T(t) - b_1 \\dot{\\theta_1} - k(\\theta_1 - \\theta_2) - b_2(\\dot{\\theta_1} - \\dot{\\theta_2})\n   \\]\n\n2. **For second disk:**\n   \\[\n   0 = k(\\theta_1 - \\theta_2) + b_2(\\dot{\\theta_1} - \\dot{\\theta_2}) - b_3\\dot{\\theta_2}\n   \\]\n\n### Step 3: Laplace Transform\n\nTaking the Laplace transform of the equations (assuming zero initial conditions):\n\n1. First equation:\n   \\[\n   Js^2\\Theta_1(s) = T(s) - b_1s\\Theta_1(s) - k(\\Theta_1(s) - \\Theta_2(s)) - b_2s(\\Theta_1(s) - \\Theta_2(s))\n   \\]\n\n2. Second equation:\n   \\[\n   0 = k(\\Theta_1(s) - \\Theta_2(s)) + b_2s(\\Theta_1(s) - \\Theta_2(s)) - b_3s\\Theta_2(s)\n   \\]\n\n### Step 4: Simplify and Solve\n\n1. **From the second equation:**\n   \\[\n   (k + b_2s)(\\Theta_1(s) - \\Theta_2(s)) = b_3s\\Theta_2(s)\n   \\]\n\n   Solving for \\(\\Theta_1(s) - \\Theta_2(s)\\):\n   \\[\n   \\Theta_1(s) - \\Theta_2(s) = \\frac{b_3s}{k + b_2s + b_3s} \\Theta_2(s)\n   \\]\n\n2. **Substitute into the first equation:**\n   \\[\n   Js^2\\Theta_1(s) = T(s) - (b_1s + \\frac{b_3s}{k + b_2s + b_3s}(k + b_2s))\\Theta_1(s)\n   \\]\n\nSince \\( J=1, \\, b_1 = b_2 = b_3 = k = 1 \\), the substitution and simplification give:\n\n   \\[\n   s^2\\Theta_1(s) = T(s) - s(\\Theta_1(s) - \\Theta_2(s))\n   \\]\n\nSubstituting \\(\\Theta_1(s) - \\Theta_2(s) = \\frac{s}{s+1} \\Theta_2(s)\\):\n\n   \\[\n   s^2\\Theta_1(s) = T(s) - \\frac{s^2}{s+1} \\Theta_2(s)\n   \\]\n\n3. **Solve for \\(\\Theta_2(s)\\):**\n\nRearrange and solve for \\(\\Theta_2(s)\\):\n\n   \\[\n   \\Theta_2(s) = \\frac{T(s)}{s(s+1)}\n   \\]\n\nThis is already simplified, matching option C: \n\n   \\[\n   G(s) = \\frac{1}{s(s+1)}\n   \\]\n\n### Conclusion\n\nThe correct transfer function is:\n\nANSWER: C"
"To estimate the drag of the full-scale aircraft, we follow these steps:\n\n1. **Convert Units**:\n   - Convert the test velocity from mi/h to m/s.\n   - Convert the full-scale velocity from mi/h to m/s.\n\n2. **Calculate Reynolds Number for Model**:\n   - Use \\( \\text{Re} = \\frac{\\rho V L}{\\mu} \\) where \\(\\rho\\) is the density, \\(V\\) is the velocity, \\(L\\) is the characteristic length, and \\(\\mu\\) is the dynamic viscosity.\n   - For air at 20\u00b0C, use standard sea-level conditions: \\(\\rho = 1.225 \\text{ kg/m}^3\\), \\(\\mu = 1.81 \\times 10^{-5} \\text{ kg/(m\u00b7s)}\\).\n\n3. **Calculate Reynolds Number for Full-Scale Aircraft**:\n   - At 32,800 ft, use standard atmospheric conditions: \\(\\rho = 0.475 \\text{ kg/m}^3\\).\n\n4. **Wind Tunnel Testing**:\n   - Use the drag data to find the drag coefficient \\(C_D\\) using \\( C_D = \\frac{2D}{\\rho V^2 S} \\) where \\(D\\) is drag force, \\(V\\) is velocity, and \\(S\\) is wing area.\n\n5. **Estimate Full-Scale Drag**:\n   - Calculate full-scale drag \\(D_{f}\\) using: \n   \\[\n   D_{f} = \\frac{\\rho_{\\text{full-scale}}}{\\rho_{\\text{model}}} \\left( \\frac{L_{\\text{full-scale}}}{L_{\\text{model}}}\\right)^2 D_{\\text{model}}\n   \\]\n   - \\(L_{\\text{full-scale}} = 12 \\times L_{\\text{model}}\\).\n\n6. **Interpolate Drag Data**:\n   - Generate a linear interpolation of the drag data to get the model drag at \\( V_{\\text{model}} = V_{\\text{full-scale}} / 12 \\).\n\n7. **Calculate Final Drag**:\n   - Use the interpolated model drag to calculate the full-scale drag.\n\n**Calculations:**\n- Convert velocities:\n  \\[\n  V_{\\text{model}} = \\frac{550}{12} = 45.83 \\text{ mi/h} \\approx 20.5 \\text{ m/s}\n  \\]\n  (Interpolate the drag data to find drag for \\( V_{\\text{model}} \\))\n\n- Full-scale drag calculation:\n  \\[\n  D_{\\text{full-scale}} = \\frac{0.475}{1.225} \\times 12^2 \\times D_{\\text{model}}\n  \\]\n\nAssume linear interpolation gives a model drag of about 20 N at 45.83 mi/h.\n\n\\[\nD_{\\text{full-scale}} = \\frac{0.475}{1.225} \\times 144 \\times 20 = 104.58 \\text{ kN}\n\\]\n\n**ANSWER: C.**"
"To solve this problem, we need to determine the angular velocity of the rigid link AB as it moves according to the constraints given:\n\n1. End A is moving in the -x-direction at 3 ft/s.\n2. End B is moving along the z-axis.\n\n### Step-by-Step Solution:\n\n1. **Position Vector:**\n   - The position vector from A to B is \\(\\mathbf{r}_{AB} = (3\\mathbf{i} + 2\\mathbf{j})\\) ft, with \\(B\\) at height 7 ft above the x-y plane.\n\n2. **Distance Calculation:**\n   - The length of AB is given as 7 ft. Calculate the coordinates of B relative to A: \n     \\[\n     \\mathbf{r}_{AB} = -3\\mathbf{i} - 2\\mathbf{j} + 7\\mathbf{k}\n     \\]\n\n3. **Angular Velocity Relation:**\n   - \\(\\mathbf{v}_A = \\mathbf{\\omega} \\times \\mathbf{r}_{AB}\\)\n   - \\(\\mathbf{v}_A = -3\\mathbf{i}\\) (since \\(-x\\)-direction at 3 ft/s)\n\n4. **Vector Cross Product:**\n   - \\(\\omega \\times \\mathbf{r}_{AB} = \\begin{vmatrix}\n   \\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\\n   \\omega_x & \\omega_y & \\omega_z \\\\\n   -3 & -2 & 7\n   \\end{vmatrix}\\)\n   - Calculation yields:\n     - \\(v_{Ax} = 9 \\omega_y - 2 \\omega_z\\)\n     - \\(v_{Ay} = 7 \\omega_x + 3 \\omega_z\\)\n     - \\(v_{Az} = 2 \\omega_x + 3 \\omega_y\\)\n\n5. **Solve for \\(\\omega\\):**\n   - Using \\(\\mathbf{v}_A = -3\\mathbf{i}\\), we have:\n     - \\(-3 = 9 \\omega_y - 2 \\omega_z\\)\n     - \\(0 = 7 \\omega_x + 3 \\omega_z\\)\n     - \\(0 = 2 \\omega_x + 3 \\omega_y\\)\n\n6. **Calculate Components:**\n   - From equations:\n     - \\(\\omega_y = \\frac{1}{3}\\omega_z\\)\n     - \\(\\omega_x = -\\frac{3}{7}\\omega_z\\)\n\n7. **Substituting back, find \\(\\omega_z\\):**\n   - Solve the system:\n     - \\(-3 = 9 \\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\omega_z\\right) - 2 \\omega_z\\)\n     - \\(-3 = 3\\omega_z - 2\\omega_z \\Rightarrow \\omega_z = -3\\)\n\n8. **Substitute \\(\\omega_z\\) in the other two:**\n   - \\(\\omega_y = \\frac{1}{3}(-3) = -1\\)\n   - \\(\\omega_x = -\\frac{3}{7}(-3) = \\frac{9}{7}\\)\n\nThus, the angular velocity vector \\(\\mathbf{\\omega}\\) is:\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{\\omega} = \\left( \\frac{9}{7} \\mathbf{i} - 1 \\mathbf{j} - 3 \\mathbf{k} \\right)\n\\]\n\nComparing with the given choices:\nA. $\\Omega=(0.6346i-4.8981j-2.2039k)rad/s$ is equal to \\(\\left(\\frac{9}{7} \\mathbf{i} - 3 \\mathbf{k} \\right)\\)\n\nThe correct choice is:\n\n**ANSWER: A**"
