"To find the minimum permissible diameter \\(d_{\\text{min}}\\) of the rod, we have to consider both tensile and shear stresses and choose the larger diameter to ensure safety under both conditions.\n\n1. **Tensile Stress**: \n\n   The tensile stress \\(\\sigma\\) is given by:\n   \\[\n   \\sigma = \\frac{P}{A} = \\frac{P}{\\frac{\\pi d^2}{4}}\n   \\]\n\n   Setting \\(\\sigma\\) equal to the allowable tensile stress:\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{P}{\\frac{\\pi d^2}{4}} \\leq 118 \\, \\text{MPa}\n   \\]\n\n   Solving for \\(d\\):\n   \\[\n   \\frac{4P}{\\pi d^2} \\leq 118\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   d^2 \\geq \\frac{4 \\times 3.5 \\times 10^3}{\\pi \\times 118}\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   d \\geq \\sqrt{\\frac{14000}{370.88}}\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   d \\geq \\sqrt{37.76}\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   d \\geq 6.144 \\, \\text{mm}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Shear Stress**:\n\n   The shear stress \\(\\tau\\) is also critical because the rod can experience torsion which is not shown but considered in design:\n\n   Assuming shear stress to be on a critical area (hypothetical for this consideration):\n   \\[\n   \\tau = \\frac{P}{A}=\\frac{P}{\\frac{\\pi d^2}{4}}\n   \\]\n\n   Solving similar to tensile for conservative design:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{P}{\\frac{\\pi d^2}{4}} \\leq 48\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   d^2 \\geq \\frac{4 \\times 3.5 \\times 10^3}{\\pi \\times 48}\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   d \\geq \\sqrt{\\frac{14000}{150.8}}\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   d \\geq \\sqrt{92.84}\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   d \\geq 9.638 \\, \\text{mm}\n   \\]\n\nThe larger diameter required for safety considering both tensile and shear stress is \\(9.638 \\, \\text{mm}\\).\n\nSince our options are:\n\n- A. 5.813 mm\n- B. 6.813 mm\n- C. 7.813 mm\n\nThe calculations from above suggest 9.638 mm is necessary for shear, but none of our options that meet this requirement. Among the choices, only considering tension, option B fits closest above the tensile requirement, understanding our calculations.\n\nThus, from provided options:\n\nANSWER: B."
"To solve this problem, we need to apply the principles of conservation of mass and the concept of rolling. The basic idea is that the mass flow rate before rolling is equal to the mass flow rate after rolling.\n\nFirst, we calculate the mass flow rate entering and exiting the roll:\n\n1. **Initial (Entry) Mass Flow Rate:**\n   \\[\n   \\text{Initial Thickness} = 25 \\, \\text{mm} = 0.025 \\, \\text{m}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\text{Initial Speed} = 0.4 \\, \\text{m/s}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\text{Width} = 1.2 \\, \\text{m}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\text{Initial Mass Flow Rate} = \\text{Density} \\times (\\text{Width} \\times \\text{Initial Thickness} \\times \\text{Initial Speed})\n   \\]\n\n2. **Final (Exit) Mass Flow Rate:**\n   \\[\n   \\text{Final Thickness} = 19 \\, \\text{mm} = 0.019 \\, \\text{m}\n   \\]\n   Let the final speed be \\(v_f\\).\n   \\[\n   \\text{Final Mass Flow Rate} = \\text{Density} \\times (\\text{Width} \\times \\text{Final Thickness} \\times v_f)\n   \\]\n\nSetting the initial mass flow rate equal to the final mass flow rate:\n\\[\n0.025 \\times 0.4 = 0.019 \\times v_f\n\\]\nSolving for \\(v_f\\):\n\\[\nv_f = \\frac{0.025 \\times 0.4}{0.019} \\approx 0.526 \\, \\text{m/s}\n\\]\n\nNext, to find the thrust on the bearings, we consider horizontal forces using the momentum principle. The horizontal thrust (T) is due to the change of momentum of the strip in the direction of rolling.\n\n3. **Calculate the thrust T:**\n\n   \\[\n   \\text{Change in momentum per unit time} = \\text{mass flow rate} \\times (\\text{Final speed} - \\text{Initial speed})\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   \\text{Mass flow rate} = \\text{Density} \\times \\text{Width} \\times \\text{Initial Thickness} \\times \\text{Initial Speed}\n   \\]\n\n   Assume unit density for simplicity, as it will cancel out:\n   \\[\n   T = \\text{Width} \\times 0.025 \\times 0.4 \\times (0.526 - 0.4) \n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   T = 1.2 \\times 0.025 \\times 0.4 \\times 0.126 \n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   T = 0.001512 \\, \\text{N} \n   \\]\n\nSince the problem asks for only one of the options as the correct horizontal thrust:\n\nANSWER: A."
