"To solve this problem, we need to analyze the vertical momentum balance for the fluid flowing through the box. The vertical forces are balanced by the change in momentum.\n\n**Given:**\n- Velocity at ports \\(v = 7 \\, \\text{m/s}\\)\n- Vertical momentum force on the box = 36 N\n- The angle at which ports (2) and (3) are inclined = 40\u00b0\n\n**Steps:**\n\n1. **Identify the Forces:**\n   - The vertical component of the momentum at ports (2) and (3) acts as a force on the box due to the change in the vertical momentum of the exit streams.\n   - There is no vertical component of momentum at port (1), as it is a horizontal inlet.\n\n2. **Vertical Momentum Balance:**\n   \\[\n   \\dot{m} \\times v \\times \\sin 40^\\circ \\text{ (at port 2)} + \\dot{m} \\times v \\times \\sin 40^\\circ \\text{ (at port 3)} = 36 \\, \\text{N}\n   \\]\n\n   This equation simplifies to:\n   \\[\n   2 \\cdot \\dot{m} \\times v \\times \\sin 40^\\circ = 36\n   \\]\n\n3. **Solve for \\(\\dot{m}\\):**\n   \\[\n   2 \\cdot \\dot{m} \\cdot 7 \\cdot \\sin 40^\\circ = 36\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   \\dot{m} = \\frac{36}{2 \\cdot 7 \\cdot \\sin 40^\\circ}\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   \\dot{m} = \\frac{36}{14 \\cdot 0.6428}\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   \\dot{m} \\approx \\frac{36}{8.9992} \\approx 4.000 \\, \\text{kg/s}\n   \\]\n\nThe required inlet mass flow rate (\\(\\dot{m}\\)) needed is approximately \\(4.000 \\, \\text{kg/s}\\).\n\nIt seems there might be an error or additional information needed because the calculated value doesn't match any of the provided options. Let's combine the math and the figure correctly:\n\nContinuing with the calculations:\n\n   \\[\n   \\dot{m} = \\frac{36}{2 \\times 7 \\times 0.6428} = \\frac{36}{8.9992} \\approx 4 \\, \\text{kg/s}\n   \\]\n\nI'll reconsider the original options interpretation:\nSuppose a proportion exists to consider multiple inlets and outlets (presuming the primary analysis was correct): The angles might rely initially on not mental assumptions.\n\nConfirming st