"To find the value of \\( h \\) at which vapor cavities will form at the throat of the nozzle, we need to use Bernoulli's equation and the concept of cavitation.\n\n### Given:\n- The ambient pressure \\( p_a = 100 \\, \\text{kPa} \\)\n- Vapor pressure of water at \\( 30^\\circ C \\approx 4.24 \\, \\text{kPa} \\)\n- Diameter at the throat \\( D_1 = 5 \\, \\text{cm} \\)\n- Diameter at exit \\( D_2 = 8 \\, \\text{cm} \\)\n\n### Approach:\n1. **Apply Bernoulli\u2019s Equation from the water surface to the throat:**\n\n   \\( p_a + \\rho g h = p_v + \\frac{1}{2} \\rho v_1^2 \\)\n\n   Rearranging to find \\( v_1 \\):\n\n   \\[\n   v_1 = \\sqrt{\\frac{2(p_a - p_v + \\rho g h)}{\\rho}}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Continuity Equation:**\n\n   \\[\n   A_1 v_1 = A_2 v_2\n   \\]\n\n   Where \\( A_1 = \\frac{\\pi}{4} D_1^2 \\) and \\( A_2 = \\frac{\\pi}{4} D_2^2 \\).\n\n   \\[\n   v_2 = \\frac{A_1}{A_2} v_1 = \\left(\\frac{D_1}{D_2}\\right)^2 v_1\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculate:**\n\n   \\[\n   \\frac{1}{2} \\rho v_1^2 = p_a - p_v + \\rho g h\n   \\]\n\n   Solving:\n\n   \\[\n   \\rho \\times 9.81 \\times h = 100,000 - 4,240\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   h = \\frac{(100,000 - 4,240)}{\\rho \\times 9.81}\n   \\]\n\n   Given water density \\( \\rho = 1000 \\, \\text{kg/m}^3 \\):\n\n   \\[\n   h = \\frac{95,760}{1000 \\times 9.81} \\approx 9.76 \\, \\text{m}\n   \\]\n\n   However, reconciling with above, cavitation condition \\( p_v \\) is at diam 1 where velocity \\( v_1 = \\sqrt{2gh} \\) applies.\n\n   Let's set up:\n\n\\[\nh = \\frac{p_a - p_v}{\\rho g} = \\frac{95.76}{9.81} \\approx 9.76 \\, \\text{m}\n\\]\n\nThis computation confirms that the cavitation will certainly occurs when this height is surpassed.\n\n### Choices\n- A. 1.56 m (incorrect, miscalculation in earlier assumptions)\n- B. 1.76 m (incorrect, also earlier assumptions based)\n- C. 1.96 m\n\nGiven no direct match in similar rounded off practical application tests with lossless assumptions, consider:\n\nANSWER: C"
