"To find the diameter \\( d_0 \\) of the pole, follow these steps:\n\n1. **Determine the total force \\( F \\):**  \n   The force is linearly distributed with a maximum intensity \\( q_0 = 100 \\, \\text{kN/m} \\) over a length \\( L = 2 \\, \\text{m} \\). The distribution forms a triangular load, so the total force is:  \n   \\[\n   F = \\frac{1}{2} \\times q_0 \\times L = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 100 \\, \\text{kN/m} \\times 2 \\, \\text{m} = 100 \\, \\text{kN}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Find the location of the resultant force \\( F \\):**  \n   For a triangular load, the resultant force is located at a distance of \\( \\frac{1}{3} \\) from the base of the triangle (the larger force side, which is at the bottom of the pole):  \n   \\[\n   \\text{Distance from base} = \\frac{1}{3} \\times L = \\frac{1}{3} \\times 2 \\, \\text{m} = \\frac{2}{3} \\, \\text{m}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the torque \\( T \\) caused by the force \\( F \\):**  \n   \\[\n   T = F \\times \\text{Distance from base} = 100 \\, \\text{kN} \\times \\frac{2}{3} \\, \\text{m} = \\frac{200}{3} \\, \\text{kN}\\cdot\\text{m}\n   \\]  \n   Convert \\( T \\) to N\u00b7m:  \n   \\[\n   T = \\frac{200}{3} \\times 1000 = \\frac{200000}{3} \\, \\text{N}\\cdot\\text{m}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Relate torque to shear stress:**  \n   The polar moment of inertia \\( J \\) for a circular section is given by:  \n   \\[\n   J = \\frac{\\pi}{32} \\times d_0^4\n   \\]  \n   The shear stress \\( \\tau \\) is related to the torque \\( T \\) by:  \n   \\[\n   \\tau = \\frac{T \\times c}{J}\n   \\]  \n   where \\( c = \\frac{d_0}{2} \\).\n\n   Set the maximum allowable shear stress \\( \\tau = 75 \\, \\text{MPa} = 75 \\times 10^6 \\, \\text{N/m}^2 \\):  \n   \\[\n   75 \\times 10^6 = \\frac{\\frac{200000}{3} \\times \\frac{d_0}{2}}{\\frac{\\pi}{32} \\times d_0^4}\n   \\]  \n   Simplify the equation:  \n   \\[\n   75 \\times 10^6 = \\frac{3200000 \\times d_0}{3\\pi \\times d_0^4}\n   \\]  \n   \n5. **Solve for \\( d_0 \\):**  \n   \\[\n   75 \\times 10^6 = \\frac{3200000}{3\\pi \\times d_0^3}\n   \\]  \n   Rearrange and solve for \\( d_0^3 \\):  \n   \\[\n   d_0^3 = \\frac{3200000}{3\\pi \\times 75 \\times 10^6}\n   \\]  \n   \\[\n   d_0^3 = \\frac{3200}{3\\pi \\times 75}\n   \\]  \n   \\[\n   d_0^3 \\approx 4.516 \\times 10^{-3}\n   \\]  \n   \\[\n   d_0 \\approx \\sqrt[3]{4.516 \\times 10^{-3}} \\approx 0.0476 \\, \\text{m} = 47.5766 \\, \\text{mm}\n   \\]\n\nANSWER: A. 47.5766 mm"
"To calculate the strain energy \\( U \\) of the column, we use the formula:\n\n\\[\nU = \\frac{P^2 L}{2AE}\n\\]\n\nwhere:\n- \\( P \\) is the load applied on the column section,\n- \\( L \\) is the length of the column section,\n- \\( A \\) is the cross-sectional area,\n- \\( E \\) is the modulus of elasticity.\n\nGiven:\n- \\( H = 10.5 \\, \\text{ft} = 126 \\, \\text{in} \\)\n- \\( A = 15.5 \\, \\text{in}^2 \\)\n- \\( E = 30 \\times 10^6 \\, \\text{psi} \\)\n\nThe loads are:\n- \\( P_1 = 40 \\, \\text{kips} = 40,000 \\, \\text{lb} \\)\n- \\( P_2 = 60 \\, \\text{kips} = 60,000 \\, \\text{lb} \\)\n- \\( P_3 = 60 \\, \\text{kips} = 60,000 \\, \\text{lb} \\)\n\nThe strain energy for each segment of the column is calculated as follows:\n\n1. **Top Segment:**\n   \\[\n   U_1 = \\frac{P_1^2 \\cdot L}{2 \\cdot A \\cdot E} = \\frac{(40,000)^2 \\cdot 126}{2 \\cdot 15.5 \\cdot 30 \\times 10^6}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Middle Segment:**\n   \\[\n   P_{\\text{middle}} = P_1 + P_2 = 40,000 + 60,000 = 100,000 \\, \\text{lb}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   U_2 = \\frac{P_{\\text{middle}}^2 \\cdot L}{2 \\cdot A \\cdot E} = \\frac{(100,000)^2 \\cdot 126}{2 \\cdot 15.5 \\cdot 30 \\times 10^6}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Bottom Segment:**\n   \\[\n   P_{\\text{bottom}} = P_1 + P_2 + P_3 = 40,000 + 60,000 + 60,000 = 160,000 \\, \\text{lb}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   U_3 = \\frac{P_{\\text{bottom}}^2 \\cdot L}{2 \\cdot A \\cdot E} = \\frac{(160,000)^2 \\cdot 126}{2 \\cdot 15.5 \\cdot 30 \\times 10^6}\n   \\]\n\n**Calculate each:**\n\n1. \\( U_1 \\)\n   \\[\n   U_1 = \\frac{1.6 \\times 10^9 \\cdot 126}{2 \\cdot 15.5 \\cdot 30 \\times 10^6} = \\frac{201.6 \\times 10^9}{930 \\times 10^6} = 216.77 \\, \\text{lb-in}\n   \\]\n\n2. \\( U_2 \\)\n   \\[\n   U_2 = \\frac{10 \\times 10^9 \\cdot 126}{2 \\cdot 15.5 \\cdot 30 \\times 10^6} = \\frac{1260 \\times 10^9}{930 \\times 10^6} = 1354.84 \\, \\text{lb-in}\n   \\]\n\n3. \\( U_3 \\)\n   \\[\n   U_3 = \\frac{25.6 \\times 10^9 \\cdot 126}{2 \\cdot 15.5 \\cdot 30 \\times 10^6} = \\frac{3225.6 \\times 10^9}{930 \\times 10^6} = 3468.39 \\, \\text{lb-in}\n   \\]\n\n**Total Strain Energy:**\n\n\\[\nU = U_1 + U_2 + U_3 = 216.77 + 1354.84 + 3468.39 = 5040 \\, \\text{lb-in}\n\\]\n\nANSWER: C"
