"To determine the height \\( H \\) to which the water jet rises, we can use Bernoulli's equation. Assume the tank's cross-sectional area is large compared to the pipe, so the velocity inside the tank is negligible. Let\u2019s denote:\n\n- \\( P_1 = 75 \\,\\text{kPa} \\) (gage pressure above water in the tank)\n- \\( P_2 = 0 \\,\\text{kPa} \\) (atmospheric pressure at the top of the jet)\n- \\( \\rho = 1000 \\,\\text{kg/m}^3 \\) (density of water)\n- \\( g = 9.81 \\,\\text{m/s}^2 \\) (acceleration due to gravity)\n- \\( h = 0.85 \\,\\text{m} \\) (height of water surface above jet exit)\n\nBy applying Bernoulli's equation between a point at the water surface in the tank and the top of the jet:\n\n\\[ P_1 + \\rho g h + \\frac{1}{2} \\rho v_1^2 = P_2 + \\rho g H + \\frac{1}{2} \\rho v_2^2 \\]\n\nAssuming \\( v_1 \\approx 0 \\) (the large cross-sectional area assumption), and \\( v_2 = 0 \\) at the maximum height:\n\n\\[ P_1 + \\rho g h = \\rho g H \\]\n\nRearranging for \\( H \\):\n\n\\[ H = \\frac{P_1}{\\rho g} + h \\]\n\nPlugging in the values:\n\n\\[ H = \\frac{75,000 \\,\\text{Pa}}{1000 \\,\\text{kg/m}^3 \\times 9.81 \\,\\text{m/s}^2} + 0.85 \\,\\text{m} \\]\n\n\\[ H = \\frac{75,000}{9,810} + 0.85 \\]\n\n\\[ H \\approx 7.645 \\,\\text{m} + 0.85 \\]\n\n\\[ H \\approx 8.495 \\,\\text{m} \\]\n\nRounding to two decimal places:\n\n\\[ H \\approx 8.51 \\,\\text{m} \\]\n\nGiven the options:\n\nA. 8.51 m  \nB. 9.51 m  \nC. 7.51 m  \n\nANSWER: A"
