"To solve this problem, we need to determine both the power transmitted to the pulley in horsepower and the rotational speed of the pulley in revolutions per minute (rpm).\n\n### 1. Power Transmitted to the Pulley\n\nThe power (\\(P\\)) transmitted to the pulley can be calculated as the product of the force acting on the object and its velocity. The force is equal to the weight of the object:\n\n\\[\nF = mg = 5 \\, \\text{lb} \n\\]\n\nThe object is moving at a constant velocity, so the power (\\(P\\)) is:\n\n\\[\nP = F \\cdot v = 5 \\, \\text{lb} \\times 5 \\, \\text{ft/s} = 25 \\, \\text{ft} \\cdot \\text{lb/s}\n\\]\n\nTo convert this to horsepower (\\(1 \\, \\text{hp} = 550 \\, \\text{ft} \\cdot \\text{lb/s}\\)):\n\n\\[\nP = \\frac{25}{550} \\, \\text{hp} \\approx 0.0455 \\, \\text{hp}\n\\]\n\n### 2. Rotational Speed of the Pulley\n\nThe linear velocity (\\(v\\)) is related to the angular velocity (\\(\\omega\\)) by:\n\n\\[\nv = \\omega \\cdot r\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(v = 5 \\, \\text{ft/s}\\) and the radius \\(r\\) is 3 inches. We need to convert inches to feet (\\(r = 3 \\, \\text{in} = 0.25 \\, \\text{ft}\\)).\n\n\\[\n5 \\, \\text{ft/s} = \\omega \\cdot 0.25 \\, \\text{ft}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\omega = \\frac{5}{0.25} = 20 \\, \\text{rad/s}\n\\]\n\nConvert angular velocity to revolutions per minute (rpm):\n\n\\[\n\\omega_{\\text{rpm}} = \\omega \\cdot \\frac{60}{2\\pi} = 20 \\cdot \\frac{60}{2\\pi} \\approx 191 \\, \\text{rpm}\n\\]\n\n### Conclusion\n\n- Power: approximately 0.045 hp\n- Rotational speed: approximately 191 rpm\n\nBased on these calculations, the correct choice is:\n\nANSWER: B. 0.045hp,191rpm."
