"To determine the maximum load \\( P \\) that can be applied without causing sudden fracture, we use the fracture toughness equation for a central crack:\n\n\\[ K_I = \\sigma \\sqrt{\\pi c} \\]\n\nwhere:\n- \\( K_I \\) is the stress intensity factor\n- \\( \\sigma \\) is the applied stress\n- \\( c \\) is half the crack length\n\nGiven:\n- \\( K_{Ic} = 110 \\, \\text{ksi} \\, \\sqrt{\\text{in}} \\)\n- \\( 2c = 1 \\, \\text{in} \\) so \\( c = 0.5 \\, \\text{in} \\)\n- Width \\( 2w = 6 \\, \\text{in} \\)\n\n\nWe set \\( K_I = K_{Ic} \\) for the condition right before sudden fracture:\n\n\\[ 110 = \\sigma \\sqrt{\\pi \\times 0.5} \\]\n\nSolving for \\( \\sigma \\):\n\n\\[ \\sigma = \\frac{110}{\\sqrt{\\pi \\times 0.5}} \\]\n\nCalculate:\n\n\\[ \\sigma = \\frac{110}{\\sqrt{\\pi \\times 0.5}} \\approx \\frac{110}{1.2533} \\approx 87.77 \\, \\text{ksi} \\]\n\nNow, we find the load \\( P \\) using the relation:\n\n\\[ \\sigma = \\frac{P}{t \\times w} \\]\n\nwhere \\( t = 0.06 \\, \\text{in} \\) and \\( w = 3 \\, \\text{in} \\) (since \\( 2w = 6 \\, \\text{in} \\)):\n\n\\[ P = \\sigma \\times t \\times w = 87.77 \\times 0.06 \\times 3 \\]\n\nCalculate:\n\n\\[ P \\approx 87.77 \\times 0.18 \\approx 15.7986 \\, \\text{ksi-in}\\]\n\nSince \\( P \\) must be in pounds, convert \\( \\text{ksi-in} \\) to pounds by multiplying by 1000 (since ksi = kips per square inch):\n\n\\[ P \\approx 15798.6 \\, \\text{lb} \\]\n\nTherefore, the closest answer choice, given the options, is:\n\nANSWER: A. 21245.12 lb."
