"To estimate the shaft diameter required to produce a critical speed of rotation, we can apply the formula for the critical speed of a shaft carrying a center load:\n\n\\[\nN_c = \\frac{1}{2\\pi} \\sqrt{\\frac{g}{\\delta}}\n\\]\n\nWhere:\n- \\( N_c \\) = critical speed in revolutions per minute (rpm)\n- \\( g \\) = acceleration due to gravity (~9.81 m/s\u00b2)\n- \\( \\delta \\) = deflection at the center of the shaft in meters\n\nThe deflection \\(\\delta\\) for a beam carrying a centralized load is given by:\n\n\\[\n\\delta = \\frac{PL^3}{48EI}\n\\]\n\nWhere:\n- \\( P \\) = load on the shaft (in newtons)\n- \\( L \\) = length of the shaft (in meters)\n- \\( E \\) = modulus of elasticity of aluminum (approximately \\(70 \\times 10^9 \\, \\text{N/m}^2\\))\n- \\( I \\) = moment of inertia\n\nThe moment of inertia \\( I \\) for a cylindrical shaft is:\n\n\\[\nI = \\frac{\\pi d^4}{64}\n\\]\n\nWhere \\( d \\) is the diameter of the shaft. \n\nSteps:\n\n1. Convert load to newtons: \\( P = 40 \\, \\text{kg} \\times 9.81 \\, \\text{m/s}^2 = 392.4 \\, \\text{N} \\)\n\n2. We need \\(N_c = 250 \\, \\text{rpm}\\). Convert it to radians per second using the formula:\n   \\[\n   N_c = \\frac{1}{2\\pi} \\sqrt{\\frac{g}{\\delta}}\n   \\]\n\n3. Rearrange for \\(\\delta\\) and equate it to deflection formula:\n   \\[\n   \\delta = \\frac{g}{(2\\pi N_c)^2}\n   \\]\n\n4. Calculate \\(\\delta\\):\n   \\[\n   \\delta = \\frac{9.81}{(2\\pi \\times 250/60)^2} = \\frac{9.81}{(2\\pi \\times 4.1667)^2} \\approx 0.0002406 \\, \\text{m}\n   \\]\n\n5. Equate \\(\\frac{PL^3}{48EI} = \\delta\\) and solve for \\(d\\):\n   \\[\n   \\frac{392.4 \\times 1^3}{48 \\times 70 \\times 10^9 \\times \\frac{\\pi d^4}{64}} = 0.0002406\n   \\]\n\n6. Solve for \\(d\\):\n   \\[\n   d = \\left(\\frac{392.4 \\times 64}{48 \\times 70 \\times 10^9 \\times 0.0002406 \\times \\pi}\\right)^{0.25}\n   \\]\n\n   \\[\n   d \\approx 0.05422 \\, \\text{m} = 54.22 \\, \\text{mm}\n   \\]\n\nThe correct diameter required is closest to option B.\n\nANSWER: B."
