"To determine whether silver (Ag) is oxidized or reduced, and to identify the oxidizing and reducing agents, we need to look at the changes in oxidation states.\n\n1. **Assign oxidation states:**\n   - Ag starts as Ag(s) with an oxidation state of 0.\n   - In AgS, Ag typically has an oxidation state of +1, assuming Ag is Ag\u207a and S is S\u00b2\u207b.\n\n2. **Oxidation/reduction:**\n   - Ag goes from 0 to +1, which means it is oxidized (loss of electrons).\n   - H\u2082 goes from +1 (in H\u2082S) to 0 (in H\u2082), which means it is reduced (gain of electrons).\n\n3. **Identify agents:**\n   - The oxidizing agent is the species that causes oxidation by accepting electrons; in this case, it's hydrogen (H) in H\u2082S.\n   - The reducing agent is the species that causes reduction by donating electrons; in this case, it's silver (Ag).\n\nTherefore, silver is oxidized, the oxidizing agent is H, and the reducing agent is Ag.\n\nANSWER: C."
