"To find the empirical formula, let's look at the percent composition of phenylalanine shown in the graph and compare it to the atomic masses:\n\n1. **Carbon (C):** Approximately 65%  \n   - Molar mass of C = 12 g/mol\n\n2. **Hydrogen (H):** Approximately 7%  \n   - Molar mass of H = 1 g/mol\n\n3. **Oxygen (O):** Approximately 19%  \n   - Molar mass of O = 16 g/mol\n\n4. **Nitrogen (N):** Approximately 9%  \n   - Molar mass of N = 14 g/mol\n\nNow, convert these percentages to moles for a sample size of 100 g:\n\n- Moles of C = 65 g / 12 g/mol = 5.42\n- Moles of H = 7 g / 1 g/mol = 7\n- Moles of O = 19 g / 16 g/mol = 1.19\n- Moles of N = 9 g / 14 g/mol = 0.64\n\nNext, find the simplest whole number ratio:\n\n1. Divide each by the smallest number of moles calculated (0.64 moles of N):\n\n   - C: 5.42 / 0.64 \u2248 8.47\n   - H: 7 / 0.64 \u2248 10.94\n   - O: 1.19 / 0.64 \u2248 1.86\n   - N: 0.64 / 0.64 = 1\n\n2. Round these numbers to the nearest whole numbers:\n\n   - C: 8.47 \u2248 9\n   - H: 10.94 \u2248 11\n   - O: 1.86 \u2248 2\n   - N: 1\n\nThus, the empirical formula for phenylalanine is \\( \\text{C}_9\\text{H}_{11}\\text{O}_2\\text{N} \\).\n\nANSWER: D"
"To determine the activation energy (Ea) from the reaction diagram:\n\n1. **Identify Activation Energy (Ea):**\n   - Ea is the energy difference between the reactants and the transition state (the highest point on the diagram).\n\n2. **Locate Key Points on the Diagram:**\n   - **V**: Energy level of reactants.\n   - **X**: Energy level of the transition state.\n\n3. **Calculate Ea:**\n   - Ea = Energy of the transition state (X) \u2212 Energy of the reactants (V).\n   - Therefore, Ea = X \u2212 V.\n\nDouble-check the options given:\n\nA. V-Z  \nB. V+Z  \nC. X  \nD. W+Y  \nE. X-V\n\nThe correct calculation that matches our determination of Ea is option **E: X-V**.\n\nANSWER: E"
