"To solve this problem, we need to balance the chemical equation and then perform stoichiometric calculations.\n\n1. **Balance the Equation**:  \n   The unbalanced equation is:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Fe}_2\\text{O}_3(s) + \\text{CO}(g) \\rightarrow \\text{Fe}(s) + \\text{CO}_2(g)\n   \\]\n   Balanced equation:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Fe}_2\\text{O}_3(s) + 3\\text{CO}(g) \\rightarrow 2\\text{Fe}(s) + 3\\text{CO}_2(g)\n   \\]\n\n2. **Calculate Moles of Fe\u2082O\u2083**:\n   - Molar mass of Fe\u2082O\u2083 = \\(2 \\times 55.85 + 3 \\times 16.00 = 159.70 \\text{ g/mol}\\)\n   - Convert 4.00 kg to grams: \\(4000 \\text{ g}\\)\n   - Moles of Fe\u2082O\u2083 = \\(\\frac{4000 \\text{ g}}{159.70 \\text{ g/mol}} \\approx 25.05 \\text{ moles}\\)\n\n3. **Use Stoichiometry to Find Moles of CO**:\n   - According to the balanced equation, 1 mole of Fe\u2082O\u2083 requires 3 moles of CO.\n   - Moles of CO needed = \\(25.05 \\text{ moles Fe}_2\\text{O}_3 \\times 3 \\text{ moles CO/mole Fe}_2\\text{O}_3 = 75.15 \\text{ moles CO}\\)\n\nANSWER: A."
"To determine which diagram represents a gram-positive bacterium, we consider the following:\n\n1. **Gram-positive bacteria**: \n   - Have a thick peptidoglycan layer.\n   - Contain teichoic acids.\n   - Lack an outer membrane that contains lipopolysaccharides.\n\n2. **Diagram (a)**:\n   - Displays a thick peptidoglycan layer.\n   - Shows teichoic acid present.\n   - Lacks an outer lipid layer with lipopolysaccharides.\n\n3. **Diagram (b)**:\n   - Shows a thinner peptidoglycan layer.\n   - Includes an outer membrane with lipopolysaccharides and phospholipids.\n   - Does not have teichoic acids.\n\nBased on these observations, diagram (a) represents a gram-positive bacterium.\n\nANSWER: A."
"To determine the Minimum Inhibitory Concentration (MIC), we need to find the lowest concentration of the antibiotic that prevents visible growth of the microorganism.\n\nLet's analyze the data:\n\n- At **200 \u00b5g/ml**: No growth (\u2013), no growth in subculture (\u2013)\n- At **100 \u00b5g/ml**: No growth (\u2013), but growth in subculture (+)\n- At **50 \u00b5g/ml**: Growth (+)\n- At **25 \u00b5g/ml**: Growth (+)\n\nThe MIC is the lowest concentration where no visible growth is observed. Here, it's 100 \u00b5g/ml where there is no growth initially, even though growth is seen in subculture. However, the test indicates 100 \u00b5g/ml effectively inhibits growth in the main culture.\n\nANSWER: C"
