"To determine if a reaction will occur between \\( P \\) and \\( T^+ \\), and what the products will be, follow these steps using the activity series provided:\n\n1. **Identify Reactivity**: \n   - \\( P \\) and \\( T \\) are in the series. \\( P \\) is above \\( T \\), which means \\( P \\) is a stronger reducing agent than \\( T \\).\n\n2. **Determine the Reaction**:\n   - Since \\( P \\) is a stronger reducing agent, it can reduce \\( T^+ \\) to \\( T \\).\n\n3. **Determine the Products**:\n   - If \\( P \\) reacts with \\( T^+ \\), \\( P \\) will lose electrons to form \\( P^+ \\), and \\( T^+ \\) will gain electrons to form \\( T \\).\n\nGiven the above analysis, the products of the reaction will be \\( P^+ \\) and \\( T \\). \n\nThus, the correct answer is:\n\nANSWER: B."
"To determine the product indicated by the letter C in the diagram, follow these steps:\n\n1. **Identify the starting material (A):** The starting molecule, A, is phosphatidylinositol 4,5-bisphosphate (PIP2), which consists of a glycerol backbone, two fatty acid chains, and an inositol ring with phosphate groups.\n\n2. **Identify the products (C and D):** The reaction shows the cleavage of PIP2 into two separate molecules. \n\n   - Molecule D contains the glycerol backbone with the two fatty acid chains (diacylglycerol, or DAG).\n   - Molecule C is left with the inositol ring bound to two phosphate groups.\n\n3. **Analyze molecule C:** The inositol ring has two phosphate groups attached, suggesting it is inositol diphosphate.\n\nBased on these observations, the product identified by the letter C is:\n\nANSWER: C. inositol diphosphate."
