"To determine which sphere represents a potassium ion (K\u207a) versus a potassium atom (K), we need to consider the fundamental difference between atoms and ions:\n\n1. **Potassium Atom (K):** \n   - Neutral atom.\n   - Contains an equal number of protons and electrons.\n\n2. **Potassium Ion (K\u207a):**\n   - Formed when a potassium atom loses one electron.\n   - Positively charged with one less electron than a neutral potassium atom.\n\nWhen potassium loses an electron to form a K\u207a ion, it loses part of its outer electron shell, resulting in a smaller radius. Therefore, the potassium ion is smaller than the neutral potassium atom.\n\nGiven this information:\n\n- Sphere A is smaller than Sphere B.\n- Sphere A represents the potassium ion (due to its smaller size).\n- Sphere B represents the potassium atom.\n\nANSWER: A."
"To find the number of moles of each product, we'll follow these steps:\n\n1. **Balance the Chemical Equation:**\n   \\[\n   \\text{Fe}_2\\text{O}_3(s) + 3\\text{CO}(g) \\rightarrow 2\\text{Fe}(s) + 3\\text{CO}_2(g)\n   \\]\n\n2. **Moles of Fe\\(_2\\)O\\(_3\\) given:**\n   You need this information to calculate the moles of products formed. Assuming 25.05 moles of Fe\\(_2\\)O\\(_3\\) (since options revolve around three times this value).\n\n3. **Mole Ratio:**\n   - From the balanced equation, 1 mole of Fe\\(_2\\)O\\(_3\\) produces 2 moles of Fe and 3 moles of CO\\(_2\\).\n\n4. **Calculate Moles of Products:**\n   - Moles of Fe produced = 2 \u00d7 moles of Fe\\(_2\\)O\\(_3\\) = 2 \u00d7 25.05 = 50.10 moles\n   - Moles of CO\\(_2\\) produced = 3 \u00d7 moles of Fe\\(_2\\)O\\(_3\\) = 3 \u00d7 25.05 = 75.15 moles\n\nThe moles of CO\\(_2\\) formed match option A.\n\nANSWER: A"
"To determine which element is the strongest oxidizing agent, we need to understand the properties of the metals listed. In the context of this chart:\n\n1. The left column lists metals in order of their tendency to lose electrons and form positive ions. Metals higher up are more likely to lose electrons, making them better reducing agents. \n\n2. The right column lists metal ions in order of their tendency to gain electrons. Ions lower down have a higher tendency to gain electrons, making them better oxidizing agents. \n\nGiven this:\n\n- Na is high in the metal column, meaning it is a strong reducing agent, not an oxidizing agent.\n- Fe is in the middle.\n- Cu is lower on the list in the metal column but higher in the ion column.\n\nSince Cu^2+ ions are lower in the ion column, they are better at gaining electrons. Therefore, Cu is the strongest oxidizing agent among Na, Fe, and Cu.\n\nANSWER: C."
