"To determine the order of increasing entropy, we need to understand entropy as a measure of disorder or randomness in a system. In the context of particles or atoms, higher entropy corresponds to more random distribution and less order.\n\nAnalyzing the images:\n\n- Window a: The particles appear to be loosely arranged and more spread out, indicating higher randomness.\n- Window b: The particles are more densely packed but still retain some degree of randomness.\n- Window c: The particles are closely packed and ordered, suggesting the lowest entropy among the three.\n\nArranging in order of increasing entropy, we get:\n\nc (lowest disorder) < b < a (highest disorder).\n\nHence, the correct order is c, b, a.\n\nANSWER: C. c>b>a."
