"To determine the phase changes for CO2 when heated from -100\u00b0C to -10\u00b0C at a constant pressure of 6 atm, follow these steps:\n\n1. **Identify Starting Point:**\n   - At -100\u00b0C and 6 atm, CO2 is in the solid state (as per the phase diagram).\n\n2. **Temperature Increase:**\n   - As temperature increases, CO2 will stay in the solid phase until it reaches the sublimation curve (where solid turns directly into gas).\n\n3. **Phase Change:**\n   - At around -78.5\u00b0C (the sublimation point at 6 atm), CO2 will change from solid to gas because it bypasses the liquid phase at this pressure.\n\n4. **End Point:**\n   - Beyond -78.5\u00b0C up to -10\u00b0C, CO2 remains in the gaseous state.\n\nBased on this analysis, CO2 undergoes sublimation from solid to gas without forming a liquid phase. \n\nANSWER: C. Gaseous state."
