"To calculate the standard cell potential (\\(E^\\circ_{\\text{cell}}\\)) for this voltaic cell, we need to use the standard reduction potentials of the two half reactions.\n\n1. **Identify the half-reactions**:\n   - The oxidation half-reaction (anode): \n     \\[ \\text{Fe(s)} \\rightarrow \\text{Fe}^{2+}(\\text{aq}) + 2\\text{e}^- \\]\n     The standard reduction potential for \\(\\text{Fe}^{2+}/\\text{Fe}\\) is \\( -0.44 \\, \\text{V} \\).\n\n   - The reduction half-reaction (cathode):\n     \\[ \\text{Ni}^{2+}(\\text{aq}) + 2\\text{e}^- \\rightarrow \\text{Ni(s)} \\]\n     The standard reduction potential for \\(\\text{Ni}^{2+}/\\text{Ni}\\) is \\( -0.25 \\, \\text{V} \\).\n\n2. **Calculate the standard cell potential**:\n   - The standard cell potential is calculated using the formula:\n     \\[\n     E^\\circ_{\\text{cell}} = E^\\circ_{\\text{cathode}} - E^\\circ_{\\text{anode}}\n     \\]\n   - Substitute the values:\n     \\[\n     E^\\circ_{\\text{cell}} = (-0.25 \\, \\text{V}) - (-0.44 \\, \\text{V})\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     E^\\circ_{\\text{cell}} = -0.25 \\, \\text{V} + 0.44 \\, \\text{V}\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     E^\\circ_{\\text{cell}} = 0.19 \\, \\text{V}\n     \\]\n\nThus, the standard cell potential is 0.19 V.\n\nANSWER: D"
"To determine if the reaction between P and M\\(^+\\) will occur, we need to assess their positions in the activity series.\n\n1. **Understand the Activity Series:** The table lists reducing agents on the left and corresponding oxidizing agents on the right. The upward trend on the left column specifies increasing reducing strength, while the downward trend on the right column specifies increasing oxidizing strength. \n\n2. **Locate Reactants:** \n   - P is stronger than M as a reducing agent (P is below M in the reducing agents column).\n   - M\\(^+\\) is stronger than P\\(^+\\) as an oxidizing agent (M\\(^+\\) is above P\\(^+\\) in the oxidizing agents column).\n\n3. **Determine Reaction Feasibility:**\n   - A reaction will occur if the reducing agent is stronger than the oxidizing agent. Here, P is stronger than M\\(^+\\), so the reaction should occur.\n\nBased on this reasoning, the statement is true.\n\nANSWER: A. True."
