"To determine spontaneity in reactions based on temperature, we consider the signs of \\(\\Delta S\\) (entropy change) and \\(\\Delta H\\) (enthalpy change).\n\n**(A) \\( \\text{H}_2\\text{O(l)} \\rightarrow \\text{H}_2\\text{O(g)} \\):** \n- \\(\\Delta S = +\\) (increasing disorder when going from liquid to gas)\n- \\(\\Delta H = +\\) (endothermic, requires energy)\n- Follows the pattern with \\(\\Delta G\\) positive or negative at high \\(T\\)\n\n**(B) \\( \\text{H}_2\\text{O(g)} \\rightarrow \\text{H}_2\\text{O(l)} \\):** \n- \\(\\Delta S = -\\) (decreasing disorder when going from gas to liquid)\n- \\(\\Delta H = -\\) (exothermic, releases energy)\n- Follows the pattern with \\(\\Delta G\\) negative at low \\(T\\)\n\n**(C) \\( \\text{H}_2\\text{O(s)} \\rightarrow \\text{H}_2\\text{O(l)} \\):** \n- \\(\\Delta S = +\\) (increasing disorder when going from solid to liquid)\n- \\(\\Delta H = +\\) (endothermic, requires energy)\n- Follows the pattern with \\(\\Delta G\\) positive or negative at high \\(T\\)\n\nSince the reactions that are favored at low temperatures require negative \\(\\Delta S\\) and \\(\\Delta H\\) as in option (B), and those favored at high temperatures require positive \\(\\Delta S\\) and \\(\\Delta H\\) as in options (A) and (C), \n\nThe correct spontaneous condition is:\n\n**ANSWER: D (high, low, high).**"
