"To determine if penicillin has no effect on most gram-negative cells, we need to understand the structure of their cell walls.\n\n1. **Diagram Analysis**: \n   - Diagram (a) shows a thick layer of peptidoglycan above the plasma membrane, typical for gram-positive bacteria.\n   - Diagram (b) shows a thin layer of peptidoglycan sandwiched between an outer membrane and the plasma membrane, typical for gram-negative bacteria.\n\n2. **Penicillin Target**:\n   - Penicillin primarily targets the peptidoglycan layer, inhibiting its synthesis. This is why it's effective against bacteria with a thick peptidoglycan layer.\n\n3. **Effectiveness**:\n   - In gram-negative bacteria (diagram b), the outer membrane protects the thinner peptidoglycan layer from penicillin, reducing its effectiveness.\n\nGiven these points, penicillin is generally less effective against gram-negative bacteria due to their additional outer membrane, which acts as a barrier.\n\nANSWER: A. True."
