"To solve the problem, let's analyze each statement individually:\n\nI. Voltage-gated potassium channels will open but not close.\n\n- If voltage-gated sodium channels remain open, the membrane will continue to be depolarized. As a result, the voltage-gated potassium channels will open in response to depolarization, but they might not close if the membrane remains in a depolarized state. However, formulating this as \"will open but not close\" might not strictly be correct depending on other conditions influencing potassium channel closing. Thus, this statement is less certain.\n\nII. The membrane will not repolarize to the normal resting membrane potential.\n\n- If the sodium channels stay open, sodium continues to flow into the cell, keeping the membrane depolarized. Therefore, repolarization to the resting potential will not occur effectively.\n\nIII. The Na+/K+ ATPase will be inactivated.\n\n- The Na+/K+ ATPase is an active transport mechanism that does not depend directly on the state of the sodium channels for activation or inactivation. It keeps operating consistently to maintain ion gradients, so it will not be inactivated by the opening or closing of the sodium channels.\n\nWith this analysis:\n\n- Statement II is correct.\n- Statement I is uncertain and not explicitly correct.\n- Statement III is incorrect.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer based on this analysis is:\n\nANSWER: B. II only."
