"To isolate the responses of each receptor type based on the physiology of the eye, we need to consider the placement and function of rods and cones:\n\n- **Rods** are more sensitive to low light and are scattered throughout the retina, primarily responsible for peripheral and night vision.\n- **Cones** are concentrated in the fovea and are responsible for color vision and visual acuity in well-lit conditions.\n\nGiven these characteristics, changing the location of the test light will be effective:\n\n- **Placing the test light on the fovea** should predominantly activate cones, as they are concentrated there.\n- **Placing the test light in the periphery** of the retina should predominantly activate rods, which are more scattered throughout the retina.\n\nTherefore, adjusting the test light placement from the focal point (fovea) to the periphery effectively isolates the responses of cones and rods, respectively.\n\nANSWER: B."
