"To determine which model supports the results of the adolescent sexual networks study, let's analyze the described network structure and compare it to the four models.\n\n- **Core Infection Model (Panel A):** This model features a core group of high-activity individuals who are interconnected and also link to others outside the core. This doesn\u2019t fit the description from the study, as there was no core group.\n\n- **Inverse Core Model (Panel B):** Similar to the core model, this emphasizes a core but with high-risk individuals not directly connected to each other. Again, this doesn't match the study results.\n\n- **Bridging Model (Panel C):** This model involves subgroups or disjoint populations connected through a few individuals playing a bridging role. The study mentioned a long chain rather than distinct subgroups.\n\n- **Spanning Tree Model (Panel D):** This describes a network where individuals are mostly connected in a long chain without a core group, resembling telephone lines from a main line. The study clearly states that the high school network was one long chain of romantically involved students.\n\nGiven these observations, the **Spanning Tree Model** (Panel D) best represents the structure of the adolescent sexual networks in the study.\n\nANSWER: D"
