"To determine the most serious limitation of the study, we need to consider potential effects that can impact the validity of the results.\n\n- **Selection Effect**: This effect concerns how participants are chosen for the study. Since the problem doesn't mention any issue with participant selection, this is likely not the main limitation.\n\n- **Ceiling Effect**: A ceiling effect occurs when the measurement instrument can't detect a higher level of performance; scores cluster at the high end. This doesn't seem to be an issue here, as scores decrease with higher medication doses.\n\n- **Sleeper Effect**: The sleeper effect is when a delayed effect becomes manifest after time has passed. There's no indication of delayed effects being a limitation given this study's structure.\n\n- **Carryover Effect**: This effect happens when effects of the earlier treatments persist and affect performance in later treatments. Since participants were exposed to escalating doses without a washout period, their current scores might still be influenced by the previous dosages.\n\nConsidering these options, the most serious limitation in this study, where participants' responses may be influenced by previous dosages, is the **carryover effect**.\n\nANSWER: D."
