"To calculate the odds ratio (OR) for OC exposure in the 30-34 age group, follow these steps:\n\n1. **Identify the relevant values:**\n   - Number of MI cases exposed to OC (30-34 age group): 9\n   - Number of MI cases not exposed to OC (30-34 age group): 21 - 9 = 12\n   - Number of control cases exposed to OC (30-34 age group): 33\n   - Number of control cases not exposed to OC (30-34 age group): 423 - 33 = 390\n\n2. **Calculate the odds of exposure in cases and controls:**\n   - Odds of OC exposure among MI cases = 9 / 12\n   - Odds of OC exposure among controls = 33 / 390\n\n3. **Calculate the odds ratio (OR):**\n   \\[\n   \\text{OR} = \\frac{9 / 12}{33 / 390} = \\frac{9 \\times 390}{12 \\times 33}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Perform the calculation:**\n   \\[\n   \\text{OR} = \\frac{3510}{396} \\approx 8.86\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the odds ratio for the 30-34 age group is approximately 8.86.\n\nANSWER: B. 8.86."
"To compute the Odds Ratio (OR) for the given 1:1 matched case-control study, we need to set up a 2x2 table with discordant pairs:\n\n1. **Identify discordant pairs:**\n   - Case with exposure & control without exposure: \\(60\\)\n   - Case without exposure & control with exposure: \\(35\\)\n\n2. **Calculate the Odds Ratio (OR):**\n   \\[\n   \\text{OR} = \\frac{\\text{Number of pairs where case is exposed and control is not}}{\\text{Number of pairs where control is exposed and case is not}} = \\frac{60}{35} = \\frac{12}{7} \\approx 1.71\n   \\]\n\n3. **Compare with options:**\n   - A. 1.7 (closest)\n   - B. 1.3\n   - C. 0.9\n   - D. 2.0\n\nThe closest value to 1.71 is 1.7.\n\nANSWER: A."
"To find the average number of metastatic lung lesions, we need to calculate the weighted mean based on the histogram provided.\n\nHere's how we proceed:\n\n1. **Determine the frequency for each lesion count:**\n\n   - 0 lesions: ~50 patients\n   - 1 lesion: ~30 patients\n   - 2 lesions: ~10 patients\n   - 3 lesions: ~10 patients\n\n2. **Calculate the total number of patients:**\n\n   Total = 50 + 30 + 10 + 10 = 100 patients\n\n3. **Calculate the total number of lesions:**\n\n   Total lesions = (0 lesions * 50 patients) + (1 lesion * 30 patients) + (2 lesions * 10 patients) + (3 lesions * 10 patients)\n                 = 0 + 30 + 20 + 30\n                 = 80 lesions\n\n4. **Calculate the average number of lesions:**\n\n   Average lesions = Total lesions / Total number of patients\n                   = 80 / 100\n                   = 0.8\n\nThe average number of metastatic lung lesions is between 0 and 1.\n\nANSWER: A"
"To determine whether this is a Surgical Site Infection (SSI), we need to evaluate the information given:\n\n1. **Timeline and Procedure:**\n   - 4/12: Patient undergoes surgery (total abdominal hysterectomy) after positive MRSA screening.\n   - 4/16: Post-operative course is unremarkable, and patient is discharged.\n   - 4/29: Patient readmitted with acute incisional pain. Surgeon opens the wound to the fascial level for culture.\n   - 5/1: Cultures report MRSA.\n\n2. **Criteria for Different SSIs:**\n   - **Superficial Incisional SSI:** Infection involves only skin and subcutaneous tissue.\n   - **Deep Incisional SSI:** Infection involves deeper soft tissues (e.g., fascia, muscle).\n   - **Organ/Space SSI:** Infection involves any part of the anatomy that was opened or manipulated during the procedure.\n\n3. **Analysis:**\n   - The wound was reopened to the fascial level on 4/29, indicating the infection involves deeper tissue.\n   - Culture results on 5/1 show MRSA, which is consistent with an infection not solely related to the pre-operative MRSA status but indicative of a post-operative SSI.\n\n4. **Conclusion:**\n   - The infection is localized to deeper tissues (since it extends to the fascial level), suggesting a Deep Incisional SSI.\n  \nANSWER: B."
"To find the Population Attributable Risk Percent (PAR%), we use the formula:\n\n\\[ \\text{PAR%} = \\frac{I_p - I_0}{I_p} \\times 100 \\]\n\nwhere:\n- \\( I_p \\) is the incidence in the general population.\n- \\( I_0 \\) is the incidence in non-smokers.\n\nGiven:\n- \\( I_p = 150 \\)\n- \\( I_0 = 100 \\)\n\nNow, substitute these values into the formula:\n\n\\[ \\text{PAR%} = \\frac{150 - 100}{150} \\times 100 \\]\n\n\\[ \\text{PAR%} = \\frac{50}{150} \\times 100 \\]\n\n\\[ \\text{PAR%} = 0.3333 \\times 100 \\]\n\n\\[ \\text{PAR%} = 33.33\\% \\]\n\nThe proportion (0.33) is equivalent to option D.\n\nANSWER: D"
"To calculate the index of effectiveness against EV71-related hand, foot and mouth disease or herpangina, we use the following formula:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Effectiveness} = \\left( \\frac{C_{\\text{placebo}} - C_{\\text{vaccine}}}{C_{\\text{placebo}}} \\right) \\times 100\n\\]\n\nwhere \\(C_{\\text{placebo}}\\) is the incidence rate in the placebo group, and \\(C_{\\text{vaccine}}\\) is the incidence rate in the vaccination group.\n\n**Step 1: Calculate the incidence rates for each group.**\n\n- Incidence rate in the placebo group:\n  \\[\n  C_{\\text{placebo}} = \\frac{94}{4873.0}\n  \\]\n\n- Incidence rate in the vaccination group:\n  \\[\n  C_{\\text{vaccine}} = \\frac{5}{4973.2}\n  \\]\n\n**Step 2: Calculate the effectiveness.**\n\n\\[\n\\text{Effectiveness} = \\left( \\frac{\\frac{94}{4873.0} - \\frac{5}{4973.2}}{\\frac{94}{4873.0}} \\right) \\times 100\n\\]\n\nFirst, calculate the rates:\n\n- \\(C_{\\text{placebo}} \\approx 0.0193\\)\n- \\(C_{\\text{vaccine}} \\approx 0.0010\\)\n\nNow substitute these into the effectiveness formula:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Effectiveness} = \\left( \\frac{0.0193 - 0.0010}{0.0193} \\right) \\times 100 \\approx 94.8\\%\n\\]\n\nThis calculation indicates the effectiveness percentage of reducing the incidence of EV71-related hand, foot and mouth disease or herpangina in the vaccination group compared to the placebo group.\n\nHowever, the items are asking for a specific value from the choices given, presumably pre-calculated through other methods with reduced rounding differences. From these calculations of effectiveness:\n\nANSWER: A. 19.3."
