"To find the Attributable Risk Percent (AR%) of osteoma for female radium painters, we use the following formula:\n\n\\[\n\\text{AR\\%} = \\left(\\frac{\\text{Incidence in exposed group} - \\text{Incidence in unexposed group}}{\\text{Incidence in exposed group}}\\right) \\times 100\n\\]\n\nGiven:\n- Incidence in radium-painting women (exposed group) = 20/1000\n- Incidence in female telephone operators (unexposed group) = 4/1000\n\nSteps:\n\n1. Calculate the incidence rates:\n   - Exposed group: \\( \\frac{20}{1000} = 0.02 \\)\n   - Unexposed group: \\( \\frac{4}{1000} = 0.004 \\)\n\n2. Substitute into the AR% formula:\n   \\[\n   \\text{AR\\%} = \\left(\\frac{0.02 - 0.004}{0.02}\\right) \\times 100\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\text{AR\\%} = \\left(\\frac{0.016}{0.02}\\right) \\times 100 = 0.8 \\times 100 = 80\n   \\]\n\nBased on the options provided, none directly match 80%, therefore we must consider potential errors or adjustments. However, following given multiple choice format, it could relate to a typographical error in option representation or a referential interpretation that rationalizes a potential mistake.\n\nThe next closest calculation would be aligned with specific reference to potential formula restructuring or interpretation in academia or otherwise.\n\nANSWER: D. 0.8."
"To calculate the Potential Years of Life Lost (PYLL) due to cardiovascular and cerebrovascular diseases in the 45-49 age group, we follow these steps:\n\n1. **Identify Life Expectancy for the Age Group:**\n   - For ages 45-49, the life expectancy is 37.25 years.\n\n2. **Identify the Number of Deaths in the Age Group:**\n   - The number of deaths from cardiovascular and cerebrovascular diseases in the 45-49 age group is 208.\n\n3. **Calculate PYLL for the Age Group:**\n   - PYLL is calculated by multiplying the number of deaths by the years of life lost.\n   \\[\n   \\text{PYLL} = \\text{Number of Deaths} \\times \\text{Life Expectancy}\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\text{PYLL} = 208 \\times 37.25\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\text{PYLL} = 7748\n   \\]\n\nConsidering the available answer choices, check for any discrepancies due to rounding or provided options.\n\nANSWER: None of the choices seem to match the direct calculation exactly. I'd recommend re-checking or confirming if additional interpretation or rounding consideration is involved for the given data interpretation."
"To calculate the positive likelihood ratio (\\(+LR\\)) for electronic colposcopy, we use the formula:\n\n\\[\n+LR = \\frac{\\text{Sensitivity}}{1 - \\text{Specificity}}\n\\]\n\n**Sensitivity:**\n\nSensitivity is the proportion of true positives (patients with cervical lesions who tested positive) to all actual positives (patients with cervical lesions).\n\nTrue positives for electronic colposcopy = 15 (from the cell \\(+\\) electronic colposcopy, \\(-\\) TCT) + 70 (from the cell \\(+\\) electronic colposcopy, \\(+\\) TCT) = 85.\n\nSensitivity = \\(\\frac{85}{100} = 0.85\\).\n\n**Specificity:**\n\nSpecificity is the proportion of true negatives (patients with non-cervical lesions who tested negative) to all actual negatives (patients with non-cervical lesions).\n\nTrue negatives for electronic colposcopy = 245 (from the cell \\(-\\) electronic colposcopy, \\(-\\) TCT).\n\nSpecificity = \\(\\frac{245}{500} = 0.49\\).\n\n**Calculating +LR:**\n\n\\[\n+LR = \\frac{0.85}{1 - 0.49} = \\frac{0.85}{0.51} \\approx 1.67\n\\]\n\nANSWER: A"
