"To calculate the negative likelihood ratio (-LR), we need to find the sensitivity and specificity first.\n\n**Sensitivity** is the proportion of true positives detected:\n\\[\n\\text{Sensitivity} = \\frac{\\text{True Positives}}{\\text{True Positives + False Negatives}} = \\frac{71}{100} = 0.71\n\\]\n\n**Specificity** is the proportion of true negatives detected:\n\\[\n\\text{Specificity} = \\frac{\\text{True Negatives}}{\\text{True Negatives + False Positives}} = \\frac{192}{200} = 0.96\n\\]\n\nThe negative likelihood ratio (-LR) is calculated as:\n\\[\n\\text{-LR} = \\frac{1 - \\text{Sensitivity}}{\\text{Specificity}} = \\frac{1 - 0.71}{0.96} = \\frac{0.29}{0.96} \\approx 0.30\n\\]\n\nThus, the -LR of the screening test is approximately 0.30.\n\nANSWER: B."
"To calculate the specificity of the test, use the formula:\n\nSpecificity = (True Negatives) / (True Negatives + False Positives)\n\nFrom the table:\n- True Negatives (TN) = 300 (negative test result and no cervical cancer)\n- False Positives (FP) = 100 (positive test result but no cervical cancer)\n\nPlug these values into the formula:\n\nSpecificity = 300 / (300 + 100) = 300 / 400 = 0.75 or 75%\n\nANSWER: A. 75%."
"To calculate the Sensitivity of the new rapid test, use the following formula:\n\n\\[ \\text{Sensitivity} = \\frac{\\text{True Positives}}{\\text{True Positives} + \\text{False Negatives}} \\]\n\nFrom the table:\n- True Positives (TP) = 378 (Test + and HIV+)\n- False Negatives (FN) = 2 (Test - and HIV+)\n\nPlug these values into the formula:\n\n\\[ \\text{Sensitivity} = \\frac{378}{378 + 2} = \\frac{378}{380} = 0.99474 \\]\n\nRounding to three decimal places:\n\n\\[ \\text{Sensitivity} \\approx 0.995 \\]\n\nANSWER: A."
