"To analyze the relationship between maternal abdominal X-ray exposure and pediatric leukemia, we use the data from the table to calculate the odds ratio (OR).\n\nOdds Ratio (OR) formula:\n\\[ OR = \\frac{(a/c)}{(b/d)} = \\frac{a \\times d}{b \\times c} \\]\n\nWhere:\n- \\( a = 72 \\) (number of exposed cases)\n- \\( b = 58 \\) (number of exposed controls)\n- \\( c = 179 \\) (number of non-exposed cases)\n- \\( d = 193 \\) (number of non-exposed controls)\n\nPlug in the values:\n\\[ OR = \\frac{72 \\times 193}{58 \\times 179} \\]\n\nCalculate:\n\\[ OR = \\frac{13896}{10382} \\approx 1.34 \\]\n\nThe odds ratio is approximately 1.34, suggesting that the risk of pediatric leukemia is 1.34 times higher in children whose mothers were exposed to abdominal X-rays during pregnancy compared to those who were not exposed.\n\nNext, assess statistical significance (though exact p-value or confidence intervals are not provided in the description, typically additional statistical analyses like Chi-square tests determine significance):\n\nGiven the options and the provided interpretation:\nThe correct choice reflecting the calculated odds ratio is: \n\nANSWER: A. The risk of paediatric leukaemia in pregnant women exposed to abdominal X-rays was 1.34 times higher than in those not exposed, but the difference was not statistically significant."
