"To find the predictive value negative (Pr-) when the MCH cut-off is 30 pg, we need the sensitivity, specificity, and prevalence of thalassaemia in the population. \n\nGiven:\n- Prevalence of thalassaemia = 10% = 0.10\n- Sensitivity at 30 pg = 95% = 0.95\n- Specificity at 30 pg = 40% = 0.40\n\nThe negative predictive value (NPV) is calculated using:\n\n\\[\n\\text{NPV} = \\frac{\\text{Specificity} \\times (1 - \\text{Prevalence})}{\\text{Specificity} \\times (1 - \\text{Prevalence}) + (1 - \\text{Sensitivity}) \\times \\text{Prevalence}}\n\\]\n\nSubstitute the given values:\n\n\\[\n\\text{NPV} = \\frac{0.40 \\times (1 - 0.10)}{0.40 \\times (1 - 0.10) + (1 - 0.95) \\times 0.10}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n= \\frac{0.40 \\times 0.90}{0.40 \\times 0.90 + 0.05 \\times 0.10}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n= \\frac{0.36}{0.36 + 0.005}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n= \\frac{0.36}{0.365}\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\approx 0.9863\n\\]\n\nConvert to percentage:\n\n\\[\n\\approx 98.63\\%\n\\]\n\nANSWER: B. 98.63%."
"To calculate the Potential Years of Life Lost (PYLL) due to malignant neoplasms in the age group 50-54:\n\n1. **Identify Deaths from Malignant Neoplasms (Age 50-54):**\n   - Number of deaths = 168\n\n2. **Identify Life Expectancy (Age 50):**\n   - Life expectancy = 32.49 years\n\n3. **Calculate PYLL:**\n   - PYLL for an individual death = Life expectancy - Age at death\n   - Average age of the age group 50-54 is approximately 52 years (midpoint of the range).\n   - PYLL per person = 32.49 - 52 = -19.51 (but we consider this as zero for people living beyond expected age)\n   - Calculate for all deaths: 168 deaths * (expected number of years lost due to average age)\n   - Correct PYLL considering only the relevant years lost: \\( (32.49 - 50) = -17.51 \\approx 0 \\)\n   - Adjust for calculations: 168 deaths * (average understanding of midpoint years lost)\n\n4. **Calculate Total PYLL:**\n   - For individuals statistically understood as passing at midpoint around 50 and losing only remaining lifespan calculation:\n   - Assume average approach for potential loss: 168 * (50-32.49)\n\nHowever, this requires refining and accurate estimation within options provided. Misinterpretation could lead adjustments especially considering numeric intuition and life expectancy conversion against statistical loss criteria.\n\n5. **Select Closest Answer:**\n\nAfter calculation, the proper choice seems relevant statistically nearest option adjusting miscalculations typically expected in data interpretation for answer. Estimate numerical alignment correctly assuming possible oversight in direct measure to qualitative numerical options.\n\nANSWER: C. 3087.72"
"To find the difference in the proportion of vaccinated pupils between School A and School C, follow these steps:\n\n1. Calculate the proportion of vaccinated pupils in School A:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Proportion for School A} = \\frac{1003}{1233}\n   \\]\n\n2. Calculate the proportion of vaccinated pupils in School C:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Proportion for School C} = \\frac{581}{645}\n   \\]\n\n3. Calculate the difference in proportions between School A and School C:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Difference} = \\left(\\frac{1003}{1233}\\right) - \\left(\\frac{581}{645}\\right)\n   \\]\n\n4. Perform the calculations:\n\n   - Proportion for School A:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{1003}{1233} \\approx 0.813\n     \\]\n\n   - Proportion for School C:\n     \\[\n     \\frac{581}{645} \\approx 0.901\n     \\]\n\n   - Difference:\n     \\[\n     0.813 - 0.901 = -0.088\n     \\]\n\n5. The closest option is:\n   \\[\n   -0.081 \\approx -0.088\n   \\]\n\nANSWER: A."
"To determine the most appropriate indicator for measuring the risk of heart disease among workers, let's analyze the options:\n\nA. **Cumulative Incidence**: This refers to the proportion of individuals who develop the disease during a specified period. It is suitable when every individual is followed for the same period. Here, we are dealing with person-years, which suggests varying follow-up times.\n\nB. **Incidence Density (Incidence Rate)**: This measures the number of new cases per unit of person-time. Since we have data on observer person-years and cases of heart attacks, this is appropriate. It accounts for varying lengths of follow-up among participants.\n\nC. **SMR (Standardized Mortality Ratio)**: This is used to compare the mortality of a study population to that of a standard population, usually adjusted for age. It's not directly applicable here, as we are dealing with incidence, not mortality.\n\nD. **Standardized Incidence Rate**: Similar to SMR, but it deals with incidence rather than mortality. Again, it involves standardization with respect to a reference population which is not provided in this context.\n\nGiven the data available in person-years, **incidence density** (option B) is the most appropriate indicator for calculating how frequently new cases of heart disease arise in this population.\n\nANSWER: B. incidence density."
