"To find the positive predictive value (Pr+), we use the following formula:\n\n\\[ \\text{Pr+} = \\frac{\\text{Sensitivity} \\times \\text{Prevalence}}{\\text{Sensitivity} \\times \\text{Prevalence} + (1-\\text{Specificity}) \\times (1-\\text{Prevalence})} \\]\n\nGiven:\n- Prevalence = 10% or 0.10\n- Sensitivity at MCH 30 pg = 95% or 0.95\n- Specificity at MCH 30 pg = 40% or 0.40\n\nSubstitute these values into the formula:\n\n\\[ \\text{Pr+} = \\frac{0.95 \\times 0.10}{0.95 \\times 0.10 + (1-0.40) \\times (1-0.10)} \\]\n\n\\[ \\text{Pr+} = \\frac{0.095}{0.095 + 0.60 \\times 0.90} \\]\n\n\\[ \\text{Pr+} = \\frac{0.095}{0.095 + 0.54} \\]\n\n\\[ \\text{Pr+} = \\frac{0.095}{0.635} \\]\n\n\\[ \\text{Pr+} \\approx 0.1496 \\text{ or } 14.96\\% \\]\n\nANSWER: A. 14.96%."
