"To calculate the attributable risk percent (AR%), we use the formula:\n\n\\[ \\text{AR\\%} = \\left(\\frac{\\text{Incidence in exposed group} - \\text{Incidence in reference group}}{\\text{Incidence in exposed group}}\\right) \\times 100 \\]\n\n### Step 1: Calculate incidence in each group\n- **Reference group (114-193 mg/dl):**\n  - Incidence = \\( \\frac{2}{209} \\)\n\n- **Exposed group (256 and above mg/dl):**\n  - Incidence = \\( \\frac{32}{209} \\)\n\n### Step 2: Plug values into the formula\nIncidence in reference group = \\( \\frac{2}{209} \\approx 0.00957 \\)\n\nIncidence in exposed group = \\( \\frac{32}{209} \\approx 0.15311 \\)\n\n\\[ \\text{AR\\%} = \\left(\\frac{0.15311 - 0.00957}{0.15311}\\right) \\times 100 \\]\n\n\\[ \\text{AR\\%} = \\left(\\frac{0.14354}{0.15311}\\right) \\times 100 \\]\n\n\\[ \\text{AR\\%} \\approx 93.73 \\]\n\n### ANSWER: D"
