"To calculate the Attributable Risk (AR) for the serum cholesterol level 194-213 mg/dl group using the 114-193 mg/dl group as the reference group, follow these steps:\n\n1. **Calculate the incidence rate in each group:**\n\n   - **Reference group (114-193 mg/dl):**\n     \\[\n     \\text{Incidence} = \\frac{\\text{Number of cases}}{\\text{Number of observations}} = \\frac{2}{209} \\approx 0.00957\n     \\]\n\n   - **194-213 mg/dl group:**\n     \\[\n     \\text{Incidence} = \\frac{11}{209} \\approx 0.05263\n     \\]\n\n2. **Calculate the Attributable Risk (AR):**\n\n   - **AR formula:**\n     \\[\n     \\text{AR} = (\\text{Incidence in exposed group}) - (\\text{Incidence in reference group})\n     \\]\n     \\[\n     \\text{AR} = 0.05263 - 0.00957 = 0.04306\n     \\]\n\n   - **Convert AR to per 1000:**\n     \\[\n     \\text{AR per 1000} = 0.04306 \\times 1000 = 43.06\n     \\]\n\n3. **Identify the closest option to AR:**\n\n   The closest option to 43.06 per 1000 is choice B: 4.30 per 100 (43.06 per 1000 corresponds to approximately 4.30 per 100).\n\nANSWER: B."
