"To find the Population Attributable Risk percentage (PAR%) for systolic blood pressure \u2265 180mmHg, we use the formula:\n\n\\[ \\text{PAR\\%} = \\left(\\frac{(RR - 1) \\times Pe}{1 + (RR - 1) \\times Pe}\\right) \\times 100 \\]\n\nWhere:\n- \\( RR \\) = Relative Risk\n- \\( Pe \\) = Population exposure proportion\n\nFor systolic blood pressure \u2265 180mmHg:\n- \\( RR = 2.8 \\)\n- \\( Pe = 0.02 \\)\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula:\n\n\\[ \\text{PAR\\%} = \\left(\\frac{(2.8 - 1) \\times 0.02}{1 + (2.8 - 1) \\times 0.02}\\right) \\times 100 \\]\n\n\\[ \\text{PAR\\%} = \\left(\\frac{1.8 \\times 0.02}{1 + 1.8 \\times 0.02}\\right) \\times 100 \\]\n\n\\[ \\text{PAR\\%} = \\left(\\frac{0.036}{1 + 0.036}\\right) \\times 100 \\]\n\n\\[ \\text{PAR\\%} = \\left(\\frac{0.036}{1.036}\\right) \\times 100 \\]\n\n\\[ \\text{PAR\\%} \\approx 3.47 \\]\n\nRounding it approximately gives \\( \\text{PAR\\%} = 3.5 \\).\n\nANSWER: A."
