"To find the index of effectiveness of the vaccine, we can use the following formula for vaccine effectiveness (VE):\n\n\\[ \nVE = \\left( \\frac{ARU - ARV}{ARU} \\right) \\times 100 \n\\]\n\nwhere:\n- \\( ARU \\) is the attack rate in the unvaccinated group.\n- \\( ARV \\) is the attack rate in the vaccinated group.\n\nFirst, calculate the attack rates:\n\n\\[ \nARU = \\frac{\\text{Number of cases in the control group}}{\\text{Total in the control group}} = \\frac{500}{5000} = 0.1 \n\\]\n\n\\[ \nARV = \\frac{\\text{Number of cases in the vaccinated group}}{\\text{Total in the vaccinated group}} = \\frac{50}{5000} = 0.01 \n\\]\n\nNow, calculate the vaccine effectiveness:\n\n\\[ \nVE = \\left( \\frac{0.1 - 0.01}{0.1} \\right) \\times 100 = \\left( \\frac{0.09}{0.1} \\right) \\times 100 = 0.9 \\times 100 = 90\\%\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the index of effectiveness of this vaccine is 90%.\n\nANSWER: D"
"To find the Population Attributable Risk (PAR) for smoking and cardiovascular diseases, we use the formula:\n\n\\[ \\text{PAR} = I_p - I_0 \\]\n\nWhere:\n- \\( I_p \\) is the incidence in the general population.\n- \\( I_0 \\) is the incidence in non-smokers.\n\nFrom the table:\n- \\( I_p = 150 \\) per 100,000 person years.\n- \\( I_0 = 100 \\) per 100,000 person years.\n\nPlug these values into the formula:\n\n\\[ \\text{PAR} = 150 - 100 = 50 \\] per 100,000 person years.\n\nANSWER: D."
