"To calculate the specificity of the parallel test, we need to determine how well the test correctly identifies those without the condition (true negatives).\n\n### Steps:\n\n1. **Identify True Negatives (TN):**\n   - True negatives are those who test negative on both tests and do not have the disease.\n   - From the table, the number of true negatives is: **7620**.\n\n2. **Identify Total Non-Diabetes Mellitus Cases:**\n   - The total number of non-diabetes mellitus cases is the sum of all entries in the non-diabetes mellitus column: **7641**.\n\n3. **Calculate Specificity:**\n   \\[\n   \\text{Specificity} = \\left( \\frac{\\text{True Negatives}}{\\text{Total Non-Diabetes Mellitus}} \\right) \\times 100\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   \\text{Specificity} = \\left( \\frac{7620}{7641} \\right) \\times 100 \\approx 99.73\\%\n   \\]\n\n### Conclusion:\nThe specificity of the parallel test is **99.73%**.\n\nANSWER: A"
