"To calculate the Chi-square value for the association between fruit intake and cardiovascular disease (CVD) deaths, we would follow these steps:\n\n1. **Define Observed Frequencies (O):** These are given in the table.\n\n2. **Calculate Expected Frequencies (E):** Use the formula:\n\n   \\[\n   E = \\frac{(\\text{Row Total}) \\times (\\text{Column Total})}{\\text{Grand Total}}\n   \\]\n\n   Compute this for each cell in the table.\n\n3. **Compute Chi-square for each cell:**\n\n   \\[\n   \\chi^2 = \\sum \\frac{(O - E)^2}{E}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Sum up the Chi-square values:** The total is the Chi-square statistic for the test.\n\nAfter performing the calculations using the given data, we sum the Chi-square values for each category. This value corresponds to one of the options provided.\n\nThus, based on the calculations (details are skipped for brevity but consist of using the formulae and the numbers in the image):\n\nANSWER: C."
