"To find Youden's Index, we first need to calculate the sensitivity and specificity of the ultrasound screening.\n\n**Sensitivity:** This is the proportion of true positives (patients detected by ultrasound) out of all the actual patients (according to the gold standard H MRS).\n\n\\[\n\\text{Sensitivity} = \\frac{\\text{True Positives}}{\\text{Total Patients}} = \\frac{87}{95} = 0.9158\n\\]\n\n**Specificity:** This is the proportion of true negatives (non-patients not detected by ultrasound) out of all the actual non-patients.\n\n\\[\n\\text{Specificity} = \\frac{\\text{True Negatives}}{\\text{Total Non-patients}} = \\frac{34}{67} = 0.5075\n\\]\n\n**Youden's Index** is calculated as follows:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Youden's Index} = \\text{Sensitivity} + \\text{Specificity} - 1\n\\]\n\n\\[\n\\text{Youden's Index} = 0.9158 + 0.5075 - 1 = 0.4233\n\\]\n\nBased on the options provided:\n\n- A. 0.43\n- B. 1.88\n- C. 0.16\n- D. 0.49\n\nThe closest match is option A.\n\nANSWER: A"
"To calculate the chi-square value, we first need the expected frequencies for each cell in a 2x2 table. The formula for the expected frequency for each cell is:\n\n\\[\nE = \\frac{(row\\ total \\times column\\ total)}{grand\\ total}\n\\]\n\nGiven the data:\n\n|                          | Cases   | Control | Total |\n|--------------------------|---------|---------|-------|\n| Drinking alcohol (Yes)   | 73      | 202     | 275   |\n| Drinking alcohol (No)    | 81      | 381     | 462   |\n| Total                    | 154     | 583     | 737   |\n\n**Step 1: Calculate Expected Frequencies**\n\n1. **Expected for Yes/Cases:**\n   \\[\n   E_{11} = \\frac{(154 \\times 275)}{737} = 57.43\n   \\]\n\n2. **Expected for Yes/Control:**\n   \\[\n   E_{12} = \\frac{(583 \\times 275)}{737} = 217.57\n   \\]\n\n3. **Expected for No/Cases:**\n   \\[\n   E_{21} = \\frac{(154 \\times 462)}{737} = 96.57\n   \\]\n\n4. **Expected for No/Control:**\n   \\[\n   E_{22} = \\frac{(583 \\times 462)}{737} = 365.43\n   \\]\n\n**Step 2: Apply the Chi-Square Formula**\n\n\\[\n\\chi^2 = \\sum \\frac{(O - E)^2}{E}\n\\]\n\nWhere \\( O \\) is the observed frequency and \\( E \\) is the expected frequency.\n\n1. For Yes/Cases: \n   \\[\n   \\frac{(73 - 57.43)^2}{57.43} = 4.26\n   \\]\n\n2. For Yes/Control: \n   \\[\n   \\frac{(202 - 217.57)^2}{217.57} = 1.22\n   \\]\n\n3. For No/Cases: \n   \\[\n   \\frac{(81 - 96.57)^2}{96.57} = 2.63\n   \\]\n\n4. For No/Control: \n   \\[\n   \\frac{(381 - 365.43)^2}{365.43} = 0.69\n   \\]\n\n**Step 3: Calculate Total Chi-Square**\n\n\\[\n\\chi^2 = 4.26 + 1.22 + 2.63 + 0.69 = 8.80\n\\]\n\nAfter reviewing the options, the closest chi-square value to the calculated 8.80 is **option A. 8.47**. There might be a minor calculation or rounding difference.\n\nANSWER: A"
"To find the relative risk (RR) of cardiovascular death for those who consume fruit \"4-6 times a week\" compared to those who consume fruit \"every day,\" we need to calculate the rate of deaths per observer for each group, then divide these rates:\n\n1. Calculate the death rate for \"4-6 times a week\":  \n   - Number of deaths: 270  \n   - Number of observers: 42,519  \n   - Death rate = 270 / 42,519\n\n2. Calculate the death rate for \"every day\":  \n   - Number of deaths: 452  \n   - Number of observers: 81,386  \n   - Death rate = 452 / 81,386\n\n3. Calculate the RR:\n   - RR = (Death rate for \"4-6 times a week\") / (Death rate for \"every day\")\n\nPerforming these calculations:\n\n- Death rate for \"4-6 times a week\" = 270 / 42,519 = 0.00635 (approximately)\n- Death rate for \"every day\" = 452 / 81,386 = 0.00556 (approximately)\n\n- RR = 0.00635 / 0.00556 \u2248 1.14\n\nANSWER: D. 1.14"
