"To evaluate the validity of a screening test, we use the likelihood ratios (LR). The Positive Likelihood Ratio (+LR) is calculated as:\n\n\\[\n+LR = \\frac{\\text{Sensitivity}}{1 - \\text{Specificity}}\n\\]\n\nwhere:\n\n- Sensitivity = \\(\\frac{A}{A+C}\\)\n- Specificity = \\(\\frac{D}{B+D}\\)\n\nThe formula for +LR becomes:\n\n\\[\n+LR = \\frac{\\frac{A}{A+C}}{1 - \\frac{D}{B+D}} = \\frac{\\frac{A}{A+C}}{\\frac{B}{B+D}} = \\frac{A(B+D)}{B(A+C)} = \\frac{A}{B}\n\\]\n\nThus, the corresponding formula in the options is:\n\n\\[\n\\text{ANSWER: A} \\quad \\text{(i.e., } \\frac{AB+AD}{AB+BC} \\text{, but simplified to } \\frac{A}{B} \\text{)}\n\\]"
"To calculate the incidence density of hand, foot, and mouth disease in the placebo group, use the formula:\n\n\\[ \\text{Incidence Density} = \\frac{\\text{Number of New Cases}}{\\text{Person Years at Risk}} \\times 1000 \\]\n\nFor the placebo group:\n- Number of new cases = 94\n- Person years at risk = 4873.0\n\nCalculate the incidence density:\n\n\\[ \\text{Incidence Density} = \\frac{94}{4873.0} \\times 1000 \\]\n\n\\[ \\text{Incidence Density} = 19.3 \\]\n\nTherefore, the incidence density is 19.3 per 1000 person years.\n\nANSWER: C."
"To calculate the negative likelihood ratio (-LR), we use the formula:\n\n\\[\n\\text{-LR} = \\frac{1 - \\text{Sensitivity}}{\\text{Specificity}}\n\\]\n\nFirst, calculate sensitivity and specificity:\n\n1. **Sensitivity** is the probability of a positive test given that the patient has the disease. \n\n\\[\n\\text{Sensitivity} = \\frac{\\text{True Positives}}{\\text{True Positives + False Negatives}} = \\frac{87}{95}\n\\]\n\n2. **Specificity** is the probability of a negative test given that the patient does not have the disease. \n\n\\[\n\\text{Specificity} = \\frac{\\text{True Negatives}}{\\text{False Positives + True Negatives}} = \\frac{34}{67}\n\\]\n\nNow, calculate:\n\n- \\(1 - \\text{Sensitivity} = 1 - \\frac{87}{95} = \\frac{8}{95}\\)\n\n- \\(\\text{Specificity} = \\frac{34}{67}\\)\n\n\\[\n\\text{-LR} = \\frac{\\frac{8}{95}}{\\frac{34}{67}} = \\frac{8 \\times 67}{95 \\times 34} = \\frac{536}{3230} \\approx 0.166\n\\]\n\nANSWER: C"
"To find the sensitivity of the parallel test, we need to determine the true positive rate. In a parallel test, a positive result in either test is considered as a positive result for the screening.\n\n**Steps to calculate sensitivity:**\n\n1. **Identify True Positives (TP):** These are the cases where either or both tests are positive for diabetes mellitus.\n   - Urine positive and blood positive: 45\n   - Urine positive and blood negative: 7\n   - Urine negative and blood positive: 23\n\n   Total True Positives (TP) = 45 + 7 + 23 = 75\n\n2. **Identify Total with Condition (Diabetes Mellitus):** This is given as the total number of diabetes mellitus cases, which is 199.\n\n3. **Calculate Sensitivity:**\n   \\[\n   \\text{Sensitivity} = \\left(\\frac{\\text{True Positives}}{\\text{Total with Condition}}\\right) \\times 100 = \\left(\\frac{75}{199}\\right) \\times 100\n   \\]\n\n4. **Compute:**\n   \\[\n   \\text{Sensitivity} = \\left(\\frac{75}{199}\\right) \\times 100 \\approx 37.69\\%\n   \\]\n\nThus, the sensitivity of the parallel test is approximately 37.69%.\n\nANSWER: D. 37.69%."
"To find the point prevalence on 2010/6/1, follow these steps:\n\n1. **Identify the number of active cases on 2010/6/1:**\n   Check which cases are ongoing on this date. From the graph, cases 2, 3, 5, 6, 8, 9, 10, 12, 13, 15, and 17 are active.\n\n2. **Count the active cases:**\n   There are 11 active cases on 2010/6/1.\n\n3. **Calculate the point prevalence:**\n   \\[\n   \\text{Point prevalence} = \\left(\\frac{\\text{Number of active cases at a point in time}}{\\text{Total population}}\\right) \\times 100\n   \\]\n\n   Plugging in the values:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Point prevalence} = \\left(\\frac{11}{2528}\\right) \\times 100 \\approx 0.435\\%\n   \\]\n\n4. **Match the calculated prevalence to the given options:**\n\n   \\[\n   \\text{Closest option is 0.44\\% (D)}\n   \\]\n\nANSWER: D"
