"To calculate the odds of an infection in Hospital B:\n\n1. **Identify the number of infections in Hospital B**: 27.\n2. **Identify the number of no infections in Hospital B**: 594.\n3. **Calculate the odds of an infection**:\n   \\[\n   \\text{Odds of infection} = \\frac{\\text{Number of Infections}}{\\text{Number of No Infections}} = \\frac{27}{594}\n   \\]\n\n4. **Perform the division**:\n   \\[\n   \\frac{27}{594} \\approx 0.0454545\n   \\]\n\n5. **Round to three decimal places**: 0.045.\n\nANSWER: D."
"To calculate the positive likelihood ratio (+LR), we use the formula:\n\n\\[\n+LR = \\frac{\\text{Sensitivity}}{1 - \\text{Specificity}}\n\\]\n\n**Step 1: Calculate Sensitivity**\n\nSensitivity is the probability of a positive test result given that the patient has the disease.\n\n\\[\n\\text{Sensitivity} = \\frac{\\text{True Positives}}{\\text{True Positives + False Negatives}}\n\\]\n\nHere, True Positives = 71 and False Negatives = 29.\n\n\\[\n\\text{Sensitivity} = \\frac{71}{71 + 29} = \\frac{71}{100} = 0.71\n\\]\n\n**Step 2: Calculate Specificity**\n\nSpecificity is the probability of a negative test result given that the patient does not have the disease.\n\n\\[\n\\text{Specificity} = \\frac{\\text{True Negatives}}{\\text{True Negatives + False Positives}}\n\\]\n\nHere, True Negatives = 192 and False Positives = 8.\n\n\\[\n\\text{Specificity} = \\frac{192}{192 + 8} = \\frac{192}{200} = 0.96\n\\]\n\n**Step 3: Calculate +LR**\n\n\\[\n+LR = \\frac{0.71}{1 - 0.96} = \\frac{0.71}{0.04} = 17.75\n\\]\n\nANSWER: A. 17.75"
"To calculate the protective rate of the vaccine against influenza A, we use the following formula:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Protective Rate} = \\left( \\frac{\\text{Attack Rate in Control Group} - \\text{Attack Rate in Vaccinated Group}}{\\text{Attack Rate in Control Group}} \\right) \\times 100\n\\]\n\n**Step-by-step calculation:**\n\n1. **Attack Rate in Control Group for Influenza A:**\n\n   \\[\n   \\text{Attack Rate in Control} = \\frac{\\text{Number of Infections in Control Group}}{\\text{Total Number in Control Group}} = \\frac{35}{178}\n   \\]\n\n2. **Attack Rate in Vaccinated Group for Influenza A:**\n\n   \\[\n   \\text{Attack Rate in Vaccinated} = \\frac{\\text{Number of Infections in Vaccinated Group}}{\\text{Total Number in Vaccinated Group}} = \\frac{8}{199}\n   \\]\n\n3. **Calculating Protective Rate:**\n\n   \\[\n   \\text{Protective Rate} = \\left( \\frac{\\frac{35}{178} - \\frac{8}{199}}{\\frac{35}{178}} \\right) \\times 100\n   \\]\n\n4. **Simplifying the Numbers:**\n\n   - Calculate \\(\\frac{35}{178} \\approx 0.1966\\)\n   - Calculate \\(\\frac{8}{199} \\approx 0.0402\\)\n\n5. **Protective Rate:**\n\n   \\[\n   \\text{Protective Rate} = \\left( \\frac{0.1966 - 0.0402}{0.1966} \\right) \\times 100 \\approx 79.54\\%\n   \\]\n\nTherefore, the protective rate is approximately 80%.\n\nANSWER: D."
"To calculate the incidence density of EV71-related Hand, Foot, and Mouth Disease with Combined Neurological Complications in the placebo group, use the formula:\n\n\\[ \\text{Incidence Density} = \\frac{\\text{Number of New Cases}}{\\text{Person Years}} \\times 1000 \\]\n\nFrom the table:\n\n- New cases (Placebo group) = 8\n- Person years (Placebo group) = 4873.0\n\nPlug the values into the formula:\n\n\\[ \\text{Incidence Density} = \\frac{8}{4873.0} \\times 1000 \\]\n\n\\[ \\text{Incidence Density} \\approx 1.64 \\]\n\nRounding to one decimal place, the incidence density is approximately 1.6 per 1000 person years.\n\nANSWER: C. 1.6."
"To calculate the false negative rate, use the formula:\n\n\\[ \\text{False Negative Rate} = \\frac{\\text{False Negatives}}{\\text{False Negatives} + \\text{True Positives}} \\]\n\nFrom the table:\n- False Negatives = 8 (patients who were missed by ultrasound)\n- True Positives = 87 (patients correctly identified by ultrasound)\n\nPlug these numbers into the formula:\n\n\\[ \\text{False Negative Rate} = \\frac{8}{8 + 87} = \\frac{8}{95} \\approx 0.084 \\]\n\nSince the closest option to 0.084 is 0.08,\n\nANSWER: C. 0.08."
