"Based on the image, the term \"organic solidarity\" is used, which refers to a concept introduced by sociologist \u00c9mile Durkheim. Organic solidarity is characteristic of modern, industrial societies. It arises when the specialization of work creates interdependence among individuals, as they rely on each other to fulfill different roles and functions. This is opposed to \"mechanical solidarity,\" which is found in more traditional, homogenous societies where individuals perform similar tasks.\n\nNow, let's evaluate the options:\n\nA. Hunter-gatherer societies are typically characterized by mechanical solidarity, as they have less division of labor and a more uniform way of life.\n\nB. Industrial societies are marked by a high division of labor, specialization, and interdependence among individuals, which aligns with the concept of organic solidarity.\n\nC. Agricultural societies may have some division of labor, but they lean more toward mechanical solidarity, especially in pre-industrial settings.\n\nD. Feudal societies are more hierarchical and less characterized by the kind of interdependent specialization seen in industrial societies.\n\nGiven this analysis, the phenomenon of organic solidarity is most likely to exist in industrial societies.\n\nANSWER: B."
