"In the functionalist perspective on the rights and responsibilities of a sick person (often referred to as the \"sick role\"), the following are generally included:\n\nA. The sick person is not responsible for his or her condition - This is a part of the sick role, as people are not blamed for being ill.\n\nB. The sick person must try to get better - This is also part of the sick role, emphasizing the responsibility to recover.\n\nC. The sick person can take as long as he or she wants to get better - This is not part of the sick role. The sick role typically involves an expectation to get better within a reasonable timeframe in order to return to society.\n\nD. The sick person is exempt from the normal duties of society - This is a part of the sick role, as being sick provides a legitimate excuse to be exempt from daily responsibilities.\n\nTherefore, the option that is not part of the sick role is:\n\nANSWER: C."
